{"MINDMAP_V2_NOTES_N4":{"n4-001||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜んです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜んです","patternReading":"んです","coreMeaning":"Explain reasons / express feelings","slug":"core","definition":"〜んです (＝〜のです) is added to the END of a sentence to **explain a reason** or **express feelings / personal thoughts**. It turns a dry statement into one with context: “because…,” “the thing is…”. When Japanese people hear 〜んです, they understand that the speaker has a personal situation to share.","nuance":"It is casual spoken Japanese, used daily with friends, family, and coworkers. More formal writing usually uses 〜のです. 〜んです is stronger and more emotional than a regular 〜です sentence; it is close to “you see!” or “that’s because…” in English. In questions, 〜んですか shows concern or surprise, rather than asking a bare question.","structure":null,"examples":[{"scene":"Asking to leave early because of a headache","tokens":[{"t":"頭","r":"あたま","m":"head"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"痛い","r":"いたい","m":"hurt"},{"t":"んです","r":"んです","m":"because"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have a headache, you see."},{"scene":"Revealing that you are studying Japanese","tokens":[{"t":"実は","r":"じつは","m":"actually"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強して","r":"べんきょうして","m":"study"},{"t":"いる","m":"be doing"},{"t":"んです","r":"んです","m":"because"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Actually, I’m studying Japanese."},{"scene":"Recommending a tasty cake to a friend","tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"ケーキ","r":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おいしい","r":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"んです","r":"んです","m":"because"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"This cake is really delicious!"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-001||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜んです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜んです","patternReading":"んです","coreMeaning":"Explain reasons / express feelings","slug":"forms","definition":"With verbs and い-adjectives, 〜んです attaches directly to the **plain form** (普通形): 行くんです, 高いんです. With nouns and な-adjectives, **な** is required before んです: 学生なんです, 好きなんです. Negative and past forms also follow the plain form: 行かないんです, 行ったんです.","nuance":"Never attach んです after ます-form or です-form. This is a common beginner mistake. For more formal speech or writing, use 〜のです; in everyday conversation, 〜んです is standard and natural.","structure":[{"lhs":"V plain form","rhs":"んです","note":"行くんです・行ったんです・行かないんです"},{"lhs":"Aい plain form","rhs":"んです","note":"高いんです・高くないんです"},{"lhs":"Aな／N","rhs":"な + んです","note":"有名なんです・学生なんです"}],"examples":[{"scene":"Answering the phone while heading out","tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"んです","r":"んです","m":"because"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m leaving now."},{"scene":"Explaining why you love your mother’s cooking","tokens":[{"t":"とても","m":"very"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"なんです","r":"なんです","m":"because"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I really like it."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-001||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜んです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜んです","patternReading":"んです","coreMeaning":"Explain reasons / express feelings","slug":"usage","definition":"〜んです is used when: (1) answering どうして? by giving a reason; (2) voluntarily revealing personal information with a confiding tone; (3) asking about a reason or situation with んですか; and (4) emphasizing feelings with んですよ.","nuance":"If the listener does not expect an explanation, んです may sound defensive or as if you want to share something. Do not overuse it in objective statements. In questions, んですか is much gentler than directly asking “why?”—it shows concern for the other person’s situation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"scene":"Wondering why your friend studies Japanese","tokens":[{"t":"どうして","r":"どうして","m":"why"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強して","r":"べんきょうして","m":"study"},{"t":"いる","m":"be doing"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"why is that?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why are you studying Japanese?"},{"scene":"Recommending an interesting book to a close friend","tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"とても","m":"very"},{"t":"面白い","r":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"んですよ","r":"んですよ","m":"you know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is really interesting!"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-001||compare":{"title":"⚖️ 〜ので・〜から comparison","sub":"〜んです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜んです","patternReading":"んです","coreMeaning":"Explain reasons / express feelings","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps distinguish 〜んです from two common reason structures. The key point: 〜んです is an **independent sentence ending**, while 〜ので / 〜から are **connectors to a following clause**. 〜んです adds a personal “explaining or confiding” tone that 〜ので / 〜から do not.","nuance":"Use 〜ので in formal writing or when giving an objective reason. Use 〜んです when expressing personal feelings. Compared with 〜から, 〜んです does not work with direct commands or requests.","structure":null,"examples":[{"scene":"Giving a reason for not entering a restaurant","tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"んです","r":"んです","m":"because"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This restaurant is expensive."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ので","point":"〜ので connects two clauses and gives an objective reason → 高いので、買いません。〜んです ends the sentence and adds feeling → 高いんです。"},{"vs":"〜から","point":"〜から often connects to a command/request or emphasizes a reason; 〜んです is more confiding and is not used to give commands. Compare: '行かないからね' vs '行かないんです'."}]}},"n4-001||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜んです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜んです","patternReading":"んです","coreMeaning":"Explain reasons / express feelings","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Three common mistakes are: (1) attaching んです after ます, (2) omitting な with nouns or な-adjectives, and (3) using んです in statements that do not need an explanation. All three make the sentence sound unnatural.","nuance":"Once you avoid these mistakes, your Japanese will sound much more natural. Before speaking, ask: am I answering “why?” or expressing a feeling? If not, leave out んです.","structure":null,"examples":[{"scene":"Asking for a museum ticket discount","tokens":[{"t":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"なんです","r":"なんです","m":"because"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, I’m a student."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 行きますんです。 → ✅ 行くんです。","❌ 学生んです。 → ✅ 学生なんです。","❌ 明日は日曜日なんです。 → ✅ 明日は日曜日です。 (when simply stating an objective fact)","❌ 高いなんです。 → ✅ 高いんです。 (do not add な after an い-adjective)"],"compare":null}},"n4-001||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜んです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜んです","patternReading":"んです","coreMeaning":"Explain reasons / express feelings","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture ん as a lid lifting slightly on a box of personal thoughts. When you see ん in んです, think: the speaker is about to **explain** or **reveal** something. の → ん is simply a quick, shortened form used in conversation.","nuance":"This mnemonic keeps you from treating んです as a meaningless particle. It always highlights the grammar: a sentence with んです carries a reason or feeling.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-002||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V／Aんですか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aんですか","patternReading":"んですか","coreMeaning":"Ask why when something stands out—showing concern, gently","slug":"core","definition":"〜んですか asks for the reason when the speaker has noticed something in the situation and wants to understand it. It is not a neutral question like 〜ますか; it implies: “I noticed ~—why is that?” This pattern is very common in everyday conversation when showing concern or attention.","nuance":"It sounds familiar and gentle, more caring than a regular question. It can be used with superiors, but may sound probing, so add すみません for politeness. In formal writing, use **のですか** instead of んですか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べなかった","r":"たべなかった","m":"didn't eat"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You didn't eat breakfast?","scene":"You notice they skipped breakfast"},{"tokens":[{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"energy"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","m":"don't have"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You don't seem well. Is something wrong?","scene":"A gloomy coworker seems upset"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-002||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V／Aんですか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aんですか","patternReading":"んですか","coreMeaning":"Ask why when something stands out—showing concern, gently","slug":"forms","definition":"Attach **んですか** to the plain form of a verb or adjective. With nouns and な-adjectives, add **な**, giving **なんですか**. Never attach it to the ます-form.","nuance":"ん is the spoken contraction of の. In formal writing, use **のですか**. With verbs, use Vる for present, Vた for past, and Vている for an ongoing action.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形・た形）","rhs":"んですか","note":"Verb: 行く → 行くんですか／行ったんですか"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"んですか","note":"高い → 高いんですか"},{"lhs":"Aな／N","rhs":"なんですか","note":"静か → 静かなんですか／雨 → 雨なんですか"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"勉強して","r":"べんきょうして","m":"study"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you study every day?","scene":"You always see them carrying books"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"テスト","m":"test"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"難しかった","r":"むずかしかった","m":"was difficult"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Was the Japanese test difficult?","scene":"They look tired after the test"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-002||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"V／Aんですか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aんですか","patternReading":"んですか","coreMeaning":"Ask why when something stands out—showing concern, gently","slug":"usage","definition":"Main uses: **① notice something unusual → ask why**; **② see an action → ask its purpose or destination**; **③ use 〜んですね to empathize or confirm** “so that's how it is.” To ask the reason more strongly, どうして is often included.","nuance":"Very natural in everyday conversation with friends, close coworkers, and family. With superiors, avoid a bare んですか without clear context; add すみません to soften the probing tone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうして","m":"why"},{"t":"遅刻した","r":"ちこくした","m":"was late"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why were you late?","scene":"A manager asks a late employee"},{"tokens":[{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強した","r":"べんきょうした","m":"studied"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Where did you study Japanese?","scene":"You praise their Japanese and ask"},{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買う","r":"かう","m":"buy"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What are you going to buy?","scene":"You see them studying a supermarket shelf"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-002||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing similar patterns","sub":"V／Aんですか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aんですか","patternReading":"んですか","coreMeaning":"Ask why when something stands out—showing concern, gently","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick distinctions: **〜んですか** is a question, **〜んです** is an answer or explanation; **〜ますか** asks neutrally without needing context; **〜んですね** shows empathy after understanding.","nuance":"〜んですか normally needs context or something observed. For a general question, do not add ん; otherwise it can sound strange.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうした","m":"what happened"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What happened?","scene":"You notice they seem worried"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜んです","point":"〜んです gives a reason or explanation; 〜んですか asks for one. Ex: 熱があるんです (answer) → どうしたんですか (question)."},{"vs":"〜ますか","point":"Vますか asks neutrally; Vんですか follows an observation. Ex: どこへ行きますか (general) → どこへ行くんですか (you see a suitcase)."},{"vs":"〜んですね","point":"んですね means “so that's how it is,” responding after an explanation; んですか asks before the explanation."}]}},"n4-002||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V／Aんですか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aんですか","patternReading":"んですか","coreMeaning":"Ask why when something stands out—showing concern, gently","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes often come from using the ます-form out of habit or confusing い-adjectives with な-adjectives.","nuance":"With an Aい, never add な. With an Aな or noun, な is required before ん.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What are you going to eat?","scene":"You look at the menu together"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 行きますんですか。 → ✅ 行くんですか。","❌ 高いなんですか。 → ✅ 高いんですか。","❌ 静かだんですか。 → ✅ 静かなんですか。","❌ 雨んですか。 → ✅ 雨なんですか。"],"compare":null}},"n4-002||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"V／Aんですか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aんですか","patternReading":"んですか","coreMeaning":"Ask why when something stands out—showing concern, gently","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **ん is a shortened の**, which adds an explanatory meaning. When you **want someone to explain something you just noticed**, add **んですか** to the end of the question.","nuance":"Imagine seeing someone carrying a large suitcase on the street. You think, “Where are you going?” That is exactly when to use んですか.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-002||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V／Aんですか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aんですか","patternReading":"んですか","coreMeaning":"Ask why when something stands out—showing concern, gently","slug":"phrases","definition":"Phrases with んですか appear frequently on the JLPT and in everyday life. Learning them as chunks helps you speak naturally.","nuance":"These phrases often combine with どうして, どこ, 何, or どうした to ask about a reason, place, or purpose.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうした","m":"what happened"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What happened?","scene":"You see them sitting alone, looking sad"},{"tokens":[{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"んですか","r":"んですか","m":"asking why?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Where are you going?","scene":"They put on a coat to go out"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-003||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nなんです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなんです","patternReading":"なんです","coreMeaning":"It's N — explaining with a noun","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern explains something using a noun. なんです is the natural spoken form of なのです. The な linking a noun to んです is required: N + な + んです = Nなんです. It often implies “that's why / you see,” making the sentence softer and friendlier than the plain statement Nです.","nuance":"Common in everyday conversation, from casual to lightly polite speech. It is not highly formal; use なのです in formal writing. It expresses explanation, confirmation, or a suggested reason, and sounds natural when answering んですか or clarifying your situation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"誕生日","r":"たんじょうび","m":"birthday"},{"t":"なんです"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Today is my birthday.","scene":"You ask why I am happy today; I explain why."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本人","r":"にほんじん","m":"Japanese person"},{"t":"なんです"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She is Japanese, you know.","scene":"Introducing a foreign friend to someone."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-003||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nなんです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなんです","patternReading":"なんです","coreMeaning":"It's N — explaining with a noun","slug":"forms","definition":"General formula: N + な + んです. Since nouns and な-adjectives cannot connect directly to んです, な acts as the bridge. Verbs and い-adjectives do not need it: 行くんです / 高いんです. Negative and past forms use the noun's own tense: じゃない, だった.","nuance":"This is a friendly explanatory form common in conversation. For a more formal tone, use N + な + のです. In polite questions: Nなんですか.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"な + んです","note":"present affirmative: Nなんです"},{"lhs":"Nじゃない","rhs":"んです","note":"negative: Nじゃないんです"},{"lhs":"Nだった","rhs":"んです","note":"past: Nだったんです"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"な + んです","note":"な-adjectives also add な: 有名なんです"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"なんです"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am a student, you see.","scene":"Answering when asked your occupation."},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"day off"},{"t":"だったんです"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yesterday was a day off, you see.","scene":"Explaining why we did not meet in class."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"有名","r":"ゆうめい","m":"famous"},{"t":"なんです"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That shop is famous, you know.","scene":"Pointing out a famous restaurant."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-003||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Nなんです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなんです","patternReading":"なんです","coreMeaning":"It's N — explaining with a noun","slug":"usage","definition":"Nなんです appears when the speaker wants the listener to understand a situation or reason connected to a noun. It can introduce yourself (私は学生なんです), answer a question (そうです、Nなんです), or ask for confirmation (Nなんですか？).","nuance":"Unlike Nです, which simply presents information, Nなんです suggests “this matters / this is the reason.” With friends, use Nなんだ (casual); with superiors, Nなんです already provides polite speech (丁寧語).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"なんですか"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is that person a teacher?","scene":"Seeing a stranger teach and asking a nearby friend."},{"tokens":[{"t":"はい"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"なんです"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yes, I am a Japanese teacher.","scene":"Answering “What do you do?”"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-003||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Nです vs Nなんです","sub":"Nなんです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなんです","patternReading":"なんです","coreMeaning":"It's N — explaining with a noun","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick comparison of three easily confused patterns: Nです is a neutral statement; Nなんです explains with feeling or a reason; Nなのです is the more formal counterpart of なんです, used in writing or speeches.","nuance":"In conversation, Nなんです sounds friendlier and more natural than Nです. With verbs and い-adjectives, use only んです—never な: 行くんです, 高いんです.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a book.","scene":"Pointing to something objectively."},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"なんです"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a book, you see (so I need to read it).","scene":"Explaining why you are holding the book."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Nです","point":"Nです simply states a fact; Nなんです adds an explanatory reason. Compare: これは本です (statement) ↔ これは本なんです (explaining why I need it)."},{"vs":"〜んです (V/Aい)","point":"Before んです, verbs and い-adjectives use the plain form directly; only nouns and な-adjectives add な: 行くんです ○ / 行くなんです ✕."},{"vs":"Nなのです","point":"Same meaning, but なのです is formal and written; なんです is its natural spoken contraction."}]}},"n4-003||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nなんです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなんです","patternReading":"なんです","coreMeaning":"It's N — explaining with a noun","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often forget な because English has no equivalent noun-linking pattern. Another common mix-up is the noun past form: it is だった, not でした + んです.","nuance":"These mistakes make the sentence grammatically wrong or sound very unnatural. Memorize: noun/なA + な + んです; V/Aい + んです.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"なんです"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am a student, you see.","scene":"Correct example of the pattern."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私は学生ですんです。 → ✅ 私は学生なんです。","❌ 私は学生でしたんです。 → ✅ 私は学生だったんです。","❌ あした、行くなんです。 → ✅ あした、行くんです。","❌ ここは静かですんです。 → ✅ ここは静かなんです。"],"compare":null}},"n4-003||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nなんです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなんです","patternReading":"なんです","coreMeaning":"It's N — explaining with a noun","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine んです as the friend of explanations. Nouns cannot “stick” to it on their own, so little な stands between them as a bridge: N + な + んです. Remember: “When a noun meets んです, call な over to say hello.”","nuance":"Just remember to add な before んです when the preceding word is a noun or な-adjective. い-adjectives and verbs do not need it.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-003||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nなんです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなんです","patternReading":"なんです","coreMeaning":"It's N — explaining with a noun","slug":"phrases","definition":"In conversation, Nなんです often combines with けど / が to open a request, or よ / か to emphasize or ask. These phrases appear frequently in exams and everyday speech.","nuance":"〜なんですけど sounds polite and soft when making a request: “There is something I want to say...” なんですよ adds the nuance “this is something you should know.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あのう","m":"um"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"なんですけど","m":"it's ~, but"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Um, this is my book, but...","scene":"Politely asking for your book back."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-004||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V可能形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V可能形","patternReading":"かのうけい","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability / enabling conditions)","slug":"core","definition":"V可能形 is the verb form used to express **being able to do something**. It focuses on the subject's **ability** or on **conditions that allow** an action, in the present, past, or negative. It is one of the basic N4 forms, introduced in Lesson 27 of Minna no Nihongo.","nuance":"It is used in both speech and writing. Compared with **Vことができます**, V可能形 is **shorter and more natural** in conversation. It is not a formal way to ask permission like **〜てもいいですか**, but V可能形 + か can sound **gently polite and respectful of the listener's space**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"話せます","r":"はなせます","m":"can speak"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not...much"},{"t":"読めません","r":"よめません","m":"cannot read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can speak Japanese, but I can't read kanji very well.","scene":"Introducing your language abilities"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ころ","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ピアノ","m":"piano"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"弾けました","r":"ひけました","m":"could play"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I could play the piano when I was a child.","scene":"Talking about a childhood hobby"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ホテル","m":"hotel"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ペット","m":"pets"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飼えません","r":"かえません","m":"cannot keep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Pets cannot be kept at this hotel.","scene":"Reading hotel rules"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-004||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V可能形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V可能形","patternReading":"かのうけい","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability / enabling conditions)","slug":"forms","definition":"The potential form is created according to the three verb groups. Once you know the form, add ます/ない/た as with ordinary verbs. Most importantly, **を usually changes to が** when used with V可能形.","nuance":"The conjugation table below covers both plain and ます forms. Group 2 verbs (る→られる) look **exactly like the passive form**, so context is needed to identify the meaning.","structure":[{"lhs":"V group 1 (う→える)","rhs":"書く→書ける / 話す→話せる","note":"Change the final う sound to え + る"},{"lhs":"V group 2 (る→られる)","rhs":"食べる→食べられる / 見る→見られる","note":"Drop る and add られる"},{"lhs":"V group 3","rhs":"する→できる / 来る→来られる","note":"Two irregular verbs, but easy to remember"},{"lhs":"Particle を → が","rhs":"日本語を話す → 日本語が話せる","note":"The object usually changes to が"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"書けます","r":"かけます","m":"can write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can write kanji.","scene":"Answering whether you can write kanji"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"yet"},{"t":"泳げます","r":"およげます","m":"can swim"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Can you swim now?","scene":"Asking a friend after swimming lessons"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: dictionary form (辞書形) → 可能形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — godan む · く · ぐ → める / ける / げる","Change the final sound from the う row to the え row, then add る","飲む（のむ） → `飲める（のめる）` (drink) ・ 書く（かく） → `書ける（かける）` (write) ・ 泳ぐ（およぐ） → `泳げる（およげる）` (swim) ・ 読む（よむ） → `読める（よめる）` (read)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — godan す · う · つ · る → せる / える / てる / れる","Change the final sound from the う row to the え row, then add る","話す（はなす） → `話せる（はなせる）` (speak) ・ 買う（かう） → `買える（かえる）` (buy) ・ 待つ（まつ） → `待てる（まてる）` (wait) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰れる（かえれる）` (return)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — ichidan: drop る + られる (standard)","Drop る, then add られる","食べる（たべる） → `食べられる（たべられる）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見られる（みられる）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きられる（おきられる）` (wake up) ・ 教える（おしえる） → `教えられる（おしえられる）` (teach)"],["**Special case** — ichidan: ら抜き言葉 (colloquial)","Drop ら → drop る + れる (used only in conversation)","食べれる（たべれる） → `食べられる（たべられる (standard)）` (can eat (colloquial)) ・ 見れる（みれる） → `見られる（みられる (standard)）` (can watch (colloquial))"],["**Group III — Irregular** — Irregular: する → できる","する → できる (completely different; memorize)","する → `できる` (do) ・ 来る（くる） → `来られる（こられる）` (come) ・ 勉強する（べんきょうする） → `勉強できる（べんきょうできる）` (study)"]]}}},"n4-004||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"V可能形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V可能形","patternReading":"かのうけい","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability / enabling conditions)","slug":"usage","definition":"V可能形 talks about **ability** arising from skill, habit, conditions, or rules. It can also be used in questions to **ask for permission gently**.","nuance":"When asking “Is it allowed?”, 〜てもいいですか is quite direct; V可能形 + か sounds **objective and polite**, making it suitable with people you do not know well.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"タバコ","m":"cigarettes"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"吸えます","r":"すえます","m":"can smoke"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Can I smoke in this room?","scene":"Before smoking in a guest room"},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"歩けます","r":"あるけます","m":"can walk"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can walk there from the station.","scene":"Giving directions to a visitor"},{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"座れます","r":"すわれます","m":"can sit"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, may I sit here?","scene":"Wanting to sit beside a stranger on a train"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-004||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"V可能形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V可能形","patternReading":"かのうけい","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability / enabling conditions)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes **V可能形** from three commonly confused patterns: **Vことができます** (same meaning, different formality), **V受身形** (passive form, which looks identical in Group 2), and **見えます/聞こえます** (natural perception without intention). Understanding these differences helps you choose the right context.","nuance":"For Group 2 verbs, Vられる has two meanings: potential and passive. The listener uses context to determine which meaning is intended. **見える/聞こえる** describe what the eyes or ears **receive naturally**, without intention.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行けます","r":"いけます","m":"can go"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Can you go now?","scene":"Asking whether someone can leave now"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vことができます","point":"Both mean “can do,” but V可能形 is shorter and more natural in conversation; Vことができます is more formal and common in writing."},{"vs":"V受身形 (〜られる)","point":"Group 2 forms look identical (食べられる). Use context: does the subject do it themselves (potential), or are they affected by it (passive)?"},{"vs":"Nが見えます・聞こえます","point":"見える/聞こえる = received naturally; V可能形 = based on ability or conditions."}]}},"n4-004||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V可能形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V可能形","patternReading":"かのうけい","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability / enabling conditions)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often **keep を** with V可能形 or incorrectly form Group 1 verbs by adding られる. Correct these according to the standard rules.","nuance":"The wrong particle may not change the meaning, but it sounds unnatural; a wrong verb form can create a different meaning, such as the passive.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"弁当","r":"べんとう","m":"boxed meal"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"買えます","r":"かえます","m":"can buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can buy boxed meals at this shop.","scene":"Talking about a nearby bento shop"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 日本語を話られます。 → ✅ 日本語が話せます。","❌ 漢字を書られます。 → ✅ 漢字が書けます。","❌ ピアノが弾かれます。 → ✅ ピアノが弾けます。","❌ ここでタバコを吸られますか。 → ✅ ここでタバコが吸えますか。"],"compare":null}},"n4-004||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"V可能形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V可能形","patternReading":"かのうけい","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability / enabling conditions)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember V可能形 with this formula: **u changes to e, then add ru** for Group 1; **drop ru and add rareru** for Group 2. Do not forget the two exceptions: する→できる and くる→こられる.","nuance":"When you see を change to が, let your brain recognize “this is the potential form.” Picture **locking を away and replacing it with が**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-004||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V可能形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V可能形","patternReading":"かのうけい","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability / enabling conditions)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These verbs frequently appear in the potential form in daily life: 話せる, 読める, 書ける, 食べられる, 飲める, and できる. Memorizing them as chunks helps you respond quickly.","nuance":"At a restaurant, “〜が食べられますか” is a very natural way to check whether someone can eat a particular dish.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"刺身","r":"さしみ","m":"sashimi"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"食べられます","r":"たべられます","m":"can eat"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Can you eat sashimi?","scene":"Asking a friend at a sushi restaurant"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-005||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"みえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"See / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"core","definition":"**Nが見えます／聞こえます** describes an object or sound naturally entering the speaker’s field of vision or hearing **without any effort**. **見えます** = be visible (the eyes receive it); **聞こえます** = be audible (the ears receive it). The speaker is only the observer; the true subject is the object or sound.","nuance":"This is fully objective and non-volitional. It works in both spoken and written Japanese, especially when describing scenery or the surrounding environment. To stress “to me,” use **私には…が見えます**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"海","r":"うみ","m":"sea"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えます","r":"みえます","m":"can see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can see the sea from the room.","scene":"Discussing the view from a hotel room."},{"tokens":[{"t":"隣","r":"となり","m":"next door"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえます","r":"きこえます","m":"can hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Music can be heard from the next room.","scene":"Complaining about a neighbor’s music."},{"tokens":[{"t":"霧","r":"きり","m":"fog"},{"t":"で","m":"because of"},{"t":"山","r":"やま","m":"mountain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えません","r":"みえません","m":"cannot see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the fog, the mountain cannot be seen.","scene":"Locals discussing the weather early in the morning."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-005||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"みえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"See / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: **N** (object/sound) **が** + **見えます / 聞こえます**. Add the viewing or listening location with **から** (source) or **で** (place). These two special verbs describe natural perception and cannot be replaced by other verbs.","nuance":"Both are Group 2 verbs and conjugate normally. **見えます** does not mean “can watch TV”; it means an object is visible. Because they are non-volitional, they do not normally take causative, imperative, or volitional forms.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"が ＋ 見えます","note":"N is an object/person/scene visible in the field of vision"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"が ＋ 聞こえます","note":"N is a sound/noise reaching the ears"},{"lhs":"N1 ＋ から／で","rhs":"N2 が ＋ 見えます／聞こえます","note":"N1 is the viewing/listening place; から marks the source, で marks the location"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"富士山","r":"ふじさん","m":"Mount Fuji"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えます","r":"みえます","m":"can see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can see Mount Fuji from the window.","scene":"Describing an office window view to a coworker."},{"tokens":[{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"鳥","r":"とり","m":"bird"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"声","r":"こえ","m":"voice"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえます","r":"きこえます","m":"can hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Birdsong can be heard from outside.","scene":"Hearing birds through the open window."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-005||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"みえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"See / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern is often used to **introduce a place, view, or surrounding environment** or to **describe something perceived unexpectedly**. The speaker is not deliberately searching; the object or sound appears naturally.","nuance":"Moderately polite and very natural in conversation. It simply reports perception, without strong emotion. To emphasize that something cannot be seen or heard for a reason, use the negative with particle で.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"桜","r":"さくら","m":"cherry blossoms"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えます","r":"みえます","m":"can see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can see cherry blossoms from this room.","scene":"A landlord showing a guest the room’s view."},{"tokens":[{"t":"隣","r":"となり","m":"next door"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"声","r":"こえ","m":"voice"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえます","r":"きこえます","m":"can hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can hear the neighbor’s voice.","scene":"Hearing a neighbor while working at home."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-005||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with V potential form","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"みえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"See / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"compare","definition":"The key contrast: **Nが見えます／聞こえます** describes the eyes or ears naturally receiving something, while **V potential form** (見られます・聞けます) describes the ability to deliberately look or listen. **見えます** = an object is in view; **見られます** = you can watch it because conditions allow it.","nuance":"Use 見られます for watching TV, programs, or movies. Use 見えます for scenery in front of you. Likewise, 聞こえます means a sound reaches your ears, while 聞けます means you can listen to it deliberately.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"海","r":"うみ","m":"sea"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えます","r":"みえます","m":"can see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can see the sea from here.","scene":"Looking out to sea from a high hill."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V可能形（見られます・聞けます）","point":"The potential form emphasizes deliberate ability; ～が見えます／聞こえます emphasizes natural perception. Example: テレビが見られます (can watch TV) vs テレビが見えます (TV is in view)."},{"vs":"見る／聞く","point":"見る/聞く are deliberate actions; 見える/聞こえる happen naturally without trying. 「音楽を聞く」 = listen to music deliberately; 「音楽が聞こえる」 = hear music unintentionally."}]}},"n4-005||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"みえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"See / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"English learners may confuse this because “see” and “hear” can take direct objects in English. In Japanese, **見える・聞こえる** are intransitive verbs, so the object or sound must be the subject with **が**, not **を**. They are also easily confused with the **potential form** when you mean “can watch.”","nuance":"The error **私が見えます** is especially serious because it means “I am visible” rather than “I can see.” To say “I can see,” use **私には～が見えます** or omit the understood subject.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"で","m":"at"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"音","r":"おと","m":"sound"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえます","r":"きこえます","m":"can hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can hear the sound of rain outside.","scene":"Hearing rain while indoors."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 海を見えます。 → ✅ 海が見えます。（the object is the subject, so use が, not を）","❌ テレビが見えます（= TV is visible） → ✅ テレビが見られます。（to say “can watch TV,” use the potential form）","❌ 私が海が見えます。 → ✅ 私には海が見えます。／✅ 海が見えます。（the perceiver uses は/には, not が）","❌ 音楽を聞こえます。 → ✅ 音楽が聞こえます。"],"compare":null}},"n4-005||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"みえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"See / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture it this way: an object **runs into your eyes** (見える), and a sound **slips into your ears** (聞こえる). You do not need to do anything. In contrast, **見る** (look) and **聞く** (listen) require deliberate action.","nuance":"Because these verbs are non-volitional, you cannot say **見えたい** (want something to appear) or **聞こえよう** (let it be heard). This helps you remember that they do not take volitional or imperative forms.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-005||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"みえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"See / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are common fixed phrases with **見える／聞こえる**. Memorizing them helps you use the pattern naturally.","nuance":"In conversation, Japanese speakers often use **～が見えますか？** to ask about visibility and **～が聞こえますか？** to ask about sound. The adverb **よく** means “clearly” here.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えます","r":"みえます","m":"can see"},{"t":"か","m":"question"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What can you see from here?","scene":"Asking a friend about the view from a balcony."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-006||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nしか〜ない · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nしか〜ない","patternReading":"しか〜ない","coreMeaning":"Only N — implies scarcity or insufficiency","slug":"core","definition":"Nしか〜ない means “only N,” with an added sense of scarcity or insufficiency. The speaker stresses that nothing else exists besides N and often feels it is not enough, with mild regret or dissatisfaction. Formally, the sentence must end in a negative form, but the actual meaning still affirms that N exists.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, from casual to polite. With friends, use しかない; with superiors, use しかありません. It is more subjective than だけ: だけ states a fact, while しか〜ない adds “only this much, no more,” often sounding like a complaint or regret.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"100円","r":"ひゃくえん","m":"100 yen"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"ありません","r":"ありません","m":"not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are only 100 yen left.","scene":"At a store, you open your wallet and find only 100 yen left."},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"5分","r":"ごふん","m":"5 minutes"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are only five minutes left.","scene":"You remind a coworker that only five minutes remain."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ひらがな","m":"hiragana"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"読めません","r":"よめません","m":"cannot read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can only read hiragana.","scene":"A Japanese person asks whether you can read kanji."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-006||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"Nしか〜ない · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nしか〜ない","patternReading":"しか〜ない","coreMeaning":"Only N — implies scarcity or insufficiency","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: N + しか + a negative verb. The negative part is usually ない, ありません, ません, or the past forms なかった／ありませんでした. This is a “negative form, affirmative meaning” pattern: 100円しかありません means “there are 100 yen, not nothing.”","nuance":"This pattern works with many verbs: あります／います (existence), できます (ability), and potential forms (読める・行ける…). If the noun is followed by に/で/と, しか comes AFTER the particle: 会社に田中さんしかいません. It also works with quantities and time: 5分しかない.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"しか + 動詞（ない形）","note":"The following clause is always negative; means “only N.”"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"しか + ありません／いません","note":"Used with inanimate/animate nouns; polite."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"しか + 可能形の否定","note":"Such as 読めません、話せません、できません."},{"lhs":"Nに／で／と","rhs":"しか + 動詞（ない形）","note":"If another particle appears, しか comes after it."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"残り時間","r":"のこりじかん","m":"remaining time"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"10分","r":"じゅっぷん","m":"10 minutes"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"ありません","r":"ありません","m":"not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are only ten minutes remaining.","scene":"A meeting is ending, so the person in charge reminds everyone."},{"tokens":[{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"いません","r":"いません","m":"not present"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Only Tanaka is at the office.","scene":"You arrive early and see only Tanaka there."},{"tokens":[{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"行けません","r":"いけません","m":"cannot go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can only go by bus.","scene":"A visitor asks how to get to the mountain."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-006||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Nしか〜ない · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nしか〜ない","patternReading":"しか〜ない","coreMeaning":"Only N — implies scarcity or insufficiency","slug":"usage","definition":"Japanese uses しか〜ない for small quantities, limited ability, or a sole option. In class: ひらがなしか読めません. In a store: これしかありません. At work: あと5分しかありません. The listener immediately understands “there is some, but only that much,” with a sense that it is insufficient.","nuance":"With friends, short sentences often omit the particle: お金しかないよ. With superiors, use ありません／ません for politeness. With これ（これしかない), it means “this is the last acceptable option.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あと","m":"remaining"},{"t":"ジュース","m":"juice"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"1本","r":"いっぽん","m":"one bottle"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"残って","r":"のこって","m":"remaining"},{"t":"いません","r":"いません","m":"not remain"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is only one bottle of juice left.","scene":"Your mother opens the refrigerator and sees the juice is almost gone."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"話せません","r":"はなせません","m":"cannot speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can speak only a little Japanese.","scene":"You introduce yourself to someone new."},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not have"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使おう","r":"つかおう","m":"let’s use"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is all we have, so let’s use it.","scene":"You need a wrench, but only one remains in the box."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-006||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with だけ","sub":"Nしか〜ない · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nしか〜ない","patternReading":"しか〜ない","coreMeaning":"Only N — implies scarcity or insufficiency","slug":"compare","definition":"English-speaking learners may confuse だけ and しか〜ない because both can mean “only.” The difference is that だけ is neutral, while しか〜ない stresses scarcity. 100円だけあります simply states that there are 100 yen. 100円しかありません also means there are 100 yen, but sounds like “only this much,” with regret or complaint. Also distinguish Vるしかない (have no choice but to), which uses a verb.","nuance":"For praise or neutral statements, だけ is safer. To complain “that’s too little” or “this is all there is,” use しか〜ない. Vるしかない is not about quantity; it means there is no other option and you must accept doing V.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"100円","r":"ひゃくえん","m":"100 yen"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"ありません","r":"ありません","m":"not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are only 100 yen (sadly, very little).","scene":"You open your wallet to pay and see very little money."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"だけ","point":"だけ is neutral; しか〜ない stresses scarcity and often regret. Compare: 100円だけあります (only 100 yen — neutral) → 100円しかありません (only 100 yen — feels too little)."},{"vs":"Vるしかない","point":"Nしか〜ない takes a noun and means “only N”; Vるしかない takes a verb and means “have no choice but to do V.” Example: バスでしか行けません (can only go by bus) vs 歩いていくしかない (have no choice but to walk)."}]}},"n4-006||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nしか〜ない · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nしか〜ない","patternReading":"しか〜ない","coreMeaning":"Only N — implies scarcity or insufficiency","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common mistakes are using しか with an affirmative clause or forgetting to make the verb negative. Remember: しか must be followed by ない/ありません/ません. Also, do not use しか for a neutral or positive “only”; use だけ instead.","nuance":"This mistake sounds immediately wrong to Japanese listeners: 100円しかあります is confusing because it uses しか with “there are.” It violates the form rule. Build the habit of checking: if the sentence has しか, it must end with a negative clause.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"ありません","r":"ありません","m":"not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is only a little time left.","scene":"An assistant reminds the boss that the meeting is nearly over."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 100円しかあります。 → ✅ 100円しかありません。","❌ ひらがなしか読めます。 → ✅ ひらがなしか読めません。","❌ 少ししかあります。 → ✅ 少ししかありません。","⚠️ だけ uses an affirmative, while しか requires a negative: 100円だけあります = 100円しかありません."],"compare":null}},"n4-006||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nしか〜ない · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nしか〜ない","patternReading":"しか〜ない","coreMeaning":"Only N — implies scarcity or insufficiency","slug":"memory","definition":"Use an image: a wallet with only one coin left — お金が100円しかない. “Only this much, no more” captures both the meaning and the regretful feeling. For a wordplay mnemonic, make up your own short phrase linking しか with “a tiny amount” and regret.","nuance":"The mantra “しか DEMANDS ない” helps prevent forgetting the negative. Whenever you write しか, immediately check whether a negative verb follows it.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-006||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nしか〜ない · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nしか〜ない","patternReading":"しか〜ない","coreMeaning":"Only N — implies scarcity or insufficiency","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases include これしかない (this is the only option), 少ししかない (only a little remains), and 1時間しかない (only one hour remains). They appear frequently on JLPT tests and in everyday conversation.","nuance":"Note that Vるしかない (have no choice but to) extends the pattern but uses a verb instead of a noun. In conversation, これしかない can also mean “this is the last resort.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"しか","r":"しか","m":"only"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is the only one left.","scene":"You choose what to buy because there are no other options."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-007||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V1stemながらV2 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V1stemながらV2","patternReading":"ながら","coreMeaning":"Do V1 while doing V2 — two simultaneous actions by the same person","slug":"core","definition":"〜ながら describes **two actions happening at the same time** by the same person. V1 is the **secondary/background** action, while V2 is the **main** action. For example, in '音楽を聞きながら、料理します', cooking is the main action and listening to music happens in the background.","nuance":"Used in both spoken and written Japanese, in casual and formal contexts. It emphasizes the **parallel** nature of two actions, not their order. Do not use it for two different people.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞きながら","r":"ききながら","m":"while listening"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"料理します","r":"りょうりします","m":"cook"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I cook while listening to music.","scene":"At home, playing music while preparing dinner"},{"tokens":[{"t":"テレビ","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ながら","r":"みながら","m":"while watching"},{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"rice"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat while watching TV.","scene":"At home in the evening, watching a favorite show while eating"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-007||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"V1stemながらV2 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V1stemながらV2","patternReading":"ながら","coreMeaning":"Do V1 while doing V2 — two simultaneous actions by the same person","slug":"forms","definition":"Take **V1 in its ます-stem form**, add **ながら**, and then add V2. V1 stays in the stem form and does **not** show tense or negation. All tense, past, and negative information appears in V2. For a negative sentence: '〜ながら、V2ません'.","nuance":"This is a neutral structure that sounds natural in everyday conversation. V1 is usually a short background action that does not need emphasis; V2 carries the main meaning. When both actions need emphasis, Japanese still uses ながら but stresses V2 in intonation.","structure":[{"lhs":"V1ます-stem","rhs":"ながら＋V2","note":"V1 is the background; V2 sets tense and negation."},{"lhs":"V1ます-stem","rhs":"ながら＋V2（た）","note":"Both actions are in the past: 聞きながら食べました。"},{"lhs":"V1ます-stem","rhs":"ながら＋V2（ません）","note":"Negation is placed on V2: 聞きながら勉強しません。"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞きながら","r":"ききながら","m":"while listening"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every morning, I go to school while listening to music.","scene":"Describing a morning routine on the way to school"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"話しながら","r":"はなしながら","m":"while talking"},{"t":"帰りました","r":"かえりました","m":"went home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yesterday, I went home while talking with a friend.","scene":"Recounting yesterday’s trip home after school"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-007||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"V1stemながらV2 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V1stemながらV2","patternReading":"ながら","coreMeaning":"Do V1 while doing V2 — two simultaneous actions by the same person","slug":"usage","definition":"In practice, ながら says that **one person is doing one thing at the same time as another**. The secondary action (V1) is often a background activity such as listening to music, watching TV, or walking; the main action (V2) is the one emphasized. It is common for habits, advice, and warnings.","nuance":"It is used mainly with intentional action verbs; avoid using it with stative verbs such as ある、いる、わかる. The sentence is usually **descriptive** rather than imperative, unless V2 is in a command or advisory form.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"rice"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べながら","r":"たべながら","m":"while eating"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"テレビ","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ます","r":"みます","m":"watch"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I watch TV while eating.","scene":"Describing the habit of watching TV while eating"},{"tokens":[{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"運転しながら","r":"うんてんしながら","m":"while driving"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","m":"use"},{"t":"の","m":"the act"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"危ない","r":"あぶない","m":"dangerous"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Driving while talking on the phone is dangerous.","scene":"Warning a friend not to use a phone while driving"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-007||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused forms","sub":"V1stemながらV2 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V1stemながらV2","patternReading":"ながら","coreMeaning":"Do V1 while doing V2 — two simultaneous actions by the same person","slug":"compare","definition":"The key distinction: **ながら = simultaneous**, **Vたあとで / Vてから = before and after**, and **Vています = one ongoing action**. If two actions happen at the same time, use ながら; if one finishes before the other begins, use a sequence pattern.","nuance":"In the N4 test, a common trap is choosing ながら when the sentence describes an order. Ask yourself: do the two actions happen at the same time? If yes, use ながら; if not, use a before/after pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞きながら","r":"ききながら","m":"while listening"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お茶","r":"おちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みました","r":"のみました","m":"drank"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I drank tea while listening to music.","scene":"Relaxing at home with music and tea"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vたあとで","point":"Vたあとで = V2 happens after V1 is finished → **before/after**; ながら = **simultaneous**. Example: 仕事が終わったあとで、飲みに行きます。vs 歌を歌いながら、運転します。"},{"vs":"Vてから","point":"Vてから = do V1 **and then** V2, in that order; ながら = two actions **overlap in time**. Example: 手を洗ってから、食べます。vs 音楽を聞きながら、食べます。"},{"vs":"Vています","point":"Vています = one ongoing action or state; ながら = **two** actions by the same person. Example: 本を読んでいます。vs 本を読みながら、コーヒーを飲みます。"}]}},"n4-007||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V1stemながらV2 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V1stemながらV2","patternReading":"ながら","coreMeaning":"Do V1 while doing V2 — two simultaneous actions by the same person","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate this as “while...while...” and then use the wrong subject or conjugate V1. Remember: V1 **must be in the ます-stem form**, and V1 and V2 must have the **same subject**.","nuance":"The error '帰りながら、ご飯を食べます' comes from treating “going home and eating” as simultaneous, even though these actions do not naturally overlap. Distinguish simultaneous actions from sequence clearly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞きながら","r":"ききながら","m":"while listening"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study while listening to music.","scene":"Describing studying with music on"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 姉が料理しながら、私がテレビを見ます。 → ✅ 姉は料理しながら、テレビを見ます。（same subject）","❌ 音楽を聞きますながら、勉強します。 → ✅ 音楽を聞きながら、勉強します。","❌ 音楽を聞いたながら、勉強しました。 → ✅ 音楽を聞きながら、勉強しました。","❌ 家に帰りながら、ご飯を食べます。 → ✅ 家に帰ってから、ご飯を食べます。（帰る and eating do not happen together）"],"compare":null}},"n4-007||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V1stemながらV2 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V1stemながらV2","patternReading":"ながら","coreMeaning":"Do V1 while doing V2 — two simultaneous actions by the same person","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine V1 as the **background** and V2 as the **main action**. ながら is like “while” in English: singing while dancing. When two verbs by the same person happen at the same time, connect the ます-stem with ながら.","nuance":"Visual tip: V1 is the background light, while V2 is the host speaking on stage — both operate at once, and neither switches the other off.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-007||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V1stemながらV2 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V1stemながらV2","patternReading":"ながら","coreMeaning":"Do V1 while doing V2 — two simultaneous actions by the same person","slug":"phrases","definition":"In conversation and on tests, ながら often appears with familiar V1 verbs: 聞く, 見る, 食べる, 歩く, 話す, and 運転する. Remembering these phrases helps you respond faster.","nuance":"These phrases usually serve as background V1 actions. For example, '歩きながらスマホを見る' is a very common warning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"歩きながら","r":"あるきながら","m":"while walking"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食べないで","r":"たべないで","m":"do not eat"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please do not eat while walking.","scene":"A mother warning her child not to eat while walking outside"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-008||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vています (習慣) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (習慣)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Habits / ongoing states","slug":"core","definition":"〜ています describes a **habit** or **ongoing state** that began in the past and **continues into the present**. It emphasizes regular repetition or a stable condition. This is one of the three basic meanings of 〜ています, along with 'in progress' and 'remaining result'.","nuance":"With the habit meaning, the sentence does more than say 'what I do'; it shows **part of my current life**. It often appears with words such as 毎日, 毎朝, ずっと, or periods such as 3年間. In conversation, use 〜ています instead of Vる to emphasize an established routine.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"6時","r":"ろくじ","m":"6 o'clock"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"起きています","r":"おきています","m":"wake up (habit)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wake up at 6 every morning.","scene":"Telling a coworker your daily schedule."},{"tokens":[{"t":"銀行","r":"ぎんこう","m":"bank"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"働いています","r":"はたらいています","m":"work (ongoing)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I work at a bank.","scene":"Introducing your job to someone new."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-008||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vています (習慣) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (習慣)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Habits / ongoing states","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern is formed by changing the verb to the **て-form** and adding **います**. It is the polite form of 〜ている. Here, we study its use for habits and ongoing states.","nuance":"In casual speech, 〜ています can be shortened to 〜てる (for example 知ってる, 住んでる). In writing and polite speech, however, use the full form 〜ています.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"います","note":"Present affirmative: habit/state"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いません","note":"Present negative"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いました","note":"Past: former habit/state"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いますか","note":"Present question"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"去年","r":"きょねん","m":"last year"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"住んでいました","r":"すんでいました","m":"lived (ongoing)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I lived in Osaka until last year.","scene":"Talking about a former residence."},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"たばこ"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"吸っていません","r":"すっていません","m":"do not smoke (ongoing)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't smoke now.","scene":"Describing a current non-habit."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-008||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Vています (習慣) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (習慣)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Habits / ongoing states","slug":"usage","definition":"For habits and states, 〜ています appears in four main situations: cyclical routines, jobs and social roles, ongoing study or work, and residence.","nuance":"If the sentence contains 今, it usually means 'in progress'. 毎日, よく, ずっと, or a time period signal a habit or ongoing state. For jobs, 働いています is the most natural way to introduce your work.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"パン"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べています","r":"たべています","m":"eat (habit)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat bread every morning.","scene":"Telling coworkers about breakfast."},{"tokens":[{"t":"3年間","r":"さんねんかん","m":"three years"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強しています","r":"べんきょうしています","m":"study (ongoing)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have been studying Japanese for three years.","scene":"Answering how long you have studied Japanese."},{"tokens":[{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"住んでいます","r":"すんでいます","m":"live (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I live in Tokyo.","scene":"Introducing yourself to someone new."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-008||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions from similar patterns","sub":"Vています (習慣) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (習慣)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Habits / ongoing states","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare 〜ています (習慣) with the two patterns most easily confused with it: the same 〜ています form meaning an action in progress, and Vる for general habits.","nuance":"The deciding factors are **time adverbs** and **context**. '今' → in progress; '毎日/ずっと' → habit. Vる simply states a general fact and does not emphasize a state that is 'being maintained'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"走っています","r":"はしっています","m":"am running"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am running now.","scene":"Contrasting it with 毎朝走っています — a habit."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vています (進行)","point":"進行: an action happening as you speak, often with '今'. 習慣: an action repeated as a routine, often with '毎日'. Example: 今走っています (running now) vs 毎朝走っています (running every morning)."},{"vs":"Vる (hiện tại)","point":"Vる states a habit or schedule as a general fact. Vています emphasizes an established state that continues. Example: 毎朝6時に起きます (schedule) vs 毎朝6時に起きています (current routine)."}]}},"n4-008||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vています (習慣) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (習慣)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Habits / ongoing states","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse 〜ています with 'in progress' or use Vる for every habit. This branch highlights common errors and their corrections.","nuance":"Note: with instantaneous verbs (死ぬ, 着く, 始まる), 〜ています indicates a remaining result, not a habit or ongoing action. To negate a habit, use 〜ていません rather than 〜ません.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"走っています","r":"はしっています","m":"run (habit)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I run every day.","scene":"Correct form instead of Vる for a lifestyle routine."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 銀行で働きます。 (when describing your current job) → ✅ 銀行で働いています。","❌ 去年からずっとたばこを吸いませんでした。 → ✅ 去年からずっとたばこを吸っていません。","❌ 新しい家に住む。 (when you have already lived there) → ✅ 新しい家に住んでいます。"],"compare":null}},"n4-008||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vています (習慣) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (習慣)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Habits / ongoing states","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory aid: て is a linking form, and います means 'be present'. Together: 'an action present in your life' = a habit. When you see 毎日, よく, or ずっと, immediately consider the habit meaning.","nuance":"This shortcut helps in reading: do not automatically translate it as 'in progress'; translate it as 'still regularly, every day'. For example, 勉強しています in a profile means 'am studying' in the sense of being enrolled or continuing study, not necessarily studying right now.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-008||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vています (習慣) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (習慣)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Habits / ongoing states","slug":"phrases","definition":"These verbs commonly combine with 〜ています to describe lifestyle patterns: 住む, 働く, 勉強する, 通う, and 続ける. They are essential N4 vocabulary.","nuance":"When introducing themselves, Japanese speakers often use 住んでいます and 働いています to describe a stable current situation. 通っています emphasizes regularly going to a place.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強しています","r":"べんきょうしています","m":"study (ongoing)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study Japanese every day.","scene":"Talking about your study routine."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-009||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vやすい／Vにくい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／Vにくい","patternReading":"やすいにくい","coreMeaning":"Comment on an object being EASY/HARD to do something with","slug":"core","definition":"Vます-stem + やすい／にくい describes an **object's quality** that makes an action easy or difficult. The speaker may say “these shoes are easy to walk in,” “this book is hard to read,” or “this app is easy to use.” It does not describe the speaker's ability.","nuance":"Used in both spoken and written Japanese with a neutral tone. やすい often suggests convenience; にくい can suggest inconvenience, but usually mildly. With people, common examples include 話しやすい人 (a person who is easy to talk to) and 頼みにくい人 (a person who is hard to ask for favors).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"靴","r":"くつ","m":"shoes"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"歩きやすい","r":"あるきやすい","m":"easy to walk in"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"These shoes are easy to walk in.","scene":"Trying on shoes and praising them"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"アプリ","m":"app"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"使いやすい","r":"つかいやすい","m":"easy to use"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This app is easy to use.","scene":"Recommending an app to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-009||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vやすい／Vにくい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／Vにくい","patternReading":"やすいにくい","coreMeaning":"Comment on an object being EASY/HARD to do something with","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: **remove ます from the polite verb form**, take the remaining stem, and add やすい or にくい. This works with groups 1, 2, and 3 verbs. The resulting expression is an **い-adjective**, so it conjugates normally for negatives, past forms, and linking clauses.","nuance":"Never attach やすい／にくい to the dictionary form. For linking, use 〜やすくて／〜にくくて. For negatives, use 〜やすくない／〜にくくない. For past forms, use 〜やすかった／〜にくかった.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます (drop ます)","rhs":"やすい","note":"Easy to do ~ (object's quality)"},{"lhs":"Vます (drop ます)","rhs":"にくい","note":"Hard to do ~ (object's quality)"},{"lhs":"〜やすい・〜にくい","rhs":"conjugates like an い-adjective","note":"Examples: やすくない / やすかった / やすくて"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ペン","m":"pen"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"書きやすい","r":"かきやすい","m":"easy to write with"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This pen is easy to write with.","scene":"Borrowing a friend's pen and commenting on it"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"読みにくかった","r":"よみにくかった","m":"was hard to read"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book was hard to read.","scene":"Describing a difficult book after finishing it"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-009||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Vやすい／Vにくい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／Vにくい","patternReading":"やすいにくい","coreMeaning":"Comment on an object being EASY/HARD to do something with","slug":"usage","definition":"Common situations include objects that are easy or hard to use, writing that is easy or hard to read, food that is easy or hard to eat, and people who are easy to talk to or hard to ask favors of. In all four cases, the subject is **the thing being evaluated**.","nuance":"In daily conversation, you will often hear sentences such as このアプリは使いやすい, この字は読みにくい, and この店は入りにくい. 食べやすい means “easy or convenient to eat,” not simply “edible.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"入りにくい","r":"はいりにくい","m":"hard to enter"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This shop is hard to enter.","scene":"Hesitating outside an upscale-looking restaurant"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"肉","r":"にく","m":"meat"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大きくて","r":"おおきくて","m":"large and"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食べにくい","r":"たべにくい","m":"hard to eat"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This piece of meat is so large that it is hard to eat.","scene":"At a restaurant with an oversized piece of meat"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"話しやすい","r":"はなしやすい","m":"easy to talk to"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is easy to talk to.","scene":"Introducing a close friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-009||compare":{"title":"⚖️ V potential form: contrast","sub":"Vやすい／Vにくい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／Vにくい","patternReading":"やすいにくい","coreMeaning":"Comment on an object being EASY/HARD to do something with","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes Vやすい／Vにくい from easily confused patterns, especially the potential form. These are often mixed up because both relate to whether something can be done. The potential form describes **a person's ability** (I can read or write), while Vやすい／Vにくい describes **an object's quality** (easy to read or hard to write). For example, この本は読めます means “I can read this book,” while この本は読みやすい means “this book is easy to read.” Also distinguish Vることができます, which likewise expresses ability rather than an object's quality.","nuance":"Use the potential form or Vることができます when emphasizing what a person can do. Use Vやすい／にくい when evaluating what an object is like for an action. 読みにくい does not mean “unreadable”; it means difficult to read, though it remains possible with effort. For completely impossible, use the negative potential form 読めない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"読めます","r":"よめます","m":"can read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can read this book.","scene":"Answering whether you can read Japanese books"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"読みやすい","r":"よみやすい","m":"easy to read"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is easy to read.","scene":"Praising a clear, easy-to-follow book"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V可能形","point":"The potential form describes a person's ABILITY; Vやすい describes an object's QUALITY. この本は読めます = I can read this book; この本は読みやすい = this book is easy to read."},{"vs":"Vることができます","point":"Like the potential form, it expresses ability rather than an object's characteristics."}]}},"n4-009||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vやすい／Vにくい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／Vにくい","patternReading":"やすいにくい","coreMeaning":"Comment on an object being EASY/HARD to do something with","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This branch covers three common mistakes with Vやすい／Vにくい. First, learners may make themselves the subject, as in “私は日本語が話しやすいです,” which is wrong because やすい／にくい does not describe a person's ability. Second, they may attach it directly to the dictionary form, as in “覚えるやすい,” instead of using the ます-stem. Third, they may use the person as subject when the thing being evaluated is actually an object. Remember: Vやすい／にくい **evaluates an object**, rather than **describing your ability**.","nuance":"Pay close attention when translating “easy” and “hard.” English can also use a person as subject in ability statements, but Japanese uses the potential form for that meaning: 話せます, not 話しやすい. 話しやすい describes someone who is easy to talk to, or a topic that is easy to discuss.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"覚えやすい","r":"おぼえやすい","m":"easy to remember"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This kanji is easy to remember.","scene":"Studying kanji and finding one easy to remember"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私は日本語が話しやすいです。 → ✅ 私は日本語が話せます。 (use the potential form for personal ability)","❌ この漢字は覚えるやすいです。 → ✅ この漢字は覚えやすいです。 (use the ます-stem: 覚えます → 覚え)","❌ 私はピアノを弾きやすいです。 → ✅ このピアノは弾きやすいです。 (the object should be the subject)"],"compare":null}},"n4-009||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vやすい／Vにくい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／Vにくい","patternReading":"やすいにくい","coreMeaning":"Comment on an object being EASY/HARD to do something with","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember where やすい／にくい attach, imagine the **ます-stem** as an electrical outlet and やすい／にくい as accessories that plug into it. Use やすい for “easy” and にくい for “hard.” This helps prevent attaching them to the dictionary form. Also picture a door that **opens easily** (開けやすい) versus one that is **stuck and hard to open** (開けにくい).","nuance":"When you meet a new verb, make a sentence using the pattern “this object + ます-stem + やすい／にくい.” For example: easy to write with → このペンは書きやすい. Repetition will build a natural reflex.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-009||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vやすい／Vにくい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／Vにくい","patternReading":"やすいにくい","coreMeaning":"Comment on an object being EASY/HARD to do something with","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases appear often in textbooks, tests, and daily life. Learning them as chunks helps you respond quickly and speak naturally. 使いやすい praises a useful object, 分かりやすい praises a clear explanation, 読みにくい describes hard-to-read writing or text, 話しやすい人 means someone easy to talk to, and 歩きやすい靴 means comfortable shoes.","nuance":"These expressions are almost stock phrases in Japanese, so you will encounter them everywhere. Remembering them helps you understand listening passages faster in the JLPT and in conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"辞書","r":"じしょ","m":"dictionary"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"とても","m":"very"},{"t":"使いやすい","r":"つかいやすい","m":"easy to use"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This dictionary is very easy to use.","scene":"Praising the dictionary you are holding"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-010||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"自動詞 vs 他動詞 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"自動詞 vs 他動詞","patternReading":"じどうし たどうし","coreMeaning":"Happens on its own (自動詞) vs actively caused (他動詞)","slug":"core","definition":"自動詞 is a verb that **does not need an object**; the event happens on its own: ドアが開く. 他動詞 is a verb that **needs an object with を**; someone causes the action: ドアを開ける. The same door-opening event is viewed differently: the door opens itself versus someone opens it.","nuance":"自動詞 describes **a state/result** or a natural event; 他動詞 describes **an intentional action**. With ～ている, an intransitive verb describes the resulting state (ドアが開いています), while a transitive verb describes an action in progress (ドアを開けています).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"どあ","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開きました","r":"あきました","m":"opened"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The door opened.","scene":"A closed door suddenly opens on its own"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"どあ","m":"door"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けました","r":"あけました","m":"opened"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I opened the door.","scene":"Answering “Who opened the door?”"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-010||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & linking forms","sub":"自動詞 vs 他動詞 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"自動詞 vs 他動詞","patternReading":"じどうし たどうし","coreMeaning":"Happens on its own (自動詞) vs actively caused (他動詞)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **自動詞** takes **が**, while **他動詞** takes **を**. Many intransitive/transitive pairs share **kanji roots** but have different endings: 開く（あく） / 開ける（あける）, つく / つける, 閉まる（しまる） / 閉める（しめる）.","nuance":"At N4, the common forms are **ます／ました／ています**. Remember: **を** marks something being acted on; **が** marks something changing on its own.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（物）＋ が","rhs":"自動詞","note":"The subject changes on its own; no agent is needed: ドアが開く。"},{"lhs":"N（物）＋ を","rhs":"他動詞","note":"A person or thing acts on the object: ドアを開ける。"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"light"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"つきます","m":"turns on"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The light turns on.","scene":"The light turns on automatically as it gets dark"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"light"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つけます","m":"turn on"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I turn on the light.","scene":"Telling someone you will turn on the light"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-010||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"自動詞 vs 他動詞 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"自動詞 vs 他動詞","patternReading":"じどうし たどうし","coreMeaning":"Happens on its own (自動詞) vs actively caused (他動詞)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use 自動詞 to **describe a state, result**, or natural event: ドアが開いています. Use 他動詞 when **someone does something**: ドアを開けています. ① Report an observed result → 自動詞; ② describe an intentional action → 他動詞; ③ with ～ている, 自動詞 marks a remaining state, while 他動詞 marks an ongoing action.","nuance":"To ask “Is the door open?” say ドアが開いていますか. To ask someone to open it, say ドアを開けてください. Japanese naturally uses 自動詞＋ている to describe the surrounding scene.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"閉まって","r":"しまって","m":"closed"},{"t":"います"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The window is closed.","scene":"Seeing the classroom window closed"},{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"閉めて","r":"しめて","m":"close"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please close the window.","scene":"A teacher asks a student to close it"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"どあ","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開きました","r":"あきました","m":"opened"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The door opened by itself, didn’t it?","scene":"Two people are surprised as the door opens"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-010||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused","sub":"自動詞 vs 他動詞 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"自動詞 vs 他動詞","patternReading":"じどうし たどうし","coreMeaning":"Happens on its own (自動詞) vs actively caused (他動詞)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse **自動詞＋ている**, **他動詞＋ている**, and **他動詞＋てある**. The key questions are: **was it intentional, and who caused it?**","nuance":"自動詞＋ている = natural state: ドアが開いています. 他動詞＋ている = action in progress: ドアを開けています. 他動詞＋てあります = someone has done it in advance: ドアを開けてあります.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"どあ","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開いて","r":"あいて","m":"open"},{"t":"います"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The door is open.","scene":"Compared with 他動詞: ドアを開けています (opening the door)"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"どあ","m":"door"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けて","r":"あけて","m":"left open"},{"t":"あります","m":"has been done"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The door has been left open.","scene":"Someone opened it earlier; the result remains"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vています (結果状態)","point":"自動詞＋ている → resulting state: ドアが開いています. 他動詞＋ている → action in progress: ドアを開けています."},{"vs":"Vてあります","point":"他動詞＋てあります → someone did it for a purpose: ドアを開けてあります. 自動詞＋ている → only a state; no agent is implied: ドアが開いています."}]}},"n4-010||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"自動詞 vs 他動詞 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"自動詞 vs 他動詞","patternReading":"じどうし たどうし","coreMeaning":"Happens on its own (自動詞) vs actively caused (他動詞)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse “the door opens” with “I open the door” because English does not always mark this contrast as clearly as Japanese. The main errors are **choosing the wrong particle が/を** or using ～ている with the wrong verb type.","nuance":"These errors either make the sentence ungrammatical or change its meaning. Golden rule: **自動詞 → が, 他動詞 → を**; with ～ている, an intransitive verb marks a result, while a transitive verb marks an action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"どあ","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開いて","r":"あいて","m":"open"},{"t":"います"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The door is open.","scene":"Correctly using 自動詞＋ている for a state"}],"pitfalls":["❌ ドアを開いています。 (intended: the door is open) → ✅ ドアが開いています。","❌ 電気をつきます。 (intended: turn on the light) → ✅ 電気をつけます。","❌ 窓が閉めます。 (intended: close the window) → ✅ 窓を閉めます。"],"compare":null}},"n4-011||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vています (結果状態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (結果状態)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in the resulting state of an action that has already occurred","slug":"core","definition":"〜ています with an **intransitive verb** says that something happened in the past and **its result still exists** at the moment of speaking. Example: the light was turned on → it is now lit. This does not describe an ongoing action, but a **current state**.","nuance":"Neutral and usable in both speech and writing. The speaker paints a picture of the present: the result can be seen or sensed. The focus is not who acted or when, but “what state is it in now?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"light"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ついて","r":"ついて","m":"lit"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The light is on (it was turned on).","scene":"You enter a room and see the light on"},{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開いて","r":"あいて","m":"open"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The window is open (it was opened).","scene":"You see an open window in the room"},{"tokens":[{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"入って","r":"はいって","m":"inside"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is money in the wallet (it was put there).","scene":"Checking your wallet before going out"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-011||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vています (結果状態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (結果状態)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in the resulting state of an action that has already occurred","slug":"forms","definition":"With an **intransitive verb**, connect the て-form to います. The state-holder usually takes **が** (は is also possible for emphasis or contrast). The negative is **〜ていません**, the past is **〜ていました**, and the question is **〜ていますか**.","nuance":"〜ています is the polite sentence ending. In casual speech, Japanese speakers often shorten it to **〜てる** (例: ついてる). 〜ていました describes a state at a past point in time.","structure":[{"lhs":"Nが","rhs":"自動詞て＋います","note":"Current resulting state"},{"lhs":"〜","rhs":"〜ていません","note":"Negative: not in the state"},{"lhs":"〜","rhs":"〜ていました","note":"Past: was in the state then"},{"lhs":"〜","rhs":"〜ていますか","note":"Ask about the current state"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"閉まって","r":"しまって","m":"closed"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The door is closed.","scene":"You see the meeting-room door closed"},{"tokens":[{"t":"コップ","m":"cup"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"割れて","r":"われて","m":"broken"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The cup is broken (it broke).","scene":"You see a cup broken on the table"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"light"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ついて","r":"ついて","m":"lit"},{"t":"いました","r":"いました","m":"was (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The light was on then.","scene":"You arrive in the morning and see it was on all night"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-011||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vています (結果状態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (結果状態)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in the resulting state of an action that has already occurred","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when the speaker **directly observes** the current state of something: a light being on, a door being open, or a cup being broken. It also answers “What state is it in now?”","nuance":"This is an **objective, descriptive** expression with little emotion. In conversation, it often follows **あ** or **もう** when noticing something new. Do not use it for an action still in progress.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あ、","m":"oh"},{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開いて","r":"あいて","m":"open"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"ね","m":"right"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Oh, the window is open.","scene":"You enter class, see the window open, and tell a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"コップ","m":"cup"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"割れて","r":"われて","m":"broken"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"よ","m":"you know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This cup is broken.","scene":"Picking up a cup from the floor"},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"空いて","r":"あいて","m":"available"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is the station restroom available?","scene":"Asking a station employee"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-011||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"Vています (結果状態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (結果状態)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in the resulting state of an action that has already occurred","slug":"compare","definition":"These three patterns often confuse learners because all can be translated with “is/are.” Ask: do I see an **action** in progress or a **completed state**? Is there evidence of **someone's intervention**?","nuance":"〜てあります uses transitive verbs and emphasizes that someone intentionally prepared something; result-state 〜ています uses intransitive verbs and simply describes the current state.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"閉まって","r":"しまって","m":"closed"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The window is closed (it was closed).","scene":"Seeing the closed window and using the result pattern"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ています（進行）","point":"進行: action in progress; result: action finished. 本を読んでいます = reading, ドアが開いています = the door is open."},{"vs":"〜てあります","point":"てあります: intentionally prepared, with evidence of someone's action; ています: a state. 窓が開けてあります = someone left it open, 窓が開いています = the window is open."},{"vs":"他動詞＋ています","point":"Intransitive: changes state on its own → ています is a result; transitive: acts on an object → ています usually means an action in progress."}]}},"n4-011||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vています (結果状態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (結果状態)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in the resulting state of an action that has already occurred","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse these because English does not always clearly distinguish intransitive and transitive verbs. Remember: to express a **remaining state**, use **intransitive verb + ています**, with the state-holder marked by **が**, not **を**.","nuance":"With a transitive verb, ています usually means **an action in progress**, not a resulting state. With 知っています, the negative is specially 知りません, not 知っていません.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開いて","r":"あいて","m":"open"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The door is open.","scene":"Correcting the sentence to describe the state"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 電気をついています。 → ✅ 電気がついています。 (つく is intransitive and does not take を)","❌ ドアを開いています。 → ✅ ドアが開いています。 (開く is intransitive and takes が, not を)","❌ まだ知っていません。 → ✅ まだ知りません。 (the negative of 知っています is 知りません)"],"compare":null}},"n4-011||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"Vています (結果状態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (結果状態)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in the resulting state of an action that has already occurred","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a **snapshot of the current scene**. If the picture shows a result that already exists, use **〜ています**. Example: you see a lit lamp in a photo → 電気がついています.","nuance":"This avoids confusion with 〜ています meaning “in progress”: an ongoing action is like a video, while a resulting state is like a still photo.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-011||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vています (結果状態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (結果状態)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in the resulting state of an action that has already occurred","slug":"phrases","definition":"These common intransitive result-state phrases appear often in exams and daily life. Learning them as chunks builds faster recall: 入る, つく, 閉まる, 壊れる, 割れる, 空く…","nuance":"All these phrases use **が**, not **を**: 電気がつく, 窓が閉まる, コップが割れる…","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"エアコン","m":"air conditioner"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ついて","r":"ついて","m":"on"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The air conditioner is running (it was turned on).","scene":"You enter and see the air conditioner on"},{"tokens":[{"t":"パソコン","m":"computer"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"壊れて","r":"こわれて","m":"broken"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The computer is broken.","scene":"The computer will not power on"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"閉まって","r":"しまって","m":"closed"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is (state)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This shop is already closed.","scene":"You arrive and find the shop closed"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-012||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vてしまいました · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてしまいました","patternReading":"てしまいました","coreMeaning":"I ended up doing it — with regret or relief","slug":"core","definition":"〜てしまいました describes an action that was **completed** in the past while also showing the speaker’s feelings: **regret** (I ended up doing it) or **relief** (it is finally done). The key idea is that the event is **finished and cannot be undone**.","nuance":"This is neutral polite speech and also appears in writing. With friends or family, it is often shortened to **〜ちゃった** for a casual tone. It can show **regret or embarrassment** after something bad, or **relief or satisfaction** after finishing something necessary. Compared with 〜ました, it carries much more emotion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"忘れて","r":"わすれて","m":"forget"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I forgot my wallet.","scene":"You notice at school that you left your wallet at home."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"all"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I ate the whole cake.","scene":"You could not stop and finished the cake meant for later."},{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"all"},{"t":"やって","m":"do"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I finished all my homework.","scene":"You tell your mother after finishing late at night."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-012||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"Vてしまいました · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてしまいました","patternReading":"てしまいました","coreMeaning":"I ended up doing it — with regret or relief","slug":"forms","definition":"Make this pattern by taking the verb’s **て-form** and adding **しまいます**. **しまいました** is the past form of しまいます and is used for completed past events. In casual speech, **〜てしまう → 〜ちゃう** and **〜てしまった → 〜ちゃった**. Group 1 verbs ending in む/ぶ/ぬ change to んで in the て-form, so the casual form can become **〜んじゃった**.","nuance":"しまいます refers to the present or future (“will end up…”), while **しまいました** is past and is most common when telling what happened. 〜ちゃった sounds natural and casual; avoid it in formal writing.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"しまいます","note":"Present/future: “will end up…” or an unavoidable habit"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"しまいました","note":"Past: ended up doing it / finished it"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"しまった","note":"普通形 past: no way around it; casual speech or inner thought"},{"lhs":"Vてしまった","rhs":"→ 〜ちゃった","note":"Spoken contraction: 〜てしまった → 〜ちゃった"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"パスポート","m":"passport"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"忘れて","r":"わすれて","m":"forget"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I forgot my passport.","scene":"At the airport, you discover it is missing during check-in."},{"tokens":[{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"落とし","r":"おとし","m":"drop"},{"t":"ちゃった","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I dropped my wallet.","scene":"A close friend asks why you look sad."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-012||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"Vてしまいました · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてしまいました","patternReading":"てしまいました","coreMeaning":"I ended up doing it — with regret or relief","slug":"usage","definition":"Use 〜てしまいました in three main situations: ① **regret** — something unwanted happened; ② **relief or completion** — a necessary task is finally done; ③ **accidentally or unavoidably** — the action happened and cannot be undone. Context determines the intended meaning.","nuance":"Speakers use this pattern to emphasize a result that is **finished and emotionally meaningful**. If you only need to report an event objectively, use 〜ました. In texts or conversations with friends, replace it with 〜ちゃった for a more natural casual tone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗り遅れて","r":"のりおくれて","m":"miss"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I missed the train.","scene":"You reach the station too late and call your friend."},{"tokens":[{"t":"やっと","m":"finally"},{"t":"レポート","m":"report"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出して","r":"だして","m":"submit"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I finally submitted the report.","scene":"After days of work, you submit it with relief."},{"tokens":[{"t":"つい","m":"could not resist"},{"t":"甘い","r":"あまい","m":"sweet"},{"t":"もの","m":"things"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"all"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I ate all the sweets.","scene":"You are dieting but finish every snack in the cupboard."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-012||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Vてしまいました · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてしまいました","patternReading":"てしまいました","coreMeaning":"I ended up doing it — with regret or relief","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare this pattern with two easily confused forms: **〜ました** and **〜てあります**. The core of 〜てしまいました is **completion plus emotion**; the other patterns do not carry this same emotional nuance.","nuance":"〜ました simply reports that something happened. 〜てあります describes a remaining state created **intentionally** by someone. 〜てしまいました focuses on what was **done or happened**, often unintentionally or with regret.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けて","r":"あけて","m":"open"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I opened the window by accident.","scene":"You open it while the air conditioner is running."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ました","point":"〜ました reports neutrally; 〜てしまいました adds regret or relief. Example: 財布を忘れました (report) vs 財布を忘れてしまいました (I forgot it—oh no)."},{"vs":"〜てあります","point":"〜てあります: someone intentionally did something and the result remains (NがVてある). 〜てしまいました: the action is complete, often with regret. Example: 窓が開けてあります (left open) vs 窓を開けてしまいました (I opened it)."}]}},"n4-012||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vてしまいました · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてしまいました","patternReading":"てしまいました","coreMeaning":"I ended up doing it — with regret or relief","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors include using 〜てしまいました for every neutral past statement, forming the て-form incorrectly, or confusing it with 〜ていました. Ask yourself: do I want to show **regret or relief**? If you are only reporting an event, use 〜ました.","nuance":"In formal situations, avoid 〜ちゃった with superiors. 〜ちゃった is for casual conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"書類","r":"しょるい","m":"documents"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"忘れて","r":"わすれて","m":"forget"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I forgot the documents.","scene":"You call your boss to apologize for leaving them at home."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 昨日、映画を見てしまいました。(neutral report) → ✅ 昨日、映画を見ました。","❌ コーヒーを飲みてしまいました。 → ✅ コーヒーを飲んでしまいました。 (て-form of 飲む is 飲んで)","💡 昼ごはんを食べていました (was eating, not finished) ≠ ✅ 昼ごはんを食べてしまいました (finished it all).","❌ 部長に書類を忘れちゃった。 (too casual with a superior) → ✅ 部長、書類を忘れてしまいました。"],"compare":null}},"n4-012||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vてしまいました · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてしまいました","patternReading":"てしまいました","coreMeaning":"I ended up doing it — with regret or relief","slug":"memory","definition":"The basic meaning of **しまう** is “to put away” or “to store.” Once something is put away, it is hard to take back out—like an action that is **finished and cannot be undone**. **ちゃった** is the quick, shortened form of **てしまった**.","nuance":"Imagine accidentally breaking a bowl and saying, “Oh no, it’s ruined!” That feeling is the core of 〜てしまいました. When you feel regret or relief, think of しまう.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-012||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful expressions","sub":"Vてしまいました · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてしまいました","patternReading":"てしまいました","coreMeaning":"I ended up doing it — with regret or relief","slug":"phrases","definition":"These expressions commonly appear in exams and daily life: **忘れてしまう** (forget), **食べてしまう** (eat all), **壊してしまう** (break), **遅れてしまう** (be late), and **なくしてしまう** (lose). Learning them as chunks makes the pattern easier to remember.","nuance":"These verbs describe unintended events or completed actions, so they fit the pattern’s nuance well. In casual speech, they are often heard as **忘れちゃった**, **食べちゃった**, and **壊しちゃった**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"財布","r":"さいふ","m":"wallet"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"なくして","m":"lose"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I lost my wallet.","scene":"You search your bag and realize it must have fallen out."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-013||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vてあります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあります","patternReading":"てあります","coreMeaning":"An intentional result that remains in place","slug":"core","definition":"〜てあります describes an existing state that resulted from an intentional action someone performed earlier. The speaker sees the result and points out that “this has been prepared” rather than happening naturally. Therefore, a transitive verb is required, and the affected item is usually marked with が.","nuance":"This pattern does not emphasize who did the action, so it is polite for reporting preparation: 資料が用意してあります. Compared with 〜ておきます, 〜てあります focuses on the **existing result**, while 〜ておきます focuses on **preparing for the future**. The tone is objective and emotionally neutral.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"黒板","r":"こくばん","m":"blackboard"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"予定","r":"よてい","m":"schedule"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"書いてあります","r":"かいてあります","m":"written in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The schedule has already been written on the blackboard.","scene":"The teacher enters; the class schedule is already on the board."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ビール","m":"beer"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"冷蔵庫","r":"れいぞうこ","m":"refrigerator"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"冷やしてあります","r":"ひやしてあります","m":"chilled in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The beer has been chilled in the refrigerator.","scene":"You open the fridge and find cold beer ready for the guests."},{"tokens":[{"t":"パーティー","m":"party"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"準備","r":"じゅんび","m":"preparations"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"してあります","m":"prepared in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The party has already been prepared for.","scene":"You tell a friend everything is ready for the party."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-013||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"Vてあります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあります","patternReading":"てあります","coreMeaning":"An intentional result that remains in place","slug":"forms","definition":"Formula: **transitive verb in the te-form + あります**. The affected item usually takes **が** to emphasize the resulting state (or **を** when emphasizing the subject's preparatory action). Negative and past forms follow the rules for あります.","nuance":"This pattern is most common in the present affirmative. The negative 〜てありません is somewhat formal; in conversation, 〜てない is more common. The past 〜てありました describes a result that was already in place at a past time.","structure":[{"lhs":"他動詞（て形）","rhs":"あります","note":"Resulting state"},{"lhs":"N（対象）","rhs":"が Vてあります","note":"Usually が instead of を"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"ありません","note":"Negative"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"ありました","note":"Past"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"予約","r":"よやく","m":"reservation"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"してあります","m":"reserved in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It has already been reserved.","scene":"Confirming that the arrangement is ready."},{"tokens":[{"t":"エアコン","m":"air conditioner"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"つけてあります","m":"turned on in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The air conditioner has been turned on.","scene":"You enter the room and find it running."},{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"light"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"消してあります","r":"けしてあります","m":"turned off in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The light has already been turned off.","scene":"Checking before going out."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-013||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"Vてあります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあります","patternReading":"てあります","coreMeaning":"An intentional result that remains in place","slug":"usage","definition":"〜てあります often describes preparations involving objects, times, and places. The speaker is at the scene, sees the result, and directs attention to that state. It commonly uses transitive verbs such as 書く、冷やす、用意する、予約する、準備する、閉める、消す.","nuance":"At work, use it as a brief report meaning “it has been prepared.” At home, use it to tell family that something is ready. Do not use it for an action in progress; use 〜ています for that.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議室","r":"かいぎしつ","m":"meeting room"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"materials"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"用意してあります","r":"よういしてあります","m":"prepared in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The materials have been prepared in the meeting room.","scene":"An employee reports to the boss before the meeting."},{"tokens":[{"t":"お茶","r":"おちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"入れてあります","r":"いれてあります","m":"brewed in advance"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is tea ready for you.","scene":"A host welcomes guests."},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"会場","r":"かいじょう","m":"venue"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"予約してあります","r":"よやくしてあります","m":"reserved in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The venue for tomorrow has already been reserved.","scene":"Confirming the plan with a coworker."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-013||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with ～ています・～ておきます","sub":"Vてあります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあります","patternReading":"てあります","coreMeaning":"An intentional result that remains in place","slug":"compare","definition":"This section helps distinguish 〜てあります from similar patterns. The key point: **〜てあります = an intentional prepared result**, while 〜ておきます = a preparatory action, and 〜ています (result) = a natural state.","nuance":"When Japanese speakers hear 〜てあります, they understand that someone’s “hand” arranged it; 〜ています only presents the state as it is. When reporting, ask yourself: do I mean “it has been done” or “it is in that state”?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開けてあります","r":"あけてあります","m":"left open"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The window has been left open, hasn't it?","scene":"You enter the room and see the window was left open."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ておきます","point":"〜ておきます emphasizes a PREPARATORY ACTION for the future; 〜てあります emphasizes the RESULT that exists now. VD: 窓を開けておきます。 (I will leave it open) / 窓が開けてあります。 (it is left open)"},{"vs":"〜ています","point":"〜ています with an INTRANSITIVE VERB describes a natural state; 〜てあります with a TRANSITIVE VERB emphasizes intention. VD: ドアが開いています。 (it is open naturally) / ドアが開けてあります。 (someone opened it)"}]}},"n4-013||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vてあります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあります","patternReading":"てあります","coreMeaning":"An intentional result that remains in place","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often apply passive-like patterns from English or forget the transitive-verb rule. Ask yourself: does this sentence describe a result that remains, with the affected item marked by が?","nuance":"These errors may still be understood, but they sound unnatural. Fix them by omitting the doer, using a transitive verb, and making the affected item the subject with が.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"閉めてあります","r":"しめてあります","m":"closed in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The door has been closed.","scene":"Confirming the door is closed before bed."}],"pitfalls":["❌ ドアが閉まってあります。 → ✅ ドアが閉めてあります。 (An intransitive verb cannot be used with 〜てあります)","❌ 会場に予約してあります。 → ✅ 会場が予約してあります。 (The affected item uses が or を, not に)","❌ 私は窓が開けてあります。 → ✅ 窓が開けてあります。 (〜てあります does not emphasize the doer)"],"compare":null}},"n4-013||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"Vてあります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあります","patternReading":"てあります","coreMeaning":"An intentional result that remains in place","slug":"memory","definition":"A simple memory aid: あります means “to exist”; after Vて, it means “a result exists.” Whenever you see 〜てあります, think: **something is ready because someone did it**. Picture a set table and tea already poured.","nuance":"Connect it with the situation “preparing things before guests arrive,” and the pattern will stick. You do not need to memorize a long theory.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-013||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vてあります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあります","patternReading":"てあります","coreMeaning":"An intentional result that remains in place","slug":"phrases","definition":"In N4 tests and workplace Japanese, 〜てあります mainly appears with transitive verbs such as する、用意する、予約する、書く、冷やす. Memorizing these phrases helps you recognize the pattern quickly.","nuance":"The sentence 〜てありますから is common in conversation: お茶が入れてありますから、どうぞ. It both reports the ready state and offers something politely.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"準備","r":"じゅんび","m":"preparations"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"してあります","m":"prepared in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The preparations are already done.","scene":"A brief reply when the boss asks."},{"tokens":[{"t":"会場","r":"かいじょう","m":"venue"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"予約してあります","r":"よやくしてあります","m":"reserved in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The venue has already been reserved.","scene":"Confirming the venue with everyone."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-014||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vておきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vておきます","patternReading":"ておきます","coreMeaning":"Do in advance / prepare for later","slug":"core","definition":"〜ておきます describes the speaker **intentionally** doing something **in advance** (or preparing it) for an upcoming purpose. The focus is **conscious preparation**, not something that happens naturally or by chance.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. In casual speech, Japanese speakers often shorten it to 〜とく. It has a clearer “ready and reassured” nuance than 〜てある.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"trip"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"before"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ホテル","r":"ホテル","m":"hotel"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"予約","r":"よやく","m":"reserve"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"おきます","r":"おきます","m":"in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will book the hotel before the trip.","scene":"Preparing for an upcoming vacation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"単語","r":"たんご","m":"vocabulary"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"覚えて","r":"おぼえて","m":"memorize"},{"t":"おきます","r":"おきます","m":"in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"To prepare for tomorrow's exam, I will memorize the vocabulary in advance.","scene":"Studying tonight for the upcoming exam"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-014||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"Vておきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vておきます","patternReading":"ておきます","coreMeaning":"Do in advance / prepare for later","slug":"forms","definition":"The basic structure is **Vて + おきます**. This is the polite form of **Vて + おく**. おく is an auxiliary verb and itself is a Group 1 (五段) verb. It conjugates like a regular verb: おきます (polite nonpast), おいた (plain past), おきました (polite past), and so on.","nuance":"In casual speech, Japanese speakers shorten 〜ておく to 〜とく and 〜でおく to 〜どく. Examples: 食べておく→食べとく, 読んでおく→読んどく. Use the full form in writing or formal situations.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて形","rhs":"おきます","note":"Polite form"},{"lhs":"Vて形","rhs":"おく","note":"Dictionary / casual form"},{"lhs":"Vないで","rhs":"おきます","note":"Not do in advance"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"おきました","note":"Polite past"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"おいてください","note":"Polite request"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"documents"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"コピー","r":"コピー","m":"copy"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"おきます","r":"おきます","m":"in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will copy the documents in advance.","scene":"Preparing documents for a meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"おきました","r":"おきました","m":"in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I did my homework in advance.","scene":"Telling a friend it was done earlier"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"light/electricity"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"消して","r":"けして","m":"turn off"},{"t":"おいて","r":"おいて","m":"in advance"},{"t":"ください","r":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please turn off the lights before you leave.","scene":"Reminding staff at closing time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-014||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"Vておきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vておきます","patternReading":"ておきます","coreMeaning":"Do in advance / prepare for later","slug":"usage","definition":"〜ておきます has two main meanings: (1) **do something in advance** for later use, and (2) **leave a state unchanged** after performing an action. Both involve setting something aside for later.","nuance":"Meaning (2) is often used with transitive verbs: 窓を開けておく (open the window and leave it open). To describe a resulting state with てある, the object is marked with が instead of を. ておきます is more formal than ておく and is common at work and in polite communication.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"before"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"documents"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"コピー","r":"コピー","m":"copy"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"おきます","r":"おきます","m":"in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will copy the documents before the meeting.","scene":"Preparing papers before a meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けて","r":"あけて","m":"open"},{"t":"おきます","r":"おきます","m":"in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will leave the window open.","scene":"Keeping the room airy until returning"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-014||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Vておきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vておきます","patternReading":"ておきます","coreMeaning":"Do in advance / prepare for later","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish ておきます from three commonly confused patterns: **てあります**, **てみます**, and **てしまいました**. Each has a different focus.","nuance":"Learners often confuse ておきます and てあります because both can involve a result. Remember: ておきます is *preparatory action*, while てあります is a *visible resulting state*.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ホテル","r":"ホテル","m":"hotel"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"予約","r":"よやく","m":"reserve"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"おきました","r":"おきました","m":"in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I booked the hotel in advance.","scene":"Accommodation already prepared"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてあります","point":"てあります emphasizes the RESULTING STATE that remains. ておきます emphasizes PREPARATION for the future. E.g.: 窓が開けてあります (the window is left open) / 窓を開けておきます (I will open the window beforehand)"},{"vs":"Vてみます","point":"てみます: TRY doing something to see the result. ておきます: do something IN ADVANCE according to a plan. E.g.: 着てみます (try it on) / 用意しておきます (prepare it in advance)"},{"vs":"Vてしまいました","point":"てしまいました: an action was completed, often with regret or relief. ておきます: emphasizes intentional preparation for peace of mind."}]}},"n4-014||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes and traps","sub":"Vておきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vておきます","patternReading":"ておきます","coreMeaning":"Do in advance / prepare for later","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make these mistakes with ておきます. Most importantly, ておく is used only for **intentional actions**, and the negative form must use **ないで**.","nuance":"Distinguish these clearly: to describe a resulting state caused by someone, use てあります with が; ておきます uses を to emphasize preparatory action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"パスポート","r":"パスポート","m":"passport"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"コピー","r":"コピー","m":"copy"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"おきます","r":"おきます","m":"in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will copy my passport in advance.","scene":"Before applying for a visa"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 電車が来ておきます。 → ✅ 電車が来ています。 (ておく requires intentional action; 来る is an unintentional intransitive verb)","❌ ドアが開けておきます。 → ✅ ドアを開けておきます。 (preparatory action uses を; for a resulting state use ドアが開けてあります)","❌ 資料を読まないておきます。 → ✅ 資料を読まないでおきます。 (the negative of Vて is Vないで)","❌ メールで「資料をコピーしとく」 → ✅ 「資料をコピーしておきます」 (とく is colloquial and not used in formal writing)"],"compare":null}},"n4-014||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vておきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vておきます","patternReading":"ておきます","coreMeaning":"Do in advance / prepare for later","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember that the basic meaning of **おく** is “put, leave.” Thus **ておく** evokes the image of **setting an action in place** for later use. In fast speech, Japanese speakers shorten it to **とく**.","nuance":"This tip helps you avoid confusing it with てしまう (completion or regret) and てある (a resulting state).","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-014||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vておきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vておきます","patternReading":"ておきます","coreMeaning":"Do in advance / prepare for later","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases used with ておきます in conversation and on exams:","nuance":"These phrases express preparation and often appear in travel, work, and study contexts.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"おきます","r":"おきます","m":"in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will contact my friend beforehand.","scene":"Messaging a friend before visiting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-015||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V意向形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V意向形","patternReading":"いこうけい","coreMeaning":"Speaker's will / inviting someone to act together (volitional form)","slug":"core","definition":"The volitional form (V意向形) expresses the speaker's intention or invites the listener to act together. A sentence like “明日早く起きよう” can be self-talk and convey determination. It is the basis for patterns such as **〜ようと思います** and **〜ようにします**.","nuance":"The volitional form is **casual and conversational**. It sounds natural with friends and family; with superiors, use **〜ましょう** instead. It conveys determination, enthusiasm, or encouragement, and is not used to describe a third person's action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"起きよう","r":"おきよう","m":"let's get up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let's get up early tomorrow.","scene":"Telling yourself to get up early and exercise"},{"tokens":[{"t":"一緒に","r":"いっしょに","m":"together"},{"t":"勉強しよう","r":"べんきょうしよう","m":"let's study"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Let's study together!","scene":"Inviting a friend during exam review"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-015||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V意向形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V意向形","patternReading":"いこうけい","coreMeaning":"Speaker's will / inviting someone to act together (volitional form)","slug":"forms","definition":"Volitional-form conjugation depends on the verb group. **Group I**: change the い sound to the お sound, then add う. **Group II**: drop る, then add よう. **Group III**: する→しよう and 来る→こよう. Remember that the い→お change is based on the **ます形**.","nuance":"There is no separate polite volitional form; use **〜ましょう** for politeness. A negative volitional form is rare, so use **〜ないつもり** or **〜ないことにします** instead.","structure":[{"lhs":"V1 (い→お) + う","rhs":"書く→書こう、買う→買おう、話す→話そう","note":"Drop ます, change the い sound to お, then add う."},{"lhs":"V2 (bỏ る) + よう","rhs":"食べる→食べよう、見る→見よう","note":"る disappears, and よう attaches directly after it."},{"lhs":"V3","rhs":"する→しよう、来る→こよう","note":"The only two irregular verbs; memorize them."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲もう","r":"のもう","m":"let's drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let's drink some water.","scene":"Telling yourself this when thirsty"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"考えよう","r":"かんがえよう","m":"let's think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let me think for a moment.","scene":"Calming yourself to think before deciding"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: 辞書形 → 意向形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 — う row → お row + う","Change the final sound from the う row to the お row, then add う","飲む（のむ） → `飲もう（のもう）` (drink) ・ 書く（かく） → `書こう（かこう）` (write) ・ 話す（はなす） → `話そう（はなそう）` (speak) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰ろう（かえろう）` (return)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — 一段 — drop る + よう","Drop る, then add よう","食べる（たべる） → `食べよう（たべよう）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見よう（みよう）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きよう（おきよう）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝よう（ねよう）` (sleep)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — する → しよう","する → しよう","する → `しよう` (do) ・ 勉強する（べんきょうする） → `勉強しよう（べんきょうしよう）` (study) ・ 運動する（うんどうする） → `運動しよう（うんどうしよう）` (exercise)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — 来る → 来よう (こよう)","来る → 来よう (こよう)","来る（くる） → `来よう（こよう）` (come) ・ 戻ってくる（もどってくる） → `戻ってこよう（もどってこよう）` (return)"]]}}},"n4-015||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"V意向形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V意向形","patternReading":"いこうけい","coreMeaning":"Speaker's will / inviting someone to act together (volitional form)","slug":"usage","definition":"The volitional form has four main uses: (1) **self-talk/determination**: 明日早く起きよう; (2) **inviting someone to act together**: 一緒に勉強しよう; (3) **gently asking for an opinion**: add か to make 〜ようか; (4) **shared encouragement**: さあ、頑張ろう.","nuance":"With か, the sentence becomes a **tentative suggestion**, close to “Shall we do that?”. Do not use the volitional form for a third person: “彼が行こう” is unnatural; use **行くつもり** or **行きたい** instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"帰ろう","r":"かえろう","m":"let's go home"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall we head home?","scene":"Inviting a friend to leave after evening class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"さあ","m":"come on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"始めよう","r":"はじめよう","m":"let's begin"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Come on, let's begin.","scene":"An instructor opens the class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今度","r":"こんど","m":"this time"},{"t":"こそ","m":"definitely"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"頑張ろう","r":"がんばろう","m":"let's try"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This time, I'll definitely do my best.","scene":"Telling yourself this after failing an exam"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-015||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare 〜ましょう・つもり・予定","sub":"V意向形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V意向形","patternReading":"いこうけい","coreMeaning":"Speaker's will / inviting someone to act together (volitional form)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse the volitional form with **〜ましょう**, **〜つもりです**, and **〜予定です**. All can relate to “will do,” but their degree of planning and nuance differ clearly.","nuance":"〜ましょう is **polite and formal**; V意向形 is **casual and spontaneous**. 〜つもり is a **considered intention**; V意向形 is an **on-the-spot decision** or invitation. 〜予定 is a **scheduled plan**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一緒に","r":"いっしょに","m":"together"},{"t":"行こう","r":"いこう","m":"let's go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let's go together.","scene":"Inviting a friend out for a drink"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ましょう","point":"Both invite someone to act together, but 〜ましょう is polite and works with superiors; V意向形 is casual. 一緒に行きましょう (coworker) → 一緒に行こう (close friend)."},{"vs":"〜つもりです","point":"〜つもり expresses a deliberate, clearly considered intention; V意向形 can be impulsive and also serves as an invitation. 来年留学するつもりです ≠ 留学しよう (a decision made now)."},{"vs":"〜予定です","point":"〜予定 indicates an objectively scheduled plan; V意向形 expresses the speaker's subjective wish, which may not happen."}]}},"n4-015||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V意向形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V意向形","patternReading":"いこうけい","coreMeaning":"Speaker's will / inviting someone to act together (volitional form)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include using the wrong verb-group rule, speaking too casually to superiors, and trying to create a nonexistent negative volitional form. Spot these early to avoid them in conversation.","nuance":"Conjugation errors often occur with Group I because you must **change the sound**, not merely add う. Politeness errors are common because English uses casual “let's” broadly, while Japanese carefully marks social status.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一緒に","r":"いっしょに","m":"together"},{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べよう","r":"たべよう","m":"let's eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let's have lunch together.","scene":"Inviting a coworker to lunch"}],"pitfalls":["❌ かきます→かきよう → ✅ かきます→かこう. For Group I, change い to お, then add う.","❌ 先生、いっしょに飲もう。 → ✅ 先生、いっしょに飲みましょう。 Use 〜ましょう or 〜ませんか with a superior.","❌ たばこをすわないよう。 → ✅ たばこをやめよう. There is no Vないよう for negative volition.","❌ 彼は行こう。 → ✅ 彼は行くつもりだ. V意向形 is for the speaker or an invitation to the listener."],"compare":null}},"n4-015||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"V意向形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V意向形","patternReading":"いこうけい","coreMeaning":"Speaker's will / inviting someone to act together (volitional form)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tips: for Group I, picture the お sound as **a loudspeaker shouting a slogan**; for Group II, る **falls off** and よう sticks on; Group III has only two words, so **memorize them**.","nuance":"These are visual memory aids for quick recall, not official grammar rules.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-015||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V意向形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V意向形","patternReading":"いこうけい","coreMeaning":"Speaker's will / inviting someone to act together (volitional form)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Highly common phrases with the volitional form include **一緒に〜しよう**, **もう〜しようか**, and **〜しようと思う**. They appear often in daily conversation and on the N4 exam.","nuance":"〜しようと思う expresses “I am thinking of doing it,” used to tell the listener your intention, **not to invite them**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見よう","r":"みよう","m":"let's watch"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let's watch a movie this weekend!","scene":"Inviting a friend to see a movie"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-016||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vるつもりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるつもりです","patternReading":"つもりです","coreMeaning":"Intention / plan to do something (personal intention)","slug":"core","definition":"〜つもりです expresses a **personal intention or plan** that has been decided internally. Unlike 予定 (a specific schedule), つもり does not require a detailed plan. This is one of the most important beginner patterns for talking about intentions.","nuance":"It has a **personal, self-directed** nuance. When used for a third person, the speaker needs information or a basis for knowing that person's intention. It is neither forceful nor absolute; it simply means 'I intend to do it.'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"うち","r":"うち","m":"my home"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"ゆっくり","r":"ゆっくり","m":"relax"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"つもりです","r":"つもりです","m":"intend to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I intend to relax at home this weekend.","scene":"A roommate asks about your weekend plans"},{"tokens":[{"t":"来年","r":"らいねん","m":"next year"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"つもりです","r":"つもりです","m":"intend to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I intend to study Japanese next year.","scene":"Answering a question about next year's plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-016||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vるつもりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるつもりです","patternReading":"つもりです","coreMeaning":"Intention / plan to do something (personal intention)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **V dictionary form + つもりです**. Do not use Vます before つもり. For negation, use **Vない + つもりです** (intend not to do) or **Vるつもりはない** (have no intention of doing).","nuance":"The past form つもりでした often implies 'I intended to, but in the end I didn't' when followed by a contrasting clause. つもりだ is casual speech, while つもりです is used in ordinary conversation and writing.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"つもりです","note":"use only with dictionary-form verbs"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"つもりです","note":"intend not to do"},{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"つもりでした","note":"past; often followed by 'が' + a contrasting result"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お酒","r":"おさけ","m":"alcohol"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲ま","r":"のま","m":"drink"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not"},{"t":"つもりです","r":"つもりです","m":"intend to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I intend not to drink alcohol today.","scene":"Turning down alcohol at a party"},{"tokens":[{"t":"大学","r":"だいがく","m":"university"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"卒業","r":"そつぎょう","m":"graduate"},{"t":"したら","r":"したら","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"働く","r":"はたらく","m":"work"},{"t":"つもりです","r":"つもりです","m":"intend to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After graduating from university, I intend to work.","scene":"Discussing future plans with friends"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会う","r":"あう","m":"meet"},{"t":"つもりでした","r":"つもりでした","m":"intended to"},{"t":"が","m":"but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会えませんでした","r":"あえませんでした","m":"could not meet"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I intended to meet my friend yesterday, but I couldn't.","scene":"Telling a colleague about a failed plan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-016||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vるつもりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるつもりです","patternReading":"つもりです","coreMeaning":"Intention / plan to do something (personal intention)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜つもりです is commonly used when the speaker **wants to share a personal intention** with the listener. Common situations include asking about vacation plans, plans after graduation, weekend activities, or whether to accept an invitation.","nuance":"In negative answers, Japanese speakers often use **Vるつもりはありません/ない** when they want to emphasize that they have no intention. This pattern is neutral and works with friends and coworkers.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今度","r":"こんど","m":"this time"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"連休","r":"れんきゅう","m":"long weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どこも","r":"どこも","m":"anywhere"},{"t":"行かない","r":"いかない","m":"not go"},{"t":"つもりです","r":"つもりです","m":"intend to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't intend to go anywhere this long weekend.","scene":"Friends invite you out, but you want to stay home"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来年","r":"らいねん","m":"next year"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やめる","r":"やめる","m":"quit"},{"t":"つもりです","r":"つもりです","m":"intend to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He intends to quit his job next year.","scene":"Commenting on a coworker who told you this"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-016||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Vるつもりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるつもりです","patternReading":"つもりです","coreMeaning":"Intention / plan to do something (personal intention)","slug":"compare","definition":"Clearly distinguish these three commonly confused patterns: **〜つもりです** is a personal intention; **〜予定です** is a plan or schedule; **V意向形** (ましょう/よう) is an invitation or shared will to act.","nuance":"With 予定, work or circumstances often determine the plan: '出張に行く予定です'. With つもり, you choose it yourself: '海外に住むつもりです'. V意向形 + と思っています expresses an emerging intention and is softer than つもり.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今年","r":"ことし","m":"this year"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"パソコン","r":"ぱそこん","m":"computer"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買う","r":"かう","m":"buy"},{"t":"つもりです","r":"つもりです","m":"intend to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I intend to buy a new computer this year.","scene":"Chatting with a friend about personal plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜予定です","point":"予定 = an arranged schedule (trains, company, etc.); つもり = a personal intention that may change. Example: 来週、大阪へ出張に行く予定です vs 夏休みに北海道へ行くつもりです。"},{"vs":"V意向形 (〜よう)","point":"意向形 is often used to invite someone: 'let's do it,' or to talk oneself into action; つもり simply states one's own intention. Example: 一緒に行こうよ vs 私は行くつもりです。"}]}},"n4-016||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vるつもりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるつもりです","patternReading":"つもりです","coreMeaning":"Intention / plan to do something (personal intention)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: **1)** using Vます + つもり; **2)** using つもり for a fixed schedule; **3)** overusing Vないつもり when they mean the stronger 'have no intention of doing.'","nuance":"Remember the golden rule: before つもり, always use the **dictionary form**. For strong negation, **Vるつもりはない** sounds more natural and definite. To say 'there is no plan to do something,' you can use 予定はない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"つもりです","r":"つもりです","m":"intend to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I intend to go shopping tomorrow.","scene":"A correct example replacing the incorrect sentence"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 明日、買い物をしますつもりです。 → ✅ 明日、買い物をするつもりです。","❌ 出張は大阪に行くつもりです。 (if it is a company schedule) → ✅ 出張は大阪に行く予定です。","❌ 日本に留学するのつもりです。 → ✅ 日本に留学するつもりです。 (do not add の between Vる and つもり)"],"compare":null}},"n4-016||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vるつもりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるつもりです","patternReading":"つもりです","coreMeaning":"Intention / plan to do something (personal intention)","slug":"memory","definition":"The root 積もり means 'to pile up.' Imagine **piling intentions up in your mind**, without needing to put them on a schedule. When you 'pile up' an intention, that is つもり.","nuance":"Remember this chant: **Vる + つもり**—and only Vる! Do not let Vます or Vた come before it.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-016||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vるつもりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるつもりです","patternReading":"つもりです","coreMeaning":"Intention / plan to do something (personal intention)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common combinations in tests and conversation include **行くつもり**, **買うつもり**, **やめるつもり**, **結婚するつもり**, and **しないつもり**. Memorizing them as chunks helps you speak more quickly.","nuance":"When saying 'I don't intend to...', **Vるつもりはない** is stronger than Vないつもり. Example: '彼は謝るつもりはない'—he has no intention of apologizing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今年","r":"ことし","m":"this year"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"海外旅行","r":"かいがいりょこう","m":"overseas trip"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"つもりです","r":"つもりです","m":"intend to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I intend to travel abroad this year.","scene":"Answering a question about this year's plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-017||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vる予定です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる予定です","patternReading":"よていです","coreMeaning":"Scheduled / planned according to a fixed schedule","slug":"core","definition":"Vる予定です describes something that has been **scheduled** or **arranged in advance**. The speaker uses it to tell the listener about a specific, fairly definite plan. It is common for work schedules, appointments, and events booked in advance.","nuance":"Fairly neutral and polite, usable in both speech and writing. It is **more certain than つもりです** because it is tied to a fixed plan. However, it still means “planned,” so it is not an absolute guarantee.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"予定です","r":"よていです","m":"plan to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I plan to go to Japan next month.","scene":"Answering a boss about next month’s plans"},{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"3時","r":"さんじ","m":"3 o’clock"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"始まる","r":"はじまる","m":"begin"},{"t":"予定です","r":"よていです","m":"is scheduled"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The meeting is scheduled to begin at 3 o’clock.","scene":"Announcing the meeting schedule"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-017||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vる予定です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる予定です","patternReading":"よていです","coreMeaning":"Scheduled / planned according to a fixed schedule","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes how to connect and vary 予定です. The verb before it must be in the **dictionary form**, not the past or て-form. For negation, **予定はありません** is usually used instead of 予定じゃありません.","nuance":"In everyday speech, Japanese speakers often shorten this to 〜する予定. In business emails, the full forms 予定です / 予定があります sound more formal.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"予定です","note":"affirmative: plan to do V"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"予定はありません","note":"negative: have no plan to do V"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"予定があります","note":"emphasizes that a plan exists"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"予定でした","note":"past: was scheduled (may have changed)"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"の予定です","note":"when modified by a noun"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"予定です","r":"よていです","m":"plan to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I plan to go shopping this weekend.","scene":"Answering a friend about the weekend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"残業","r":"ざんぎょう","m":"overtime"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"予定はありません","r":"よていはありません","m":"have no plan"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am not scheduled to work overtime today.","scene":"A coworker asks about overtime"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-017||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vる予定です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる予定です","patternReading":"よていです","coreMeaning":"Scheduled / planned according to a fixed schedule","slug":"usage","definition":"予定です appears often when **reporting plans** to a boss, coworker, or family member, or when discussing events that have been **booked or arranged in advance**, such as tickets, tables, or appointments.","nuance":"To ask about someone’s schedule, use 〜予定ですか？ A short answer is はい、〜する予定です. For a company schedule, つもり sounds like a personal intention, so 予定 is more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"来週","r":"らいしゅう","m":"next week"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"出張","r":"しゅっちょう","m":"business trip"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"予定です","r":"よていです","m":"plan to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am scheduled to go on a business trip to Osaka next week.","scene":"Reporting a business-trip plan to a boss"},{"tokens":[{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"結婚式","r":"けっこんしき","m":"wedding"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出席する","r":"しゅっせきする","m":"attend"},{"t":"予定です","r":"よていです","m":"plan to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I plan to attend my friend’s wedding next month.","scene":"Discussing summer plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-017||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing from つもり","sub":"Vる予定です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる予定です","patternReading":"よていです","coreMeaning":"Scheduled / planned according to a fixed schedule","slug":"compare","definition":"The pattern most easily confused with 予定です is **Vるつもりです**. Both can mean “plan to,” but the source of the plan differs: 予定 is an **external schedule already arranged**, while つもり is the speaker’s **internal intention**.","nuance":"予定 means “I have booked it / it is scheduled”; つもり means “I intend to, but it may change.” Therefore, for a company meeting schedule, 予定 is more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今度","r":"こんど","m":"next"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"holiday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"温泉","r":"おんせん","m":"hot spring"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"予定です","r":"よていです","m":"plan to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I plan to visit a hot spring on my next holiday.","scene":"A ryokan has already been booked"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるつもりです","point":"予定 = a scheduled/fixed plan; つもり = an uncertain personal intention. E.g. 来月東京へ行く予定です (ticket booked) / 来月東京へ行くつもりです (only considering it)."},{"vs":"Vるはずです","point":"はず = a logical inference or expectation, not a schedule. 予定 = an actual schedule. E.g. 彼は来るはずです (he said he would) / 彼は来る予定です (he is scheduled to come)."},{"vs":"Vます","point":"The present-tense verb Vます can describe a fixed timetable, such as train times or official programs; 予定 describes a plan arranged by the speaker or organization. E.g. 電車は10時に出発します / 私は10時に出発する予定です."}]}},"n4-017||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vる予定です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる予定です","patternReading":"よていです","coreMeaning":"Scheduled / planned according to a fixed schedule","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common errors when using 予定です: using the wrong verb form, using つもり instead of 予定, or forming the negative with じゃありません.","nuance":"Remember: **Vる + 予定**; use **予定はありません** for negation; and do not confuse it with the personal intention expressed by つもり.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"土曜日","r":"どようび","m":"Saturday"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見","r":"み","m":"see"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"予定です","r":"よていです","m":"plan to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I plan to go see a movie with a friend on Saturday.","scene":"Inviting a friend out for the weekend"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 来月、日本に行った予定です。 → ✅ 来月、日本に行く予定です。","❌ 明日は会議があるつもりです。 → ✅ 明日は会議がある予定です。 (the schedule is already set)","❌ 明日、勉強するの予定です。 → ✅ 明日、勉強する予定です。"],"compare":null}},"n4-017||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vる予定です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる予定です","patternReading":"よていです","coreMeaning":"Scheduled / planned according to a fixed schedule","slug":"memory","definition":"This memory tip helps you remember the nuance of 予定: 予 means “in advance” or “prepared beforehand,” while 定 means “decide” or “fix.” Together, they suggest something **decided in advance**, like an entry in a schedule.","nuance":"Build this association: 予定 is like a **calendar entry on your phone**; つもり is like a **note in your head**. If it is written in the calendar, use 予定.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-017||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vる予定です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる予定です","patternReading":"よていです","coreMeaning":"Scheduled / planned according to a fixed schedule","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions with 予定 in exams and daily life include 〜する予定です (plan to do something), 予定があります (have plans), 予定を入れる (schedule something), 予定を変更する (change a schedule), and 予定が入っています (already have plans / be busy).","nuance":"In conversation, if a friend asks 週末、暇？ and you already have plans, you can answer ごめん、予定があるから. 予定が入っています is often used as a polite way to decline.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"バス"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"3時","r":"さんじ","m":"3 o’clock"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出発","r":"しゅっぱつ","m":"depart"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"予定です","r":"よていです","m":"is scheduled"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The bus is scheduled to depart at 3 o’clock.","scene":"Checking the bus departure notice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-018||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"まだVていません · N4","v2":{"pattern":"まだVていません","patternReading":"まだ ていません","coreMeaning":"Not yet ~ (the action has not happened as expected)","slug":"core","definition":"まだVていません describes an action that has not happened at the time of speaking, although it was expected to happen by then. For example, not having eaten by noon or not having submitted work by the deadline. It is the negative form of Vています, while まだ adds the meaning 'still not yet' and highlights the unfinished state.","nuance":"Polite and suitable for teachers, bosses, and strangers because it uses ます. It often reports an incomplete state or gives a mild apology: 'Sorry, I haven't done it yet.' Casually, Japanese speakers may shorten it to 'まだ食べてない', but at N4 use the full 'まだ食べていません'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いいえ","m":"no"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","m":"not yet"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やっていません","r":"やっていません","m":"haven't done"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No, I haven't done my homework yet.","scene":"The teacher asks whether the class has done the homework."},{"tokens":[{"t":"まだ","m":"not yet"},{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べていません","r":"たべていません","m":"haven't eaten"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I haven't eaten lunch yet.","scene":"A seatmate invites you to lunch, but you have not eaten."},{"tokens":[{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まだ","m":"not yet"},{"t":"見ていません","r":"みていません","m":"haven't seen"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I haven't seen that movie yet.","scene":"A friend talks about a great movie you have not seen."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-018||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"まだVていません · N4","v2":{"pattern":"まだVていません","patternReading":"まだ ていません","coreMeaning":"Not yet ~ (the action has not happened as expected)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is **まだ + verb in te-form + いません**. Conjugate the verb into te-form as with Vています, then add いません. Do **not** use ません alone or conjugate it as ありませんでした.","nuance":"This is the polite negative form. For group 1 (五段) verbs, te-form endings may become て or で; verbs ending in む・ぶ・ぬ change to んで. When followed by いる, the form before いる remains te-form.","structure":[{"lhs":"まだ + Vて + いません","rhs":"not yet V (unfinished state)","note":"V is in te-form, followed by いません."},{"lhs":"V（ます形）: 食べます","rhs":"Vて: 食べて","note":"Drop ます and add て/で to make te-form."},{"lhs":"Vて + いません","rhs":"negative of Vています","note":"Vています shows a state; its negative says it has not been reached."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"まだ","m":"not yet"},{"t":"レポート","m":"report"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いていません","r":"かいていません","m":"haven't written"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I haven't written the report yet.","scene":"The boss asks about the missing weekly report."},{"tokens":[{"t":"まだ","m":"not yet"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"package"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"届いていません","r":"とどいていません","m":"hasn't arrived"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The package still hasn't arrived.","scene":"You call to ask about an online order."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-018||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"まだVていません · N4","v2":{"pattern":"まだVていません","patternReading":"まだ ていません","coreMeaning":"Not yet ~ (the action has not happened as expected)","slug":"usage","definition":"Most commonly used in question-and-answer conversations when someone asks 'もう〜しましたか?' (have you ... yet?) and you have not done it: 'いいえ、まだ〜ていません'. It also reports unfinished tasks such as not submitting a report, not arriving, or not eating.","nuance":"Do not use it for actions with no expected timing. For 'I have never been to Japan' (一度も行ったことがない), do not use まだ行っていません. With まだ, the sentence usually suggests that the action is planned or expected but has not happened yet.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いいえ","m":"no"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","m":"not yet"},{"t":"切符","r":"きっぷ","m":"ticket"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買っていません","r":"かっていません","m":"haven't bought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No, I still haven't bought the tickets.","scene":"A friend asks if you bought tickets before inviting you to a movie."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まだ","m":"not yet"},{"t":"来ていません","r":"きていません","m":"hasn't arrived"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He still hasn't arrived.","scene":"The meeting has started, but one person is missing."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-018||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions from similar patterns","sub":"まだVていません · N4","v2":{"pattern":"まだVていません","patternReading":"まだ ていません","coreMeaning":"Not yet ~ (the action has not happened as expected)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish まだVていません from three easily confused patterns. The key point is that **not yet** signals an incomplete state, while the others signal something done or ongoing. Focus on the time state, not just the verb.","nuance":"Vてしまいました can add regret; まだVていません simply states that something remains undone. Vたばかり means 'just finished and still fresh', while まだVていません means the action has not begun.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いいえ","m":"no"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","m":"not yet"},{"t":"予約","r":"よやく","m":"reservation"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"していません","r":"していません","m":"haven't made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Not yet, I haven't made a reservation.","scene":"A friend asks whether you booked a restaurant table."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vています","point":"Vています = doing V or in a state resulting from V; まだVていません = still not in that state. '今は食べています' (eating now) differs from 'まだ食べていません' (not eaten yet)."},{"vs":"Vてしまいました","point":"Vてしまいました = finished or accidentally did it; まだVていません = have not done it. 'もう食べましたか?' → done: '食べてしまいました'; not done: 'まだ食べていません'."},{"vs":"Vたばかりです","point":"Vたばかり = just finished, a short time ago; まだVていません = have not done it. '食べたばかりです' (just ate) vs 'まだ食べていません' (haven't eaten)."}]}},"n4-018||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"まだVていません · N4","v2":{"pattern":"まだVていません","patternReading":"まだ ていません","coreMeaning":"Not yet ~ (the action has not happened as expected)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate 'not yet' directly as まだ + Vません, which is incorrect. To say that an expected action has not happened yet, use **Vていません**. まだ + Vません often sounds like 'will not do it'.","nuance":"In casual conversation, Japanese speakers may omit い and say 'まだ食べてない', but this is a spoken contraction; use the full form in N4 writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"まだ","m":"not yet"},{"t":"掃除","r":"そうじ","m":"cleaning"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"していません","r":"していません","m":"haven't done"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I haven't cleaned yet.","scene":"Your mother asks whether you cleaned your room."}],"pitfalls":["❌ まだ昼ごはんを食べません。 → ✅ まだ昼ごはんを食べていません。 (With まだ, use Vていません, not Vません.)","❌ まだレポートを出しませんでした。 → ✅ まだレポートを出していません。 (Do not use past ませんでした with まだ; the unfinished state is current.)","❌ まだ寝ています。(meaning 'not asleep yet') → ✅ まだ寝ていません。 (まだ寝ています = still sleeping, the opposite meaning!)","❌ まだ日本に行きません。(meaning 'have never been to Japan') → ✅ 一度も日本に行ったことがありません。 (Use ことがない for lack of experience.)"],"compare":null}},"n4-018||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"まだVていません · N4","v2":{"pattern":"まだVていません","patternReading":"まだ ていません","coreMeaning":"Not yet ~ (the action has not happened as expected)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: まだ at the beginning signals that something is **not finished yet**. **Vていません** means the state of V has not been reached. Together they form the mnemonic: もう…? → まだVていません.","nuance":"Learn the question-and-answer pair rather than isolated words. When you see もう with ました, think of まだ with ていません; they work together like 'already/not yet'.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-018||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"まだVていません · N4","v2":{"pattern":"まだVていません","patternReading":"まだ ていません","coreMeaning":"Not yet ~ (the action has not happened as expected)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear often in workplace conversations, classrooms, and tests. Learn them as chunks: **まだ決めていません**, **まだ始まっていません**, **まだ終わっていません**, and so on.","nuance":"For 'not known yet' meaning information has not been grasped or confirmed, Japanese commonly uses まだ分かっていません. For an undecided choice, まだ決めていません is very natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","m":"sorry"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","m":"not yet"},{"t":"決めていません","r":"きめていません","m":"haven't decided"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Sorry, I still haven't decided.","scene":"A restaurant employee asks whether the customer has chosen."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-019||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vたほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたほうがいいです","patternReading":"たほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should do ~ (gentle advice, optional)","slug":"core","definition":"「Vた + ほうがいいです」 gives advice that someone **should do something**. The speaker does not command them, but points out a **better** choice than the alternatives. This is the most common beginner pattern for everyday advice.","nuance":"The tone is **friendly and gentle**, suitable for friends, family, and coworkers. Adding よ makes it sound **caring and reminding**. Do not use this pattern to advise superiors, even with と思います.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"熱","r":"ねつ","m":"fever"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある"},{"t":"なら"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"病院","r":"びょういん","m":"hospital"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行った","r":"いった","m":"go"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いいです"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you have a fever, you should go to the hospital.","scene":"A feverish friend needs medical advice."},{"tokens":[{"t":"もっと","m":"more"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べた","r":"たべた","m":"eat"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いい"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"思います","r":"おもいます","m":"think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think you should eat more vegetables.","scene":"Advising a sister to eat vegetables."},{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持った","r":"もった","m":"take"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いいです"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It will rain, so you should take an umbrella.","scene":"Mom reminds you to take an umbrella."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-019||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"Vたほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたほうがいいです","patternReading":"たほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should do ~ (gentle advice, optional)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the **forms** of the pattern. The verb before ほうがいい uses the **た-form** for advice to do something, or the **ない-form** for advice not to do something. Polite, casual, and regretful variants are also possible.","nuance":"Vたほうがいいです is standard in both speech and writing. Vないほうがいいです has the opposite meaning with the same friendly level. Vたほうがよかった（です） refers to the past and expresses regret: “should have done.”","structure":[{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"ほうがいいです","note":"should do something"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"ほうがいいです","note":"should not do something"},{"lhs":"Vた + ほうがいい","rhs":"と思います","note":"polite, softened advice"},{"lhs":"Vた + ほうがいいです","rhs":"よ","note":"shows concern"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"ほうがよかった（です）","note":"regret: should have done"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強した","r":"べんきょうした","m":"study"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いいです"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should study every day.","scene":"A father advises daily study."},{"tokens":[{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"sleep"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"before"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"お風呂","r":"おふろ","m":"bath"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入った","r":"はいった","m":"take"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いいです"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should take a warm bath before bed.","scene":"Mom recommends a warm bath before bed."},{"tokens":[{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"overexertion"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しない","m":"not do"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いいです"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should not push yourself too hard.","scene":"Advising an overworked friend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-019||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Vたほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたほうがいいです","patternReading":"たほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should do ~ (gentle advice, optional)","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch covers common **real-life situations**. The pattern appears when the speaker notices a health problem, when someone is unsure which option to choose, or when suggesting a good habit. Adding と思います expresses a personal opinion and softens the advice.","nuance":"The sentence gives **advice or guidance**, not a command. In work and friendship contexts, よ can emphasize concern. Adding と思います makes the advice more personal and less pressuring.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"疲れて","r":"つかれて","m":"tired"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"なら"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"寝た","r":"ねた","m":"sleep"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いいです"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you are tired, you should go to bed early.","scene":"A tired friend needs rest."},{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅れる","r":"おくれる","m":"be late"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"タクシー","m":"taxi"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"行った","r":"いった","m":"go"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いいです"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The train is delayed, so taking a taxi is better.","scene":"Choosing transport to work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"もっと","m":"more"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強した","r":"べんきょうした","m":"study"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いい"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"思います","r":"おもいます","m":"think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think you should study more Japanese.","scene":"Advising an aspiring JLPT N3 candidate."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-019||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Vたほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたほうがいいです","patternReading":"たほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should do ~ (gentle advice, optional)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes 〜たほうがいい from easily confused patterns. The key idea is that **should do** is optional advice, not pressure, and contrasts with 〜ないほうがいい.","nuance":"Compared with 〜てください, this pattern is gentler. Compared with 〜なければなりません, it is not obligatory. It belongs with Vないほうがいい, but gives the opposite advice.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲んだ","r":"のんだ","m":"take"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いいです"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should take your medicine.","scene":"Reminding a sick friend to take medicine."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてください","point":"Vてください is a direct request or command; Vたほうがいい is only advice. Example: 薬を飲んだほうがいいです (you should take medicine) ↔ 薬を飲んでください (please take the medicine)."},{"vs":"Vなければなりません","point":"Vなければなりません expresses an obligation; Vたほうがいい only suggests an action, which the listener may choose not to do."},{"vs":"Vないほうがいいです","point":"Opposite advice: Vた = should do, Vない = should not do. Both have the same friendly level."},{"vs":"疑問詞＋Vたらいいですか","point":"Vたほうがいい gives advice as an answer; 疑問詞＋Vたらいいですか asks for advice. Example: 電車で行ったほうがいいです (you should take the train) ↔ どう行ったらいいですか (how should I go?)."}]}},"n4-019||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vたほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたほうがいいです","patternReading":"たほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should do ~ (gentle advice, optional)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This branch lists common errors learners make with 〜たほうがいい. Remember that the verb before ほうがいい uses the **た-form** for advice to do something, or the **ない-form** for advice not to do something.","nuance":"The most common errors are using the dictionary form or the ます-stem before ほうがいい. Also, do not use the present form for past regret; change it to よかった.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちゃんと","m":"properly"},{"t":"朝ご飯","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べた","r":"たべた","m":"eat"},{"t":"ほうが"},{"t":"いいです"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You should eat a proper breakfast.","scene":"Mom reminds you to eat breakfast."}],"pitfalls":["❌ もっと野菜を食べますほうがいいです。 → ✅ もっと野菜を食べたほうがいいです。 (Use the た-form, not ます.)","❌ 休みほうがいいです。 → ✅ 休んだほうがいいです。 (Use the た-form.)","❌ 行かないですほうがいいです。 → ✅ 行かないほうがいいです。 (Do not add です after 行かない.)","❌ 昨日行ったほうがいいです。(ngữ cảnh tiếc nuối) → ✅ 昨日行ったほうがよかったです。 (Use よかった for past regret.)"],"compare":null}},"n4-019||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vたほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたほうがいいです","patternReading":"たほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should do ~ (gentle advice, optional)","slug":"memory","definition":"This memory trick helps you remember to use **Vた** before ほうがいい. Imagine た as “completed” and ほう as “side.” For advice, place the action on the **completed side** so the scale leans toward the better choice.","nuance":"This is a memory aid, not a grammatical nuance. For advice not to do something, simply replace Vた with Vない.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-020||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vないほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vないほうがいいです","patternReading":"ないほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should not do ~ (advice to avoid bad consequences)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern **Vないほうがいいです** advises someone **not to do something** because doing it may lead to a bad result. The speaker gives a personal comparison: ‘doing it is not good; not doing it is better’.","nuance":"This is gentle advice, not a command. Use it with friends, family, or people below you in status; add **よ** in casual speech, or use **と思います** to sound more polite and softer. It is somewhat stronger than a simple suggestion but much weaker than **Vてはいけない**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"テスト","m":"test"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ゲーム","m":"game"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しない","m":"not play"},{"t":"ほうがいい","m":"better to"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a test tomorrow, so you shouldn't play games.","scene":"Your roommate plans to stay up gaming before a test."},{"tokens":[{"t":"古い","r":"ふるい","m":"old"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"それ","r":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べない","r":"たべない","m":"not eat"},{"t":"ほうがいいです","m":"better to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It's old, so you shouldn't eat it.","scene":"Your friend is about to eat food kept too long."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-020||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"Vないほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vないほうがいいです","patternReading":"ないほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should not do ~ (advice to avoid bad consequences)","slug":"forms","definition":"The first part is always **Vない** (the negative form of a verb), followed by **ほうがいいです**. Example: 行く → 行かない → 行かないほうがいいです. Group I: 書く→書かない, 読む→読まない; Group II: 食べる→食べない; Group III: する→しない, 来る→こない.","nuance":"This is an everyday advice pattern. **です** keeps it polite; adding **と思います** makes it a personal opinion and sounds gentler when speaking to someone older or of higher status.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（ない形）","rhs":"ほうがいいです","note":"Advises not doing it."},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"ほうがよかったです","note":"Regrets doing it; should not have done it."},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"ほうがいいと思います","note":"Polite personal opinion."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"any more"},{"t":"飲まない","r":"のまない","m":"not drink"},{"t":"ほうがいいです","m":"better to"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You'd better not drink any more.","scene":"A friend wants more alcohol despite being drunk."},{"tokens":[{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"出ない","r":"でない","m":"not go out"},{"t":"ほうがいい","m":"better to"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think you shouldn't go outside.","scene":"Your sister plans to go out during a storm."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-020||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Vないほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vないほうがいいです","patternReading":"ないほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should not do ~ (advice to avoid bad consequences)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern appears in many everyday situations: parents advising children, friends warning each other, doctors advising patients, and so on. The common point is that the speaker sees a risk and advises against it before something bad happens.","nuance":"Because this is subjective advice, add **と思います** or use a gentle tone with older people or superiors. With close friends, adding **よ** creates a caring, familiar feel.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"overdo it"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しない","m":"not do"},{"t":"ほうがいいです","m":"better to"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You shouldn't push yourself.","scene":"A coworker works nonstop and may burn out."},{"tokens":[{"t":"夜","r":"よる","m":"night"},{"t":"遅く","r":"おそく","m":"late"},{"t":"出かけない","r":"でかけない","m":"not go out"},{"t":"ほうがいい","m":"better to"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You shouldn't go out late at night.","scene":"An older brother warns his sister not to go out late."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-020||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with Vたほうがいい","sub":"Vないほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vないほうがいいです","patternReading":"ないほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should not do ~ (advice to avoid bad consequences)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes **Vないほうがいい** from related patterns in conversation. **Vたほうがいい** advises doing something, while **Vないほうがいい** advises against it; both are personal suggestions. **Vてはいけない** is a strong prohibition, often based on rules or morality. **Vないで** is a shortened form of **Vないでください**, making it more direct and command-like.","nuance":"For gentle advice, use **Vないほうがいい**. For a prohibition, use **Vてはいけない**. **Vないで** is a direct, casual command and is less polite.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"たくさん","m":"a lot"},{"t":"飲まない","r":"のまない","m":"not take"},{"t":"ほうがいいです","m":"better to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You shouldn't take a lot of medicine.","scene":"A friend is about to swallow a handful of painkillers."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vたほうがいいです","point":"Vたほうがいい = advice to SHOULD do; Vないほうがいい = advice to SHOULD NOT do. Same structure, opposite meanings."},{"vs":"Vてはいけません","point":"Vてはいけません = FORBIDDEN, strong and rule-based; Vないほうがいい = only ADVICE, not a requirement."}]}},"n4-020||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vないほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vないほうがいいです","patternReading":"ないほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should not do ~ (advice to avoid bad consequences)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often forget **が** in **ほうがいい**, or confuse it with **Vないで**. Remember that the fixed structure is **Vない + ほうがいい**. **Vないで** usually appears with **ください** or in a direct command. Also, **Vないから** (because someone does not do…) is wrong when giving advice.","nuance":"The most common error is dropping **が** from **ほうがいい**. In casual speech you may hear **Vないほういい**, but writing and test answers require **が**. Distinguish **Vないで** (don't) from **Vないほうがいい** (shouldn't).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗らない","r":"のらない","m":"not ride"},{"t":"ほうがいいです","m":"better to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You shouldn't take the train.","scene":"The railway has problems today, so take a bus."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 間に合わないほういいです。 → ✅ 間に合わないほうがいいです。","❌ 無理しないでほうがいいです。 → ✅ 無理しないほうがいいです。","❌ 試験があるから、ゲームをしないからいいです。 → ✅ 試験があるから、ゲームをしないほうがいいです。","❌ ここでタバコを吸わなくほうがいいです。 → ✅ ここでタバコを吸わないほうがいいです。"],"compare":null}},"n4-020||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vないほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vないほうがいいです","patternReading":"ないほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should not do ~ (advice to avoid bad consequences)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: **Vたほうがいい** = should do it (choose ✔), **Vないほうがいい** = should not do it (choose ✘). Picture a scale: doing it is risky, not doing it is safe → choose the **not doing** side because it is better.","nuance":"There is no special nuance beyond advice. This tip helps prevent confusing the two directions.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-020||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vないほうがいいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vないほうがいいです","patternReading":"ないほうがいいです","coreMeaning":"Should not do ~ (advice to avoid bad consequences)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in textbooks and everyday life include **無理をしないほうがいい** (don't overdo it), **遅くまで働かないほうがいい** (don't work late), **たくさん食べないほうがいい** (don't eat a lot), and **外に出ないほうがいい** (don't go outside).","nuance":"These phrases give familiar, everyday advice and often appear in spoken Japanese and listening exercises.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お酒","r":"おさけ","m":"alcohol"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みすぎない","r":"のみすぎない","m":"not overdrink"},{"t":"ほうがいいです","m":"better to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You shouldn't drink too much alcohol.","scene":"A manager reminds an employee at a party."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-021||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V命令形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V命令形","patternReading":"ぶいめいれいけい","coreMeaning":"Strong commands: Do ~! (rough, direct)","slug":"core","definition":"V命令形 (the imperative form) gives a **direct command**, meaning 'Do it!' or 'Do it now!'. It sounds **very rough and forceful**, and is used only in emergencies, with close friends or subordinates, on signs, or in sports. Do not use it with elders, bosses, or new acquaintances.","nuance":"Colloquial, with a **commanding, coercive** tone. Men use it more often than women. In cheers (頑張れ), it shows **strong encouragement**; on signs (止まれ), it is emotionless and simply mandatory.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もっと","r":"もっと","m":"more"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"quickly"},{"t":"起きろ","r":"おきろ","m":"get up"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Get up even faster!","scene":"Mother wakes her son late for school"},{"tokens":[{"t":"みんな","r":"みんな","m":"everyone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ぜったいに","r":"ぜったいに","m":"definitely"},{"t":"頑張れ","r":"がんばれ","m":"do your best"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Everyone, give it your all!","scene":"Cheering for the home team before a game"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"じっと","r":"じっと","m":"stay still"},{"t":"止まれ","r":"とまれ","m":"stop"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Stay right here!","scene":"Traffic sign at an intersection"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-021||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"V命令形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V命令形","patternReading":"ぶいめいれいけい","coreMeaning":"Strong commands: Do ~! (rough, direct)","slug":"forms","definition":"Conjugate **V命令形** by verb group: **Group I** (五段): change the final う sound to え (書く→書け, 話す→話せ); **Group II** (一段): drop る and add ろ (食べる→食べろ); **Group III** (不規則): する→しろ, 来る→こい. くれる→くれ is a special irregular form.","nuance":"This short form has no separate past or negative form. For a negative command, use **Vるな** (forbid doing). Group II verbs often end in る after an e or i sound (食べる, 見る), but exceptions exist (帰る → Group I).","structure":[{"lhs":"Group I","rhs":"v(い) → v(え)","note":"書く→書け, 話す→話せ, 走る→走れ"},{"lhs":"Group II","rhs":"v(る) → v(ろ)","note":"食べる→食べろ, 見る→見ろ"},{"lhs":"Group III","rhs":"する→しろ / 来る→こい","note":"勉強する→勉強しろ"},{"lhs":"Irregular","rhs":"くれる→くれ","note":"no regular pattern"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"まっすぐ","r":"まっすぐ","m":"straight"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"ahead"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"方","r":"ほう","m":"direction"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"走れ","r":"はしれ","m":"run"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Run straight ahead!","scene":"Coach shouts at trainees in the gym"},{"tokens":[{"t":"こっち","r":"こっち","m":"this way"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"方","r":"ほう","m":"direction"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ろ","r":"みろ","m":"look"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Look this way!","scene":"Friend calls someone to look over"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: 辞書形 → 命令形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 — う row → え row","Change the final sound from the う row to the え row; add nothing","飲む（のむ） → `飲め（のめ）` (drink) ・ 書く（かく） → `書け（かけ）` (write) ・ 話す（はなす） → `話せ（はなせ）` (speak) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰れ（かえれ）` (return)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — 一段 — drop る + ろ","Drop る, then add ろ","食べる（たべる） → `食べろ（たべろ）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見ろ（みろ）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きろ（おきろ）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝ろ（ねろ）` (sleep)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — する → しろ / せよ","しろ (rough colloquial) or せよ (written/formal)","する → `しろ` (do) ・ 勉強する（べんきょうする） → `勉強しろ（べんきょうしろ）` (study)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — 来る → 来い (こい)","来る → 来い (こい) — irregular","来る（くる） → `来い（こい）` (come)"],["**Special case** — くれる → くれ (special)","Ichidan, but an irregular imperative: くれ (not くれろ)","くれる → `くれ` (give)"]]}}},"n4-021||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"V命令形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V命令形","patternReading":"ぶいめいれいけい","coreMeaning":"Strong commands: Do ~! (rough, direct)","slug":"usage","definition":"V命令形 appears in **emergencies** (逃げろ, 動くな), on **signs** (止まれ), in **cheers** (頑張れ, 走れ), or when commanding family, friends, or subordinates (早くしろ, 持って来い). Avoid it completely with elders, customers, and bosses.","nuance":"Very **forceful**, but sports use can add positive energy (ポジティブ). On signs, it is a mandatory rule without emotion. Among close friends, it can be a playful command or a strong prompt.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"quickly"},{"t":"逃げろ","r":"にげろ","m":"run away"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Run, hurry!","scene":"Someone shouts during a fire"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もっと","r":"もっと","m":"more"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"quickly"},{"t":"しろ","r":"しろ","m":"do it"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Do it even faster!","scene":"Older brother hurries his slow sibling"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-021||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar forms","sub":"V命令形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V命令形","patternReading":"ぶいめいれいけい","coreMeaning":"Strong commands: Do ~! (rough, direct)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare **V命令形** with two easily confused forms: **〜てください** means 'please do' and is polite, while **Vるな** means 'don't do' and is prohibitive. All three concern actions, but their tones differ completely.","nuance":"V命令形 is rough and forceful, for closeness or emergencies. てください is polite and safe with strangers or superiors. Vるな is a very strong negative command that absolutely forbids an action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こっち","r":"こっち","m":"this way"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"来い","r":"こい","m":"come here"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Come here!","scene":"Father calls his child to help (informal)"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てください","point":"てください is polite for superiors or strangers; V命令形 is rough for subordinates or emergencies. Ex: 早く食べてください vs 早く食べろ."},{"vs":"Vるな","point":"Vるな forbids an action (negative command). Ex: 飲め！ (Drink) is the opposite of 飲むな！ (Don't drink)."}]}},"n4-021||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V命令形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V命令形","patternReading":"ぶいめいれいけい","coreMeaning":"Strong commands: Do ~! (rough, direct)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often **overuse imperatives** because 'please' and 'do' commands can sound fairly casual in English. With bosses or customers, V命令形 is **rude**. Also watch for wrong **Group II** forms (見る→見ろ, 起きる→起きろ) and **来る→こい**.","nuance":"For politeness, avoid V命令形; use てください or 〜なさい (with children). Note: 'きろ' is wrong because 来る is an irregular Group III verb.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちゃんと","r":"ちゃんと","m":"properly"},{"t":"見ろ","r":"みろ","m":"look"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Look carefully!","scene":"Teacher makes students look at the board"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生に「早くしろ！」 → ✅ 先生に「早くしてください」 (use polite speech with a teacher)","❌ 見れ！ → ✅ 見ろ！ (Group II: drop る + ろ)","❌ 起きれ！ → ✅ 起きろ！ (Group II)","❌ きろ！ → ✅ こい！ (irregular 来る)"],"compare":null}},"n4-021||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V命令形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V命令形","patternReading":"ぶいめいれいけい","coreMeaning":"Strong commands: Do ~! (rough, direct)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory aid: **Group I** changes the final sound to the 'え' row (い→え). **Group II** changes る → ろ (like 'roar'). **Group III**: する→しろ, 来る→こい. Think of a sharp shout like 'Hey!' or 'Roar!' to remember commands.","nuance":"Visualize an angry person with wide eyes and a pointing finger shouting: 'Hey! Do it now!' or 'Roar! Do it now!'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-021||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V命令形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V命令形","patternReading":"ぶいめいれいけい","coreMeaning":"Strong commands: Do ~! (rough, direct)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common imperative phrases in **daily life/anime/manga** include **止まれ** (stop), **頑張れ** (do your best), **逃げろ** (run), and **早くしろ** (hurry up). In JLPT N4, you may see lines like '早く起きろ' in dialogue.","nuance":"These phrases are all **strong** and belong in close or emergency contexts. In sports, '頑張れ' is a very common cheer.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"all"},{"t":"飲め","r":"のめ","m":"drink"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Drink all of this!","scene":"Friend pressures someone to drink water"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-022||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vるな (禁止) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるな (禁止)","patternReading":"るな","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: Don't do it! — a very strong negative command","slug":"core","definition":"Vるな means **prohibit an action; don't do it!** It is a very strong negative command with no softening. The speaker wants to stop an action immediately, often because of danger, irritation, or playful familiarity.","nuance":"Spoken, blunt, and forceful. Do not use it with superiors, in formal writing, or with strangers. With close friends it can be playful; with subordinates, it sounds like a direct prohibition.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入る","r":"はいる","m":"enter"},{"t":"な","m":"don't"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Don't come in here!","scene":"His younger brother is about to enter while he is changing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"危ない","r":"あぶない","m":"dangerous"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"触る","r":"さわる","m":"touch"},{"t":"な","m":"don't"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"It's dangerous—don't touch it!","scene":"Shouting as a child reaches for boiling water"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-022||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and related forms","sub":"Vるな (禁止) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるな (禁止)","patternReading":"るな","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: Don't do it! — a very strong negative command","slug":"forms","definition":"Form this prohibition by taking the **dictionary form of a verb** and adding **な** at the end. All Group 1, Group 2, and Group 3 verbs follow the same rule. It has no separate past, negative, or polite form.","nuance":"な completes the sentence and never combines with です/ます. To quote a prohibition, use 「Vるな」と言う / と言われる.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"な","note":"入る → 入るな / 食べる → 食べるな / する → するな / 来る → くるな"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"するな","note":"Not しな"},{"lhs":"来る","rhs":"くるな","note":"Not きな"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ゴミ","m":"trash"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"捨てる","r":"すてる","m":"throw away"},{"t":"な","m":"don't"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do not throw trash here.","scene":"Posting a reminder in an apartment building"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"な","m":"don't"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Don't eat here!","scene":"Joking with a friend before photographing the food"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: 辞書形 → 禁止形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 + な","辞書形 (unchanged) + な","飲む（のむ） → `飲むな（のむな）` (drink) ・ 書く（かく） → `書くな（かくな）` (write) ・ 話す（はなす） → `話すな（はなすな）` (speak) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰るな（かえるな）` (return)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — 一段 + な","辞書形 (unchanged) + な","食べる（たべる） → `食べるな（たべるな）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見るな（みるな）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きるな（おきるな）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝るな（ねるな）` (sleep)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — する → するな","する + な = するな","する → `するな` (do) ・ 心配する（しんぱいする） → `心配するな（しんぱいするな）` (worry)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — 来る → 来るな","来る + な = 来るな (くるな)","来る（くる） → `来るな（くるな）` (come)"],["**Special case** — べからず · Classical (signs, regulations)","辞書形 + べからず — archaic written form, used only on signs/slogans/proverbs","入る（はいる） → `入るべからず（はいるべからず）` (enter) ・ 駐車する（ちゅうしゃする） → `駐車するべからず（ちゅうしゃするべからず）` (park) ・ 初心忘るべからず（しょしんわするべからず） → `初心忘るべからず（しょしんわするべからず）` (do not forget your original intention (proverb))"]]}}},"n4-022||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Vるな (禁止) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるな (禁止)","patternReading":"るな","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: Don't do it! — a very strong negative command","slug":"usage","definition":"Use it when you want to **order someone to stop an action immediately**. Typical situations include warning signs, imminent danger, anger, or playful encouragement such as “don't give up!”","nuance":"It is not advice like “you shouldn't”; it is a prohibition. The tone is strong, though it can be humorous among close friends. Signs often use short forms such as 入るな or the more formal-looking word '禁止'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"危ない","r":"あぶない","m":"dangerous"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"触る","r":"さわる","m":"touch"},{"t":"な","m":"don't"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"It's dangerous, so don't touch it!","scene":"Seeing a child reach for a hot iron"},{"tokens":[{"t":"嘘","r":"うそ","m":"lie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つく","m":"tell"},{"t":"な","m":"don't"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Don't lie!","scene":"Arguing with a close friend after catching a lie"},{"tokens":[{"t":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"諦める","r":"あきらめる","m":"give up"},{"t":"な","m":"don't"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Don't give up yet!","scene":"A teacher encourages students before an exam"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-022||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with confusing patterns","sub":"Vるな (禁止) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるな (禁止)","patternReading":"るな","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: Don't do it! — a very strong negative command","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes Vるな from commonly confused patterns: a more polite request, an affirmative command, and advice. Each differs in **strength and purpose**.","nuance":"Vるな is the strongest negative prohibition here. If you need to sound polite, do not use Vるな.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"走る","r":"はしる","m":"run"},{"t":"な","m":"don't"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Don't run here.","scene":"A teacher shouts at students running in the hallway"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vないでください","point":"Both mean “don't,” but ないでください is polite: 入らないでください. Vるな is blunt and used only informally: 入るな."},{"vs":"V命令形","point":"命令形 commands someone TO DO something: 入れ！ Vるな commands someone NOT TO DO it: 入るな！."},{"vs":"Vないほうがいいです","point":"ないほうがいい gives advice: 行かないほうがいいです. Vるな is a prohibition: 行くな！."}]}},"n4-022||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vるな (禁止) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるな (禁止)","patternReading":"るな","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: Don't do it! — a very strong negative command","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate “don't” as ないで and then add な, or use Vるな with older people. Remember: Vるな is a blunt prohibition used only with subordinates or close friends.","nuance":"To be polite with a superior, switch to 〜ないでください. Adding です/ます cannot make Vるな polite.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"遊ぶ","r":"あそぶ","m":"play"},{"t":"な","m":"don't"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Don't play here.","scene":"A mother sternly tells her child not to play near an outlet"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生、ここでたばこを吸うな。 → ✅ 先生、ここでたばこを吸わないでください。","❌ 食べないな。 → ✅ 食べるな。（must be dictionary form + な）","❌ 入るなです。 → ✅ 入るな。（な does not combine with です）","❌ 入るなください。 → ✅ 入らないでください。"],"compare":null}},"n4-022||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vるな (禁止) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるな (禁止)","patternReading":"るな","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: Don't do it! — a very strong negative command","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember **な** as a shouted “NO!” when someone is about to do something forbidden. Because it is dictionary form + な, it sounds short and sharp: 入るな! 触るな!","nuance":"This trick helps you remember the form; do not forget its blunt tone and limited usage.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-022||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common fixed phrases","sub":"Vるな (禁止) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるな (禁止)","patternReading":"るな","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: Don't do it! — a very strong negative command","slug":"phrases","definition":"These Vるな phrases are common in daily life and fiction: 入るな, 触るな, 諦めるな, 嘘をつくな. They appear often in casual speech, films, and warning signs.","nuance":"These phrases usually carry strong emotion: warning, encouragement, or playful teasing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忘れる","r":"わすれる","m":"forget"},{"t":"な","m":"don't"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Don't forget, okay!","scene":"A close friend texts before an outing: remember an umbrella"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-023||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nと読みます／書きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nと読みます／書きます","patternReading":"とよみます／かきます","coreMeaning":"N is read/written as ~ (quoted verbatim)","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern **quotes verbatim** how to read (と読みます) or write (と書きます) a kanji, word, or proper name. The speaker gives information such as “A is read/written as B,” helping the listener know the correct pronunciation or spelling. It is very common when learning vocabulary and kanji.","nuance":"**Neutral and clear**, used in classes, at work, or in everyday conversation. It simply provides information and expresses no strong emotion. Unlike 〜というN, this pattern focuses on **reading or writing**, not what something is called.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"森","r":"もり","m":"forest"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"読みます","r":"よみます","m":"read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This kanji is read as “mori” (forest).","scene":"The teacher points to 森 on the board and teaches its reading."},{"tokens":[{"t":"「ともだち」","m":"friend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"書きます","r":"かきます","m":"write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"“Tomodachi” is written in kanji as “友達”.","scene":"Explaining how to write “friend” in kanji."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-023||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"Nと読みます／書きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nと読みます／書きます","patternReading":"とよみます／かきます","coreMeaning":"N is read/written as ~ (quoted verbatim)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure always includes the **particle と**, which functions as quotation marks and follows the quoted part. The verb that follows is 読みます (read) or 書きます (write).","nuance":"The part before と may be a kanji, vocabulary item, proper name, and so on. Use **漢字で** (in kanji) to specify the writing system. Negative forms are uncommon and mainly used in explanations: “It is not read as ~.”","structure":[{"lhs":"漢字（言葉）","rhs":"＋ と ＋ 読みます","note":"Read as… (pronunciation)"},{"lhs":"漢字（言葉）","rhs":"＋ と ＋ 書きます","note":"Written as… (spelling)"},{"lhs":"何","rhs":"と読みますか","note":"Ask how to read it"},{"lhs":"どう","rhs":"書きますか","note":"Ask how to write it"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"読みます","r":"よみます","m":"read"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How is this kanji read?","scene":"Asking a teacher about an unfamiliar kanji."},{"tokens":[{"t":"「きょう」","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"書きます","r":"かきます","m":"write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"“Kyou” is written as “今日”.","scene":"Comparing hiragana and kanji in a lesson."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-023||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nと読みます／書きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nと読みます／書きます","patternReading":"とよみます／かきます","coreMeaning":"N is read/written as ~ (quoted verbatim)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern is commonly used to **introduce new words**, especially kanji; **ask how to read** a difficult character; and **confirm the spelling** of a person’s or place’s name.","nuance":"In conversation, Japanese speakers often use **何と読みますか** to ask politely. For proper names, use **漢字で...と書きます** to explain the writing clearly. This pattern does not describe reading books or newspapers.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"読みます","r":"よみます","m":"read"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, how is “駅” read?","scene":"A traveler sees 駅 on a train-station map and asks."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"川田","r":"かわだ","m":"Kawada"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"書きます","r":"かきます","m":"write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My name is written in kanji as “Kawada”.","scene":"Introducing yourself at a new company."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-023||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish similar patterns","sub":"Nと読みます／書きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nと読みます／書きます","patternReading":"とよみます／かきます","coreMeaning":"N is read/written as ~ (quoted verbatim)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps prevent confusion between と読みます/書きます and related patterns such as **Nを読みます** and **〜というN**.","nuance":"Unlike を読みます, this pattern does not mean reading a book or newspaper; it quotes **a character’s reading**. Unlike というN, it emphasizes pronunciation or spelling, not what something is called.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"ほん","m":"hon"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"読みます","r":"よみます","m":"read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The kanji “本” is read as “hon”.","scene":"Distinguishing reading a book from a kanji’s reading."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Nを読みます","point":"「Nを読みます」= read something (a book/newspaper); 「Nは〜と読みます」= N is read as ~. 例: 本を読みます (read a book) ≠ 「本」は「ほん」と読みます (本 is read as hon)."},{"vs":"〜というN","point":"「〜というN」introduces what something is called; 「〜と読みます/書きます」only gives its reading/writing. 例: これは「さくら」という花です (a flower called sakura) ≠ 「桜」は「さくら」と読みます (桜 is read as sakura)."}]}},"n4-023||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nと読みます／書きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nと読みます／書きます","patternReading":"とよみます／かきます","coreMeaning":"N is read/written as ~ (quoted verbatim)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Three common mistakes: using the wrong particle **を** instead of **は**, forgetting **と**, or putting the verb in the wrong position. Remember that と must appear like quotation marks.","nuance":"Distinguish **を** (the object read, as in 本を読みます) from **と** (quoting a reading, as in 「やま」と読みます). Omitting と or changing the word order makes the sentence ungrammatical.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"山","r":"やま","m":"mountain"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"やま","m":"mountain"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"読みます","r":"よみます","m":"read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The kanji “山” is read as “yama”.","scene":"Correcting a learner’s mistake in class."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 「やま」を読みます。 → ✅ 「やま」と読みます。 (を means 'read yama' rather than 'read as yama')","❌ と読みます「やま」。 → ✅ 「やま」と読みます。","❌ 「やま」読みます。 → ✅ 「やま」と読みます。"],"compare":null}},"n4-023||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"Nと読みます／書きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nと読みます／書きます","patternReading":"とよみます／かきます","coreMeaning":"N is read/written as ~ (quoted verbatim)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine と as a pair of **quotation marks**: everything inside is quoted verbatim. Therefore, the reading or writing must come before と.","nuance":"This trick helps you remember where と goes and avoid confusing it with を.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-023||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nと読みます／書きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nと読みます／書きます","patternReading":"とよみます／かきます","coreMeaning":"N is read/written as ~ (quoted verbatim)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases found in textbooks and JLPT questions. The question form **何と読みますか** is especially useful for unfamiliar proper names or kanji.","nuance":"In conversation, Japanese speakers often ask 何と読みますか? about proper names and use 漢字で...と書きます to explain spelling. Both are basic polite forms suitable for teachers and coworkers.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"「かわいい」","m":"cute"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"可愛い","r":"かわいい","m":"cute"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"書きます","r":"かきます","m":"write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"“Kawaii” is written in kanji as “可愛い”.","scene":"A learner asks how to write かわいい in kanji."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-024||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜というN · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜というN","patternReading":"というえぬ","coreMeaning":"Introduce a name: “N called ~ / named ~”","slug":"core","definition":"〜というN introduces the name of a person, thing, place, or concept unfamiliar to the listener. It means “an N called ~” or “an N named ~.” This pattern is very common in everyday speech.","nuance":"This pattern is neutral and works with friends or older people when the sentence ends in です/ます. It simply explains a name; it does not express emotion or emphasize a reason. Once the listener knows N, という is unnecessary; just say そのN.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"キムさん","m":"Kim"},{"t":"という","m":"named"},{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"います","m":"have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have a friend named Kim.","scene":"Introducing a friend to a classmate."},{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"コンビニ","m":"convenience store"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"という","m":"called"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"store"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"たくさん","m":"many"},{"t":"あります","m":"there are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are many stores called konbini in Japan.","scene":"Telling a foreign friend about Japan."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-024||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"〜というN · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜というN","patternReading":"というえぬ","coreMeaning":"Introduce a name: “N called ~ / named ~”","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern has the form **[Name] + という + [general noun]**. The という part **never conjugates**; tense, negation, and politeness appear at the sentence ending, such as です, でした, ではありません, or あります.","nuance":"The noun after という is usually a general noun: 人, 店, 料理, 言葉, 名前, 本, 映画… The name before という may be a proper noun (キム, ユニクロ), a loanword (コンビニ), or a Japanese word (さくら).","structure":[{"lhs":"[Name / Proper noun, word]","rhs":"という + [Noun for a person/thing/concept]","note":"The name comes first; the general noun follows"},{"lhs":"〜というN","rhs":"＋ です／があります／を知っていますか","note":"Politeness appears at the sentence ending"},{"lhs":"〜というN","rhs":"＋ ではありません／でした","note":"Negation/past changes only the ending; という stays the same"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"「"},{"t":"さくら","m":"cherry blossom"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"という","m":"called"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flower"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a flower called sakura.","scene":"Pointing to a flower photo in a book."},{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"ありがとう","m":"thank you"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"という","m":"called"},{"t":"言葉","r":"ことば","m":"word"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The word “arigatou” is important.","scene":"Explaining the word for thanks to a foreign friend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-024||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜というN · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜というN","patternReading":"というえぬ","coreMeaning":"Introduce a name: “N called ~ / named ~”","slug":"usage","definition":"Use 〜というN when you want to give a name and then add a general noun so the listener can identify the type of thing. It appears often when introducing food, places, people, books, and films.","nuance":"In conversation, Japanese speakers often use 〜というN to open a story: “〜という店がある” or “〜という人を知っていますか”. It is not overly formal or casual. For more polite speech, add です/ます at the end.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"天ぷら","r":"てんぷら","m":"tempura"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"という","m":"called"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べた","r":"たべた","m":"have eaten"},{"t":"こと","m":"experience"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"have"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Have you ever eaten the dish called tempura?","scene":"Asking a classmate about Japanese food."},{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"スミス","m":"Smith"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"という","m":"named"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"know"},{"t":"います"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know someone named Smith?","scene":"Looking for someone at school."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-024||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"〜というN · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜というN","patternReading":"というえぬ","coreMeaning":"Introduce a name: “N called ~ / named ~”","slug":"compare","definition":"〜というN is easily confused with 〜と言います and Nと読みます. Remember: **というN modifies a noun**, **と言います** is the predicate at the end of a sentence, and **と読みます** describes how a character or kanji is read.","nuance":"Learners often confuse these because all three can be translated as “called.” However, というN always has a general noun after it (という人, という店), while と言います appears at the end as the predicate. Nと読みます is used only to describe the reading of a written character.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"ラーメン","m":"ramen"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"という","m":"called"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"人気","r":"にんき","m":"popular"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The dish called ramen is very popular.","scene":"Introducing Japanese food to a foreign friend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜と言います","point":"というN modifies the noun that follows (「さくら」という花). と言います appears at the end as the predicate (「さくら」と言います)."},{"vs":"Nと読みます","point":"というN gives the name of a person or thing (「さくら」という花です). と読みます gives the reading of a written character (「桜」と読みます)."}]}},"n4-024||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜というN · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜というN","patternReading":"というえぬ","coreMeaning":"Introduce a name: “N called ~ / named ~”","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: using **と** instead of **という**, **omitting という**, or **reversing** the name and general noun. Correct these so the sentence sounds natural in Japanese.","nuance":"The first mistake comes from English expressions such as “Kim’s friend” or “the Family Mart store,” which can lead learners to omit という in Japanese. Remember: when introducing a name, という must come between the name and the general noun.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"「"},{"t":"さくら","m":"cherry blossom"},{"t":"」"},{"t":"という","m":"called"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flower"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"きれい","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The flower called sakura is beautiful.","scene":"Praising cherry blossoms in a photo."}],"pitfalls":["❌ キムさんと友だちがいます。 → ✅ キムさんという友だちがいます。","❌ 「さくら」花です。 → ✅ 「さくら」という花です。","❌ 友だちというキムさん。 → ✅ キムさんという友だち。"],"compare":null}},"n4-024||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"〜というN · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜というN","patternReading":"というえぬ","coreMeaning":"Introduce a name: “N called ~ / named ~”","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine という as a **label** you stick onto something unfamiliar. You point to the object and say its name: “This is **something called** ~.” When introducing a name, remind yourself: **you need という**.","nuance":"This trick helps you remember という when introducing a proper name and remember the order: proper name + という + general noun.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-024||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜というN · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜というN","patternReading":"というえぬ","coreMeaning":"Introduce a name: “N called ~ / named ~”","slug":"phrases","definition":"Phrases with というN appear often in JLPT questions and conversation: **〜という人**, **〜という店**, **〜という料理**, **〜という言葉**, and **〜という名前**. Memorizing them as chunks helps you speak faster.","nuance":"In these phrases, という usually comes before the general noun. To ask “What does N mean?”, you can use: 〜という意味です.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"という","m":"named"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The person named Tanaka is a teacher.","scene":"Introducing a new teacher to the class."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-025||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V／Nのとおりに · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Nのとおりに","patternReading":"のとおりに（どおりに）","coreMeaning":"Follow an existing model or instruction exactly","slug":"core","definition":"〜とおりに describes doing something exactly according to an existing model, instruction, or expectation. With Vた, the model is something someone did; with Nの, it is a noun such as a map, plan, or statement. The key idea is an exact match: do it just as shown, without deviation.","nuance":"Used in speech and writing; neutral and somewhat formal. The speaker follows an existing standard rather than acting freely. At N4, it often appears in instructions and reports of results that match expectations. Compared with 〜ように (like / aiming for), とおりに focuses more on following a set model.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"言ったとおりに","r":"いったとおりに","m":"just as said"},{"t":"書いて","r":"かいて","m":"write"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please write exactly as the teacher said.","scene":"A teacher guides students writing characters"},{"tokens":[{"t":"地図","r":"ちず","m":"map"},{"t":"のとおりに","m":"as shown by"},{"t":"行ったら","r":"いったら","m":"when going"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"すぐ","m":"right away"},{"t":"着きました","r":"つきました","m":"arrived"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I followed the map and arrived right away.","scene":"Traveling in an unfamiliar city"},{"tokens":[{"t":"予定","r":"よてい","m":"schedule"},{"t":"どおりに","m":"as scheduled"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"終わりました","r":"おわりました","m":"ended"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The meeting ended as scheduled.","scene":"A manager confirms progress after a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-025||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"V／Nのとおりに · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Nのとおりに","patternReading":"のとおりに（どおりに）","coreMeaning":"Follow an existing model or instruction exactly","slug":"forms","definition":"There are two main forms: **V (dictionary / た-form) + とおりに** and **N + のとおりに**. Some familiar nouns can drop の and become **Nどおりに** (予定どおりに...). Use the dictionary form for general or upcoming actions, and the た-form when the model action has already happened.","nuance":"With nouns, **のとおりに** is most common. With 予定・計画・説明・命令, Japanese often uses **どおりに** (read どおり because of rendaku). Do not attach とおりに directly to the ます-form; use dictionary form Vる or Vた instead.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる / Vた","rhs":"とおりに + V","note":"Do exactly as someone did or said"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"のとおりに + V","note":"Follow N exactly (map, book, instruction)"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"どおりに + V","note":"Common with 予定・計画・説明・命令"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"言ったとおりに","r":"いったとおりに","m":"just as I said"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please do exactly as I said.","scene":"A team leader gives operating instructions"},{"tokens":[{"t":"説明書","r":"せつめいしょ","m":"instruction manual"},{"t":"のとおりに","m":"according to"},{"t":"組み立てました","r":"くみたてました","m":"assembled"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I assembled it exactly according to the instruction manual.","scene":"Assembling a bookcase bought at a store"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-025||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"V／Nのとおりに · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Nのとおりに","patternReading":"のとおりに（どおりに）","coreMeaning":"Follow an existing model or instruction exactly","slug":"usage","definition":"There are three main uses: (1) **Instructions** — tell someone to follow directions exactly; (2) **Reporting results** — something happens as planned or predicted; (3) **Comparison** — an action or result matches a prior wish or thought. The following clause usually expresses an intentional action or an achieved result.","nuance":"When saying 'according to what someone said,' the first part is often **Vた** (言ったとおりに, 思ったとおりに). For 'according to the plan,' **Nどおりに** is very natural: 予定どおりに・計画どおりに. This pattern does not express sequence like 〜たあとで.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"説明書","r":"せつめいしょ","m":"instruction manual"},{"t":"のとおりに","m":"according to"},{"t":"作れば","r":"つくれば","m":"if making"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you follow the instructions, it will be delicious.","scene":"Friends share how to cook a new dish"},{"tokens":[{"t":"思ったとおりに","r":"おもったとおりに","m":"just as I thought"},{"t":"できました","m":"was completed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Everything turned out just as I thought.","scene":"The result matches the initial prediction"},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"どおりに","m":"on time"},{"t":"来て","r":"きて","m":"come"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please arrive on time.","scene":"Reminding a coworker about a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-025||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with 〜たあとで・まま","sub":"V／Nのとおりに · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Nのとおりに","patternReading":"のとおりに（どおりに）","coreMeaning":"Follow an existing model or instruction exactly","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish **〜とおりに** from two easily confused patterns: **〜たあとで** (after doing something) and **〜まま** (leave something as it is). All three can follow Vた, but their meanings are quite different. とおりに emphasizes matching a model; あとで shows only time order; まま means keeping a condition unchanged.","nuance":"N4 learners often confuse 言ったとおりに (according to what was said) with 言ったあとで (after saying it). Follow the context: use とおりに for doing something according to the content, and あとで for simply saying that something happens after speaking. まま usually describes an unchanged state, while とおり involves actively following a model.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"言ったとおりに","r":"いったとおりに","m":"just as said"},{"t":"書きました","r":"かきました","m":"wrote"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wrote exactly as the teacher said.","scene":"Confirming that instructions were followed"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜たあとで","point":"とおりに: do something exactly according to the CONTENT of the earlier action. あとで: do it only AFTER that action ends. Example: 言ったとおりに書く (write as told) / 言ったあとで書く (write after speaking)."},{"vs":"〜まま","point":"とおりに: FOLLOW a model. まま: LEAVE a state unchanged. Example: 言ったとおりにする (follow the instructions) / つけたままにする (leave it switched on)."}]}},"n4-025||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"V／Nのとおりに · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Nのとおりに","patternReading":"のとおりに（どおりに）","coreMeaning":"Follow an existing model or instruction exactly","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Three common errors are using the wrong verb form, forgetting の after a noun, and confusing the meaning with 〜たあとで. Correct these immediately for natural Japanese.","nuance":"A common mistake is adding の after a verb, as in V-のとおりに, by analogy with Nのとおりに. Verbs connect directly to とおりに in dictionary or た-form. With nouns, の is normally required, except in set compounds such as どおり.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"説明書","r":"せつめいしょ","m":"instruction manual"},{"t":"のとおりに","m":"according to"},{"t":"やって","m":"do"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please follow the instruction manual exactly.","scene":"Reminding someone to follow instructions"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生が言ったのとおりに書いてください。 → ✅ 先生が言ったとおりに書いてください。","❌ 地図とおりに行きます。 → ✅ 地図のとおりに行きます。","❌ 言ったあとで書いてください (when you mean 'write as told') → ✅ 言ったとおりに書いてください。"],"compare":null}},"n4-025||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V／Nのとおりに · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Nのとおりに","patternReading":"のとおりに（どおりに）","coreMeaning":"Follow an existing model or instruction exactly","slug":"memory","definition":"Visual memory tip: **とおり** also means a road or avenue. Think of 'following the road that has already been mapped out'—that is とおり. When an existing model is present, you simply move along it. Since the model already exists, the preceding verb is often **Vた**.","nuance":"To distinguish it from まま: とおり means actively following the road, while まま means staying as you are. With あとで: とおり concerns matching content, while あとで concerns time passing.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-026||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vたあとで · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたあとで","patternReading":"たあとで","coreMeaning":"After V (action 2 follows completion of action 1)","slug":"core","definition":"Describes an action in the second clause that occurs **after** the action in the first clause is complete. The pattern sets a **time point** on the timeline without focusing on the cause or stating an inevitable result. In Minna lesson 34, it means “after doing something, do something else.”","nuance":"Neutral and usable in speech or writing, including with superiors when the second clause is polite. It carries little emotion and mainly **lists a sequence**. Compared with てから, it places less emphasis on the connection between the two actions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"finished"},{"t":"あとで","r":"あとで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"テレビ","r":"テレビ","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ます","r":"みます","m":"watch"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After finishing my homework, I watch TV.","scene":"Describing an evening habit after class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲んだ","r":"のんだ","m":"took"},{"t":"あとで","r":"あとで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出かけます","r":"でかけます","m":"go out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After taking my medicine, I go out.","scene":"A mother tells her child to take medicine before going out"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-026||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vたあとで · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたあとで","patternReading":"たあとで","coreMeaning":"After V (action 2 follows completion of action 1)","slug":"forms","definition":"The main structure is **Vた + あとで**. The verb must be in the た-form: 食べる → 食べた、する → した、来る → 来た. With a noun, use **Nのあとで** (食事のあとで). In speech, で can be dropped to form “あと” without changing the meaning. The second clause carries the main information.","nuance":"This is a basic polite-use pattern; using ます/です in the second clause makes the sentence polite. The first clause does not change to polite form; it always uses the plain た-form.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"あとで + Vế sau","note":"The first action is the time point; use the た-form"},{"lhs":"Nの","rhs":"あとで + Vế sau","note":"A noun marks an event / time point"},{"lhs":"Vた / Nの","rhs":"あと","note":"Drop で in casual speech"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昼ご飯","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べた","r":"たべた","m":"finished eating"},{"t":"あとで","r":"あとで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"散歩","r":"さんぽ","m":"walk"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After lunch, I take a walk.","scene":"Describing a lunchtime habit at work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"あとで","r":"あとで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"materials"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"送って","r":"おくって","m":"send"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After the meeting, please send the materials.","scene":"A manager tells an employee to send materials after a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-026||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vたあとで · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたあとで","patternReading":"たあとで","coreMeaning":"After V (action 2 follows completion of action 1)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when the speaker wants to **arrange action points in a day's schedule**: what happens first and what happens later. The second clause usually contains the main verb and new information. It commonly appears with **〜てください** (request), **〜ましょう** (suggestion), and **〜ます** (describing habits).","nuance":"Neutral and suitable for casual conversation or work. Do not use it when two actions happen in parallel. If the actions are tightly connected and immediate, てから is more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日曜日","r":"にちようび","m":"Sunday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"finished"},{"t":"あとで","r":"あとで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"うち","r":"うち","m":"home"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"ゆっくり","r":"ゆっくり","m":"leisurely"},{"t":"休みます","r":"やすみます","m":"relax"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On Sunday, after shopping, I relax at home.","scene":"Answering a friend about weekend plans"},{"tokens":[{"t":"おふろ","r":"おふろ","m":"bath"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入った","r":"はいった","m":"bathed"},{"t":"あとで","r":"あとで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"牛乳","r":"ぎゅうにゅう","m":"milk"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みます","r":"のみます","m":"drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After taking a bath, I drink milk.","scene":"Describing a bedtime habit"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-026||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with てから・たら","sub":"Vたあとで · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたあとで","patternReading":"たあとで","coreMeaning":"After V (action 2 follows completion of action 1)","slug":"compare","definition":"All three patterns can express “after.” **てから** emphasizes that the first action is a necessary condition or step for the next. **たあとで** only marks a time point and does not imply cause and effect. **たら** creates a hypothetical condition “if A, then B”; the second clause can be a suggestion or volitional statement.","nuance":"For obligatory routines such as “wash your face → eat breakfast,” Japanese speakers usually prefer てから. For schedule time points, あとで is more common. あとに is close in meaning to あとで, but に emphasizes “at the exact time afterward.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"終わった","r":"おわった","m":"finished"},{"t":"あとで","r":"あとで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone call"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After work, I will call.","scene":"Promising to call a friend after work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てから","point":"てから: the first action is a necessary step for the next. あとで: only states a time point. Example: 薬を飲んでから、食べます (take medicine, then eat—as instructed) vs 薬を飲んだあとで、食べます (eat after taking medicine—time only)."},{"vs":"〜たあとに","point":"あとに stresses ‘at the time afterward’ and can pair with で/に; あとで is most common in conversation. In schedules, they are nearly interchangeable."},{"vs":"〜たら","point":"たら means ‘if/after’ and the second clause can be a condition, command, or suggestion. あとで only expresses time order and has no conditional meaning."}]}},"n4-026||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vたあとで · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたあとで","patternReading":"たあとで","coreMeaning":"After V (action 2 follows completion of action 1)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often mistakenly use the **て-form** before あとで or use あとで for two simultaneous actions. Remember the fixed rule: **あとで always follows the た-form**. For parallel actions, use ながら or て.","nuance":"This pattern is not especially formal, but it is fine with superiors if the second clause is polite. Avoid it when the actions are immediate and obligatory; てから sounds more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"歯","r":"は","m":"teeth"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"磨いた","r":"みがいた","m":"brushed"},{"t":"あとで","r":"あとで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寝ます","r":"ねます","m":"sleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After brushing my teeth, I go to bed.","scene":"Telling a child what to do before bed"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べてあとで、出かけます。 → ✅ 食べたあとで、出かけます。 (The first clause must use the た-form, not the て-form)","❌ テレビを見たあとで、勉強しました。(I meant watching and studying at the same time) → ✅ テレビを見ながら、勉強しました。 (Use ながら for simultaneous actions)","❌ 朝起きたあとで、顔を洗います。 → ✅ 朝起きてから、顔を洗います。 (Use てから for a natural/required sequence)"],"compare":null}},"n4-026||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vたあとで · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたあとで","patternReading":"たあとで","coreMeaning":"After V (action 2 follows completion of action 1)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **あとで as a point on a timeline**. The first action ends in the た-form as the “finish line”; cross that line before starting the next action. あと means “behind/after” and で means “at,” so あとで means “at the point after the action is done.”","nuance":"The image of “crossing the た line” helps you avoid confusing it with the て-form. Whenever you see あとで, ask: “Is the first action already DONE?” If not, do not use it.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-026||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vたあとで · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたあとで","patternReading":"たあとで","coreMeaning":"After V (action 2 follows completion of action 1)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions in exams and daily life include **食事のあとで**, **宿題をしたあとで**, **仕事が終わったあとで**, and **お風呂に入ったあとで**. When the context is clear, Japanese speakers shorten this to **あとで**, meaning “later.”","nuance":"The phrase “Nのあとで” is shorter than “Vたあとで” and very common in daily conversation. When あとで appears at the end of a sentence, it sounds casual and gentle.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食事","r":"しょくじ","m":"meal"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"あとで","r":"あとで","m":"after"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"散歩","r":"さんぽ","m":"walk"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After the meal, I take a walk.","scene":"Describing a habit after eating"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-027||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V／Aいまま · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aいまま","patternReading":"まま","coreMeaning":"Leave a state unchanged; keep it as is","slug":"core","definition":"〜まま describes a **state that remains unchanged** when moving to the next action or situation. Use Vたまま to say: **after doing V, the result is left as it is**. Use Vないまま to say: **without doing V, something continues anyway**. This is a key N4 pattern, often used with verbs such as 寝る, 出かける, and 入る.","nuance":"This pattern does not emphasize the action; it emphasizes a **state left unattended**. To say “after doing something, then do something else,” use 〜あとで. Use 〜まま when the state **continues unchanged**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"electric light"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つけた","r":"つけた","m":"turned on"},{"t":"まま","r":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寝て","r":"ねて","m":"sleep"},{"t":"しまいました","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I fell asleep with the light still on.","scene":"The next morning, the light is still on."},{"tokens":[{"t":"靴","r":"くつ","m":"shoes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"履いた","r":"はいた","m":"put on"},{"t":"まま","r":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"house"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入らないで","r":"はいらないで","m":"do not enter"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please do not enter the house with your shoes on.","scene":"The host reminds a guest to remove their shoes."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-027||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"V／Aいまま · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aいまま","patternReading":"まま","coreMeaning":"Leave a state unchanged; keep it as is","slug":"forms","definition":"Full structure: **Vた + まま** (after V), **Vない + まま** (without V), **Aい + まま** (keep the quality), **Aな + まま**, and **Nの + まま**. With Vたまま, use the **た-form**, not the dictionary form. With Vないまま, use the **ない-form**, not なかった.","nuance":"In polite speech, add です or use a polite form in the following clause: 〜たままです / 〜たまま〜てください. To express **deliberately keeping something unchanged**, use **〜ままにする**.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"まま","note":"after V, keep the result unchanged"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"まま","note":"without V, keep the missing state"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"まま","note":"keep the quality unchanged (熱いまま…)"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"まま","note":"with na-adjectives: 静かなまま"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"のまま","note":"keep the noun unchanged (元のまま…)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けた","r":"あけた","m":"opened"},{"t":"まま","r":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寝ました","r":"ねました","m":"slept"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went to sleep with the window open.","scene":"It was a hot summer night, so I opened the window and went to sleep."},{"tokens":[{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"言わない","r":"いわない","m":"not say"},{"t":"まま","r":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"帰りました","r":"かえりました","m":"went home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went home without saying anything.","scene":"Upset with my friend, I left in silence."},{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"熱い","r":"あつい","m":"hot"},{"t":"まま","r":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"飲みます","r":"のみます","m":"drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I drink coffee while it is still hot.","scene":"Asked whether I drink it hot or iced."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-027||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"V／Aいまま · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aいまま","patternReading":"まま","coreMeaning":"Leave a state unchanged; keep it as is","slug":"usage","definition":"Main uses of まま: ① **Leave it**: do one thing without changing another state. ② **Missing condition**: use ないまま when V should have been done but was not. ③ **Keep the current state**: use Aい／Nのまま when keeping the state unchanged at the time of speaking.","nuance":"It often appears in requests and prohibitions: **Vたまま + 〜てください / 〜ないでください**. In everyday speech, this pattern often describes rushed or inconvenient actions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"エアコン","m":"air conditioner"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つけた","r":"つけた","m":"turned on"},{"t":"まま","r":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出かけました","r":"でかけました","m":"went out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went out with the air conditioner still on.","scene":"I rushed to work and forgot to turn it off."},{"tokens":[{"t":"パジャマ","m":"pajamas"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"まま","r":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"コンビニ","m":"convenience store"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went to the convenience store in my pajamas.","scene":"Late at night, I was too lazy to change before getting snacks."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-027||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing similar patterns","sub":"V／Aいまま · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aいまま","patternReading":"まま","coreMeaning":"Leave a state unchanged; keep it as is","slug":"compare","definition":"The three most easily confused patterns are **Vたあとで**, **Vています (resulting state)**, and **Vてしまいました**. Remember: まま = **leave the state unchanged**.","nuance":"English often translates all of these with “then,” but Japanese distinguishes them clearly: did the state change, and is there an emotional nuance?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"テレビ","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つけた","r":"つけた","m":"turned on"},{"t":"まま","r":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出かけました","r":"でかけました","m":"went out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went out with the TV still on.","scene":"I rushed to work and forgot to turn off the TV."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vたあとで","point":"あとで shows only order: do V → do something else. It does not say whether the state is maintained. まま explicitly says the state is kept unchanged before the next action."},{"vs":"Vています","point":"ています emphasizes an existing result: 窓が開いています = the window is open. まま emphasizes “leave it as is”: 窓を開けたまま = leave the window open (do not close it)."},{"vs":"Vてしまいました","point":"てしまう emphasizes a completed action, often with regret or relief. まま emphasizes a state that remains unchanged and may have no emotional nuance."}]}},"n4-027||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V／Aいまま · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aいまま","patternReading":"まま","coreMeaning":"Leave a state unchanged; keep it as is","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes: using **ながら** when the intended meaning is “leave a state unchanged”; using **Vなかったまま** instead of **Vないまま**; using **Vるまま** instead of **Vたまま**; or omitting **〜ままにする** when expressing deliberate maintenance of a state.","nuance":"When correcting a sentence, ask: is the state being **kept unchanged**? If so, use まま. If two actions happen simultaneously, use ながら.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けた","r":"あけた","m":"opened"},{"t":"まま","r":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"しました","m":"made it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I deliberately left the window open.","scene":"Answering: Who left the window open?"}],"pitfalls":["❌ テレビをつけながら寝てしまいました。 → ✅ テレビをつけたまま寝てしまいました。 (To mean “leave the TV on and go to sleep”)","❌ 朝ごはんを食べなかったまま、学校へ行きました。 → ✅ 朝ごはんを食べないまま、学校へ行きました。","❌ 窓を開けるまま寝ました。 → ✅ 窓を開けたまま寝ました。","❌ 会議中、スマホをつけたまましました。 → ✅ 会議中、スマホをつけたままにしました。"],"compare":null}},"n4-027||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V／Aいまま · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aいまま","patternReading":"まま","coreMeaning":"Leave a state unchanged; keep it as is","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **まま** sounds like “ma-ma”—just **leave it as is and move on**. Formula: DONE / NOT DONE → LEAVE IT UNCHANGED → DO SOMETHING ELSE. Image: it is raining, you put on a raincoat and **do not take it off**, wearing it **as is** into the house.","nuance":"This has no grammatical nuance; it is only a memory aid.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-027||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V／Aいまま · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aいまま","patternReading":"まま","coreMeaning":"Leave a state unchanged; keep it as is","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common まま phrases: **つけたまま** (leave it on), **履いたまま** (keep wearing shoes), **開けたまま** (leave it open), **黙ったまま** (remain silent), **食べないまま** (without eating), and **言わないまま** (without saying).","nuance":"In tests, the following clause is often 出かける, 寝る, 入る, 帰る, or 働く. The form **〜たままにする** means deliberately leaving something unchanged and often appears in conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"食べない","r":"たべない","m":"not eat"},{"t":"まま","r":"まま","m":"left as is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"夜","r":"よる","m":"evening"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"働きました","r":"はたらきました","m":"worked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I worked until evening without eating anything.","scene":"I was so busy that I even forgot lunch."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-028||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V／Aば · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aば","patternReading":"ば","coreMeaning":"If A (condition), then B (result)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ば is a common hypothetical conditional form at N4 level. It means “If A, then B,” where A is the condition and B is the result. Unlike “when A, then B,” ば emphasizes that setting condition A naturally produces B. Learners commonly meet it as Vば and Aいば.","nuance":"Clause A usually uses the plain form. Clause B can express an evaluation, intention, ability, or invitation, as in textbooks. Compared with たら, ば feels like “the condition is sufficient” and is slightly more formal and objective. Japanese speakers still use ば in daily casual conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"安ければ","r":"やすければ","m":"if cheap"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it is cheap, I will buy it.","scene":"Comparing prices in a store and deciding to buy if the item is cheap."},{"tokens":[{"t":"天気","r":"てんき","m":"weather"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"良ければ","r":"よければ","m":"if good"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出かけます","r":"でかけます","m":"go out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If the weather is good, I will go out.","scene":"Wondering whether to go for a walk this weekend."},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"読みます","r":"よみます","m":"read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I have time, I will read.","scene":"Answering a friend who asks whether you will read this weekend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-028||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"V／Aば · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aば","patternReading":"ば","coreMeaning":"If A (condition), then B (result)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes how to form the ば conditional. Group III verbs (する/来る) are irregular; Group I shifts the final sound to the え-row; Group II drops る and adds れば. For い-adjectives, drop い and add ければ. Nouns and な-adjectives do not take ば directly; use なら (or であれば).","nuance":"There is no separate polite form of ば; to be polite, simply use ます/です in clause B. Practice conjugation until it feels automatic before building full sentences.","structure":[{"lhs":"V group I","rhs":"う ending → え + ば","note":"書く→書けば, 飲む→飲めば"},{"lhs":"V group II","rhs":"drop る + れば","note":"食べる→食べれば, 見る→見れば"},{"lhs":"V group III","rhs":"する→すれば / 来る→来れば","note":"くる→くれば"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"drop い + ければ","note":"安い→安ければ, 高い→高ければ"},{"lhs":"N / Aな","rhs":"use なら (or であれば)","note":"静かなら, 学生なら"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"来れば","r":"くれば","m":"if you come"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"間に合います","r":"まにあいます","m":"be on time"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you come early, you will be on time.","scene":"Reminding a friend that the train leaves soon."},{"tokens":[{"t":"高ければ","r":"たかければ","m":"if expensive"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買いません","r":"かいません","m":"will not buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it is expensive, I will not buy it.","scene":"Shopping, you leave an item because it is too expensive."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: 辞書形 → ば形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 — う row → え row + ば","Change the final sound from the う row to the え row, then add ば","飲む（のむ） → `飲めば（のめば）` (drink) ・ 書く（かく） → `書けば（かけば）` (write) ・ 話す（はなす） → `話せば（はなせば）` (speak) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰れば（かえれば）` (return)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — 一段 — drop る + れば","Drop る, then add れば","食べる（たべる） → `食べれば（たべれば）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見れば（みれば）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きれば（おきれば）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝れば（ねれば）` (sleep)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — する → すれば","する → すれば","する → `すれば` (do) ・ 勉強する（べんきょうする） → `勉強すれば（べんきょうすれば）` (study)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — 来る → 来れば (くれば)","来る → 来れば (くれば)","来る（くる） → `来れば（くれば）` (come)"]]}}},"n4-028||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"V／Aば · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aば","patternReading":"ば","coreMeaning":"If A (condition), then B (result)","slug":"usage","definition":"Common uses are: (1) stating a hypothetical condition before a result; (2) describing a rule or habit when a condition is met; and (3) giving advice with 〜ばいい. If the following clause is a command or request, prefer たら over ば.","nuance":"In the JLPT, common test patterns are Vば + an evaluation (Vます・Aです・Nです) and Vばいい. Do not use ば to mean “after A is finished, B”; for a time sequence, たら is more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"天気","r":"てんき","m":"weather"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"良ければ","r":"よければ","m":"if good"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ピクニック","m":"picnic"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きましょう","r":"いきましょう","m":"let's go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If the weather is good, let’s go on a picnic.","scene":"Inviting a friend out this weekend if the weather is good."},{"tokens":[{"t":"もっと","r":"もっと","m":"more"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"すれば","r":"すれば","m":"if you do"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"skilled"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なります","r":"なります","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you study harder, your Japanese will improve.","scene":"An N4 teacher encouraging a student."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲めば","r":"のめば","m":"if you take"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"熱","r":"ねつ","m":"fever"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"下がります","r":"さがります","m":"go down"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you take this medicine, your fever will go down.","scene":"A doctor giving instructions at a clinic."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-028||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with たら・なら","sub":"V／Aば · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aば","patternReading":"ば","coreMeaning":"If A (condition), then B (result)","slug":"compare","definition":"Comparing ば with たら, なら, and と helps you choose the right conditional. Key points: use たら for commands or requests; for evaluations, intentions, or abilities, both ば and たら work, but ば emphasizes a logical condition; use と for obvious results or natural laws. To respond to a topic, whether a noun or previously mentioned sentence, use なら.","nuance":"Quick rule: command/request in B → たら. Evaluation/intention in B → ば/たら both work. Obvious result/natural law in B → と. Noun in A or responding to a topic → なら.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I have money, I will go to Japan.","scene":"Discussing plans to study abroad and the need for money."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"たら","point":"たら: A finishes, then B; suitable for requests (時間があったら、来てください). ば: A is a logical condition leading to B (時間があれば、手伝います)."},{"vs":"なら","point":"なら: responds to a topic already being discussed (Nなら). ば: states a direct condition (安ければ、買います)."},{"vs":"と","point":"と: an obvious or automatic result; not used with volition (春になると、花が咲く). ば: a hypothetical condition that may or may not occur (春になれば、花見に行きたい)."}]}},"n4-028||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner mistakes","sub":"V／Aば · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aば","patternReading":"ば","coreMeaning":"If A (condition), then B (result)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with ば include using the wrong form after nouns, conjugating the ば form incorrectly, and using ば in requests.","nuance":"Learners often translate “if” as ば in every situation, which causes grammar errors. Remember: “if + noun” → なら; “if A happens, then request B” → たら; “if condition A leads to result B” → ば.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あれば","r":"あれば","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"手伝います","r":"てつだいます","m":"help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I have time, I will help.","scene":"Replying to a colleague who asked for help."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 学生ば、割引があります。 → ✅ 学生なら、割引があります。","❌ 早く来るば、間に合います。 → ✅ 早く来れば、間に合います。","❌ 日本に着けば、電話してください。 → ✅ 日本に着いたら、電話してください。"],"compare":null}},"n4-028||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V／Aば · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aば","patternReading":"ば","coreMeaning":"If A (condition), then B (result)","slug":"memory","definition":"To form ば: Group I shifts the final sound to the e-row and adds ば (書く→書けば); Group II drops る and adds れば (食べる→食べれば); Aい drops い and adds ければ (安い→安ければ). Nouns and な-adjectives use なら, not ば. Picture ば as a fork in a tree diagram: the condition determines the direction of the action.","nuance":"Repeat three times: “Group I shifts to e; Group II drops る and adds れば; Aい drops い and adds ければ.” With nouns, do not attach ば; use なら.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-028||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V／Aば · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aば","patternReading":"ば","coreMeaning":"If A (condition), then B (result)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Fixed phrases with ば appear frequently in tests and everyday conversation.","nuance":"〜ばいい and どうすればいい are “lifeline” phrases for asking for advice. Memorize the full expressions so you can use them immediately.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どう","r":"どう","m":"how"},{"t":"すれば","r":"すれば","m":"if you do"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What should I do?","scene":"A student asks a teacher how to study new vocabulary."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-029||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nなら／V普通形なら · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなら／V普通形なら","patternReading":"なら","coreMeaning":"If it's ~ (topic-based response or suggestion)","slug":"core","definition":"「Nなら／V普通形なら」expresses **advice, suggestions, or proposals** based on **information or a topic** just mentioned. It is a **direct response** to what someone said, not a general condition. The speaker **takes in the information** and then gives an opinion.","nuance":"なら has a **casual, familiar** tone and is common in daily conversation. Compared with ば, なら **emphasizes the topic** more than the condition. The following sentence is often **advice, a wish, or a soft command**. It can end with the polite form, but なら itself does not change.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"なら","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"パーティー","m":"party"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開き","r":"ひらき","m":"hold"},{"t":"ましょう"}],"vi":"If she is coming, let's have a party.","scene":"You hear she may come; you suggest a party"},{"tokens":[{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"なら","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買い","r":"かい","m":"buy"},{"t":"ます"}],"vi":"If it's cheap, I'll buy it.","scene":"Someone says this shop is cheap"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-029||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and usage","sub":"Nなら／V普通形なら · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなら／V普通形なら","patternReading":"なら","coreMeaning":"If it's ~ (topic-based response or suggestion)","slug":"forms","definition":"なら connects to **nouns**, **plain-form verbs**, **i-adjectives**, and **na-adjectives**. With na-adjectives, drop な before adding なら. With verbs, use **Vる**, **Vた**, or **Vない**. Do not attach it directly to the ます-form.","nuance":"In speech, なら can sometimes follow **N alone**, without another particle. With verbs, **Vている + なら** can emphasize an ongoing state. なら does not change with time; it stays the same for past and present contexts.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + なら","rhs":"…","note":"Emphasizes N as the topic"},{"lhs":"Vる + なら","rhs":"…","note":"If (planning to) do V"},{"lhs":"Vた + なら","rhs":"…","note":"If V has been done"},{"lhs":"Vない + なら","rhs":"…","note":"If V is not done"},{"lhs":"Aい + なら","rhs":"…","note":"If the i-adjective applies"},{"lhs":"Na + なら","rhs":"…","note":"Drop な from the na-adjective"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"なら","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"飛行機","r":"ひこうき","m":"plane"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"}],"vi":"If you're going to Japan, flying is best.","scene":"Asking how to travel to Japan"},{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"なら","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"割引","r":"わりびき","m":"discount"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あり","m":"exist"},{"t":"ます"}],"vi":"If you're a student, there is a discount.","scene":"At a museum or cinema"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-029||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Nなら／V普通形なら · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなら／V普通形なら","patternReading":"なら","coreMeaning":"If it's ~ (topic-based response or suggestion)","slug":"usage","definition":"なら often appears when the listener has **just received new information** from the other person, such as 'I want to go to Japan' or 'I'm hungry.' The speaker uses なら to **pick up that topic** and offer a **suggestion, piece of advice, or proposal**. It can also introduce a topic to ask for an opinion.","nuance":"In formal writing, なら is less common and ば or 場合 may be used instead. In conversation, なら sounds very natural and often pairs with **「〜がいいですよ」**, **「〜ましょう」**, or **「〜てください」** to soften advice.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"なら","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"教科書","r":"きょうかしょ","m":"textbook"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"よ"}],"vi":"If you want to study Japanese, this textbook is good.","scene":"You recommend a book after hearing they want to study Japanese"},{"tokens":[{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"たい","m":"want to"},{"t":"なら","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"一緒","r":"いっしょ","m":"together"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行き","r":"いき","m":"go"},{"t":"ましょう"}],"vi":"If you want to eat, let's go together.","scene":"They crave ramen, so you invite them to eat"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-029||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing なら, ば, and たら","sub":"Nなら／V普通形なら · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなら／V普通形なら","patternReading":"なら","coreMeaning":"If it's ~ (topic-based response or suggestion)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish なら from the two common conditional patterns ば and たら. The key difference is: **なら = response to a topic**, **ば = general condition**, **たら = after an action is completed**.","nuance":"ば is common for objective 'if...then...' conditions. たら emphasizes 'after doing something.' なら is special because the speaker **has just heard information** or **assumes a topic** before giving an opinion, so it is closely tied to conversational context.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"京都","r":"きょうと","m":"Kyoto"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"なら","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"清水寺","r":"きよみずでら","m":"Kiyomizu Temple"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"よ"}],"vi":"If you're going to Kyoto, Kiyomizu Temple is a good choice.","scene":"You say you plan to visit Kyoto; I suggest a sight"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ば","point":"ば expresses a general condition: 春になれば、花が咲きます。 (If spring comes, flowers bloom). なら responds to a topic: 京都へ行くなら、新幹線がいいですよ。 (If you're going to Kyoto, the bullet train is best)."},{"vs":"〜たら","point":"たら emphasizes completion: 家に帰ったら、電話してください。 (After you get home, call me). なら emphasizes the topic: 家に帰るなら、一緒に帰りましょう。 (If you're going home, let's go together)."}]}},"n4-029||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nなら／V普通形なら · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなら／V普通形なら","patternReading":"なら","coreMeaning":"If it's ~ (topic-based response or suggestion)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often **use なら for every type of condition** or **forget that なら needs a response context**. This makes the sentence sound unnatural.","nuance":"なら is not used for natural laws or universal truths. Use ば for ordinary conditions. Use たら when you mean 'after doing something.'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"なら","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"行き","r":"いき","m":"go"},{"t":"ましょう"}],"vi":"If you're going, let's go early.","scene":"They say they plan to go; you suggest going early"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 薬を飲むなら、元気になります。 → ✅ 薬を飲んだら、元気になります。 (Use たら, not なら, when V1 must happen first for the result.)","❌ 昨日は時間があるなら、映画を見ました。 → ✅ 昨日は時間があったので、映画を見ました。 (A past event that happened is not expressed with なら.)","❌ 春になるなら、花が咲きます。 → ✅ 春になれば、花が咲きます。 (Use ば for a natural law, not なら.)"],"compare":null}},"n4-029||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"Nなら／V普通形なら · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなら／V普通形なら","patternReading":"なら","coreMeaning":"If it's ~ (topic-based response or suggestion)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine なら as **a reflecting mirror**: someone presents topic X, you **catch it** and offer your suggestion. You do not need to reason about a general condition; just follow the information you heard.","nuance":"Memory cue: **'Hear it, respond to it—なら turns information into advice.'** If there is no new information from the other person, consider using ば or たら.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-029||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nなら／V普通形なら · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nなら／V普通形なら","patternReading":"なら","coreMeaning":"If it's ~ (topic-based response or suggestion)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common patterns with なら make sentences natural: **「〜なら、〜がいいですよ」** (if ~, ~ is best), **「〜なら、〜ましょう」** (if ~, let's do ~), and **「〜なら、〜てください」** (if ~, please do ~).","nuance":"These patterns are common in JLPT questions and daily conversation. They often appear as **A-B dialogues**, where B uses なら to respond to A.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"なら","m":"if so"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"手伝っ","r":"てつだっ","m":"help"},{"t":"て"},{"t":"ください"}],"vi":"If you have time, please help me.","scene":"Asking for help when they are free"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-030||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい","patternReading":"たらいい","coreMeaning":"What/where/how should I ~? — asking for advice","slug":"core","definition":"〜たらいい is a structure for **asking for advice** by asking, 'Would it be good if I did ~?'. After a question word (何/どこ/どう/いつ…), it means 'what / where / how / when should I ~?'. The speaker cannot decide alone and wants the listener to suggest an option.","nuance":"This is a question, **not direct advice**. It works in everyday speech with friends, family, and coworkers. For greater politeness, use でしょうか instead of ですか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"したら","r":"したら","m":"if I do"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What should I do to study Japanese?","scene":"Asking a teacher how to study Japanese independently"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"置いたら","r":"おいたら","m":"if I put"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Where should I put this luggage?","scene":"A new employee asks the boss where to put documents"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-030||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい","patternReading":"たらいい","coreMeaning":"What/where/how should I ~? — asking for advice","slug":"forms","definition":"General structure: **疑問詞 + Vたら + いい(です/か)**. Vたら = the た-form + ら. Examples: 行ったら, 食べたら, したら. With adjectives and nouns, use Aかったら and Nだったら, but the advice-seeking pattern most commonly uses verbs.","nuance":"Ending with ですか is standard; でしょうか sounds softer. With 〜には, the meaning is 'to achieve a goal, what should I do?'.","structure":[{"lhs":"何を","rhs":"Vたらいいですか","note":"what should I do"},{"lhs":"どこに／どこへ","rhs":"Vたらいいですか","note":"where should I stay / go"},{"lhs":"どう","rhs":"Vたらいいですか","note":"how should I do it"},{"lhs":"いつ","rhs":"Vたらいいですか","note":"when should I do it"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"したら","r":"したら","m":"if I do"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What should I do this weekend?","scene":"Asking a close friend to plan the weekend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どう","m":"how"},{"t":"読んだら","r":"よんだら","m":"if I read"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How should I read this kanji?","scene":"Asking a teacher about an unfamiliar kanji"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-030||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい","patternReading":"たらいい","coreMeaning":"What/where/how should I ~? — asking for advice","slug":"usage","definition":"This is a flexible question pattern: どうしたらいい？ when stuck, 何を〜たらいい？ when choosing an option, どこで／どこへ〜たらいい？ when asking about place, and いつ〜たらいい？ when asking about time. You can add 〜には to state a goal.","nuance":"This pattern focuses on **a suggested action for the speaker**; do not use it for plans or ability. Adding でしょうか makes the question more humble.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"止まりました","r":"とまりました","m":"stopped"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"どう","m":"how"},{"t":"したら","r":"したら","m":"if I do"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The train has stopped. What should I do?","scene":"Asking station staff after a train problem"},{"tokens":[{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作ったら","r":"つくったら","m":"if I make"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What should I cook?","scene":"Asking Mom what to make for dinner"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"言ったら","r":"いったら","m":"if I say"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What should I say to my teacher?","scene":"About to apologize for forgetting homework"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-030||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast: たほうがいい・たらどう","sub":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい","patternReading":"たらいい","coreMeaning":"What/where/how should I ~? — asking for advice","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps distinguish 疑問詞＋たらいい from two easily confused patterns: 〜たほうがいい (direct advice) and 〜たらどうですか (suggesting that someone try something). This is the central pattern of the lesson. When a question word such as なに・どこ・どう appears, use たらいい; for direct advice, use たほうがいい. To suggest that the listener perform a specific action, use たらどうですか.","nuance":"たらいい is a **question asking for advice**; たほうがいい is **the answer or advice**; たらどうですか is **a suggestion to try something**, often gentle. All three appear in speech, with greater formality when you add ですか/どうですか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どう","m":"how"},{"t":"行ったら","r":"いったら","m":"if I go"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How should I get there?","scene":"Asking for directions when the route is blocked"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vたほうがいい","point":"Vたほうがいい is **advice** ('you should ~'), while 疑問詞＋Vたらいい is **a question asking for advice** ('what should I do?')."},{"vs":"Vたらどうですか","point":"Vたらどうですか **suggests that the listener try something** ('why not try ~?'), while Vたらいい asks **what the speaker should do**."}]}},"n4-030||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい","patternReading":"たらいい","coreMeaning":"What/where/how should I ~? — asking for advice","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This branch focuses on **three common mistakes learners make** with 疑問詞＋たらいい. First, forming the verb's た-form incorrectly, such as saying 行くたら instead of 行ったら. Second, **forgetting the question word**, so the sentence no longer asks 'what should I do?'. Third, using たらいい as direct advice (equivalent to たほうがいい)—a common meaning error in translation exercises.","nuance":"These mistakes often appear in fill-in-the-blank or choose-the-correct-sentence exercises. Always ask: does the sentence contain a question word? Is the verb before ら in the た-form? Is the goal to ask for advice or advise someone?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"行ったら","r":"いったら","m":"if I go"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When should I go?","scene":"Asking about the time"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 何をするたらいいですか。 → ✅ 何をしたらいいですか。 (する＋たら = したら, not するたら)","❌ どこへ行くたらいいですか。 → ✅ どこへ行ったらいいですか。 (行く → 行った → 行ったら)","❌ したらいいですか。 (missing a question word) → ✅ 何をしたらいいですか。 (must include a question word such as 何/どう/どこ)"],"compare":null}},"n4-030||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい","patternReading":"たらいい","coreMeaning":"What/where/how should I ~? — asking for advice","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: たら = 'if', いい = 'good' → 'it is good if I do ~'. Add a question word at the beginning → 'what is good to do?' = 'what should I do?'. Remember the mnemonic **どうしたらいい？**.","nuance":"When stuck, picture someone standing at a crossroads asking どうしたらいい？ — that is exactly this pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-030||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋V／Aたらいい","patternReading":"たらいい","coreMeaning":"What/where/how should I ~? — asking for advice","slug":"phrases","definition":"Useful phrases in daily life and on exams: どうしたらいい, 何を食べたらいい, どこへ行ったらいい, and いつ行ったらいい.","nuance":"You can use these phrases immediately to ask for opinions in small everyday situations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べたら","r":"たべたら","m":"if I eat"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"What should I eat?","scene":"Asking a friend when unsure what to order"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-031||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vる／Vないように · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vないように","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ / so as not to ~ (aiming for a desired state)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ように follows a verb in plain form (Vる/Vない) to state a **purpose aimed at a certain state**, rather than a direct action. Examples include “so as not to be late” and “so that one can read.” The speaker wants to **make that state become possible** through effort in the following clause.","nuance":"This neutral expression works in both speech and writing. It is less direct than 〜ために when the purpose is a direct action; it emphasizes a **target state** and often accompanies practice, preparation, or careful behavior.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忘れない","r":"わすれない","m":"not forget"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"so that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"メモ","m":"note"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So I don't forget, I take notes.","scene":"Taking notes after the boss gives many instructions"},{"tokens":[{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"書ける","r":"かける","m":"can write"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"so that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"して","m":"doing"},{"t":"います","m":"am"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So that I can write kanji, I practice every day.","scene":"Talking with a friend about studying kanji"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-031||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & connections","sub":"Vる／Vないように · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vないように","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ / so as not to ~ (aiming for a desired state)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the forms that connect to 〜ように. General rule: use a **verb in plain form (辞書形) or negative ない form**, then add ように. To say “so that one can ~,” use the potential verb form (V可能形) ending in る.","nuance":"All forms are usable; in N4 textbooks, **V可能形 + ように** and **Vない + ように** appear most often. Do not use Vます with ように; change it to the dictionary form or ない form.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"ように、…","note":"Purpose aimed at a state (Vる may be V可能形)"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"ように、…","note":"Purpose of avoiding an unwanted result"},{"lhs":"V可能形","rhs":"ように、…","note":"So that one can do something — common in N4 questions"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"間に合う","r":"まにあう","m":"be on time"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"so that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出ました","r":"でました","m":"left"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I left home early so that I would be on time.","scene":"An important appointment that morning"},{"tokens":[{"t":"風邪","r":"かぜ","m":"a cold"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"引かない","r":"ひかない","m":"not catch"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"so that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"手","r":"て","m":"hands"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"洗っています","r":"あらっています","m":"wash"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So I don't catch a cold, I am washing my hands.","scene":"Explaining frequent handwashing during flu season"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-031||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vる／Vないように · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vないように","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ / so as not to ~ (aiming for a desired state)","slug":"usage","definition":"In everyday conversation, 〜ように is used when talking about **one's efforts** to reach a state: trying not to forget or trying to become able to speak Japanese. The following clause is usually an intentional action or habit.","nuance":"This is a soft, natural way to explain one's habits or plans. Avoid using a direct command as the following clause; for a gentle request, use ようにしてください, introduced later.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寝坊","r":"ねぼう","m":"oversleeping"},{"t":"しない","r":"しない","m":"not do"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"so that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"アラーム","m":"alarm"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"セット","m":"set"},{"t":"しました","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So I wouldn't oversleep, I set an alarm.","scene":"Setting an alarm before an important workday"},{"tokens":[{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"覚えられる","r":"おぼえられる","m":"can remember"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"so that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"カード","m":"cards"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作りました","r":"つくりました","m":"made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So that I could remember kanji, I made study cards.","scene":"Making vocabulary study cards"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-031||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish ために・ようになる","sub":"Vる／Vないように · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vないように","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ / so as not to ~ (aiming for a desired state)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch clears up patterns that are easily confused. Learners often replace ように with ために because both can mean “so that.” The key difference is the **nature of the first clause**: use ように for a state, ability, or avoidance; use ために for an intentional action.","nuance":"The two patterns do not differ in formality; they differ in the type of verb before them. With V可能 or Vない, ように is almost always the correct choice, not ために.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べられる","r":"たべられる","m":"can eat"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"so that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"小さく","r":"ちいさく","m":"small"},{"t":"切っています","r":"きっています","m":"cut"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So that my child can eat vegetables, I cut them into small pieces.","scene":"A mother cooking for her young child"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるために","point":"ために is used when the first clause is an intentional action; ように is used for a state, ability, or prevention. Ex: 日本語を勉強するために本を買う ≠ 日本語が話せるように本を読む."},{"vs":"Vるようにします","point":"ようにします = resolve to build a habit; ように = purpose. Ex: 早く起きるようにします ≠ 早く起きられるように、早く寝ます."},{"vs":"Vるようになります","point":"ようになります = change into a new state as a result; ように = effort toward a goal. Ex: 泳げるようになりました ≠ 泳げるように毎日練習します."}]}},"n4-031||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vる／Vないように · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vないように","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ / so as not to ~ (aiming for a desired state)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This branch is especially important because learners often translate both ように and ために as “so that,” which causes confusion. Three common errors are shown here: using V可能 with ために, using Vない with ために, and using ように with an intentional action verb. The simple rule is: use ように when the first clause expresses a state, ability, or avoidance; use ために for a specific intentional action.","nuance":"The main problem is translating “so that” mechanically without considering the type of verb before it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忘れ物","r":"わすれもの","m":"forgotten item"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しない","r":"しない","m":"not do"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"so that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"カバン","m":"bag"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"チェックします","m":"check"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So I don't forget anything, I check my bag in the morning.","scene":"Talking about a morning habit"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 日本に行けるために、勉強しています。 → ✅ 日本に行けるように、勉強しています。 (V可能 + ために is wrong)","❌ 勉強 costume ように、本を買いました。 → ✅ 勉強のために、本を買いました。 (For a noun purpose, use N のために)","❌ 日本語を勉強するように、本を買いました。 → ✅ 日本語を勉強するために、本を買いました。 (Use ために for an intentional action purpose)"],"compare":null}},"n4-031||memory":{"title":"🎯 Visual memory tip","sub":"Vる／Vないように · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vないように","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ / so as not to ~ (aiming for a desired state)","slug":"memory","definition":"A quick memory aid: 様 (ように) originally means **shape**, so ように suggests that the action in the following clause makes the state in the first clause “take shape.”","nuance":"This image is only a memory aid; it does not replace grammatical analysis.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-031||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common communication phrases","sub":"Vる／Vないように · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vないように","patternReading":"ように","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ / so as not to ~ (aiming for a desired state)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases are very common in JLPT N4 and everyday conversation. Memorizing them helps you speak and write more naturally and recognize the 〜ように pattern in tests. Notice that they often accompany practice, preparation, or habits.","nuance":"These phrases work as ready-made chunks; memorizing them helps you speak and write faster.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"読める","r":"よめる","m":"can read"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"so that"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"字","r":"じ","m":"text"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"大きく","r":"おおきく","m":"larger"},{"t":"しました","m":"made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I enlarged the text so that my grandparents could read the book.","scene":"Making phone text larger for grandparents"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-032||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vるようになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようになります","patternReading":"ようになります","coreMeaning":"Become able to do ~ (natural change)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ようになる describes a change of state that happens naturally over time: from being unable to do something to being able to do it, from not having a habit to having one, or from one state to another. The speaker does not intentionally force the change; it results from practice, the living environment, or time. With the potential form (V可能形), its most common meaning is: become able to do ~.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with a moderately polite tone that sounds natural in everyday conversation. The nuance is often one of happy surprise, pride, or relief when noticing that oneself or someone else has improved. Unlike 〜ようにする (to try to/keep doing), ようになる is completely neutral—it reports an achieved result without emphasizing intention.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"したら","r":"したら","m":"if I practice"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"泳げる","r":"およげる","m":"can swim"},{"t":"ようになりました","r":"ようになりました","m":"became able"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After practicing, I became able to swim.","scene":"Bragging to a friend after a swim course"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"住んで","r":"すんで","m":"live"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"since"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"箸","r":"はし","m":"chopsticks"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"使える","r":"つかえる","m":"can use"},{"t":"ようになりました","r":"ようになりました","m":"became able"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Since living in Japan, I have become able to use chopsticks.","scene":"Talking with a Japanese coworker over a meal"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"読んでいたら","r":"よんでいたら","m":"if I keep reading"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"速く","r":"はやく","m":"quickly"},{"t":"読める","r":"よめる","m":"can read"},{"t":"ようになりました","r":"ようになりました","m":"became able"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After reading every day, I can read faster now.","scene":"Reflecting on reading speed after steady practice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-032||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"Vるようになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようになります","patternReading":"ようになります","coreMeaning":"Become able to do ~ (natural change)","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern is **Vる / Vない / V可能形** plus **ようになる**. よう is an abstract noun meaning 'state or manner,' and になる means 'become,' so the literal meaning is 'become a state in which ~.' It conjugates like an ordinary verb: ようになります (present), ようになりました (past), and ようになりません (negative).","nuance":"With a potential verb, do not add ことができます, as this repeats the same meaning. With the Vない form, keep ない: 飲まないようになる, rather than 飲まなくなる (although なくなる is also possible, ようになる better suits a gradual change with a gentler nuance).","structure":[{"lhs":"V辞書形 (Vる)","rhs":"ようになります","note":"Become ~ (new habit/state)"},{"lhs":"Vない形","rhs":"ようになります","note":"Become no longer ~"},{"lhs":"V可能形","rhs":"ようになります","note":"Become able to ~ (ability)"},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"ようになりました","note":"Became ~ (past)"},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"ようになりません","note":"Do not become ~ (negative)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"ようになりました","r":"ようになりました","m":"became able"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have become able to speak Japanese.","scene":"Talking to a friend after years of study"},{"tokens":[{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"起きられる","r":"おきられる","m":"can wake up"},{"t":"ようになりました","r":"ようになりました","m":"became able"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have become able to wake up early.","scene":"Feeling proud of breaking the habit of sleeping in"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-032||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Vるようになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようになります","patternReading":"ようになります","coreMeaning":"Become able to do ~ (natural change)","slug":"usage","definition":"The main use is to talk about a change achieved after a process. Common situations include gaining a skill through sports practice or language study, adapting after moving to a new environment, or realizing that you have changed. The past ようになりました is very common when reporting a completed change.","nuance":"Do not use it for an inborn ability—if you were born knowing how to swim, do not say 泳げるようになりました. Use it only when there is a change from an old state to a new one. The present ようになります is more common in writing, describing a habit that is gradually forming.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"recently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"食べられる","r":"たべられる","m":"can eat"},{"t":"ようになりました","r":"ようになりました","m":"became able"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Recently, I have become able to eat vegetables.","scene":"Telling your mother about a change in taste"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"自分で","r":"じぶんで","m":"by themself"},{"t":"服","r":"ふく","m":"clothes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"着られる","r":"きられる","m":"can put on"},{"t":"ようになりました","r":"ようになりました","m":"became able"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My child has become able to dress themself.","scene":"A mother telling a friend how her child is growing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-032||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"Vるようになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようになります","patternReading":"ようになります","coreMeaning":"Become able to do ~ (natural change)","slug":"compare","definition":"This pattern is often confused with **ようにする**, **ことができます**, and **ようになっている**. Each has a different focus: ようにする emphasizes deliberate effort; ことができます states a current ability; and ようになっている describes a prearranged state established by design or rules.","nuance":"To say 'I have become able to speak Japanese,' use ようになりました when emphasizing the learning process; use 話せます when simply stating your current ability. ようにする is generally not used with abilities, but with habits: 早く起きるようにしています.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"try to"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"am doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I try to exercise every day.","scene":"Talking about your personal commitment"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"ようにする","point":"ようにする: deliberate effort to maintain something; ようになる: a natural result. Example: 毎日運動するようにしています (I try to exercise every day) vs 毎日運動するようになりました (I have developed a daily exercise habit)."},{"vs":"ことができます","point":"ことができます: states a current ability without emphasizing the process; ようになる: emphasizes the process of change. Example: 日本語が話せます (I can speak Japanese) vs 日本語が話せるようになりました (I became able to speak Japanese; I could not before)."},{"vs":"ようになっている","point":"ようになっている: a designed feature or state; ようになる: a natural change. Example: この機械は止まるようになっています (This machine is designed to stop) vs 止まるようになりました (It has started stopping)."}]}},"n4-032||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vるようになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようになります","patternReading":"ようになります","coreMeaning":"Become able to do ~ (natural change)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse **ようになる** and **ようにする** because both can be translated as 'become.' They also add **ことができます** together with **ようになる**, creating a redundant sentence. Another common mistake is forgetting **よう** in the pattern.","nuance":"The most serious error is using ようにする for an acquired ability—it changes the meaning completely. Vる＋なる sounds unnatural; Japanese speakers may understand it, but it feels abrupt and incomplete.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"ようになりました","r":"ようになりました","m":"became able"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have become able to speak Japanese.","scene":"Correct sentence replacing the incorrect one above"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 日本語が話せるようにしました。 → ✅ 日本語が話せるようになりました。 (Use ようにする for trying to maintain something, not for acquiring an ability.)","❌ 日本語を話すことができますようになりました。 → ✅ 日本語が話せるようになりました。 (Do not combine ことができます with ようになる; the potential form already expresses ability.)","❌ 泳げるになりました。 → ✅ 泳げるようになりました。 (よう is required.)","❌ 毎日運動するようになっています。 (When describing a formed habit) → ✅ 毎日運動するようになりました。 (ようになっています is for designed states, not habits.)"],"compare":null}},"n4-032||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vるようになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようになります","patternReading":"ようになります","coreMeaning":"Become able to do ~ (natural change)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember **よう** as 'form or state' (様子), and **なる** as 'become.' Together: 'become a state in which ~.' Picture a **sapling growing into a tall tree**—a natural process that takes time and cannot be forced. That is the idea of ようになる.","nuance":"This tip helps distinguish it from ようにする: する means 'do,' showing intention; なる means 'become,' showing a natural result.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-032||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vるようになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようになります","patternReading":"ようになります","coreMeaning":"Become able to do ~ (natural change)","slug":"phrases","definition":"The most common combinations with ようになる use potential verbs: **できる**, **読める**, **話せる**, **食べられる**, **書ける**, and **聞ける**. In everyday conversation, you will often hear sentences such as '日本語が話せるようになりたい' (I want to become able to speak Japanese) and '辛い物が食べられるようになりました' (I have become able to eat spicy food).","nuance":"These phrases often use the past tense to report a result. In the present (ようになります), they sound somewhat formal and suit writing or presentations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"become able"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have become able to speak a little Japanese.","scene":"Answering someone who asks about your Japanese level"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-033||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vるようにします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようにします","patternReading":"ようにします","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ (determination/effort)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ようにします describes using one's will to perform an action, often to start or maintain a habit. It means “try to ~.” This is voluntary, not a natural change.","nuance":"This is common in speech with friends, family, and coworkers. It is stronger than つもり (intention): it shows repeated effort, not just a one-time plan.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"watch"},{"t":"ようにしています","r":"ようにしています","m":"am trying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am trying to watch the news every morning.","scene":"Sharing a new habit with a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"たくさん","m":"a lot"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"ようにしています","r":"ようにしています","m":"am trying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am trying to eat lots of vegetables.","scene":"Explaining a health tip"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-033||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vるようにします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようにします","patternReading":"ようにします","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ (determination/effort)","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: Vる（辞書形）＋ようにします. Use Vない to say “try not to do.” These forms express the timing and degree of effort.","nuance":"Vる is the dictionary form; do not use ます-form before ように. ています emphasizes maintaining an ongoing effort, while します emphasizes the decision to start.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる (辞書形)","rhs":"ようにします","note":"determination to do V"},{"lhs":"Vない (ない形)","rhs":"ようにします","note":"determination not to do V"},{"lhs":"Vるように","rhs":"しています","note":"maintaining effort"},{"lhs":"Vるように","rhs":"しました","note":"made it a habit"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"起きる","r":"おきる","m":"get up"},{"t":"ようにします","r":"ようにします","m":"will try"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Starting tomorrow, I will try to get up early.","scene":"Making a promise before bed"},{"tokens":[{"t":"夜","r":"よる","m":"at night"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲まない","r":"のまない","m":"not drink"},{"t":"ようにしています","r":"ようにしています","m":"am trying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am trying not to drink coffee at night.","scene":"Discussing a sleep habit"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-033||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vるようにします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようにします","patternReading":"ようにします","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ (determination/effort)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜ようにします is used when the speaker sets a personal goal to improve, build a good habit, or give up a bad one. It is common in conversations about health, study, and work.","nuance":"It can be used for any person, but most often describes the speaker's own actions. To advise someone else, Japanese commonly uses 〜ようにしてください (please try to).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"歩く","r":"あるく","m":"walk"},{"t":"ようにしています","r":"ようにしています","m":"am trying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For my health, I am trying to walk every day.","scene":"Answering how you stay fit"},{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"at work"},{"t":"では"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"メモ","m":"notes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"取る","r":"とる","m":"take"},{"t":"ようにしています","r":"ようにしています","m":"am trying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"At work, I try to take notes.","scene":"Discussing professional habits"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-033||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from ようになる / つもり","sub":"Vるようにします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようにします","patternReading":"ようにします","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ (determination/effort)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps you quickly distinguish Vるようにします from the easily confused patterns Vるようになります and Vるつもりです. The key: ようにします = intentional action; ようになります = a natural change of state; つもり = an intention still in the mind, not necessarily carried out. Once you understand the axis of intention, nature, and planning, you can choose correctly in conversation.","nuance":"All three can translate as “will do,” but the contexts differ. ようにします often appears with habit adverbs such as 毎日・毎朝, while つもり often goes with a specific time. ようになる emphasizes the result, with less focus on the person's effort.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"ようにしています","r":"ようにしています","m":"am trying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am trying to eat vegetables every day.","scene":"Compared with ようになる: active effort"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるようになります","point":"ようになる = become (natural/automatic change); ようにする = try (active intention). E.g. 日本語が話せるようになりました (natural progress) vs 毎日練習するようにしています (deliberate practice)."},{"vs":"Vるつもりです","point":"つもり = intention (only a plan); ようにする = determination to make it a habit. E.g. 週末に勉強するつもりです (weekend plan) vs 毎日少し勉強するようにしています (trying to build a daily habit)."}]}},"n4-033||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"Vるようにします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようにします","patternReading":"ようにします","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ (determination/effort)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common errors when using 〜ようにします: confusing it with ようになる, using the wrong verb form, or misunderstanding the direction of “try to do” versus “try not to do.” Study the wrong/right pairs to avoid mistakes on tests and in real conversation.","nuance":"Learners often confuse ようにする with ようになる and use the wrong verb form before ように. To say “try not to do,” use Vないようにする, not a negated ようにします.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強する","r":"べんきょうする","m":"study"},{"t":"ようにしています","r":"ようにしています","m":"am trying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am trying to study kanji every day.","scene":"Talking about self-study"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 毎日、運動するようになります。 → ✅ 毎日、運動するようにしています。 (natural change vs active effort)","❌ 早く寝ますようにします。 → ✅ 早く寝るようにします。 (use dictionary form before ように)","❌ 野菜を食べるようにしません。 → ✅ 野菜を食べないようにしています。 (to say 'try not to eat,' use Vない)"],"compare":null}},"n4-033||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"Vるようにします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるようにします","patternReading":"ようにします","coreMeaning":"Try to ~ (determination/effort)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: ように means “in the way / as,” so ようにします means “act in the way intended” → set the behavior yourself. ようになります means “become that way” → change naturally. The pair する / なる determines the meaning: する = active, なる = natural.","nuance":"This short memory tip can be applied immediately in exercises. When you see “I do something,” choose する; when you see “something happens,” choose なる.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-034||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V受身形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形","patternReading":"うけみけい","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive an action (passive voice)","slug":"core","definition":"The passive form (受身形) describes someone or something being affected by or receiving an action from another person or thing. It often translates as “be done to” or “receive.” The speaker focuses on the affected person, not the doer. Example: 先生に名前を呼ばれました = I was called by the teacher.","nuance":"受身形 often reflects the affected person’s viewpoint: feeling happy when praised or annoyed when bothered. In everyday speech, **に** commonly marks the agent. For completed past actions, use 〜られた／〜られました. Besides passive meaning, it can also be used as a **mild honorific** (e.g. 先生は何を読まれますか), but at N4 focus on the basic meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"呼ばれました","r":"よばれました","m":"was called"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher called my name.","scene":"The teacher calls on me in class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"手","r":"て","m":"hand"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"噛まれました","r":"かまれました","m":"was bitten"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was bitten on the hand by a dog.","scene":"A dog bites me while I’m walking"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-034||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V受身形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形","patternReading":"うけみけい","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive an action (passive voice)","slug":"forms","definition":"Group I: change the final **う → あ** sound, then add **れる**: 書く → 書かれる, 飲む → 飲まれる. Group II: drop **る** and add **られる**: 食べる → 食べられる, 見る → 見られる. Group III: **する → される**, **来る → 来られる**. Tense follows Group II verbs: 〜られない (negative), 〜られた (past), 〜られています (ongoing).","nuance":"In polite speech, add ます: 呼ばれます, 食べられます. The polite negative is 〜られません. With Group I verbs, carefully change the final sound: 待つ → 待たれる, 話す → 話される.","structure":[{"lhs":"Nhóm I (書く)","rhs":"書かれる","note":"う→あ + れる (書く→書かれる)"},{"lhs":"Nhóm I (飲む)","rhs":"飲まれる","note":"のむ→のまれる"},{"lhs":"Nhóm II (食べる)","rhs":"食べられる","note":"drop る + られる"},{"lhs":"Nhóm II (見る)","rhs":"見られる","note":"みる→みられる"},{"lhs":"Nhóm III (する)","rhs":"される","note":"する→される"},{"lhs":"Nhóm III (来る)","rhs":"来られる","note":"くる→こられる"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"起こされます","r":"おこされます","m":"am woken"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother wakes me up every day.","scene":"My mother wakes me every morning"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"多くの","r":"おおくの","m":"many"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"読まれています","r":"よまれています","m":"is read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is being read by many people.","scene":"The book is said to be popular"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: 辞書形 → 受身形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — godan む · く · ぐ → まれる / かれる / がれる","Change the final sound from the う row to the あ row (う→わ), then add れる","飲む（のむ） → `飲まれる（のまれる）` (drink) ・ 書く（かく） → `書かれる（かかれる）` (write) ・ 泳ぐ（およぐ） → `泳がれる（およがれる）` (swim) ・ 読む（よむ） → `読まれる（よまれる）` (read)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — godan す · う · つ · る → される / われる / たれる / られる","あ row + れる (う→わ, る→ら)","話す（はなす） → `話される（はなされる）` (speak) ・ 言う（いう） → `言われる（いわれる）` (say) ・ 待つ（まつ） → `待たれる（またれる）` (wait) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰られる（かえられる）` (return)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — ichidan: drop る + られる","Drop る, then add られる — same as the potential form!","食べる（たべる） → `食べられる（たべられる）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見られる（みられる）` (watch) ・ 褒める（ほめる） → `褒められる（ほめられる）` (praise) ・ 叱る（しかる） → `叱られる（しかられる）` (scold)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — する → される · 来る → 来られる","Memorize","する → `される` (do) ・ 来る（くる） → `来られる（こられる）` (come) ・ 尊敬する（そんけいする） → `尊敬される（そんけいされる）` (respect)"],["**Special case** — 直接受身 · Direct passive","Subject = the direct object affected. Use に for the agent.","先生（せんせい） → `先生に褒められた（先生に ほめられた）` (teacher (agent)) ・ 犬（いぬ） → `犬に噛まれた（いぬに かまれた）` (dog (agent))"],["**Special case** — 間接受身 · Indirect passive","The subject is negatively affected by another person's action — a characteristic feature of Japanese.","雨に降られた（あめにふられた） → `雨に降られた（あめにふられた）` (get rained on (annoying)) ・ 子供に泣かれた（こどもになかれた） → `子供に泣かれた（こどもになかれた）` (have one's child cry (annoying))"]]}}},"n4-034||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"V受身形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形","patternReading":"うけみけい","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive an action (passive voice)","slug":"usage","definition":"受身形 is commonly used in three situations: ① Being affected by another person: 先生に褒められました. ② Suffering a loss involving your belongings: 弟にケーキを食べられました. ③ Presenting a general fact objectively: この映画は多くの人に見られています.","nuance":"Use **に** for the agent. If the agent is unimportant or unknown, it can be omitted. To emphasize the affected person, place them at the beginning as the topic. In news writing, によって can mark the agent, but at N4, focus on に.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"弟","r":"おとうと","m":"younger brother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べられました","r":"たべられました","m":"was eaten"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My younger brother ate my cake.","scene":"I find my birthday cake eaten"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"アニメ","m":"anime"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"世界","r":"せかい","m":"world"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"見られています","r":"みられています","m":"is watched"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This anime is watched around the world.","scene":"Discussing a popular anime"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"作られます","r":"つくられます","m":"will be built"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A new station will be built next month.","scene":"Hearing a city announcement"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-034||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"V受身形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形","patternReading":"うけみけい","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive an action (passive voice)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps you distinguish 受身形 from two nearby patterns: **Vてもらいます** (have someone do something for you) and **自動詞＋ています** (a natural state).","nuance":"受身形 focuses on **an action done to you by someone else**; Vてもらいます focuses on **your gratitude for a favor**, even though someone else also performs the action. 自動詞＋ています simply describes **a state**, without requiring an agent.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"褒められました","r":"ほめられました","m":"was praised"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My teacher praised me.","scene":"I did well on a test"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてもらいます","point":"受身形: the speaker receives an action, mainly reporting the result. Example: 私は先生に褒められました. / Vてもらいます: the speaker receives a favor, emphasizing asking or gratitude. Example: 私は先生に褒めてもらいました."},{"vs":"自動詞＋ています","point":"受身形: an explicit or implied agent acts, using a transitive verb. Example: ドアが閉められました. / 自動詞＋ています: only a natural state, with no agent. Example: ドアが閉まっています."}]}},"n4-034||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V受身形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形","patternReading":"うけみけい","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive an action (passive voice)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Three common mistakes: ① Using the wrong particle for the agent (が instead of に). ② Keeping the active structure after making the sentence passive. ③ Forgetting that a belonging affected by someone can remain the object marked with を.","nuance":"In passive sentences, the agent takes **に**. Do not use が for the person causing the action; が marks the affected subject when it appears at the beginning. Correct: 私は先生に名前を呼ばれました.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"妹","r":"いもうと","m":"younger sister"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べられました","r":"たべられました","m":"was eaten"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My younger sister ate my cake.","scene":"I come home and find the cake gone"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私は先生が名前を呼びました。 → ✅ 私は先生に名前を呼ばれました。","❌ 私のケーキが弟に食べられました。 → ✅ 私は弟にケーキを食べられました。","❌ 私は先生にほめました。 → ✅ 私は先生にほめられました。","❌ 雨が降られました。 → ✅ 雨に降られました。(the rain bothered me)"],"compare":null}},"n4-034||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V受身形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形","patternReading":"うけみけい","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive an action (passive voice)","slug":"memory","definition":"The passive form has the suffix **〜れる／〜られる**. Imagine “reru” as an action that **falls on** you from outside. Or look at **受** in 受身形: a hand receiving something from another person.","nuance":"To choose the form, remember: for Group I, “**A** changes to **reru**” — change the final sound to **あ**. For Group II, “drop ru, add rare-ru.” Memorize Group III: “suru → sareru,” “kuru → korareru.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-034||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V受身形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形","patternReading":"うけみけい","coreMeaning":"Be done to / receive an action (passive voice)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common passive verbs in tests and daily life include 誘われる (be invited), 呼ばれる (be called), 褒められる (be praised), 叱られる (be scolded), 起こされる (be woken), 食べられる (be eaten), 見られる (be watched), and 作られる (be made).","nuance":"When a sentence includes an agent such as 先生に, 母に, or 友だちに, think of 受身形. The patterns 〜に褒められる and 〜に叱られる appear frequently in conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"誘われました","r":"さそわれました","m":"was invited"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend invited me.","scene":"Plans to go out on the weekend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"叱られました","r":"しかられました","m":"was scolded"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother scolded me.","scene":"I came home late"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-035||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史)","patternReading":"られます","coreMeaning":"Be ~ (objective events / history)","slug":"core","definition":"〜Nが／はVられます is the **objective passive**, used to describe an event without needing to know or mention who performed it. The focus is on the action or result itself: a building was constructed, a rule was issued, or a festival was held.","nuance":"It has a **neutral** tone, with no sense of harm or annoyance. It is formal and common in writing, newspapers, and history books. Japanese speakers also use it when reporting news. Unlike 迷惑の受身, this pattern has no negative meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"寺","r":"てら","m":"temple"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"300年前","r":"さんびゃくねんまえ","m":"300 years ago"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"建てられました","r":"たてられました","m":"was built"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This temple was built 300 years ago.","scene":"Reading a local history book"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎年","r":"まいとし","m":"every year"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"祭り","r":"まつり","m":"festival"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開かれます","r":"ひらかれます","m":"is held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This festival is held every year.","scene":"Hearing a local festival advertisement"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"ルール","r":"るーる","m":"rule"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"発表されました","r":"はっぴょうされました","m":"was announced"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The new rule was announced.","scene":"Hearing a school announcement"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-035||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史)","patternReading":"られます","coreMeaning":"Be ~ (objective events / history)","slug":"forms","definition":"This section explains how to form the **passive** for each verb group, including polite, negative, and past forms. General structure: N (thing/event) + が/は + V（passive）. The subject is the affected object, not the performer.","nuance":"This is a core grammar rule that must be memorized. In this objective pattern, common verbs include 建てる, 作る, 開く, 行う, 発表する, 使う, and 知る in passive form. Polite: Vられます. Negative: Vられません.","structure":[{"lhs":"Group 1 (う-verbs)","rhs":"う → われる","note":"例: かいます → かわれます, つくります → つくられます"},{"lhs":"Group 2 (る-verbs)","rhs":"る → られる","note":"例: たべます → たべられます, みます → みられます"},{"lhs":"Irregular","rhs":"する→される, くる→こられる","note":"例: せつめいします → せつめいされます"},{"lhs":"Polite","rhs":"Vられる → Vられます／Vられました","note":"例: たてられました"},{"lhs":"Negative / past negative","rhs":"Vられません / Vられませんでした","note":"例: たてられませんでした"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"作られました","r":"つくられました","m":"was made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This car was made in Japan.","scene":"Looking at product information"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"使われていません","r":"つかわれていません","m":"is not used"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This room is not being used.","scene":"Asking about an empty room"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-035||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史)","patternReading":"られます","coreMeaning":"Be ~ (objective events / history)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **describe events, history, and rules** when the speaker wants to emphasize the event itself and the performer is unimportant or unknown. It is common in reading passages, notices, and presentations.","nuance":"It suits written and formal contexts. In speech, it can avoid assigning personal responsibility. If the action causes annoyance, use 迷惑の受身. If you need to name the performer, use に + Vられる.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"市","r":"し","m":"city"},{"t":"では"},{"t":"毎年","r":"まいとし","m":"every year"},{"t":"10月","r":"じゅうがつ","m":"October"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"お祭り","r":"おまつり","m":"festival"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"行われます","r":"おこなわれます","m":"is held"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In this city, a festival is held every October.","scene":"Reading a city brochure"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先週","r":"せんしゅう","m":"last week"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"図書館","r":"としょかん","m":"library"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開かれました","r":"ひらかれました","m":"was opened"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The new library opened last week.","scene":"Hearing local news"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-035||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史)","patternReading":"られます","coreMeaning":"Be ~ (objective events / history)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish the objective passive (Nが／はVられます) from other passive forms so you can choose the right nuance.","nuance":"The key points are that this pattern focuses on an event, has no sense of annoyance, and usually omits the agent. Commonly confused patterns include 迷惑の受身 (Vられて…) and agent-marked passive (にVられる).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"寺","r":"てら","m":"temple"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"建てられました","r":"たてられました","m":"was built"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This temple was built.","scene":"Talking about an old building"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜にVられる (迷惑受身)","point":"This pattern is objective, with no negative emotion. 迷惑の受身 expresses trouble or harm to the subject. E.g. 雨に降られた (was caught in the rain) uses 迷惑; この建物は建てられた is objective."},{"vs":"〜にVられる (動作主)","point":"This pattern hides the agent because it is unnecessary. To name the agent, use Vられる with に. E.g. この寺は建てられた (agent omitted) vs この寺は有名な建築家によって建てられた (agent named)."}]}},"n4-035||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史)","patternReading":"られます","coreMeaning":"Be ~ (objective events / history)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with the objective passive include adding an unnecessary agent, confusing its neutral nuance with annoyance, and conjugating verbs incorrectly. Remember that this pattern emphasizes the event, not who performed it.","nuance":"Most errors come from adding an agent, using the wrong annoyance nuance, or conjugating the verb incorrectly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"去年","r":"きょねん","m":"last year"},{"t":"作られました","r":"つくられました","m":"was built"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This park was built last year.","scene":"Asking about a new park"}],"pitfalls":["❌ Add に for an unnecessary agent: この建物は職人に建てられました → ✅ この建物は建てられました (when the agent need not be stated).","❌ Use the objective passive for personal trouble: 雨が降られました → use 迷惑の受身 (雨に降られた).","❌ Wrong verb-group conjugation: 書きます → 書けます (wrong) → ✅ 書かれます.","❌ In casual conversation it can sound stiff: use an active sentence when the agent is known, or use 〜んです."],"compare":null}},"n4-035||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史)","patternReading":"られます","coreMeaning":"Be ~ (objective events / history)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember this usage and distinguish it from other passive forms, picture a historical news report: a thing or event is the subject, and the verb is in Vられます form.","nuance":"Picture a news report: 'Built in 1200' → Vられました. The subject is a thing, so the passive is objective.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-035||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが／はVられます (社会・歴史)","patternReading":"られます","coreMeaning":"Be ~ (objective events / history)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These objective passive phrases appear frequently in tests and formal texts.","nuance":"They are common in writing; learners should memorize them as chunks.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ベトナム","r":"べとなむ","m":"Vietnam"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"よく"},{"t":"食べられています","r":"たべられています","m":"is eaten"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This dish is often eaten in Vietnam.","scene":"Introducing a dish to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-036||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"迷惑の受身 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"迷惑の受身","patternReading":"めいわくのうけみ","coreMeaning":"Being negatively affected by someone else's action","slug":"core","definition":"迷惑の受身 is a special passive: the speaker is **troubled, annoyed, or harmed** by someone else's action. Unlike the ordinary passive, the subject is the **person affected**, and the verb can be **intransitive** (something that happens on its own) without an object.","nuance":"A **complaining, irritated, or regretful** tone is essential. Do not use it for good or neutral events. For example, do not say 合格されて嬉しい. It is used naturally in both everyday speech and writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"降られて","r":"ふられて","m":"be rained on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"風邪","r":"かぜ","m":"a cold"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"引きました","r":"ひきました","m":"caught"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was caught in the rain yesterday and caught a cold.","scene":"Rain starts after work, and I have no umbrella"},{"tokens":[{"t":"隣","r":"となり","m":"next door"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ピアノ","m":"piano"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"弾かれて","r":"ひかれて","m":"have piano played"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"眠れませんでした","r":"ねむれませんでした","m":"could not sleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My neighbor played the piano and kept me from sleeping.","scene":"A neighbor practices piano at midnight"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-036||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"迷惑の受身 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"迷惑の受身","patternReading":"めいわくのうけみ","coreMeaning":"Being negatively affected by someone else's action","slug":"forms","definition":"General structure: **person affected は／が + person causing it に + passive verb**. Conjugate the verb into the passive (受身形), then add polite, past, negative, and other forms as usual.","nuance":"The special point is that in 迷惑の受身, **intransitive verbs** such as 泣く、来る、降る, and 騒ぐ can still be used. This differs sharply from ordinary passive patterns in English and Vietnamese.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1（人）は／が","rhs":"N2（人）に ＋ V受身形","note":"N1 is inconvenienced by N2's action. Conjugate V into the passive form."},{"lhs":"V他動詞","rhs":"V～れる／られる","note":"食べる → 食べられる；書く → 書かれる"},{"lhs":"V自動詞","rhs":"V～れる／られる","note":"泣く → 泣かれる；来る → 来られる；降る → 降られる"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来られて","r":"こられて","m":"have come unexpectedly"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studying"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できませんでした","m":"could not do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend came over, so I could not study.","scene":"A close friend drops by while I am rushing to finish homework"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"赤ちゃん","r":"あかちゃん","m":"baby"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"泣かれて","r":"なかれて","m":"have a baby cry"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"困りました","r":"こまりました","m":"was troubled"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A baby cried on the train, leaving me at a loss.","scene":"A baby cries loudly on the train"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-036||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"迷惑の受身 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"迷惑の受身","patternReading":"めいわくのうけみ","coreMeaning":"Being negatively affected by someone else's action","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when the speaker is **inconvenienced** by an unwanted action caused by another person or thing. The action may be **accidental** (rain or a baby crying) or **intentional** (someone smoking beside you).","nuance":"Japanese speakers use it naturally for mild complaints. It often appears with feeling words such as 困る、眠れない、迷惑だ, and 嫌だ. Do not use it to praise something or describe a positive event.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eating"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"とき","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"急に","r":"きゅうに","m":"suddenly"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来られました","r":"こられました","m":"have come unexpectedly"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend suddenly came over while I was eating breakfast.","scene":"A friend rings the doorbell unexpectedly during breakfast"},{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"降られて","r":"ふられて","m":"be rained on"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"シャツ","m":"shirt"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"濡れました","r":"ぬれました","m":"got wet"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was caught in the rain, and my shirt got completely wet.","scene":"A sudden shower hits while riding a motorbike"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-036||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"迷惑の受身 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"迷惑の受身","patternReading":"めいわくのうけみ","coreMeaning":"Being negatively affected by someone else's action","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare this pattern with the **ordinary passive** (which simply reports an event) and **〜てしまう** (which emphasizes regret or accidentally doing something).","nuance":"In conversation, 迷惑の受身 is natural for complaining. The ordinary passive only reports an event; てしまう expresses the speaker's regret and does not necessarily involve another person.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食事","r":"しょくじ","m":"meal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"having"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"とき","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"隣","r":"となり","m":"next door"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"たばこ","m":"cigarette"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"吸われて","r":"すわれて","m":"have smoked"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"いやな","m":"unpleasant"},{"t":"気持ち","r":"きもち","m":"feeling"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Someone beside me smoked while I was eating, making me uncomfortable.","scene":"Someone smokes beside me in a restaurant"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V受身形 (thường)","point":"The ordinary passive simply describes being affected: 財布を盗まれた (my wallet was stolen) → not necessarily troublesome. 迷惑の受身 expresses annoyance and can use intransitive verbs: 雨に降られた (be rained on) → an inconvenience."},{"vs":"〜てしまいました","point":"てしまう emphasizes completion plus regret, usually caused by oneself: 財布を忘れてしまった. 迷惑の受身 emphasizes someone else causing harm: 友達に来られて、困った."}]}},"n4-036||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner mistakes","sub":"迷惑の受身 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"迷惑の受身","patternReading":"めいわくのうけみ","coreMeaning":"Being negatively affected by someone else's action","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with the affected-person passive include forgetting the particle **に**, assuming intransitive verbs cannot be passive, and confusing it with the causative.","nuance":"Learners often correctly say 雨に降られました for “I was rained on,” but forget to conjugate the verb into the passive and use the dictionary form instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ほえられて","m":"be barked at"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"びっくりしました","m":"was startled"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A dog barked at me this morning and startled me.","scene":"A dog barks loudly during a morning walk"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 子どもを泣かれました。（wrong particle を） → ✅ 子どもに泣かれました。","❌ 雨に降らせました。 → ✅ 雨に降られました。","❌ 友達が来られました。（using が instead of に for the agent） → ✅ 友達に来られました。"],"compare":null}},"n4-036||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"迷惑の受身 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"迷惑の受身","patternReading":"めいわくのうけみ","coreMeaning":"Being negatively affected by someone else's action","slug":"memory","definition":"Visual memory tip: remember the classic sentence **隣の人にピアノを弾かれて、眠れません** — you are the victim of a troublesome action. When someone else's action inconveniences you, use the passive.","nuance":"The name 迷惑の受身 says it all: 迷惑 means “trouble” or “inconvenience.” Use it only when something **bothers** you, not for neutral or positive events.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-036||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"迷惑の受身 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"迷惑の受身","patternReading":"めいわくのうけみ","coreMeaning":"Being negatively affected by someone else's action","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are common fixed phrases with **intransitive verbs** found in listening exercises and conversation. Memorize them for quick recall.","nuance":"Japanese speakers often use 迷惑の受身 to make a mild complaint instead of directly saying “I am annoyed.” These phrases sound very natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"降られました","r":"ふられました","m":"was rained on"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was caught in the rain this morning.","scene":"It starts raining just after I leave home"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-037||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vるのは〜です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのは〜です","patternReading":"の は です","coreMeaning":"Doing V is ~ (clause nominalizer, experience evaluation)","slug":"core","definition":"ーVるのは〜です nominalizes an entire verb clause: の follows Vる and turns the “action” into “the act of ~ / doing ~.” のは marks that clause as the topic, and the following part gives a comment or evaluation. This is very common in everyday conversation.","nuance":"This pattern feels familiar and conversational. Japanese speakers often use it when sharing their feelings or evaluating an action or experience as difficult, fun, tiring, time-consuming, and so on. The final part is usually an evaluative adjective or noun.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"話す","r":"はなす","m":"speak"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Speaking Japanese is difficult.","scene":"Complaining to a classmate about speaking Japanese"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"早起き","r":"はやおき","m":"rising early"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"辛い","r":"つらい","m":"tough"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Getting up early every day is tough.","scene":"An office worker talks over morning coffee about rising early"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-037||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vるのは〜です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのは〜です","patternReading":"の は です","coreMeaning":"Doing V is ~ (clause nominalizer, experience evaluation)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic pattern: **V（辞書形）＋ の ＋ は ＋ [い-adjective / な-adjective / noun] ＋ です**. の turns the verb into a noun, は marks the topic, and the final part gives the evaluation. Tense and negation go at the end; the verb before の does not change.","nuance":"In casual speech, です may become だ. With な-adjectives such as 大変・簡単・便利, do not add な before の—the の follows the verb directly, not an adjective.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）＋の","rhs":"は＋Aい＋です","note":"Speaking is difficult"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）＋の","rhs":"は＋Aな＋です","note":"Tough, easy, convenient"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）＋の","rhs":"は＋N＋です","note":"Running in the morning is a habit"},{"lhs":"Vた／Vない＋の","rhs":"は＋〜＋です","note":"It is regrettable that they did not come"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝早く","r":"あさはやく","m":"early morning"},{"t":"起きる","r":"おきる","m":"wake up"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"大変","r":"たいへん","m":"tough"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"楽しい","r":"たのしい","m":"fun"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Getting up early is tough but fun.","scene":"A morning runner talks about their routine"},{"tokens":[{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"歩く","r":"あるく","m":"walk"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"30分","r":"さんじゅっぷん","m":"30 minutes"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Walking to work takes 30 minutes.","scene":"Answering a coworker who asks about the commute"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-037||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vるのは〜です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのは〜です","patternReading":"の は です","coreMeaning":"Doing V is ~ (clause nominalizer, experience evaluation)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to make an action the topic and then comment on or evaluate it. It commonly appears with evaluative adjectives such as 難しい, 楽しい, 大変, 簡単, and 便利. It can also describe time or cost as features of the action.","nuance":"In conversation, this pattern is natural when sharing personal experiences. To ask “What is the most difficult thing?”, use 一番難しいのは何ですか？ Do not use のは when the nominalized clause is an object; use のを in that case.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"一人で","r":"ひとりで","m":"alone"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"watch"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"ちょっと"},{"t":"寂しい","r":"さびしい","m":"lonely"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Watching a movie alone is a little lonely.","scene":"Sharing feelings after seeing a movie alone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"地下鉄","r":"ちかてつ","m":"subway"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"速い","r":"はやい","m":"fast"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Taking the subway is fast but expensive.","scene":"Discussing commute options with a coworker"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-037||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions from similar patterns","sub":"Vるのは〜です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのは〜です","patternReading":"の は です","coreMeaning":"Doing V is ~ (clause nominalizer, experience evaluation)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse のは with のが / のを, or の with こと. This branch highlights the key distinction for choosing the right pattern.","nuance":"In Minna no Nihongo Lesson 38, learners often ask when to use が after learning のは. A simple rule is to identify the role of the verb before の: topic → は; object of liking, disliking, or ability → が; object of forgetting or knowing → を.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"歌う","r":"うたう","m":"sing"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"簡単","r":"かんたん","m":"easy"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Singing is easy.","scene":"Answering a friend who asks whether singing is difficult"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるのが好きです／上手です","point":"が marks the object of an emotion or ability: “like/be good at doing something.” は marks a topic and comments on it: “As for doing ~, it is ~.” Example: 歌うのが好きです (I like singing) → 歌うのは楽しいです (Singing is fun)."},{"vs":"Vること","point":"の feels concrete and familiar; こと is general, objective, and more formal. In conversation, のは sounds more natural; ことは is common for definitions and concepts."},{"vs":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています","point":"When の is the object, use を; のは is used when the nominalized clause is the topic. Compare: 買うのを忘れた (forgot to buy) / 買うのは大変 (buying it is tough)."}]}},"n4-037||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"Vるのは〜です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのは〜です","patternReading":"の は です","coreMeaning":"Doing V is ~ (clause nominalizer, experience evaluation)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate “doing” directly and forget の, or conjugate the verb into ます form before の. Remember that の follows the **dictionary form** (or Vた/Vない in variants).","nuance":"These errors make the sentence incorrect or unnatural. When the final word is 好き・嫌い・上手・下手—words for feelings or ability—use が rather than は, unless you want to emphasize a contrast.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"簡単","r":"かんたん","m":"easy"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Cooking is easy.","scene":"Encouraging a friend who is learning to cook"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 日本語を話すは難しいです。 → ✅ 日本語を話す**の**は難しいです。","❌ 話しますのは難しいです。 → ✅ 話すのは難しいです。","❌ 料理するは好きです。 → ✅ 料理する**のが**好きです。 (To say “like doing something,” use のが; do not omit の.)"],"compare":null}},"n4-037||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"Vるのは〜です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのは〜です","patternReading":"の は です","coreMeaning":"Doing V is ~ (clause nominalizer, experience evaluation)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember の, picture it as a **bag**: put the whole action into the bag, then attach は to make it the topic. The English equivalent is “Doing ... is ...”.","nuance":"For a long sentence, split it into [Vる + の] + は + [evaluation]. Read it as “Speaking Japanese is difficult”—the structure becomes easy to recognize. Do not translate の word-for-word as “of.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-037||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vるのは〜です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのは〜です","patternReading":"の は です","coreMeaning":"Doing V is ~ (clause nominalizer, experience evaluation)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These のは evaluation phrases appear often on N4 tests and in everyday life. You can learn them as fixed chunks.","nuance":"All three are common evaluation frames: difficult, tough, and important. Learning the accompanying vocabulary helps you form natural sentences.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書く","r":"かく","m":"write"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Writing kanji is difficult.","scene":"A student talks to the teacher about kanji homework"},{"tokens":[{"t":"一人で","r":"ひとりで","m":"alone"},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"楽しい","r":"たのしい","m":"fun"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Traveling alone is fun.","scene":"Telling a friend about a solo trip"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"のは","r":"のは","m":"doing (topic)"},{"t":"大切","r":"たいせつ","m":"important"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Exercising every day is important.","scene":"Advising a younger brother to exercise regularly"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-038||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vるのが好きです／上手です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのが好きです／上手です","patternReading":"の が すき です / じょうず です","coreMeaning":"Like / be good at doing something (の nominalizes the verb)","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern uses **の** to nominalize a verb in dictionary form: Vる + の means “doing ~.” It then connects with が + an adjective/na-adjective showing **attitude** (好き/嫌い) or **ability** (上手/下手/得意/苦手). This is the natural way to say “like/hate doing something” or “be good/bad at something.”","nuance":"Highly conversational, suitable for both daily speech and the N4 exam. 好き/嫌い express preferences; 上手/下手/得意/苦手 express skill. 上手 often praises technique, while 苦手 is softer than 下手 when describing your own weakness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"のが好き","r":"のがすき","m":"like doing"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like studying Japanese.","scene":"When asked about study preferences"},{"tokens":[{"t":"歌","r":"うた","m":"song"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"歌う","r":"うたう","m":"sing"},{"t":"のが上手","r":"のがじょうず","m":"be good at"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He sings well.","scene":"Praising someone’s talent"},{"tokens":[{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"起きる","r":"おきる","m":"wake up"},{"t":"のが苦手","r":"のがにがて","m":"be bad at"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m bad at getting up early.","scene":"Talking about difficulty waking early"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-038||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vるのが好きです／上手です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのが好きです／上手です","patternReading":"の が すき です / じょうず です","coreMeaning":"Like / be good at doing something (の nominalizes the verb)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure has three parts: **verb in dictionary form** + **の** + **が** + an adjective showing preference or ability. の nominalizes the verb. The particle **が** is required because 好き・上手・苦手... use が to mark what they describe.","nuance":"The V before の is always in **dictionary form**, never ます or た form. Tense and politeness appear at the sentence ending: 好きです → 好きでした → 好きではありません. To express “become more fond/skilled,” use **になる**.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"のが好き／嫌いです","note":"Preference or attitude"},{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"のが上手／下手／得意／苦手です","note":"Ability evaluation"},{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"のが好きじゃない／上手じゃない","note":"Negative"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"watch"},{"t":"のが好き","r":"のがすき","m":"like doing"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like watching movies.","scene":"Answering a question about preferences"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"泳ぐ","r":"およぐ","m":"swim"},{"t":"のが上手","r":"のがじょうず","m":"be good at"},{"t":"でした"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He used to be good at swimming.","scene":"Talking about the past"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-038||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vるのが好きです／上手です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのが好きです／上手です","patternReading":"の が すき です / じょうず です","coreMeaning":"Like / be good at doing something (の nominalizes the verb)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to say that you **like/hate doing something** or are **good/bad at something**. In daily conversation, it replaces “Nが好き” when the object is an action rather than a noun.","nuance":"Natural and conversational, usable for yourself or when asking others. 上手 is often used to praise others, 苦手 is commonly used modestly for your own weakness, and 得意 emphasizes confidence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どんな","m":"what kind of"},{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞く","r":"きく","m":"listen to"},{"t":"のが好き","r":"のがすき","m":"like doing"},{"t":"ですか"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What kind of music do you like listening to?","scene":"Friends asking about music preferences"},{"tokens":[{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作る","r":"つくる","m":"make/cook"},{"t":"のが苦手","r":"のがにがて","m":"be bad at"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m bad at cooking.","scene":"Admitting a personal weakness"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-038||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare nearby patterns","sub":"Vるのが好きです／上手です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのが好きです／上手です","patternReading":"の が すき です / じょうず です","coreMeaning":"Like / be good at doing something (の nominalizes the verb)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish **のが** from **のは** and **のを**. With 好き・上手... use **が** because these adjectives describe the object of evaluation. With **は**, the sentence gains a contrastive or comparative nuance.","nuance":"Learners often confuse が with を/は. が attaches directly to the “thing” being felt or evaluated; は makes that thing the topic; を marks the object of another transitive verb.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞く","r":"きく","m":"listen to"},{"t":"のが好き","r":"のがすき","m":"like doing"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like listening to music.","scene":"Introducing a simple preference"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるのは〜です","point":"は makes V the topic and often suggests contrast: 勉強するのは好きですが、運動するのは嫌いです. が directly marks the object of 好き: 勉強するのが好きです."},{"vs":"Vることが好きです","point":"Both の and こと can nominalize. の feels emotional and natural; こと is more formal, objective, and common in writing."},{"vs":"Nが好きです","point":"For a noun, use it directly: ピアノが好き. For an action, add の: ピアノを弾くのが好き."}]}},"n4-038||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vるのが好きです／上手です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのが好きです／上手です","patternReading":"の が すき です / じょうず です","coreMeaning":"Like / be good at doing something (の nominalizes the verb)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate “like + verb” directly, forgetting to nominalize with の, or use を instead of が. Check two points: is the form before の Vる, and is が present after の?","nuance":"The ます-before-の error comes from adding の to polite 食べます. The を-for-が error comes from structures like Vるのを忘れました, where の marks an object—but with 好き/上手, が is required.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お風呂","r":"おふろ","m":"bath"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入る","r":"はいる","m":"enter"},{"t":"のが好き","r":"のがすき","m":"like doing"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like taking baths.","scene":"Talking about a relaxing preference"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べますのが好きです。 → ✅ 食べるのが好きです。","❌ 歌うのを好きです。 → ✅ 歌うのが好きです。","❌ ピアノが弾くのが上手です。 → ✅ ピアノを弾くのが上手です。","❌ 料理を作る上手です。 → ✅ 料理を作るのが上手です。"],"compare":null}},"n4-038||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vるのが好きです／上手です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのが好きです／上手です","patternReading":"の が すき です / じょうず です","coreMeaning":"Like / be good at doing something (の nominalizes the verb)","slug":"memory","definition":"Mentally translate の as **“doing”**: 食べるの = doing/eating. Then 食べるのが好きです becomes “As for eating, I like it.” This makes の hard to forget.","nuance":"Imagine の as a box holding an action. が is the “pin” that attaches the box to the evaluative adjective at the end.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-038||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vるのが好きです／上手です · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのが好きです／上手です","patternReading":"の が すき です / じょうず です","coreMeaning":"Like / be good at doing something (の nominalizes the verb)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently in JLPT N4 and everyday conversation. Learning them as chunks helps you remember the correct particle and verb form.","nuance":"They all follow Vる + の + が + an evaluative word. Each phrase fits a familiar situation: eating, singing, studying, speaking, or cooking.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"watch"},{"t":"のが好き","r":"のがすき","m":"like doing"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On weekends, I like watching movies.","scene":"Asking about weekend plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-039||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています","patternReading":"のをわすれました／しっています","coreMeaning":"Forget / know the act of V (の turns a clause into an object)","slug":"core","definition":"〜の follows a verb in the **dictionary form** and turns the whole clause into an **abstract noun**: 'the act of ~'. With を, this phrase becomes the **object** of perception or cognition verbs such as 忘れる, 知っている, 見る, and 聞く. For example, 薬を飲むのを忘れました means 'I forgot **the act of taking medicine**.' Once you understand this, you can use the pattern naturally without translating 'the act' literally.","nuance":"This neutral pattern is common in daily conversation and is used more often than こと. の feels closer to a DIRECT EXPERIENCE, so it sounds natural before 忘れる/知っている: 'I forgot/knew this' rather than 'I forgot/knew a generalized fact.'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲む","r":"のむ","m":"take"},{"t":"のを忘れました","r":"のをわすれました","m":"forgot doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I forgot to take my medicine.","scene":"In a rush to work, you remember only after arriving."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"のを知っています","r":"のをしっています","m":"know that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I know that she is coming.","scene":"You mention that she is coming soon; I say I already know."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-039||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています","patternReading":"のをわすれました／しっています","coreMeaning":"Forget / know the act of V (の turns a clause into an object)","slug":"forms","definition":"The connection rule is fixed: **Vる + の + を + main verb**. The main verb carries tense or negation: 忘れました, 忘れません, 知っています, 知りません. If the inner clause has its own subject, use **N が**: 彼女が来るのを知っています. Never use Vます + の (飲みますの ❌).","nuance":"知る normally becomes 知っています when saying 'know' in the present. With 忘れる, use 忘れました for 'forgot'. The pattern also extends to 見る, 聞く, 手伝う, and やめる: 子供が転ぶのを見ました.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"のを忘れました","note":"Forgot to do V; V is an unfinished task."},{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"のを知っています","note":"Know the fact/activity V; often with a separate subject N が."},{"lhs":"N ＋ が ＋ Vる","rhs":"のを〜","note":"A nominalized clause with an explicit subject."},{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"のを見る／聞く／手伝う／やめる","note":"Perception/action verbs can also take の as their object."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"のを忘れました","r":"のをわすれました","m":"forgot doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I forgot to do my homework.","scene":"You suddenly remember at school that you did not do it."},{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"のを知っていますか","r":"のをしっていますか","m":"do you know?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know that there is a meeting?","scene":"Reminding a colleague about an announced meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-039||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています","patternReading":"のをわすれました／しっています","coreMeaning":"Forget / know the act of V (の turns a clause into an object)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when someone **forgets** an action they need to do, or **knows** about an event or activity that is happening or will happen. の turns the verb into a 'thing' that can be acted on. In daily conversation, it is useful for chores, forgotten appointments, and checking whether someone has the information.","nuance":"知っています expresses the state of 'knowing'; do not use 知ります in a present affirmative sentence. With 忘れる, the past 忘れました is common for something just forgotten. This pattern is somewhat more factual and less emotional than 〜てしまった (regret/accidentally).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"lights"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"消す","r":"けす","m":"turn off"},{"t":"のを忘れました","r":"のをわすれました","m":"forgot doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I forgot to turn off the lights.","scene":"You remember only after leaving home."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"のを知っていますか","r":"のをしっていますか","m":"do you know?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know that he is going to Japan?","scene":"Asking a colleague about a friend's plans."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"転ぶ","r":"ころぶ","m":"fall"},{"t":"のを見ました","r":"のをみました","m":"saw it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I saw a child fall.","scene":"You witness it while walking through a park."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-039||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast: の as an object","sub":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています","patternReading":"のをわすれました／しっています","coreMeaning":"Forget / know the act of V (の turns a clause into an object)","slug":"compare","definition":"Because の also appears in Vるのは and Vるのが, learners often confuse them. A quick test is to look at the **particle after の**: は makes it the topic, が marks the subject of an adjective, and を marks the verb's object.","nuance":"Vるのは makes a comment or evaluation; Vるのが expresses a preference or ability; Vるのを expresses forgetting, knowing, or perceiving an act. Choosing the wrong particle changes the meaning of the whole sentence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"のを忘れました","r":"のをわすれました","m":"forgot doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I forgot to eat breakfast.","scene":"At noon, you realize you have not eaten today."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるのは〜です","point":"Vるのは + は makes 'the act of V' the TOPIC for a comment: 朝ごはんを食べるのは大事です. By contrast, Vるのを + を makes it the OBJECT: 朝ごはんを食べるのを忘れました."},{"vs":"Vるのが好きです／上手です","point":"Vるのが expresses feelings or ability with が: 料理するのが好きです. Vるのを expresses cognition, forgetting, or knowing with を: 料理するのを忘れました."},{"vs":"Vたことがあります","point":"Vたことがある = HAVE THE EXPERIENCE of doing something in your life: 日本に行ったことがあります. Vるのを知っている = KNOW a fact: 彼が日本に行くのを知っていますか."}]}},"n4-039||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています","patternReading":"のをわすれました／しっています","coreMeaning":"Forget / know the act of V (の turns a clause into an object)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"English learners may omit の because English says 'I forgot to take medicine' without a nominalizer. In Japanese, **の** is needed to turn 'take medicine' into an object. Also, do not use 知ります for present affirmative 'know', and do not confuse Vる and Vた with 忘れる.","nuance":"In casual speech, を can sometimes be omitted, but の should almost never be dropped because without it the sentence structure breaks.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ゴミ","m":"trash"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出す","r":"だす","m":"take out"},{"t":"のを忘れました","r":"のをわすれました","m":"forgot doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I forgot to take out the trash.","scene":"You leave in the morning and find the trash bag still there at night."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 薬を飲みますのを忘れました。 → ✅ 薬を飲むのを忘れました。","❌ 彼女が来るのを知ります。 → ✅ 彼女が来るのを知っています。","❌ (To say 'forgot that I took the medicine') 薬を飲むのを忘れました。 → ✅ 薬を飲んだのを忘れました。","❌ 彼女来るのを知っています。 → ✅ 彼女が来るのを知っています。"],"compare":null}},"n4-039||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています","patternReading":"のをわすれました／しっています","coreMeaning":"Forget / know the act of V (の turns a clause into an object)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture の as a **clear box**: put the action 飲む into the box and it becomes 'the act of drinking'. You can then put that box into 忘れる or place it on the table of 知っている. を is needed because this box is the thing being affected.","nuance":"Vる refers to an action still ahead (not yet done), so it fits 忘れる and 知っている well. Changing it to Vた shifts the meaning to 'forgot that it had already been done'.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-039||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのを忘れました／知っています","patternReading":"のをわすれました／しっています","coreMeaning":"Forget / know the act of V (の turns a clause into an object)","slug":"phrases","definition":"The phrases below recur in JLPT questions and everyday life. Learn them in verb groups: 忘れる – forget, 知っている – know, 見る – see, やめる – quit/stop.","nuance":"With 手伝う, this pattern is very natural: 荷物を運ぶのを手伝う = help carry the luggage. In formal writing, の can sometimes be replaced by こと, but in N4 conversation, の is the safer choice.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"タバコ","m":"cigarettes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"吸う","r":"すう","m":"smoke"},{"t":"のをやめました","r":"のをやめました","m":"quit doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I quit smoking.","scene":"Telling a friend about your new habit after quitting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-040||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情)","patternReading":"て","coreMeaning":"Because ~ (cause → emotion / natural result)","slug":"core","definition":"V／Aて、〜 connects two clauses, with the **て-clause expressing the cause** of an **emotion** (うれしい、悲しい、困る…) or a **natural result** (遅れる、風邪を引く…). The second clause must describe something **unintentional**—something the speaker cannot control. Crying after hearing good news or feeling troubled after forgetting a wallet are natural reactions.","nuance":"This pattern is **conversational** and common in daily speech. It conveys the speaker's genuine reaction. Unlike 〜ので (an objective, polite reason), 〜て emphasizes the **natural, immediate** result. Do not use it with commands, requests, or invitations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"うれしい","r":"うれしい","m":"happy"},{"t":"ニュース","r":"にゅーす","m":"news"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"hear"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"泣いて","r":"ないて","m":"cry"},{"t":"しまいました","r":"しまいました","m":"ended up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I heard the good news and ended up crying.","scene":"Reading a winning-notice message"},{"tokens":[{"t":"道","r":"みち","m":"road"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"迷って","r":"まよって","m":"get lost"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遅れました","r":"おくれました","m":"be late"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I got lost and arrived late.","scene":"Finding a new company for the first time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-040||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情)","patternReading":"て","coreMeaning":"Because ~ (cause → emotion / natural result)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure has two clauses: the **first uses the て-form** (of a verb or い-adjective), and the **second is a result clause** that may be present, past, or polite. For verbs, use **Vて**; for い-adjectives, change **い → くて**. Examples: 嬉しい → 嬉しくて, 悲しい → 悲しくて. The second clause commonly uses emotion verbs such as 泣く、驚く、困る, or results such as 遅れる、間に合う.","nuance":"This is a neutral grammar form; politeness is determined by the second clause. If the second clause uses ました, the whole sentence is polite. There is no separate polite て-form. For negative い-adjectives, use **Aくなくて** (e.g. 時間がなくて、行けません). For negative verbs, use **Vなくて** (e.g. 来なくて、心配しました).","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"+ second clause (emotion / result)","note":"Example: 聞いて、泣きました。"},{"lhs":"Aい（−い）","rhs":"+ くて ＋ second clause","note":"Example: うれしくて、泣きました。"},{"lhs":"Aい（−い）","rhs":"+ くなくて ＋ second clause","note":"Negative; example: 時間がなくて、行けません。"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"→ Vなくて ＋ second clause","note":"Negative verb; example: 来なくて、心配しました。"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅れて","r":"おくれて","m":"be delayed"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遅刻しました","r":"ちこくしました","m":"be late"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The train was delayed, so I arrived late for work.","scene":"Explaining the late arrival to your boss"},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"なくて","r":"なくて","m":"not have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行けません","r":"いけません","m":"cannot go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't have time, so I can't go.","scene":"Turning down an invitation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-040||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情)","patternReading":"て","coreMeaning":"Because ~ (cause → emotion / natural result)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜て connects a cause with an emotion or state that arises **naturally and without intention**. Common situations include hearing good news → crying, forgetting a wallet → feeling troubled, rain falling → roads becoming slippery, or being teased by a coworker → getting angry. It is especially common with verbs such as 驚く、泣く、困る、安心する、喜ぶ.","nuance":"This pattern is often used when the speaker **explains why** they have a certain feeling or reaction. Do not use it for an **objective, polite reason** (use 〜ので). Also do not use it when the second clause is a **command**, **invitation**, or **suggestion**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多くて","r":"おおくて","m":"be numerous"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"疲れました","r":"つかれました","m":"be tired"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I had a lot of work, so I am tired.","scene":"Answering your mother after work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"プレゼント","r":"ぷれぜんと","m":"gift"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらって","r":"もらって","m":"receive"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"うれしかったです","r":"うれしかったです","m":"was happy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was very happy to receive a gift.","scene":"Receiving a birthday gift from a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-040||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情)","patternReading":"て","coreMeaning":"Because ~ (cause → emotion / natural result)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare causal 〜て with easily confused patterns. The key point is that **〜て directly connects a cause to a natural reaction**, while **〜ので** presents the reason politely and objectively; **Vてから** only shows time order, not causation; and **〜んです** explains or emphasizes a reason in response to a question.","nuance":"Learners often use 〜て for every type of reason, but it can sound unnatural when the second clause expresses intention. Remember: if the second clause is an active action (行く、買う), 〜て is not causal; it simply links actions (and). To express a cause, use 〜から or 〜ので.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忙しくて","r":"いそがしくて","m":"be busy"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"パーティー","r":"ぱーてぃー","m":"party"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行けませんでした","r":"いけませんでした","m":"could not go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was busy, so I couldn't go to the party.","scene":"Turning down a friend by phone"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ので","point":"〜て: personal emotion, natural result; 〜ので: objective, formal reason, usable with requests and commands. Example: 電車が遅れて、遅刻しました。(the train was late, so I was late) vs 電車が遅れたので、タクシーで行きました。(the train was late, so I chose to take a taxi)."},{"vs":"Vてから","point":"Vてから emphasizes that the first action finished before the second began; it does not express a cause-and-effect relationship. Example: ご飯を食べて、出かけました。(I ate, then went out)—not “I went out because I ate.”"},{"vs":"〜んです","point":"〜んです is used to answer “why?” or give a reason the listener may not know, often with strong emotion. 〜て simply links a cause and result without adding emphasis."}]}},"n4-040||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情)","patternReading":"て","coreMeaning":"Because ~ (cause → emotion / natural result)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes made by Vietnamese learners with this pattern. The biggest is using 〜て before a **command, intention, or invitation**, because 〜て requires an uncontrollable result. The second is using the wrong negative form for adjectives: use **Aくなくて**, not Aないで.","nuance":"English speakers may freely say “because…,” but Japanese distinguishes these uses clearly: 〜て is for a **natural reaction** without intention. Therefore, “Because I want to buy a ticket, I went to the station” cannot use 〜て, because “going to the station” is deliberate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"なくて","r":"なくて","m":"not have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"急ぎました","r":"いそぎました","m":"hurried"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I didn't have time, so I hurried.","scene":"Explaining to a friend why you hurried"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 病気になって、薬を飲んでください。 → ✅ 病気になったので、薬を飲んでください。(because you are sick, please take the medicine—a command requires ので)","❌ 暑くて、窓を開けましょう。 → ✅ 暑いですから、窓を開けましょう。(it is hot, so let's open the window—an invitation uses から)","❌ 時間がないで、急ぎました。 → ✅ 時間がなくて、急ぎました。(a negative adjective must use なくて, not ないで)"],"compare":null}},"n4-040||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情)","patternReading":"て","coreMeaning":"Because ~ (cause → emotion / natural result)","slug":"memory","definition":"A memory aid for using this pattern correctly. Picture an **arrow from cause → sudden reaction**. “て” is like a bridge, with an uncontrollable emotion pouring out on the other side. If the second clause is a **deliberate action**, stop and reconsider.","nuance":"Study tip: insert “because” before the て-clause and check whether the second clause is an **uncontrolled reaction** (crying, laughing, surprise, fatigue, irritation). If so, use 〜て; if it is an intentional action (going, buying, eating), think of ので or から.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-040||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aて、〜 (原因・感情)","patternReading":"て","coreMeaning":"Because ~ (cause → emotion / natural result)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Fixed phrases often found in textbooks and conversation. They help you remember the pattern quickly because they describe everyday situations.","nuance":"These phrases use 〜て with familiar verbs and adjectives: 聞いて、忘れて、降って、もらって. Practice reading them aloud to get used to the intonation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース","r":"にゅーす","m":"news"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"hear"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"驚きました","r":"おどろきました","m":"be surprised"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I heard the news and was surprised.","scene":"Hearing an unexpected news report by phone"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-041||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜ので · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜ので","patternReading":"ので","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (objective, polite reason)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ので is used to state an objective, polite reason. The speaker presents the cause as an obvious fact, then gives the resulting action or outcome. It is common when asking permission, explaining something to a superior, or writing formally. ので consists of の (nominalizer) + で (particle indicating a state), with the nuance 'with that reason'.","nuance":"Compared with 〜から, ので sounds gentler, more objective, and more polite. から tends to express subjective reasons or personal feelings, while ので presents a reason that seems understandable to anyone. Therefore, ので is often used when apologizing, asking permission, or speaking to a superior. In casual conversation, ので is often shortened to 〜んで.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"熱","r":"ねつ","m":"fever"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"ので","r":"ので","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"休みます","r":"やすみます","m":"take off"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because I have a fever, I will take today off.","scene":"Texting to request time off"},{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降っている","r":"ふっている","m":"is falling"},{"t":"ので","r":"ので","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持ってきました","r":"もってきました","m":"brought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it is raining, I brought an umbrella.","scene":"Talking to a classmate in the rain"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-041||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜ので · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜ので","patternReading":"ので","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (objective, polite reason)","slug":"forms","definition":"〜ので attaches to the plain form of verbs, adjectives, and nouns. With な-adjectives and nouns, add な before ので. The sentence can be in the present, past, or negative form, and です/ます may be added for extra politeness.","nuance":"In polite conversation, ですので and ますので may be used. Important: never use だ before ので (学生だので → incorrect). With nouns and な-adjectives, な is required: 学生なので, 静かなので.","structure":[{"lhs":"V","rhs":"普通形 + ので","note":"Example: ある + ので"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"普通形 + ので","note":"Example: 安い + ので"},{"lhs":"Aな / N","rhs":"＋な + ので","note":"Example: 静かなので、学生なので"},{"lhs":"Polite","rhs":"です/ます + ので","note":"Example: ありますので、学生ですので"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"静かな","r":"しずかな","m":"quiet"},{"t":"ので","r":"ので","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"しやすい","r":"しやすい","m":"easy"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it is quiet, studying here is easy.","scene":"Explaining why you chose this café"},{"tokens":[{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"ので","r":"ので","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"もっと","r":"もっと","m":"more"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"しなければなりません","r":"しなければなりません","m":"must"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because I am a student, I have to study more.","scene":"Talking with your parents about studying"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-041||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜ので · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜ので","patternReading":"ので","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (objective, polite reason)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜ので presents a reason before a result. The first clause gives an objective cause, and the second gives the speaker's action or request. This pattern is very common in daily communication, especially when you need to explain something gently without sounding forceful.","nuance":"When asking permission or refusing tactfully, ので is a safe choice. For example: すみません、体調が悪いので、早退させてください。 The second clause often includes 〜てください, 〜たいです, 〜ましょう, or verbs expressing intention. In speech, ので can be shortened to 〜んで.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"風邪","r":"かぜ","m":"a cold"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ひいた","r":"ひいた","m":"caught"},{"t":"ので","r":"ので","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"バイト","r":"バイト","m":"part-time job"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"休みました","r":"やすみました","m":"took off"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because I caught a cold, I took time off from my part-time job.","scene":"Reporting absence to a café manager"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅れた","r":"おくれた","m":"was late"},{"t":"ので","r":"ので","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"be late"},{"t":"しました","r":"しました","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because the train was delayed, I was late.","scene":"Apologizing to your boss"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-041||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing から・て・のに","sub":"〜ので · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜ので","patternReading":"ので","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (objective, polite reason)","slug":"compare","definition":"〜ので is often confused with 〜から (also 'because'), 〜て (which indicates a cause), and 〜のに (which has an opposite meaning). This branch helps distinguish these three commonly confused patterns. Key points: (1) ので vs から: ので is objective and polite; から is subjective and casual. (2) ので vs て: て describes a natural cause, usually followed by a spontaneous result; ので can be followed by an intentional action or request. (3) ので vs のに: ので expresses a natural reason-to-result relationship; のに expresses surprise or disappointment. Each pair has an example in the comparison section.","nuance":"All three can give reasons, but they differ in objectivity and nuance. Remember: ので is polite and suitable for requests; から is stronger and common in casual speech; て focuses on describing events; のに means contrast. Understanding these core differences helps prevent context errors.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"do not have"},{"t":"ので","r":"ので","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"急ぎましょう","r":"いそぎましょう","m":"let's hurry"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because we do not have time, let's hurry.","scene":"Urging coworkers before a deadline"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜から","point":"〜から expresses a SUBJECTIVE, emotional reason and often accompanies commands or invitations (〜から、〜て). 〜ので gives an OBJECTIVE, polite reason, often for requests or explanations. Pair: 雨が降るから、出かけない。 (personal decision) / 雨が降るので、出かけません。 (objective explanation)."},{"vs":"〜て","point":"〜て indicates a NATURAL CAUSE (the second clause is a spontaneous result without intention); 〜ので gives a LOGICAL REASON, so the second clause may be a request or intentional action. Pair: 風邪をひいて、休みました。 (reporting an event) / 風邪をひいたので、休ませてください。 (asking permission)."},{"vs":"〜のに","point":"〜のに means CONTRAST ('although...but'), while 〜ので expresses a NATURAL relationship ('because...so'). Pair: 熱があるのに、出かけました。 (went out despite having a fever) / 熱があるので、出かけません。 (did not go out because of a fever)."}]}},"n4-041||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜ので · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜ので","patternReading":"ので","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (objective, polite reason)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners commonly make three mistakes with 〜ので: (1) adding だ before ので, (2) omitting な before nouns and な-adjectives, and (3) confusing it with 〜のに. This branch lists each mistake with incorrect and correct sentences for self-checking. Remember: だ never comes before ので, and ので expresses a natural reason-to-result relationship, not contrast.","nuance":"These mistakes often come from applying the だ+から rule or confusing ので and のに, which differ by only one character. Confusing のに is especially serious because the meanings are opposite. Build the reflex: after hearing ので, expect a natural result; after のに, expect surprise or contrast.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"do not have"},{"t":"ので","r":"ので","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"急ぎましょう","r":"いそぎましょう","m":"let's hurry"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because we do not have time, let's hurry.","scene":"Reminding each other to hurry"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 学生だので、勉強します。 → ✅ 学生なので、勉強します。","❌ 静かだので、好きです。 → ✅ 静かなので、好きです。","❌ 熱があるのに、休みます。 → ✅ 熱があるので、休みます。 (のに means 'although')"],"compare":null}},"n4-041||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜ので · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜ので","patternReading":"ので","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (objective, polite reason)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember 〜ので, split it into の + で: の nominalizes the preceding clause, while で means 'with' or 'by'. Together: 'with the reason that…', suggesting an objective reason. Also remember to add な before ので after a noun or な-adjective: ので behaves like a noun, so it needs な just like 静かな人. Quick comparison: から is easier and more casual; ので is more polite.","nuance":"Think of ので = の + で: の 'packs' the reason into a noun, and で means 'with' → 'with the reason that...'. This makes the reason sound objective and reliable. Also, nouns and な-adjectives need な before ので, just as before a noun: 静かな人.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-041||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜ので · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜ので","patternReading":"ので","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (objective, polite reason)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common linking phrases and situations with ので that appear in JLPT questions and everyday life.","nuance":"These phrases often appear when apologizing, asking permission, or announcing a reason.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"用事","r":"ようじ","m":"errand"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"ので","r":"ので","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"先に","r":"さきに","m":"first"},{"t":"失礼します","r":"しつれいします","m":"excuse myself"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because I have an errand, I will leave first.","scene":"Leaving work early for an urgent errand"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-042||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"Nのために / Vるために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nのために / Vるために","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (cause) / In order to ~ (purpose)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ために has two meanings. **(1) Cause/reason** – the preceding clause is a noun describing a negative or neutral event (事故、病気、台風...), used in writing, news, and notices. **(2) Purpose** – the preceding clause is a noun showing a benefit/goal or a dictionary-form verb, describing an intentional action toward a goal. Context determines the meaning.","nuance":"For purpose, ために sounds **formal, clear, and determined**. Compared with ように, ために emphasizes the speaker's active will. For cause, ために sounds bookish and objective; in everyday conversation, Japanese people usually use ので or から.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"合格する","r":"ごうかくする","m":"pass"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"in order to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"います","m":"am doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"To pass the exam, I study every day.","scene":"Answering “Why work so hard?”"},{"tokens":[{"t":"台風","r":"たいふう","m":"typhoon"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"止まりました","r":"とまりました","m":"stopped"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the typhoon, the train stopped.","scene":"Reading a station notice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-042||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"Nのために / Vるために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nのために / Vるために","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (cause) / In order to ~ (purpose)","slug":"forms","definition":"ために attaches to a **noun** or a **dictionary-form verb**. With a noun: N + のために + clause. With a verb: Vる + ために + clause. **Do not use Vます + ために**. To express negative purpose “so as not to...”, use Vないように rather than Vないために, which is rare and unnatural.","nuance":"With Vるために, the **subjects of the two clauses are usually the same**. If they differ, the sentence may sound wrong; switch to ように. With nouns such as 家族のために and 国のために, it means “for the benefit of...”","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"のために＋文","note":"If N is a negative event → cause; if N is a goal/benefit → purpose."},{"lhs":"V辞書形","rhs":"ために＋文","note":"The verb is in dictionary form; the main action is done to achieve the goal."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"働いて","r":"はたらいて","m":"work"},{"t":"います","m":"am doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For my family, I am working.","scene":"Explaining why I work away from home"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強する","r":"べんきょうする","m":"study"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"in order to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"ラジオ","m":"radio"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"listen"},{"t":"います","m":"am doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"To study Japanese, I listen to the radio every day.","scene":"Sharing a self-study method"},{"tokens":[{"t":"事故","r":"じこ","m":"accident"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"道","r":"みち","m":"road"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"混んで","r":"こんで","m":"congested"},{"t":"います","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of an accident, the road is congested.","scene":"Hearing a traffic report"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-042||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Nのために / Vるために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nのために / Vるために","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (cause) / In order to ~ (purpose)","slug":"usage","definition":"To choose the right meaning, look at **the clause before ために**. If it is **Vる**, it means purpose. If it is **N**: a negative event (事故、病気、大雨) indicates cause; a goal or beneficiary (家族、健康、将来) indicates purpose. Purpose ために requires a volitional action, while cause ために commonly appears in formal writing.","nuance":"For cause, ために sounds **objective and formal** and often appears in news: 事故のために、電車が遅れています. For purpose, ために works in speech and writing, though casual conversation often uses shorter expressions. When emphasizing “the purpose is to...”, ために remains natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"走って","r":"はしって","m":"run"},{"t":"います","m":"am doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For my health, I run every morning.","scene":"Describing an exercise habit"},{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"because of"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancellation"},{"t":"になった","m":"was canceled"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because of the rain, the match was canceled.","scene":"Reading sports news"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-042||compare":{"title":"⚖️ ために vs ように / ので","sub":"Nのために / Vるために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nのために / Vるために","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (cause) / In order to ~ (purpose)","slug":"compare","definition":"Quickly distinguish two patterns learners often confuse: **Vるために** and **Vるように**. Both can express purpose, but ために is used when **the subject actively performs an action** to reach the goal; ように is used when the goal is a **state, ability, or another person's action**. For cause, ために is formal and written, while ので is natural in conversation.","nuance":"ために emphasizes **will** and direct action. ように emphasizes **aiming for a state** or a goal beyond direct control. Examples: 日本語を勉強するために、日本へ行く (to study Japanese, I go to Japan) — correct. 日本語が話せるように、日本へ行く (to be able to speak Japanese, I go to Japan) — correct. Avoid 日本語を学ぶように.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"買う","r":"かう","m":"buy"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"in order to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貯めて","r":"ためて","m":"save"},{"t":"います","m":"am doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"To buy something, I am saving money.","scene":"Saving up to buy a car"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるように (mục đích)","point":"ために: the subject acts to reach a goal, with strong intent. ように: aims at a state/ability and does not require direct action. Examples: 合格するために勉強する/合格できるように勉強する."},{"vs":"〜ので (lý do)","point":"ために (cause) is written and objective; ので is conversational and polite. Examples: 事故のために電車が止まった (news) / 事故があったので、電車が止まりました (conversation)."}]}},"n4-042||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nのために / Vるために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nのために / Vるために","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (cause) / In order to ~ (purpose)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make **four main mistakes**: (1) using Vます + ために instead of Vる + ために; (2) using Vないために to say “so as not to,” when natural Japanese uses Vないように; (3) using ために for a positive cause such as “thanks to you”—incorrect; (4) using Vるために when the two clauses have different subjects.","nuance":"Mistake (3) is especially important: ために marks a cause leading to a negative or neutral result, not a good result. To say “thanks to ~,” use おかげで.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忘れない","r":"わすれない","m":"not forget"},{"t":"ように","r":"ように","m":"so as to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"メモ","m":"notes"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"おきます","m":"do in advance"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"So I do not forget, I make a note in advance.","scene":"Explaining a note-taking habit"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 日本語を勉強しますために、日本へ行きます。 → ✅ 日本語を勉強するために、日本へ行きます。","❌ 忘れないために、メモします。 → ✅ 忘れないように、メモします。","❌ あなたのために、元気になりました。 → ✅ あなたのおかげで、元気になりました。","❌ 彼が合格するために、私は協力した。 → ✅ 彼が合格できるように、私は協力した。"],"compare":null}},"n4-042||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nのために / Vるために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nのために / Vるために","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (cause) / In order to ~ (purpose)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **ため** is like “for” in “for a purpose.” When Vる comes before it, picture an **arrow**: action → goal. When N names an event, picture a **dark shadow**: event → unwanted consequence. If the result is positive and the preceding clause is Vる, it is definitely purpose.","nuance":"This helps you answer reading questions quickly: underline the clause before ために. Vる? → purpose. Negative N? → cause. Goal/beneficiary N? → for someone's benefit.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-042||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nのために / Vるために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nのために / Vるために","patternReading":"ために","coreMeaning":"Because of ~ (cause) / In order to ~ (purpose)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in textbooks and exams: **家族のために** (for my family), **健康のために** (for my health), **将来のために** (for the future), **試験に合格するために** (to pass the exam), and **夢を叶えるために** (to fulfill a dream). In writing, ために is sometimes shortened to **〜のため、〜** (for example, 大雨のため、中止します).","nuance":"In official notices, に is often omitted: “〜のため、〜”. This shortened form is common on signs and in work emails.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"将来","r":"しょうらい","m":"future"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","r":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"貯金","r":"ちょきん","m":"save money"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"います","m":"am doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"For the future, I am saving money.","scene":"Talking about financial plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-043||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"疑問詞＋か〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋か〜","patternReading":"ぎもんし か","coreMeaning":"Indirect questions using question words (who/where/when)","slug":"core","definition":"疑問詞＋か expresses **a question word embedded in another sentence** as a subordinate clause. The speaker does not ask directly, but presents the information needed: who, what, where, when, why, and so on. **か** acts like a “container” for the question, making the sentence sound natural and softer.","nuance":"This pattern is **neutral**, and works in both speech and writing. Embedding the question makes it more polite and less abrupt than a direct question such as `どこですか？`. The strength of the need for information comes from the main clause: `知りたいです`, `知っていますか`, or `教えてください`.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"置いた","r":"おいた","m":"put"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"覚えて","r":"おぼえて","m":"remember"},{"t":"いません","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't remember where I put it.","scene":"Looking for a wallet but forgetting where it was placed"},{"tokens":[{"t":"何時","r":"なんじ","m":"what time"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"始まる","r":"はじまる","m":"start"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"know"},{"t":"います"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know what time it starts?","scene":"Asking a classmate when class starts"},{"tokens":[{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"tell"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please tell me who will come.","scene":"Asking a colleague to report which guests will arrive"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-043||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"疑問詞＋か〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋か〜","patternReading":"ぎもんし か","coreMeaning":"Indirect questions using question words (who/where/when)","slug":"forms","definition":"Use the **plain form (普通形)** before か, not the polite form. With nouns and な-adjectives, usually **drop だ** before か: `何が好きか`, not `好きだか`. You can ask about the subject, object, place, time, method, or reason.","nuance":"In casual conversation, people also use `〜か、知らない` (don't know), or `〜か、聞いてみる` (try asking). When the main verb expresses perception or knowledge (`知る・わかる・覚える・忘れる`), the 疑問詞＋か clause functions as the **object** of the sentence.","structure":[{"lhs":"疑問詞（どこ・いつ・何…）","rhs":"Vる / Vた / Vない ＋ か","note":"Conjugate the verb in plain form; do NOT use ます."},{"lhs":"なぜ・どうして","rhs":"V普通形 ＋ か","note":"Use when asking for a reason, followed by 教えて・説明して."},{"lhs":"N / ナA","rhs":"か（bỏ だ）","note":"Example: 誰が先生か 知っていますか."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"決めて","r":"きめて","m":"decide"},{"t":"いません","m":"not yet"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I haven't decided where to eat.","scene":"During lunch break, coworkers ask what to eat"},{"tokens":[{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"tell"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please tell me when you will come.","scene":"Texting a friend about pickup time"},{"tokens":[{"t":"なぜ","m":"why"},{"t":"遅れた","r":"おくれた","m":"was late"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"説明して","r":"せつめいして","m":"explain"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please explain why you were late.","scene":"A boss asks an employee to explain the delay"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-043||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"疑問詞＋か〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋か〜","patternReading":"ぎもんし か","coreMeaning":"Indirect questions using question words (who/where/when)","slug":"usage","definition":"The first clause provides the **information gap**; the second clause shows what the speaker wants to do with that information. Common situations include asking for directions or times, requesting an explanation, and confirming a schedule.","nuance":"Japanese speakers use 疑問詞＋か to avoid making a question too blunt. Compared with `どこで食べますか?`, `どこで食べるか、決めていますか?` sounds softer and focuses on whether a decision has been made. The main clause may be `知っていますか`, `わかりますか`, `教えてください`, or `確認してください`…","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"show"},{"t":"もらえます","m":"can you"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you show me where the station is?","scene":"Asking a passerby for directions"},{"tokens":[{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"package"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"届く","r":"とどく","m":"be delivered"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"know"},{"t":"います"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know when the package will be delivered?","scene":"Asking the coworker handling delivery"},{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"何時","r":"なんじ","m":"what time"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"終わる","r":"おわる","m":"end"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"わかります","m":"know"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know what time the meeting ends?","scene":"Asking when a long meeting will finish"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-043||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish かどうか・んですか","sub":"疑問詞＋か〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋か〜","patternReading":"ぎもんし か","coreMeaning":"Indirect questions using question words (who/where/when)","slug":"compare","definition":"The pattern most easily confused with this one is **〜かどうか**. If a sentence has `どこ・いつ・だれ・なに・なぜ`, use 疑問詞＋か; if it has no question word and asks only “whether or not,” use V/Aかどうか. `んですか` is a direct question seeking a reason, often with emotion, rather than an embedded question.","nuance":"Learners often translate both patterns as “whether…or not,” which leads to mistakes. Check the beginning: **with a question word, use 疑問詞＋か**; **without one, use かどうか**. `んですか` often conveys surprise or uncertainty.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何時","r":"なんじ","m":"what time"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知りたい","r":"しりたい","m":"want to know"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to know what time you will come.","scene":"Discussing pickup plans with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V/A かどうか","point":"かどうか is used for YES/NO questions. If there is a 疑問詞 (どこ・いつ・だれ…), use 疑問詞＋か. Example: いつ行くか (when to go) ≠ 行くかどうか (whether to go)."},{"vs":"〜んですか","point":"〜んですか is a DIRECT question seeking a reason and adding emotion. 疑問詞＋か is an EMBEDDED question requesting objective information. Compare: どうして来なかったんですか (why didn't you come?) vs どうして来なかったか、教えてください (please tell me why you didn't come)."}]}},"n4-043||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"疑問詞＋か〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋か〜","patternReading":"ぎもんし か","coreMeaning":"Indirect questions using question words (who/where/when)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often write `どこですか、知っていますか` because they are used to direct questions. In an embedded clause, **always use the plain form** and place か only after the verb or adjective. Also, do not add かどうか when a question word is already present.","nuance":"These mistakes make the sentence sound unnatural in Japanese. Remember: the clause before か never uses ます/です; if かどうか is present, remove it when a 疑問詞 is already used.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ある","m":"be"},{"t":"か","m":"embedded question"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"show"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please show me where it is.","scene":"Asking a friend where the phone is"}],"pitfalls":["❌ どこですか、知っていますか。 → ✅ どこか、知っていますか。 (drop です because embedded clauses use plain form)","❌ いつ行くかどうか、教えてください。 → ✅ いつ行くか、教えてください。 (いつ is already present, so do not add かどうか)","❌ 何を食べますか、決めていない。 → ✅ 何を食べるか、決めていない。 (use plain form before か)"],"compare":null}},"n4-043||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"疑問詞＋か〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"疑問詞＋か〜","patternReading":"ぎもんし か","coreMeaning":"Indirect questions using question words (who/where/when)","slug":"memory","definition":"Think of **か** as a small box. A direct question such as `どこですか?` is like a ball thrown at the listener. To embed it in a sentence, put the ball in the box: `どこか、知っていますか?`.","nuance":"The か box always works with the **疑問詞** key (who, where, when, why, and so on). The main clause is the person receiving the box: `知っていますか`, `わかりますか`, or `教えてください`. Once the box is there, no question mark is needed.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-044||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V／Aかどうか〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aかどうか〜","patternReading":"かどうか","coreMeaning":"Whether ~ or not (indirect yes/no question)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern 〜かどうか **embeds a YES/NO question inside a larger sentence**. The speaker presents two possibilities (whether ~ / whether not ~) and attaches them to verbs such as know, worry, ask, or decide. Unlike a direct question (〜か？), this pattern is **indirect** and does not end with a question mark.","nuance":"This is a neutral pattern used widely in speech and writing. It conveys **uncertainty or indecision**; it is not as strong as はず or に違いない. It often goes with mental-activity verbs such as 知る, 分かる, 思う, 聞く, 確認する, 決める, and 心配する. The answer narrows down to **one of two options**, which need not be stated explicitly. Politeness comes from the main verb (for example, 分かりません instead of 分からない).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"分かりません","r":"わかりません","m":"do not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't know whether it will rain tomorrow.","scene":"Seeing dark clouds and wondering whether to bring an umbrella"},{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"合格できる","r":"ごうかくできる","m":"can pass"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"心配","r":"しんぱい","m":"worry"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'm worried about whether I will pass the exam.","scene":"Talking to a friend after finishing the exam"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"confirm"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"みます","m":"try"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'll try to confirm whether he is coming.","scene":"Finalizing the number of attendees"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-044||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"V／Aかどうか〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aかどうか〜","patternReading":"かどうか","coreMeaning":"Whether ~ or not (indirect yes/no question)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch shows **connection forms**. かどうか attaches directly to the **plain form** of verbs and adjectives. Important: with **nouns and な-adjectives**, **do not add だ** before かどうか.","nuance":"Basically, かどうか does not change with tense or aspect; time is shown in the preceding clause (Vた) or in the main sentence. For the past, simply conjugate the verb to the た-form and add かどうか. Politeness goes in the main sentence, not in かどうか.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"かどうか","note":"Example: 行くかどうか"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"かどうか","note":"Example: 行かないかどうか"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"かどうか","note":"Example: 高いかどうか"},{"lhs":"Aな／N","rhs":"かどうか","note":"Drop だ; example: 便利かどうか"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"かどうか","note":"Past: 行ったかどうか"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"分かりません","r":"わかりません","m":"do not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't know whether this restaurant is good.","scene":"Hesitating outside an unfamiliar restaurant"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知りません","r":"しりません","m":"do not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't know whether this place is convenient.","scene":"Looking for a new place to live and asking a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知っていますか","r":"しっていますか","m":"do you know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know whether he is a student?","scene":"Asking about someone you just met"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-044||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"V／Aかどうか〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aかどうか〜","patternReading":"かどうか","coreMeaning":"Whether ~ or not (indirect yes/no question)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern appears when the speaker needs to express **uncertainty** about a YES/NO question or **pass that question on** to someone else. Common accompanying verbs include 分かる, 知っている, 聞く, 確認する, 決める, and 心配する.","nuance":"In casual speech, the following part may be omitted and かどうか may be used like a noun. At N4 level, however, always place it before the main verb. A more formal pattern is かどうか ＋ によって／次第で, but you do not need to learn it yet.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"天気予報","r":"てんきよほう","m":"weather forecast"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"調べます","r":"しらべます","m":"check"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'll check the weather forecast to see whether it will rain tomorrow.","scene":"Planning a weekend picnic"},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"ask"},{"t":"みます","m":"try"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'll call and ask whether they have time.","scene":"Wanting to invite a friend to eat but fearing they are busy"},{"tokens":[{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"package"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"届く","r":"とどく","m":"arrive"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"分かりません","r":"わかりません","m":"do not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I still don't know whether the package will arrive.","scene":"Waiting for an online delivery and talking to a coworker"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-044||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with similar patterns","sub":"V／Aかどうか〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aかどうか〜","patternReading":"かどうか","coreMeaning":"Whether ~ or not (indirect yes/no question)","slug":"compare","definition":"This section compares かどうか with easily confused patterns such as **疑問詞＋か** and direct **か**. The goal is to help you choose the right structure when embedding a yes/no question. Distinguish the contexts clearly to avoid mistakes on tests and in conversation.","nuance":"The key point: if the embedded question has a **question word** (誰, 何, どこ...), use 疑問詞＋か. If it has only **YES / NO**, use かどうか. In speech, かどうか can be shortened to か, but the shorter form does not stress both options as clearly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"知っていますか","r":"しっていますか","m":"do you know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know whether he is coming?","scene":"Asking a coworker about an attendee"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"疑問詞＋か","point":"With a question word (who/what/where) → use 疑問詞＋か, not かどうか. Example: 誰が来るか知っていますか (Do you know who is coming?) versus 彼が来るかどうか知っていますか (Do you know whether he is coming?)."},{"vs":"〜か","point":"か at the end of a sentence creates a direct question; かどうか appears only in a subordinate clause, not as a direct question."}]}},"n4-044||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V／Aかどうか〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aかどうか〜","patternReading":"かどうか","coreMeaning":"Whether ~ or not (indirect yes/no question)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are the most common mistakes with かどうか, such as adding だ after a noun, using かどうか as a direct question, or confusing it with questions containing a question word. These errors often appear in sentence-building exercises, so memorize the distinctions.","nuance":"Most errors come from adding 'whether' at the end or placing かどうか incorrectly. When a question word is present, you must use 疑問詞＋か, never かどうか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"かどうか","r":"かどうか","m":"whether or not"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"confirm"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please confirm whether he is coming.","scene":"Asking an assistant to check the guest list"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼が学生だかどうか知りません。 → ✅ 彼が学生かどうか知りません。","❌ 誰が来るかどうか知っていますか。 → ✅ 誰が来るか知っていますか。","❌ 彼が来るかどうですか。 → ✅ 彼が来るかどうか知っていますか。"],"compare":null}},"n4-044||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V／Aかどうか〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aかどうか〜","patternReading":"かどうか","coreMeaning":"Whether ~ or not (indirect yes/no question)","slug":"memory","definition":"A memory aid: か (question mark) + どう (how) → wondering 'whether or not'.","nuance":"Picture a two-pan scale: YES and NO. かどうか places the question on the scale to see which side it tips toward.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-045||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vてみます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてみます","patternReading":"てみます","coreMeaning":"Try doing ~ (to see what happens)","slug":"core","definition":"〜てみる describes trying an action to see its result or experience. It is formed with the verb's te-form + the auxiliary verb みる (originally 見る, “to look”). **The speaker is unsure of the result** and simply wants to see what happens.","nuance":"This pattern is common, natural, and conversational. It has a **gentle, curious** nuance and does not stress effort like 挑戦する. It can be used in both speech and writing, but is especially common in conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"初めて","r":"はじめて","m":"for the first time"},{"t":"日本料理","r":"にほんりょうり","m":"Japanese food"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べてみました","r":"たべてみました","m":"tried eating"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I tried Japanese food for the first time.","scene":"Trying sushi for the first time in Japan"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"アプリ","m":"app"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使ってみてください","r":"つかってみてください","m":"please try using"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please try using this app.","scene":"Introducing a new app to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-045||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vてみます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてみます","patternReading":"てみます","coreMeaning":"Try doing ~ (to see what happens)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is always **Vて + みる**. The auxiliary verb みる conjugates like a Group II verb: みます, みました, みない, みてください, and so on.","nuance":"Only the main verb takes the te-form; never use the dictionary form before みる. As an auxiliary verb, みる does not carry its independent meaning.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"みる","note":"base form"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"みます","note":"polite present"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"みました","note":"polite past"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"みない","note":"present negative"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"みてください","note":"request to try"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"食べてみます","r":"たべてみます","m":"will try eating"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ll try a little, okay?","scene":"A new dish is on the table"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いてみました","r":"かいてみました","m":"tried writing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I tried writing a letter in Japanese.","scene":"Testing my Japanese ability"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-045||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vてみます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてみます","patternReading":"てみます","coreMeaning":"Try doing ~ (to see what happens)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜てみる has four main uses: (1) trying something with little or no prior experience; (2) experiencing it to see what it is like; (3) inviting or suggesting that someone try with 〜てみてください; and (4) talking about something you tried in the past and its result.","nuance":"Do not use 〜てみる for routine actions with no experimental aspect. For invitations, 〜てみてください is gentler than 〜てください; 〜てください is a more direct request or command.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食べてみて","r":"たべてみて","m":"try eating it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This cake is delicious, so try some.","scene":"Inviting a friend to try cake"},{"tokens":[{"t":"一人で","r":"ひとりで","m":"alone"},{"t":"やってみます","r":"やってみます","m":"will try doing it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ll try doing it on my own.","scene":"Trying a new task independently"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-045||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Vてみます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてみます","patternReading":"てみます","coreMeaning":"Try doing ~ (to see what happens)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 〜てみる from two commonly confused patterns: **可能形** (ability) and **〜てしまう** (completion or regret).","nuance":"All three combine with verbs, but their focus differs: てみる emphasizes trying, 可能形 emphasizes ability, and てしまう emphasizes completion or its result.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"けど"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買ってみました","r":"かってみました","m":"tried buying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was a little expensive, but I tried buying it.","scene":"Trying a new product"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V可能形","point":"可能形 expresses ability or enabling conditions; てみる expresses trying an action. Example: 日本語で話せます (I can speak Japanese) vs 日本語で話してみます (I’ll try speaking Japanese)."},{"vs":"Vてしまいます","point":"てしまう emphasizes that an action is completed, often with regret or relief; てみる only describes the attempt. Example: 薬を飲んでしまった (I ended up taking the medicine) vs 薬を飲んでみた (I tried taking the medicine)."}]}},"n4-045||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vてみます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてみます","patternReading":"てみます","coreMeaning":"Try doing ~ (to see what happens)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using てみる for routine actions, forming the te-form incorrectly, or confusing a direct request with an invitation to try.","nuance":"Always ask yourself: is the action meant to “see what happens”? If it is simply an obvious routine action, do not add てみる.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"レストラン","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行ってみました","r":"いってみました","m":"tried going"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I tried going to a new restaurant.","scene":"Trying a new restaurant for a change"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 映画を見ってみました。（The te-form of 見る is 見て, not 見っ） → ✅ 映画を見てみました。","❌ 勉強しってみます。（The te-form of する is して, not しって） → ✅ 勉強してみます。","❌ 薬を飲みてください。（Wrong te-form for 飲む; when suggesting someone try something, use てみてください） → ✅ 薬を飲んでみてください。"],"compare":null}},"n4-045||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vてみます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてみます","patternReading":"てみます","coreMeaning":"Try doing ~ (to see what happens)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: みる originally means “to look.” **Try doing** = do it, then **look** at what happens. Imagine pressing a test button on a machine and seeing the result appear on the screen.","nuance":"The mnemonic “Try it and see!” helps you remember quickly. When you see a verb + てみる, think of testing something to see how it goes.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-046||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vてあげます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあげます","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone — the speaker is the giver","slug":"core","definition":"〜てあげます describes **the speaker (or someone in the speaker's group) doing something for another person's benefit**. Put the verb in te-form, then add あげる (to give). Example: 友達の荷物を持ってあげました = I carried my friend's luggage for them. This is one of the three basic giving-and-receiving patterns: あげる / くれる / もらう.","nuance":"It emphasizes **the direction from giver to recipient**. Since it presents the giver's viewpoint, it can sound like 'doing someone a favor'; therefore **avoid using it with superiors** and use it only when the action is genuinely useful and wanted. Register: common in daily conversation; in writing it is also possible, but the more formal form is さしあげる.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"carry"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"did for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I carried my friend's luggage for them.","scene":"You help your friend carry a heavy load."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読んで","r":"よんで","m":"read"},{"t":"あげます","r":"あげます","m":"do for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I read a book to the child.","scene":"A mother reads a story to her child before bed."},{"tokens":[{"t":"道","r":"みち","m":"road"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"迷って","r":"まよって","m":"get lost"},{"t":"いる","r":"いる","m":"be -ing"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"地図","r":"ちず","m":"map"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見せて","r":"みせて","m":"show"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"did for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I showed the map to someone who was lost.","scene":"You help a lost stranger find the way."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-046||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vてあげます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあげます","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone — the speaker is the giver","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **Vて + あげる**; あげる is an auxiliary verb and behaves like a Group 2 (ichidan) verb, so it can be conjugated into all tenses and moods. The verb before it is always in **te-form**; te-form formation follows sound-change rules that must be learned.","nuance":"In casual conversation, あげる may appear in short forms such as あげる, あげた, and あげない; at work, use あげます or あげました. For an even more polite offer of help, use あげましょうか—it sounds natural and courteous.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"あげる／あげます","note":"Present / polite"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"あげた／あげました","note":"Past"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"あげない／あげません","note":"Negative"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"あげましょうか","note":"Offer to do it"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"carry"},{"t":"あげましょうか","r":"あげましょうか","m":"shall I help?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall I carry your luggage for you?","scene":"You offer to help carry someone's things."},{"tokens":[{"t":"弟","r":"おとうと","m":"younger brother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"did for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I helped my younger brother with his homework.","scene":"You describe helping your brother yesterday."},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"not have"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作って","r":"つくって","m":"make"},{"t":"あげません","r":"あげません","m":"will not do for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't have time, so I won't make lunch for you.","scene":"You decline a friend's request to cook."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-046||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vてあげます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあげます","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone — the speaker is the giver","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when **the speaker proactively does a specific thing for the listener or a third person**. Typical situations include helping friends carry things, teaching, cooking, reading stories, and giving directions. The **〜ましょうか** form is also a very common polite offer of help.","nuance":"It works well with friends, family, and close acquaintances. **Avoid it with superiors, teachers, and older people** because てあげる can imply 'doing them a favor.' If the recipient does not consider the action helpful (for example, taking something they are already holding), the sentence can sound condescending or irritating.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"妹","r":"いもうと","m":"younger sister"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studies"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"did for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I helped my younger sister study.","scene":"You tell a friend you helped your sister study."},{"tokens":[{"t":"おばあさん","r":"おばあさん","m":"elderly woman"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"carry"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"did for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I carried the elderly woman's luggage for her.","scene":"You help an elderly woman with heavy luggage."},{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","r":"こーひー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"入れて","r":"いれて","m":"make"},{"t":"あげましょうか","r":"あげましょうか","m":"shall I help?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall I make you some coffee?","scene":"A host offers coffee to a guest."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-046||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare てくれる・てもらう","sub":"Vてあげます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあげます","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone — the speaker is the giver","slug":"compare","definition":"The three patterns あげる / くれる / もらう are often confused because all can describe doing something for someone. Focus on **the direction of the action and who benefits**. With てあげる, the speaker or someone in the speaker's group is the giver; the key difference is the direction 'giver → recipient' and the level of politeness.","nuance":"てくれる and てもらう often sound **more natural and polite** than てあげる because they center the speaker. てあげる can sound like boasting about a favor. さしあげる is the humble version of あげる—use it when the recipient is a superior, boss, or customer.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貸して","r":"かして","m":"lend"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"did for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I lent Mr. Tanaka a book.","scene":"You describe helping a colleague by lending a book."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てくれます","point":"Action direction: SOMEONE ELSE does it for ME or someone in my group. Example: 田中さんが本を貸してくれました = Mr. Tanaka lent ME a book. With てあげます, I give the action to someone else."},{"vs":"〜てもらいます","point":"Request direction: I ASK someone else to do it, and I receive the benefit. Example: 田中さんに本を貸してもらいました = I GOT Mr. Tanaka to lend me a book. With てあげます, I VOLUNTARILY do it; I am not asking for help."},{"vs":"〜てさしあげます","point":"The humble form of てあげます—use it with superiors. Example: 先生に資料をコピーしてさしあげました = I photocopied the materials for the teacher. Do not use てあげます with teachers or bosses."}]}},"n4-046||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common pitfalls","sub":"Vてあげます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあげます","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone — the speaker is the giver","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make the mistake of **using てあげます with older people, bosses, or teachers** because they think it simply means 'help.' In reality, てあげます can imply doing someone a favor and may sound rude. Another mistake is using it for an action **the other person does not want**, making the sentence sound condescending.","nuance":"To express help toward a superior, use **〜てさしあげます**, use polite **〜ましょうか**, or switch to **〜てもらいます** from the recipient's viewpoint. Be careful at work because てあげる may be judged tactless.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ケーキ","r":"けーき","m":"cake"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作って","r":"つくって","m":"make"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"did for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I made a cake for my friend.","scene":"You make a cake and give it to a close friend."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生に資料をコピーしてあげました。 → ✅ 先生に資料をコピーしてさしあげました.(Use humble language when helping a teacher)","❌ 部長に荷物を持ってあげました。 → ✅ 部長のお荷物をお持ちしましょうか.(Politely offer to help your boss with お〜しましょうか)","❌ 相手が頼んでいないのに「教えてあげた」 → ✅ 相手の気持ちを確認してから「教えましょうか」と聞く.(Do not force help when it was not requested)","❌ コーヒーを飲みたくない人に「入れてあげましょうか」 → This sounds forced; ask instead: コーヒーを入れましょうか。"],"compare":null}},"n4-046||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vてあげます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてあげます","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone — the speaker is the giver","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember that **あげる** basically means 'give.' When attached to Vて, it means **giving away that action**. For example, 本を読んであげる = give the action of 'reading' → read something aloud for someone. This helps you avoid confusing it with くれる, which also means 'give' but points toward the speaker.","nuance":"Create a concrete image: imagine **holding out your hand and offering a gift**—the gift is the action. If you are the one offering it, use あげる. If someone else offers it toward you, use くれる.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-047||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vてもらいます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもらいます","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (the receiver)","slug":"core","definition":"「Vてもらいます」expresses that the speaker **asks** someone to do something or **gets** something done for them. The sentence focuses on the **receiver of the action** (usually the speaker), not the doer. The doer always takes the particle に.","nuance":"This is a **favor/request** pattern, carrying gratitude or viewing the action as something done for the speaker. It works in both speech and writing at a moderately polite level. When speaking to a superior, use ていただきます for greater politeness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"弁当","r":"べんとう","m":"boxed lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作って","r":"つくって","m":"make"},{"t":"もらいました","r":"もらいました","m":"got made for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother made me a boxed lunch.","scene":"In the morning, Mother prepares a lunch box for work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"送って","r":"おくって","m":"take"},{"t":"もらいました","r":"もらいました","m":"got taken to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend took me to the station.","scene":"After work, a coworker offers to drive me to the station"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-047||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"Vてもらいます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもらいます","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (the receiver)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure consists of **the verb's te-form + もらいます**. The subject is the person who **receives** the action, while the doer is marked by に. The past form もらいました is common when describing something someone did for you.","nuance":"This is the basic present/past form. For a more polite request, use てもらえませんか or てもらえますか. With a superior, use ていただきます (honorific language).","structure":[{"lhs":"（私）は + người làm に","rhs":"Vて + もらいます","note":"Someone does something for me"},{"lhs":"người làm に","rhs":"Vて + もらいました","note":"Past: got/asked someone to do it"},{"lhs":"người làm に","rhs":"Vて + もらえませんか","note":"Polite request"},{"lhs":"người làm に","rhs":"Vて + もらうつもりです","note":"Plan to ask someone to do it"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出して","r":"だして","m":"prescribe"},{"t":"もらいました","r":"もらいました","m":"got prescribed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The doctor prescribed medicine for me.","scene":"At the clinic, the doctor writes a prescription"},{"tokens":[{"t":"コピー"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"取って","r":"とって","m":"photocopy"},{"t":"もらえませんか","r":"もらえませんか","m":"could you do this?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you photocopy this for me?","scene":"Asking a coworker to photocopy documents"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-047||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Vてもらいます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもらいます","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (the receiver)","slug":"usage","definition":"「Vてもらいます」is used mainly in two situations: **(1) proactively asking** someone to do something, and **(2) getting** someone to do something for the speaker. In both cases, the speaker is the receiver. In daily conversation, the request form 「〜てもらえますか」is very common.","nuance":"The pattern implies that the **speaker views the action as a favor** from someone else. Compared with 「てくれます」, 「てもらいます」emphasizes that the speaker **asked for or received** the help, while 「てくれます」emphasizes that **someone voluntarily does it** for the speaker. In polite conversation, 「てもらえますか」sounds softer than a direct command.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"作文","r":"さくぶん","m":"composition"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"直して","r":"なおして","m":"correct"},{"t":"もらいました","r":"もらいました","m":"asked to correct"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I asked my teacher to correct my composition.","scene":"After class, I ask the teacher to check my composition"},{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いて","r":"かいて","m":"write"},{"t":"いただきました","r":"いただきました","m":"was written for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The company president wrote a letter for me.","scene":"Requesting a recommendation letter from the president"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-047||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions and comparisons","sub":"Vてもらいます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもらいます","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (the receiver)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes 「Vてもらいます」from easily confused favor patterns. The key points are **the direction of the favor** and **who performs the action**.","nuance":"All four patterns describe someone doing something for someone. 「てあげます」puts the speaker in the **giver** role, while 「てもらいます」puts the speaker in the **receiver** role. 「てくれます」also has the speaker as receiver, but emphasizes that the action **comes from the other person**, whereas 「てもらいます」often means the speaker **asked for it**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貸して","r":"かして","m":"lend"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"did for me (voluntary)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend voluntarily lent me a book.","scene":"My friend sees I need a book and offers to lend one"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてくれます","point":"てくれます = they VOLUNTARILY do it for me; てもらいます = I ASK or GET them to do it. Example: 友だちが手伝ってくれました (my friend helped voluntarily) vs 友だちに手伝ってもらいました (I asked my friend to help)."},{"vs":"Vてあげます","point":"てあげます sends the favor from me → someone else (I do it for them); てもらいます sends it the other way. ❌ 私は友だちに本を貸してもらいました is wrong if I mean 'I lend my friend a book.'"},{"vs":"Vていただきます","point":"ていただきます is the humble form of てもらいます, used when the doer is a superior or customer. Replace もらう with いただく."}]}},"n4-047||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"Vてもらいます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもらいます","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (the receiver)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse the direction of the favor and the particle. Remember: **the doer always takes に**, and てもらいます always means 'I receive an action done by someone.'","nuance":"These errors can change the meaning or make the sentence impolite. When the doer is a superior, use ていただきます instead of てもらいます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"山田","r":"やまだ","m":"Yamada"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"もらいました","r":"もらいました","m":"got help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I asked Mr. Yamada to help me.","scene":"Describing asking a coworker for help"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生は作文を直してもらいました。 → ✅ 先生に作文を直してもらいました。 (The doer must take に, not は)","❌ 友だちに本を貸してあげました。 (if I mean 'my friend lent me a book') → ✅ 友だちに本を貸してもらいました。 (Keep に and change あげる → もらう to reverse the favor.)","❌ 社長にコピーをとってもらいました。 → ✅ 社長にコピーをとっていただきました。 (Use いただく, not もらう, with a superior.)"],"compare":null}},"n4-047||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vてもらいます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもらいます","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Ask someone to do something for you (the receiver)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: the basic meaning of もらう is **receive**. When combined with a verb's te-form, you are **receiving an action** from someone else. The particle に is like the 'sender' of the action.","nuance":"This helps prevent reversing the direction with てあげます: あげる = give (I give), もらう = receive (I receive).","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-048||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vてくれます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (as a voluntary favor)","slug":"core","definition":"Vてくれます describes **someone else voluntarily doing something beneficial for me**. The recipient is the speaker (or someone in the speaker's group). The doer is usually the subject: **Aは/がVてくれます**.","nuance":"This is a **familiar**, warm expression that shows **gratitude**. Use it with friends, family, and close colleagues. With superiors, use **くださる** (honorific). It emphasizes the doer's **kindness and initiative**, not an action forced by a request.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"お弁当","r":"おべんとう","m":"boxed lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作って","r":"つくって","m":"make"},{"t":"くれます","r":"くれます","m":"do for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother makes me a boxed lunch every morning.","scene":"Telling a friend about your mother's hard work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"送って","r":"おくって","m":"see off"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"did for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend saw me off at the station.","scene":"Retelling how your friend helped you yesterday"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-048||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vてくれます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (as a voluntary favor)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **Vて** + **くれます**. The doer comes before it with **は/が**. Tense and politeness are expressed in **くれます**.","nuance":"くれませんか is a **polite** but still familiar request. With a superior, use **いただけませんか** or **くださいませんか**.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"くれます","note":"Present/future: someone does it for me"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"くれました","note":"Past: someone did it for me"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"くれませんか","note":"Polite request: Could you do it for me?"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"くれますか","note":"Familiar request: Will you do it for me?"},{"lhs":"Nが・は","rhs":"Vてくれます","note":"N is the doer; the recipient is usually 私"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"説明して","r":"せつめいして","m":"explain"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"did for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher explained it to me.","scene":"The teacher reviews a difficult lesson after class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","r":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"くれませんか","r":"くれませんか","m":"could you do for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you help me for a moment?","scene":"Asking a nearby coworker while carrying many things"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-048||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Vてくれます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (as a voluntary favor)","slug":"usage","definition":"Vてくれます appears mainly in three situations: **requests**, **thanks**, and **narration**. When speaking about a superior, くれる becomes **くださる**.","nuance":"For requests, **くれませんか?** sounds gentler than **Vてください** and expresses a hope for help. For thanks, use the fixed phrase **Vてくれてありがとう** (“thank you for ...”).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"carry"},{"t":"くれますか","r":"くれますか","m":"will you do for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you carry my luggage for me?","scene":"At the airport, your hands are full of bags"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"おいしい","r":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"ケーキ","r":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作って","r":"つくって","m":"make"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"made for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend made me a delicious cake.","scene":"Your friend brings a homemade cake for your birthday"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-048||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare あげる・もらう・いただく","sub":"Vてくれます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (as a voluntary favor)","slug":"compare","definition":"All four patterns describe **who does what for whom**, but they differ in the **direction of the favor** and **level of politeness**.","nuance":"From the speaker's viewpoint: **あげる** points outward (giving), **くれる** points inward (someone gives to me), **もらう** also points inward but emphasizes my receiving (often after asking), and **いただく** is the humble version of もらう.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貸して","r":"かして","m":"lend"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"did for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend lent me a book.","scene":"Your friend voluntarily offers you a book"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてあげます","point":"Opposite direction: あげる = I (or someone) do it for SOMEONE ELSE; くれる = someone else does it for ME. 友だちに本を貸してあげた (I lent my friend a book) ↔ 友だちが本を貸してくれた (My friend lent me a book)."},{"vs":"Vてもらいます","point":"Same direction, different nuance: もらう often follows my REQUEST (友だちに手伝ってもらった = I asked my friend to help); くれる stresses the doer's INITIATIVE (友だちが手伝ってくれた = My friend helped me voluntarily)."},{"vs":"Vていただきます","point":"ていただく is the HUMBLE form of てもらう, used with superiors; てくれる does not humble the speaker. 先生に説明していただいた (The teacher explained it — respectful) ↔ 先生が説明してくれた (The teacher explained it — ordinary, familiar)."}]}},"n4-048||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vてくれます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (as a voluntary favor)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with てくれる include confusing the subject, reversing the direction of the favor, and using an insufficiently polite form.","nuance":"Always ask: **WHO does it?** If “I do it,” use あげる. If someone else does it, use くれる. Use くれる only when the recipient is me or my group; if someone else receives the benefit, use あげる.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"セーター","r":"セーター","m":"sweater"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"送って","r":"おくって","m":"send"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"did for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother sent me a sweater.","scene":"Your mother sends you a warm sweater from home"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私は友だちに手伝ってくれました。 → ✅ 友だちは私を手伝ってくれました。 (The subject of くれる must be the DOER, not the recipient)","❌ 母は私にセーターを送ってあげました。 → ✅ 母は私にセーターを送ってくれました。 (Mother does it for me, so use くれる; あげる sounds like she is “granting” me a favor)","❌ 先生が説明してくれました。 (when speaking directly to the teacher) → ✅ 先生が説明してくださいました。 (Use くださる with teachers and other superiors)","❌ 友だちに駅まで送ってくれました。 (に marks the doer) → ✅ 友だちが駅まで送ってくれました。 (The doer takes は/が, not に)"],"compare":null}},"n4-048||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vてくれます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (as a voluntary favor)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture it this way: あげる is a hand **pushing away**, while くれる is a hand **pulling toward me**. With くれ, the favor **moves toward** the speaker.","nuance":"Memory cue: “Someone else → くれ → me.” If the favor moves **toward me**, use **くれます**; if it moves **away from me**, use **あげます**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-048||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vてくれます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (as a voluntary favor)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common verb phrases used with てくれます in everyday conversation. Learn them as set patterns for quick recognition.","nuance":"In requests, 手伝ってくれませんか, 教えてくれませんか, and similar phrases are very common. With close friends, you can drop ま: 手伝ってくれない？","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"くれます","r":"くれます","m":"teach me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher teaches me Japanese.","scene":"Introducing the course to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-049||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vていただきます (謙譲) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただきます (謙譲)","patternReading":"ていただきます","coreMeaning":"Have a higher-status person do something for me (humble form of てもらう)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ていただきます is the **humble** form of 〜てもらいます. It describes the speaker **receiving** an action from someone of higher status, such as a teacher, superior, or client, and expresses gratitude and respect. This pattern is very common at work when making requests or describing help received politely.","nuance":"Avoid using it with close friends or people below you, as it sounds overly formal. In requests, it humbles the speaker and elevates the listener, making it a safe choice with bosses, clients, and business partners. It is much more polite than 〜てもらいます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えていただきました","r":"おしえていただきました","m":"was taught"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was taught Japanese by my teacher.","scene":"Telling a friend after a private lesson"},{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department manager"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"レポート"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"チェックしていただきました","r":"チェックしていただきました","m":"was checked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The department manager checked my report for me.","scene":"Talking to a colleague after submitting a report"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-049||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"Vていただきます (謙譲) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただきます (謙譲)","patternReading":"ていただきます","coreMeaning":"Have a higher-status person do something for me (humble form of てもらう)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern is formed as Vて + いただく. Since いただく is the humble form of もらう, its tense and mood carry the conjugation. For polite requests, use **〜ていただけますか** or **〜ていただけませんか**; to say that someone did something for you, use **〜ていただきました**.","nuance":"In business writing and email, 〜ていただけますでしょうか is also used, though slightly redundant. At N4, mastering the forms above is enough.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いただきます","note":"Polite form – have someone do it for me"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いただきました","note":"Past – had it done for me"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いただけますか","note":"Polite request"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いただけませんか","note":"Very polite, gentle request"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いただきたいです","note":"Expresses a wish to have it done"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","r":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"書類","r":"しょるい","m":"document"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"サイン"},{"t":"していただけますか","r":"していただけますか","m":"could you do it for me?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, could you sign this document for me?","scene":"Handing a client a form to sign"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先輩","r":"せんぱい","m":"senior colleague"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"スカート"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作っていただきました","r":"つくっていただきました","m":"had it made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My senior colleague made a skirt for me.","scene":"Showing a friend the skirt I am wearing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-049||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vていただきます (謙譲) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただきます (謙譲)","patternReading":"ていただきます","coreMeaning":"Have a higher-status person do something for me (humble form of てもらう)","slug":"usage","definition":"Vていただきます mainly appears in two situations: (1) **polite requests**—〜ていただけますか / ませんか; and (2) **recounting** help received—〜ていただきました. In the workplace, it is natural for asking for opinions, confirmation, or trial use.","nuance":"When asking a superior, avoid 〜てください, which is a direct request; use 〜ていただけますか instead. With clients, adding よろしければ makes the request gentler.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","r":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ちょっと","r":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"手伝っていただけますか","r":"てつだっていただけますか","m":"could you help me?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, could you help me for a moment?","scene":"Asking a colleague to help move office furniture"},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"送っていただけませんか","r":"おくっていただけませんか","m":"could you take me?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you take me to the station?","scene":"Asking a superior for a ride to the station"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-049||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with てもらう/てくれます","sub":"Vていただきます (謙譲) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただきます (謙譲)","patternReading":"ていただきます","coreMeaning":"Have a higher-status person do something for me (humble form of てもらう)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare it with three nearby patterns: 〜てもらいます, 〜てくれます, and 〜てください. In short, 〜ていただきます is the polite form of てもらう; 〜てくれます views the action from the giver’s side; 〜てください is a direct request.","nuance":"Politeness generally increases as follows: てください, てもらいます, ていただきます. With superiors, ていただきます is the safest choice.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department manager"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"説明していただきました","r":"せつめいしていただきました","m":"was given an explanation"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The department manager explained it to me.","scene":"Recounting after the boss explained my question"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてもらいます","point":"Both mean “have someone do something for you,” but てもらう is neutral and common with friends or peers; ていただく is its humble form, used with superiors and showing gratitude."},{"vs":"Vてくれます","point":"てくれます focuses on the giver: “someone does something for me”; てもらう/いただく focus on the recipient: “I receive that action.” Example: 部長が説明してくれました / 部長に説明していただきました."},{"vs":"Vてください","point":"てください is a direct request and may sound somewhat commanding; ていただけますか/ませんか are polite requests that humble the speaker and suit superiors or clients."}]}},"n4-049||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vていただきます (謙譲) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただきます (謙譲)","patternReading":"ていただきます","coreMeaning":"Have a higher-status person do something for me (humble form of てもらう)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use てもらう even with teachers or bosses, making the sentence insufficiently polite, or use いただきます for an action they themselves perform, which is wrong. It is also easy to confuse Vていただきます with ごNいただきます (noun + いただく); watch for the verb in te-form.","nuance":"These mistakes can make speech unnatural or blunt. Always ask: **who performs the action**? If it is a superior or client, use いただく; if it is a friend, use もらう/くれる.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"書類","r":"しょるい","m":"document"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"確認していただけますか","r":"かくにんしていただけますか","m":"could you confirm it?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you please confirm this document?","scene":"Emailing a superior to approve a document"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 友達にコーヒーを買っていただきました。 → ✅ 友達にコーヒーを買ってもらいました。","❌ 先生、私がこの荷物を持っていただきます。 → ✅ 先生、私がこの荷物をお持ちします。","❌ その書類をご確認していただけますか。 → ✅ その書類をご確認いただけますか。"],"compare":null}},"n4-049||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vていただきます (謙譲) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただきます (謙譲)","patternReading":"ていただきます","coreMeaning":"Have a higher-status person do something for me (humble form of てもらう)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: **いただく** is the humble form of **もらう**, meaning “to receive.” When you say Vていただきます, you are “receiving” an action from a superior. Imagine receiving a gift from your boss or teacher and saying ありがとうございます.","nuance":"This helps distinguish it from くれる/あげる. If you “receive,” use もらう/いただく. If you “give,” use あげる.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-050||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vる／Nのために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Nのために","patternReading":"のために","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ (intentional purpose)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ために expresses the **intentional purpose** of an action: the speaker actively does something to achieve a desired result. Use **V dictionary form + ために** or **N + の + ために**. Unlike ように, ために emphasizes **intention and effort**.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, with a somewhat **formal, logical** tone. To express a purpose for someone's or something's benefit, use N + の + ために. Compared with ように, ために shows **stronger intention**, while ように leans toward states or abilities that cannot be fully controlled.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大学","r":"だいがく","m":"university"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ために","m":"in order to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"しています","m":"am studying"}],"vi":"I study every day in order to pass the university entrance exam.","scene":"A student discusses exam preparation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"健康","r":"けんこう","m":"health"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"ジョギング","m":"jogging"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しています"}],"vi":"I jog every morning for my health.","scene":"Replying to a coworker asking about staying healthy"},{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"働いて","r":"はたらいて","m":"work"},{"t":"います"}],"vi":"I work for my family.","scene":"When asked why you work so hard"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-050||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"Vる／Nのために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Nのために","patternReading":"のために","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ (intentional purpose)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes how to connect ために: before it, use a **verb in dictionary form** (unchanged for tense) or **noun + の**. All tense, negation, and politeness information belongs in the **following clause**.","nuance":"Do not use Vます or Vた before ために when expressing purpose. When the purpose is a state or uncontrollable ability, use **ように**.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"ために、〜","note":"The verb expresses an intentional action; do not change its form."},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"のために、〜","note":"N is the purpose or beneficiary; の is required."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"国","r":"くに","m":"country"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"帰る","r":"かえる","m":"return"},{"t":"ために","m":"in order to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"飛行機","r":"ひこうき","m":"airplane"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"予約","r":"よやく","m":"reserve"},{"t":"しました","m":"did"}],"vi":"I booked a plane ticket to return to my country.","scene":"Telling a friend about flying home"},{"tokens":[{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"包丁","r":"ほうちょう","m":"kitchen knife"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買いました","r":"かいました","m":"bought"}],"vi":"I bought a new kitchen knife for cooking.","scene":"Replying when a friend notices the new knife"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-050||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Vる／Nのために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Nのために","patternReading":"のために","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ (intentional purpose)","slug":"usage","definition":"ために expresses purpose in situations with a **clear intention**, such as studying, working, staying healthy, or helping one's family. The following clause is usually an **intentional action** by the speaker: 勉強する, 働く, 運動する...","nuance":"The corresponding question **「何のために…か」** asks about purpose. When the goal does not depend entirely on willpower, consider ように. ために often appears with effort adverbs such as 毎日, 一生懸命, and たくさん.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"her"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会う","r":"あう","m":"meet"},{"t":"ために","m":"in order to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"}],"vi":"I am going to Japan to meet her.","scene":"Answering friends who ask about your travel plans"},{"tokens":[{"t":"将来","r":"しょうらい","m":"future"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貯めて","r":"ためて","m":"save"},{"t":"います"}],"vi":"I am saving money for the future.","scene":"Talking about your saving habit"},{"tokens":[{"t":"国","r":"くに","m":"country"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ために","m":"for"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"働き","r":"はたらき","m":"work"},{"t":"たい"},{"t":"です"}],"vi":"I want to work for my country.","scene":"During a job interview"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-050||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"Vる／Nのために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Nのために","patternReading":"のために","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ (intentional purpose)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes ために from easily confused patterns such as ように, のに, and Nのために meaning cause. All can translate as “in order to,” but their underlying meanings differ: ために is for an intentional purpose, ように for an uncontrollable state or ability, のに for function or means, while Nのために can express a negative cause. Learn these core differences to avoid errors.","nuance":"All three patterns can translate as “in order to,” but differ as follows: **ために** = intention/effort; **ように** = state/ability; **のに** = function/means. Also, **Nのために** can express a cause when N is an event.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"病気","r":"びょうき","m":"illness"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"治す","r":"なおす","m":"cure"},{"t":"ために","m":"in order to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲んで","r":"のんで","m":"take"},{"t":"います"}],"vi":"I am taking medicine to cure the illness.","scene":"Explaining why you are taking medicine"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるように","point":"ように is used when the goal is an uncontrollable state or ability (V可能・Vない); ために is used for an intentional action. 日本語が話せるように練習する (practice to be able to speak) vs 日本語を話すために練習する (practice to speak Japanese)."},{"vs":"Vるのに","point":"のに expresses a purpose tied to function, means, or time cost; the next clause often contains 使う・必要・時間がかかる. ために stresses intentional effort. この本を読むのに、3時間かかった vs 日本語を勉強するために、この本を読んだ."},{"vs":"Nのために","point":"Same form, different meaning: N as an event/cause means “because of ~” (often negative and written); N as a beneficiary/purpose means “for ~.” 家族のために (for family) vs 台風のために (because of the typhoon)."}]}},"n4-050||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vる／Nのために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Nのために","patternReading":"のために","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ (intentional purpose)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often put tense in the preceding clause or forget の after a noun. Remember: **purpose always uses Vる / N + の + ために**, while tense belongs in the following clause.","nuance":"When the purpose involves **ability** or **negation**, switch to ように — this is one of the most common N4 learner errors.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"JLPT","m":"JLPT"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"合格","r":"ごうかく","m":"pass"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ために","m":"in order to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"3時間","r":"さんじかん","m":"3 hours"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"しています"}],"vi":"I study for three hours every day to pass the JLPT.","scene":"Talking about a JLPT study plan"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 大学に入ったために、勉強しています。 → ✅ 大学に入るために、勉強しています。 (Use Vる for purpose, not Vた.)","❌ 健康ために、毎日運動しています。 → ✅ 健康のために、毎日運動しています。 (A noun requires の.)","❌ 日本語が話せるために、練習しています。 → ✅ 日本語が話せるように、練習しています。 (V可能 + ために is unnatural; use ように.)","❌ 事故が起きないために、気をつけます。 → ✅ 事故が起きないように、気をつけます。 (起きる is an uncontrollable verb, so use ように.)"],"compare":null}},"n4-050||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vる／Nのために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Nのために","patternReading":"のために","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ (intentional purpose)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: always use dictionary-form V or N + の before ために; do not put tense in the preceding clause. Picture an arrow from the action (following clause) toward the purpose (preceding clause). ため means “for the benefit,” suggesting determination. Say: “Goal first — act now!”","nuance":"Picture an **arrow** from the action (following clause) toward the goal (preceding clause). ため sounds like “tame”—think of “taming” an action to achieve a purpose.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-050||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vる／Nのために · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Nのために","patternReading":"のために","coreMeaning":"In order to ~ (intentional purpose)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases commonly appear with ために in exams and everyday life, helping you remember natural usage.","nuance":"These collocations are very common in daily conversation and JLPT N4 questions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"実現","r":"じつげん","m":"realize"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"ために","m":"in order to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"努力","r":"どりょく","m":"make an effort"},{"t":"しています"}],"vi":"I work hard every day to make my dream come true.","scene":"Talking about a life goal"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-051||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vるのに〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのに〜","patternReading":"るのに","coreMeaning":"For doing ~ / used for ~ (function/cost)","slug":"core","definition":"～のに expresses the **purpose or function** of an action. It is often used for a tool, time, money, or condition needed for something. The first clause uses the **dictionary form**; the second gives a comment (便利・必要) or cost (時間がかかる・お金がかかる).","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing; it is not especially formal. Unlike ために, which states an intentional purpose, ～のに gives an **objective description** of function or cost. The speaker simply comments or evaluates, without expressing intent.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"道具","r":"どうぐ","m":"tool"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"魚","r":"さかな","m":"fish"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"さばく","r":"さばく","m":"prepare fish"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"for"},{"t":"使います","r":"つかいます","m":"use"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This tool is used for preparing fish.","scene":"A mother shows her child how to use a fish knife"},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"to"},{"t":"歩いて","r":"あるいて","m":"walk"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"for"},{"t":"15分","r":"じゅうごふん","m":"15 minutes"},{"t":"かかります","r":"かかります","m":"takes"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Walking to the station takes 15 minutes.","scene":"A tourist asks for directions to the station"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強する","r":"べんきょうする","m":"study"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"for"},{"t":"辞書","r":"じしょ","m":"dictionary"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"必要","r":"ひつよう","m":"necessary"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A dictionary is necessary for studying Japanese.","scene":"Speaking to a friend who just started Japanese"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-051||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vるのに〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのに〜","patternReading":"るのに","coreMeaning":"For doing ~ / used for ~ (function/cost)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **Vる + のに + [evaluation adjective / cost verb]**. Here, のに consists of の (nominalizer) + に (purpose marker), so it should not be split apart. Do not use Vた, Vます, or Vない before のに.","nuance":"This is a beginner-level pattern, often taught with 使う・必要・時間がかかる・便利. The adjectives are usually na-adjectives (便利・必要・大切) or an i-adjective (いい).","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"のに + 便利だ/必要だ","note":"evaluates usefulness"},{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"のに + 使う","note":"states a specific function"},{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"のに + 時間/お金 + がかかる","note":"states a cost"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"アプリ","r":"アプリ","m":"app"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"調べる","r":"しらべる","m":"look up"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"for"},{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This app is convenient for checking train times.","scene":"Introducing a useful app to a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"レポート","r":"レポート","m":"report"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書く","r":"かく","m":"write"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"for"},{"t":"2時間","r":"にじかん","m":"2 hours"},{"t":"かかりました","r":"かかりました","m":"took"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Writing the report took two hours.","scene":"Complaining after doing homework"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-051||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vるのに〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのに〜","patternReading":"るのに","coreMeaning":"For doing ~ / used for ~ (function/cost)","slug":"usage","definition":"～のに is used in three main situations: ① to discuss a function: このペンは字を書くのに使います。② to discuss the time or money needed: 駅まで歩いていくのに15分かかります。③ to discuss a necessary condition: 日本に行くのにパスポートが必要です.","nuance":"The second clause is usually not an intentional action, but a noun, adjective, or verb describing cost. In many cases, のに can be replaced by には, but のに sounds more conversational.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ペン","r":"ペン","m":"pen"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書く","r":"かく","m":"write"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"for"},{"t":"使います","r":"つかいます","m":"use"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This pen is used for writing names.","scene":"In class, showing a child how to use a pen"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"for"},{"t":"ビザ","r":"ビザ","m":"visa"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"必要","r":"ひつよう","m":"necessary"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A visa is necessary for going to Japan.","scene":"Advising a friend preparing for a trip"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-051||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from ために","sub":"Vるのに〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのに〜","patternReading":"るのに","coreMeaning":"For doing ~ / used for ~ (function/cost)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare **Vるのに** and **Vるために**. Both can translate as “to do ~,” but their focus differs. ために emphasizes an **intentional purpose**, with an intentional action in the second clause. のに emphasizes **function, cost, or necessity**, with an evaluation or expense.","nuance":"For example, 留学するために貯金します (I save money to study abroad): saving is a clear purpose. 留学するのに100万円かかります (Studying abroad costs one million yen): this states a cost, not an intentional action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"留学","r":"りゅうがく","m":"study abroad"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"for"},{"t":"100万円","r":"ひゃくまんえん","m":"one million yen"},{"t":"かかります","r":"かかります","m":"costs"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Studying abroad costs one million yen.","scene":"Discussing study-abroad costs with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるために","point":"ために: intentional purpose + action. のに: function/cost + evaluation. Example: 留学するために貯金する vs 留学するのに100万円かかる."},{"vs":"〜のに (逆接)","point":"逆接 uses Vた/i-adj/na-adj+のに. Purpose uses Vる+のに. Example: 勉強したのに、遊んだ vs 勉強するのに、時間がかかる."}]}},"n4-051||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vるのに〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのに〜","patternReading":"るのに","coreMeaning":"For doing ~ / used for ~ (function/cost)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with ～のに include confusing it with ために, confusing it with のに (逆接), and using the wrong verb form.","nuance":"Remember: のに takes the dictionary form; the second clause is not an intentional action. To express “in order to” with an intentional action in the second clause, use ために.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"切る","r":"きる","m":"cut"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"for"},{"t":"包丁","r":"ほうちょう","m":"kitchen knife"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使います","r":"つかいます","m":"use"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Use a kitchen knife to cut vegetables.","scene":"Teaching someone to cook"}],"pitfalls":["❌ この本は読んだのに使います。 → ✅ この本は読むのに使います。","❌ 家を買うために5000万円かかりました。 → ✅ 家を買うのに5000万円かかりました。 (the second clause is a cost, not an intentional purpose)","❌ 勉強するのに、遊んでいます。 → ✅ 勉強したのに、遊んでいます。 (this means “although”; use のに with the た-form)"],"compare":null}},"n4-051||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"Vるのに〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのに〜","patternReading":"るのに","coreMeaning":"For doing ~ / used for ~ (function/cost)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture ～のに as an arrow: [Vる] →(のに)→ [evaluation/cost]. の nominalizes the verb, and に marks the destination. Together, the pattern means “for the task of doing ~.”","nuance":"Memory tip: の turns the verb into a noun (“the act of”), and に means “for.” Thus ～のに means “for doing ~.” It commonly appears with 使う・必要・かかる.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-051||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vるのに〜 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるのに〜","patternReading":"るのに","coreMeaning":"For doing ~ / used for ~ (function/cost)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases used with ～のに in tests and daily life: 時間がかかる (takes time), お金がかかる (costs money), 便利だ (convenient), 必要だ (necessary), 使う (use), いい (good).","nuance":"At JLPT N4, exercises often ask you to connect Vる + のに with these words. Learn them as a collocation set.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"for"},{"t":"1時間","r":"いちじかん","m":"one hour"},{"t":"かかります","r":"かかります","m":"takes"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Going to work takes one hour each day.","scene":"Talking about commuting time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-052||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります","patternReading":"に じかん／おかね が かかります","coreMeaning":"Takes ~ time or money for something","slug":"core","definition":"〜にかかります describes the time or money needed for an activity or place. 時間/お金 is the subject, so it takes が. The part before に shows what incurs the cost. Example: Going from home to school takes 30 minutes.","nuance":"This is an objective, neutral expression without strong emotion. It works in everyday speech and writing, in both polite and casual styles. Add たくさん/すごく to emphasize the amount. Compared with 〜が必要だ, かかる emphasizes resources actually consumed.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"３時間","r":"さんじかん","m":"3 hours"},{"t":"かかります","m":"takes"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This job takes 3 hours.","scene":"Explaining to your boss why it will finish in the afternoon"},{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"パソコン","m":"computer"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"たくさん","m":"a lot of"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"かかりました","m":"cost"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The new laptop cost quite a lot.","scene":"Telling a friend about the laptop you bought"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-052||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります","patternReading":"に じかん／おかね が かかります","coreMeaning":"Takes ~ time or money for something","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **N + に + amount + が + かかります**. To specify an action, use **Vる + のに + amount + が + かかります**. For time, use 時間, 分, or どのくらい; for money, use 円 or いくら.","nuance":"Vるのに means **purpose**, while emphasizing the resources needed to achieve it. のに cannot be replaced with ために in this structure. The verb before のに is always in dictionary form (辞書形).","structure":[{"lhs":"N に","rhs":"時間／お金 が かかります","note":"N is the activity or place incurring the cost"},{"lhs":"Vる の に","rhs":"時間／お金 が かかります","note":"Vる is dictionary form; のに means 'to'"},{"lhs":"A から B まで","rhs":"（交通手段で）時間がかかります","note":"Used to state travel time"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"１時間","r":"いちじかん","m":"1 hour"},{"t":"かかります","m":"takes"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It takes 1 hour by train from home to school.","scene":"Talking with a friend about the commute to school"},{"tokens":[{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"覚える","r":"おぼえる","m":"memorize"},{"t":"のに","m":"to"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"かかります","m":"takes"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Memorizing kanji takes time.","scene":"Talking about the difficulty of studying Japanese"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-052||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります","patternReading":"に じかん／おかね が かかります","coreMeaning":"Takes ~ time or money for something","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern in many situations: asking for directions, travel time, shopping or repair costs, time to finish work, and study time. It often appears with どのくらい (how long) and いくら (how much money).","nuance":"Questions are usually brief and polite. You can use まで for the endpoint, で for the means of transport, and から for the starting point.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"ここ"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"歩いて","r":"あるいて","m":"on foot"},{"t":"どのくらい","m":"how long"},{"t":"かかります","m":"takes"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"How long does it take to walk from the station to here?","scene":"Asking a passerby for directions"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"かばん","m":"bag"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"いくら","m":"how much"},{"t":"かかりました","m":"cost"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"How much did this bag cost?","scene":"Asking a friend about a new bag's price"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-052||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with ために","sub":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります","patternReading":"に じかん／おかね が かかります","coreMeaning":"Takes ~ time or money for something","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare **Vるのに** and **Vるために**: both can mean “to do something,” but **のに** emphasizes the **cost or resources** (time, money, effort) required, while **ために** emphasizes the **purpose** of the action. With かかります, のに is required; do not use ために.","nuance":"This pattern is also often confused with 必要です. かかります describes objectively consumed resources, while 必要です describes subjective necessity.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"のに","m":"to"},{"t":"10万円","r":"じゅうまんえん","m":"100,000 yen"},{"t":"かかります","m":"costs"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Going to Japan costs 100,000 yen.","scene":"Talking about the cost of a trip to Japan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるために","point":"のに: emphasizes time/money; ために: emphasizes purpose and often combines with an intentional action."},{"vs":"必要です","point":"かかります: describes actual cost consumed; 必要です: describes a required condition or item."}]}},"n4-052||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります","patternReading":"に じかん／おかね が かかります","coreMeaning":"Takes ~ time or money for something","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include using を instead of に, omitting の when connecting a verb, or confusing に with は. Remember: **N + に + amount + が + かかります**.","nuance":"The most common error is using を for what incurs the cost: the activity is not a direct object.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作る","r":"つくる","m":"make"},{"t":"のに","m":"to"},{"t":"30分","r":"さんじゅっぷん","m":"30 minutes"},{"t":"かかります","m":"takes"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Cooking takes 30 minutes.","scene":"Answering “How long does cooking take?”"}],"pitfalls":["❌ この仕事を３時間かかります。 → ✅ この仕事に３時間かかります。","❌ 料理を作るに30分かかります。 → ✅ 料理を作るのに30分かかります。","❌ 日本へ行くために10万円かかります。 → ✅ 日本へ行くのに10万円かかります。"],"compare":null}},"n4-052||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります","patternReading":"に じかん／おかね が かかります","coreMeaning":"Takes ~ time or money for something","slug":"memory","definition":"Think of **かかる** as originally meaning “to hang” (物がかかる). Imagine time or money being “hung” on a task, meaning the task “uses up” that amount. The particle **に** marks the point of attachment.","nuance":"A visual mnemonic can make the pattern easier to remember.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-052||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜時間／お金がかかります","patternReading":"に じかん／おかね が かかります","coreMeaning":"Takes ~ time or money for something","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions include **時間がかかる** (take time), **お金がかかる** (cost money), **手間がかかる** (take effort), **いくらかかりますか** (ask the price), and **どのくらいかかりますか** (ask how long).","nuance":"These expressions appear frequently in everyday conversation and on JLPT tests.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"1時間","r":"いちじかん","m":"1 hour"},{"t":"かかりました","m":"took"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The homework took 1 hour.","scene":"Texting a friend about homework"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-053||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V／Aそうです (様態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aそうです (様態)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"It seems ~ / looks about to ~ (judging from appearance)","slug":"core","definition":"〜そうです（様態）expresses the speaker's **judgment based on direct observation**: seeing an appearance, smelling something, tasting it, or sensing a condition. It means **seems ~** or **looks about to ~**. The speaker is NOT 100% certain because the judgment is inferred from outward signs.","nuance":"Common in everyday speech and also used in writing. Certainty is about **60–80%**—weaker than に違いない but stronger than かも. Do not use it for **your own emotions** unless you are looking in a mirror or otherwise observing yourself.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降り","r":"ふり","m":"fall (rain)"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"seems"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"空","r":"そら","m":"sky"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"暗い","r":"くらい","m":"dark"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It looks like it's going to rain. The sky is dark.","scene":"Seeing dark clouds and a darkening sky"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいし","r":"おいし","m":"delicious"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"looks"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This dish looks delicious, doesn't it?","scene":"Seeing an attractively arranged dish"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-053||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V／Aそうです (様態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aそうです (様態)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"It seems ~ / looks about to ~ (judging from appearance)","slug":"forms","definition":"〜そうです（様態）combines with a **verb stem** (the ます-form minus ます) and with adjectives after removing い or だ. Two important **irregular forms** are いい → よさそう and ない → なさそう. With a verb's negative state, use なさそうです (it seems not to).","nuance":"Add です for politeness: おいしそうです. In casual speech, です may be omitted. It does not change for tense, but a past form is possible: おいしそうでした (it looked delicious; observed in the past).","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"そうです"},{"lhs":"Aい (bỏ い)","rhs":"そうです"},{"lhs":"Aな (bỏ だ)","rhs":"そうです"},{"lhs":"いい (bất quy tắc)","rhs":"よさそうです"},{"lhs":"ない (bất quy tắc)","rhs":"なさそうです"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"healthy"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"seems"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She looks healthy.","scene":"Seeing a lively classmate after vacation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"難し","r":"むずかし","m":"difficult"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"seems"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book looks difficult.","scene":"Seeing a thick book with tiny text"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-053||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"V／Aそうです (様態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aそうです (様態)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"It seems ~ / looks about to ~ (judging from appearance)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜そうです（様態）is used for **predictions of imminent events** (it is about to rain), **judgments from appearance** (looks delicious, looks tired), and **sensory impressions** (sounds noisy, smells good). With verbs, it usually describes an action that is **about to happen**. With adjectives, it describes a **current impression**.","nuance":"Do not use it for something already certain or whose result is clearly visible. It is generally subjective rather than formal or objective. It can modify nouns: おいしそうな料理.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"疲れ","r":"つかれ","m":"tired"},{"t":"そう","r":"そう","m":"seems"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"顔","r":"かお","m":"face"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"have (an expression)"},{"t":"い","r":"い","m":"be"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He looks tired.","scene":"Seeing a coworker yawn with sleepy eyes"},{"tokens":[{"t":"森","r":"もり","m":"Mori"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"忙し","r":"いそがし","m":"busy"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"seems"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Mori looks busy.","scene":"Seeing him type while on the phone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"寒","r":"さむ","m":"cold"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"seems"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It looks cold outside.","scene":"Seeing people in down jackets through the window"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-053||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare: 伝聞・らしい・ようだ","sub":"V／Aそうです (様態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aそうです (様態)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"It seems ~ / looks about to ~ (judging from appearance)","slug":"compare","definition":"〜そうです（様態）is often confused with **〜そうです（伝聞）**. Both have the form そう, but **様態** is used only when you **observe appearance yourself**, while **伝聞** is used for information **heard from another source**. Keep the distinction clear to avoid errors.","nuance":"様態: “looks/seems” based on direct observation. 伝聞: “I hear that” based on someone else's report. らしい: “apparently,” based on indirect evidence, hearsay, or information. よう: “like/as if,” used for comparison or subjective inference.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降り","r":"ふり","m":"fall"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"seems"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It looks like it is going to rain (the sky is dark).","scene":"Compare: 天気予報で雨が降るそうです (hearing a forecast)"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜そうです（伝聞）","point":"様態 = observed directly; 伝聞 = heard from someone else. The form そう is the same, but the information source is opposite."},{"vs":"〜らしいです","point":"らしい is an inference from indirect evidence (hearsay or news); direct observation is not required. そう focuses on perceivable appearance."},{"vs":"〜ようです","point":"よう expresses comparison or analogy (〜のように). そう judges appearance and does not make a comparison."}]}},"n4-053||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V／Aそうです (様態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aそうです (様態)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"It seems ~ / looks about to ~ (judging from appearance)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make mistakes with **〜そうです（様態）** by confusing it with **〜そうです（伝聞）** or using the wrong form. Remember: **do not use ます**, **do not use the dictionary form**, and **memorize the two irregular forms** いい→よさそう and ない→なさそう.","nuance":"The most serious mistake is putting ます before そう because the combination sounds familiar. In fact, そう（様態）follows only a verb stem or an adjective with its ending removed.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいし","r":"おいし","m":"delicious"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"looks"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This cake looks delicious.","scene":"Standing in front of a bakery display"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が降るそうです。 → ✅ 雨が降りそうです。（use the verb stem 降り）","❌ この本はいいそうです。 → ✅ この本はよさそうです。（いい changes irregularly to よさ）","❌ おいしいそうではありません。 → ✅ おいしくなさそうです。（or おいしそうではありません）","❌ 彼は元気なそうです。 → ✅ 彼は元気そうです。（Aな drops だ; do not add な）","❌ 台風が来るそうです。 → ✅ 台風が来そうです。（if seeing the wind and clouds; use 来るそうです if heard on the radio）"],"compare":null}},"n4-053||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V／Aそうです (様態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aそうです (様態)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"It seems ~ / looks about to ~ (judging from appearance)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: **〜そうです（様態）** is tied to the **senses**—you see, hear, smell, or taste something and make a prediction. Imagine taking a snapshot and saying, “This is what it looks like in the picture.”","nuance":"There are few additional grammatical nuances; the key idea is the mental image.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-053||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V／Aそうです (様態) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／Aそうです (様態)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"It seems ~ / looks about to ~ (judging from appearance)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases with **〜そうです（様態）** are common in tests and daily life: describing people (元気そう, 疲れそう, 忙しそう), food (おいしそう), things (高そう, 便利そう), and weather (雨が降りそう, やみそう).","nuance":"These combinations are nearly **fixed** with common adjectives. Memorizing them helps you speak naturally and respond quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降り","r":"ふり","m":"fall"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"seems"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It looks like it is going to rain.","scene":"Seeing dark clouds gather"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-054||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです","patternReading":"そうにありません ／ なさそうです","coreMeaning":"It seems unlikely that ~ (based on outward signs)","slug":"core","definition":"〜そうにありません is the negative form of 〜そうです (様態). Based on outward appearance and the current situation, the speaker predicts that something will not happen. Unfavorable signs often create worry, unease, or disappointment. Example: heavy rain → “it doesn't look like the rain will stop.”","nuance":"Natural and common in everyday conversation. It is not overly formal and works with friends or coworkers. The negative prediction is fairly strong: the speaker almost believes the event is unlikely. Unlike 〜かもしれない (maybe, about 50%), 〜そうにありません strongly leans toward “not.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"止み","r":"やみ","m":"stop"},{"t":"そうにありません","r":"そうにありません","m":"doesn't seem"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It doesn't look like the rain will stop.","scene":"Heavy rain has continued all afternoon"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今日中に","r":"きょうじゅうに","m":"today"},{"t":"終わり","r":"おわり","m":"finish"},{"t":"そうにありません","r":"そうにありません","m":"doesn't seem"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This work doesn't look like it will be finished today.","scene":"A mountain of files remains near quitting time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-054||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです","patternReading":"そうにありません ／ なさそうです","coreMeaning":"It seems unlikely that ~ (based on outward signs)","slug":"forms","definition":"Conjugate by word type: verbs use Vます-stem + そうにありません, or そうもありません for emphasis; い-adjectives drop い and add くなさそうです; nouns and あります use Nがなさそうです. Never use Vない + そうです or Vそうじゃありません.","nuance":"In speech, 〜そうにありません is often shortened to 〜そうにない, which is more casual. 〜そうもありません suggests “there seems to be no hope at all.” 〜なさそうです is the special form used with ない/あります and is very natural for talking about absence.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます (bỏ ます)","rhs":"+ そうにありません","note":"Seems unlikely that V (common)"},{"lhs":"Vます (bỏ ます)","rhs":"+ そうもありません","note":"Stronger: shows no sign of V"},{"lhs":"Aい (bỏ い)","rhs":"+ くなさそうです","note":"Seems not A (い-adjective)"},{"lhs":"Nが","rhs":"なさそうです","note":"Seems not to have N (noun)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来","r":"き","m":"come"},{"t":"そうにありません","r":"そうにありません","m":"doesn't seem"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It looks like he won't come.","scene":"Waiting a long time outside the school gate"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"美味しく","r":"おいしく","m":"delicious"},{"t":"なさそうです","r":"なさそうです","m":"doesn't seem"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This cake doesn't look tasty.","scene":"One corner of the cake is burned"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-054||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです","patternReading":"そうにありません ／ なさそうです","coreMeaning":"It seems unlikely that ~ (based on outward signs)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when directly observing a situation and seeing negative signs. Common situations include bad weather, an approaching deadline, or waiting for something that never appears. It can also express a mild complaint to the person you are speaking with.","nuance":"It is mainly conversational. With friends, use the shorter 〜そうにない; with elders or at work, 〜そうにありません is more polite. The emotion is often worry, impatience, or disappointment, so it is not normally used in cheerful situations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来","r":"き","m":"come"},{"t":"そうにない","r":"そうにない","m":"doesn't seem"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It doesn't look like the bus will come, does it?","scene":"Waiting 20 minutes at an empty bus stop"},{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"終わり","r":"おわり","m":"finish"},{"t":"そうにありません","r":"そうにありません","m":"doesn't seem"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It doesn't look like I can finish the homework.","scene":"Lots of homework remains, but it is already late"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"well"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"なさそうです","r":"なさそうです","m":"doesn't seem to have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She doesn't look well.","scene":"A tired-looking classmate sits quietly"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-054||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare similar patterns","sub":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです","patternReading":"そうにありません ／ なさそうです","coreMeaning":"It seems unlikely that ~ (based on outward signs)","slug":"compare","definition":"These comparisons highlight the key differences between 〜そうにありません and easily confused patterns. Remember: 〜そうにありません is a judgment based on what you see and strongly leans toward “not.”","nuance":"Compared with 〜そうです, these are opposite conclusions based on the same observation. Compared with 〜なさそうです, choose by word type: verbs use V-sou-ni-arimasen, while い-adjectives and nouns use na-sa-sou-desu. Compared with 〜かもしれない, the confidence differs: this pattern strongly leans toward “not,” while かもしれない means only “maybe.” Compared with 〜ないそうです, the information source differs: this pattern comes from observation, while ないそうです reports hearsay.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"止み","r":"やみ","m":"stop"},{"t":"そうにありません","r":"そうにありません","m":"doesn't seem"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It doesn't look like the rain will stop.","scene":"Compared with 止みそうです (looks like it will stop)"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜そうです (様態)","point":"〜そうです = “it looks like ~ will happen”; 〜そうにありません = “it looks like ~ will not happen.” Example: 雨が止みそうです (seems to stop) ↔ 雨が止みそうにありません (doesn't seem to stop)."},{"vs":"〜なさそうです","point":"〜なさそうです is used with い-adjectives (Aくなさそう) and nouns/あります (Nがなさそう)—NOT verbs. Verbs must use Vそうにありません."},{"vs":"〜かもしれない","point":"〜かもしれない = “maybe” (about 50%), without a strong prediction; 〜そうにありません strongly leans toward “not” based on observation. Example: 彼は来ないかもしれません (maybe he won't come) ↔ 彼は来そうにありません (he doesn't look likely to come)."},{"vs":"〜ないそうです (伝聞)","point":"〜ないそうです = “I hear that ~ will not happen”—information from someone else; 〜そうにありません = a judgment from what you see. Example: 彼は来ないそうです (I hear he won't come) ↔ 彼は来そうにありません (he doesn't look likely to come)."}]}},"n4-054||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです","patternReading":"そうにありません ／ なさそうです","coreMeaning":"It seems unlikely that ~ (based on outward signs)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are classic errors with the negative of 様態 そうです. Remember: verbs → Vそうにありません; い-adjectives/nouns → なさそうです.","nuance":"Learners often translate “not” directly and attach ない, producing Vないそうです. With verbs, use the ます-stem plus そうにありません; with い-adjectives and nouns, use なさそうです. Always identify the word type before conjugating.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"晴れ","r":"はれ","m":"sunny"},{"t":"そうにありません","r":"そうにありません","m":"doesn't seem"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It doesn't look like it will be sunny tomorrow.","scene":"Looking at the gloomy sky"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が止まなさそうです → ✅ 雨が止みそうにありません (Never use なさそう with verbs; use Vます-stem + そうにありません)","❌ このケーキはおいしそうにありません → ✅ このケーキはおいしくなさそうです (For い-adjectives, the negative of おいしそうです is おいしくなさそうです, not おいしそうにありません)","❌ 時間がないそうです → ✅ 時間がなさそうです (For an observation, do not use Nがないそうです, which reports hearsay; use Nがなさそうです)","❌ 彼は来ないそうです → ✅ 彼は来そうにありません (If you are observing and making a prediction, do not use ないそうです—it means “I hear he won't come”)","❌ 日本語が上手なさそうです → ✅ 日本語が上手じゃなさそうです (With な-adjectives, the negative is じゃなさそう, not なさそう)"],"compare":null}},"n4-054||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです","patternReading":"そうにありません ／ なさそうです","coreMeaning":"It seems unlikely that ~ (based on outward signs)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a sky full of dark clouds and say, “It probably won't stop raining.” That is 止みそうにありません. そう = “outward appearance,” and にありません = “not there,” giving “there is no sign that ~ will happen.” For なさそう, remember the sound “na-sa-sou” as “not having something”—use it for things that are absent.","nuance":"Build the habit of looking at the situation: heavy rain, a tall stack of files, or a bus that never arrives, then say the pattern aloud. Repeated practice makes it automatic without rote memorization.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-054||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vそうにありません／なさそうです","patternReading":"そうにありません ／ なさそうです","coreMeaning":"It seems unlikely that ~ (based on outward signs)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Learn these phrases with 〜そうにありません/なさそうです so you can use them quickly and naturally. They appear often on JLPT tests and in everyday conversation.","nuance":"These phrases reflect the speaker's unease or impatience when an expected event does not seem likely. They are natural in speech and can function as a mild complaint to friends or coworkers.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"美味しく","r":"おいしく","m":"tasty"},{"t":"なさそうです","r":"なさそうです","m":"doesn't seem"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This restaurant doesn't look very good.","scene":"The place is empty and the counter is dusty"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-055||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます","patternReading":"てきます／ていきます","coreMeaning":"Do V and come back / do V and go (direction of movement)","slug":"core","definition":"〜て来ます／〜て行きます connects two actions: do V first, then move in a direction. **て来ます** = finish the action and come back to the speaker; **て行きます** = finish the action and move away from the speaker. Without actual movement, they describe **change**: てきた looks from the past to the present, while ていく looks from the present to the future.","nuance":"This is a casual pattern, common in conversation, especially when saying goodbye (行ってきます) or saying you are going out (買って来ます). It is not highly formal, but sounds natural with coworkers, friends, and family. てきます often feels like 'I'll go for a moment and come back.'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"コンビニ","m":"convenience store"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"買って","r":"かって","m":"buy"},{"t":"来ます","r":"きます","m":"come back"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'll stop by the convenience store to buy something and come right back.","scene":"Telling a coworker in the office before stepping out"},{"tokens":[{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"take"},{"t":"行って","r":"いって","m":"go"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please take an umbrella with you.","scene":"A mother warns her child before they leave because rain is coming"},{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"recently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なって","r":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"来ました","r":"きました","m":"have gradually"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Your Japanese has improved lately, hasn't it?","scene":"Meeting a friend after months and noticing more natural speech"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-055||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます","patternReading":"てきます／ていきます","coreMeaning":"Do V and come back / do V and go (direction of movement)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is simple: **Vて + 来ます／行きます**. The first verb must be conjugated to the te-form. For the negative, replace て with **Vないで**, meaning 'move without doing V'. Past forms are **Vてきました／Vていきました**.","nuance":"Pay attention to the te-form of each verb group: **買う→買って**, **持つ→持って**, **なる→なって**. Do not confuse the te-form with the ます-form minus ます.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"来ます","note":"Finish V → come back here"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"行きます","note":"Finish V → leave for somewhere else"},{"lhs":"Vないで","rhs":"来ます／行きます","note":"Negative: move without doing V"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"きました／いきました","note":"Past: did V, then came back / left"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"少しずつ","r":"すこしずつ","m":"little by little"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強して","r":"べんきょうして","m":"study"},{"t":"きました","r":"きました","m":"up to now"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have studied Japanese little by little every day up to now.","scene":"Talking with a friend about your study progress"},{"tokens":[{"t":"手","r":"て","m":"hands"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"洗って","r":"あらって","m":"wash"},{"t":"来ます","r":"きます","m":"then come back"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'll go wash my hands and come back.","scene":"Leaving the dinner table briefly to wash your hands"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-055||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます","patternReading":"てきます／ていきます","coreMeaning":"Do V and come back / do V and go (direction of movement)","slug":"usage","definition":"**Use 1**: V is the action, while 来ます/行きます shows where the subject goes afterward. **Use 2**: without actual movement, the pattern describes a process of change. **〜てきました** = has gradually changed and is noticeable now; **〜ていきます** = will continue changing in the future.","nuance":"For change, **てきます** often goes with adjectives (暑くなる, 上手になる) and verbs (変わってくる). **ていきます** is used for trends that will continue. Both often appear with だんだん, これから, and 最近.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お茶","r":"おちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買って","r":"かって","m":"buy"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"then go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'll buy tea to take with me.","scene":"Telling a friend while buying tea to take away"},{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"recently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"暑く","r":"あつく","m":"hot"},{"t":"なって","r":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"きました","r":"きました","m":"has gradually"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It's been getting hotter lately, hasn't it?","scene":"Chatting with a friend in early summer"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これから","r":"これから","m":"from now"},{"t":"だんだん","m":"gradually"},{"t":"寒く","r":"さむく","m":"cold"},{"t":"なって","r":"なって","m":"become"},{"t":"いきます","r":"いきます","m":"will gradually"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It will gradually get colder from now on.","scene":"Weather forecast as autumn turns to winter"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-055||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます","patternReading":"てきます／ていきます","coreMeaning":"Do V and come back / do V and go (direction of movement)","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick distinction: **てしまいます** = an action is completed, often with regret or relief; **てきます／ていきます** = direction after an action. **ています** = an ongoing state; **てくる／ていく** = change in a direction.","nuance":"On the N4 test, if the context says 'go somewhere and come back,' choose てきます; if something is completed with a feeling of regret, think of てしまいます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"向こう","r":"むこう","m":"over there"},{"t":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"走って","r":"はしって","m":"run"},{"t":"きました","r":"きました","m":"came here"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"A student ran over from there.","scene":"Seeing a student run toward you at the school gate"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてしまいます","point":"てしまいます = completed (often regretted); てきます = do V, then come back. Example: 宿題をやってしまいました (I ended up doing all my homework) vs 宿題をやってきました (I did my homework and came here)."},{"vs":"Vています","point":"ています = an ongoing state; てきます = movement toward the speaker. Example: ドアが開いています (the door is open) vs ドアを開けてきました (I opened the door and came back)."},{"vs":"Vていく","point":"Choose based on the speaker's location: てくる = toward the speaker; ていく = away from the speaker. Example: 走ってきました (ran toward me) vs 走っていきました (ran away from me)."}]}},"n4-055||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます","patternReading":"てきます／ていきます","coreMeaning":"Do V and come back / do V and go (direction of movement)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate 'go/come' word for word and choose 来ます/行きます incorrectly, or forget the negative form. Focus on **where the speaker is standing** to choose the direction.","nuance":"These errors make a sentence sound unnatural, though it may still be understood. When practicing speaking, ask yourself: 'After V, will I return here or move away?'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"スーパー","m":"supermarket"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行って","r":"いって","m":"go"},{"t":"きました","r":"きました","m":"and came back"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother went shopping at the supermarket and came back.","scene":"Mother has just returned home and is putting things away"}],"pitfalls":["❌ コンビニで買って行きます — if you mean 'buy it and come back,' return to the speaker's location → ✅ コンビニで買って来ます","❌ 朝ごはんを食べなくて来ました — なくて does not mean 'without V' here → ✅ 朝ごはんを食べないで来ました","❌ 日本語が上手になって行きました — for change up to now, use the present as the reference point → ✅ 日本語が上手になってきました"],"compare":null}},"n4-055||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます","patternReading":"てきます／ていきます","coreMeaning":"Do V and come back / do V and go (direction of movement)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **the speaker as the reference point**. **来る** moves toward that point, while **行く** moves away from it. With '〜てきた,' draw an arrow from the past to the present; with '〜ていきます,' draw an arrow from the present to the future.","nuance":"This is a visual memory aid, not a grammar rule. Just remember: **return = 来, continue away = 行**, and it will work in most situations.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-055||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vて来ます／Vて行きます","patternReading":"てきます／ていきます","coreMeaning":"Do V and come back / do V and go (direction of movement)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear very often: **行ってきます** is said when leaving home or work; **ちょっと〜て来ます** says that you are stepping out briefly; **Vてきました** describes a process up to the present; **〜ていきます** describes continuing change.","nuance":"行ってきます is a **fixed expression**—it sounds like 'I'm off,' so there is no need to analyze the specific action. With customers or older people, you can say 行って参ります, which is more polite.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"行って","r":"いって","m":"go"},{"t":"きます","r":"きます","m":"will return"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"I'm off (I'll be back)!","scene":"A child says goodbye before leaving home for school"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行って","r":"いって","m":"go"},{"t":"きます","r":"きます","m":"then come back"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'll go to the restroom for a moment and be back.","scene":"Asking to step out briefly during a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-056||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます","patternReading":"すぎます","coreMeaning":"Beyond the normal level, usually negative (complaint/criticism)","slug":"core","definition":"〜すぎる expresses an action or quality that exceeds the normal acceptable level, so the speaker judges it as bad or undesirable. It attaches to a verb or adjective stem and means 'too ~'.","nuance":"It often conveys complaint, criticism, or concern, and can be humorous or serious. It is used in both speech and writing, but in speech it often appears as 〜すぎて + a consequence. Its negative force is stronger than とても ('very').","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"スープ","m":"soup"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"熱","r":"あつ","m":"hot"},{"t":"すぎます","r":"すぎます","m":"too"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This soup is too hot.","scene":"At a restaurant, after tasting soup that is still too hot"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"飲み","r":"のみ","m":"drink"},{"t":"すぎて","r":"すぎて","m":"too much"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"頭","r":"あたま","m":"head"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"痛い","r":"いたい","m":"hurt"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I drank too much yesterday, so I have a headache.","scene":"Talking to a coworker after the company party"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-056||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます","patternReading":"すぎます","coreMeaning":"Beyond the normal level, usually negative (complaint/criticism)","slug":"forms","definition":"General formula: **Vます-stem + すぎる**, **Aい drop い + すぎる**, **Aな drop な + すぎる**. Then conjugate it like a group 2 verb: **すぎます** (polite), **すぎた**, **すぎて**, **すぎない**, **すぎれば**.","nuance":"This is a **group 2 (一段) verb**, so it can take ordinary conjugations. Do not attach it directly to the dictionary or ます form of the base verb; use the stem.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"すぎる","note":"飲みすぎる・食べすぎる"},{"lhs":"Aい (drop い)","rhs":"すぎる","note":"暑すぎる・高すぎる"},{"lhs":"Aな (drop な)","rhs":"すぎる","note":"静かすぎる・便利すぎる"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ラーメン","m":"ramen"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"高","r":"たか","m":"expensive"},{"t":"すぎます","r":"すぎます","m":"too"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The ramen at this shop is too expensive.","scene":"Looking at the menu and commenting to a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"働き","r":"はたらき","m":"work"},{"t":"すぎました","r":"すぎました","m":"too much"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I worked too hard yesterday.","scene":"Telling a friend about working overtime"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-056||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます","patternReading":"すぎます","coreMeaning":"Beyond the normal level, usually negative (complaint/criticism)","slug":"usage","definition":"It is most often used to **state a negative result** after すぎて: 高すぎて買えない, 難しすぎてわからない... It can also express **complaints**, **mild criticism**, or **self-acknowledged flaws**.","nuance":"In everyday conversation, it often appears as **すぎて + a difficulty**, or as **すぎます** at the end of a complaining sentence. It can also be sarcastic praise: 優しすぎる = 'too kind,' implying some awkwardness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"難し","r":"むずかし","m":"difficult"},{"t":"すぎて","r":"すぎて","m":"too"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"わかりません","r":"わかりません","m":"do not understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This problem is too difficult, so I do not understand it.","scene":"A student complaining to a classmate"},{"tokens":[{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"trip"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多","r":"おお","m":"many"},{"t":"すぎます","r":"すぎます","m":"too much"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have too much luggage for the trip.","scene":"Standing at the airport beside an overflowing suitcase"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-056||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions from similar patterns","sub":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます","patternReading":"すぎます","coreMeaning":"Beyond the normal level, usually negative (complaint/criticism)","slug":"compare","definition":"When learning 〜すぎる, learners often confuse it with 〜てしまう and とても. The key distinction is that 〜すぎる emphasizes exceeding the normal level and carries a negative evaluation; 〜てしまう emphasizes a completed action and regret; とても simply marks a high, neutral degree. Study the pairs below.","nuance":"Compared with **Vてしまう**: 〜てしまう emphasizes **that something happened or is regrettable**, while 〜すぎる emphasizes **exceeding an acceptable level**. Compared with **とても**: とても marks a high degree **without evaluation**, while すぎる implies **excess beyond a standard**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"すぎて","r":"すぎて","m":"too much"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おなか","m":"stomach"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"痛い","r":"いたい","m":"hurt"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I ate too much, so my stomach hurts.","scene":"After a buffet, rubbing your stomach and complaining"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてしまう","point":"「食べてしまいました」= I ended up eating it (completed, regret);「食べすぎました」= I ate too much (excess)."},{"vs":"とても","point":"「とても高い」= very expensive (neutral);「高すぎる」= too expensive (criticized/unacceptable)."}]}},"n4-056||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます","patternReading":"すぎます","coreMeaning":"Beyond the normal level, usually negative (complaint/criticism)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Three common mistakes are **forgetting to drop い/な**, **adding ます to the preceding part**, and **combining it with とても**. Follow the corrections below.","nuance":"These errors come from translating 'too' as if it followed the entire long phrase. Always use **[stem] + すぎる**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"アパート","m":"apartment"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"すぎます","r":"すぎます","m":"too"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This apartment is too quiet.","scene":"Looking for an apartment in an unusually deserted area"}],"pitfalls":["❌ この店は高いすぎる。 → ✅ この店は高すぎる。","❌ 飲みますすぎた。 → ✅ 飲みすぎた。","❌ 静かだすぎる。 → ✅ 静かすぎる。","❌ 彼女はとてもきれいすぎる。 → ✅ 彼女はきれいすぎる。 (drop とても because すぎる already means too much)"],"compare":null}},"n4-056||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます","patternReading":"すぎます","coreMeaning":"Beyond the normal level, usually negative (complaint/criticism)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory aid: **過ぎる** contains 過, meaning 'pass through' → **pass the limit line** → 'too.' Combine it with the base word: **too hot = 熱すぎる**, **eat too much = 食べすぎる**, much like placing 'too' after a word in English.","nuance":"Imagine a cup overflowing because you **put in too much**: 入れすぎ! ('You overfilled it!'). This image creates a memorable association.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-056||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vすぎます／Aすぎます","patternReading":"すぎます","coreMeaning":"Beyond the normal level, usually negative (complaint/criticism)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases are common in exams and daily life: **食べすぎ・飲みすぎ・考えすぎ・言いすぎ・働きすぎ**. The noun form 〜すぎ is also common: 食べすぎは体に悪い. ('Eating too much is bad for your health.')","nuance":"With nouns, use **〜すぎ** (drop る) at the end of the compound: 考えすぎ, 言いすぎ. It often appears in advice or reminders.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"働き","r":"はたらき","m":"work"},{"t":"すぎ","r":"すぎ","m":"too much (noun)"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He works too much.","scene":"Worried about a close friend who often works overtime"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-057||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vやすい／にくいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／にくいです","patternReading":"やすい／にくいです","coreMeaning":"Easy to ~ / difficult to ~ (describing an action or object's quality)","slug":"core","definition":"**〜やすい／〜にくい** attach to a verb stem to mean 'easy to do' / 'difficult to do'. For example, 読みやすい = 'easy-to-read' and 書きにくい = 'difficult-to-write'. This pattern **evaluates the object**, not the performer's ability.","nuance":"Neutral and widely used in speech and writing, including formal materials. にくい suggests something is awkward or inconvenient but still doable; やすい suggests something is convenient or comfortable.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"説明書","r":"せつめいしょ","m":"instruction manual"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"読みやすい","r":"よみやすい","m":"easy to read"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This instruction manual is easy to read.","scene":"Holding a clearly laid-out assembly manual."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ペン","m":"pen"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"書きにくい","r":"かきにくい","m":"difficult to write with"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This pen is difficult to write with, isn't it?","scene":"The borrowed pen has a dry, scratchy nib."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-057||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vやすい／にくいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／にくいです","patternReading":"やすい／にくいです","coreMeaning":"Easy to ~ / difficult to ~ (describing an action or object's quality)","slug":"forms","definition":"Take **Vます**, remove **ます**, then add **やすい** or **にくい**. The result is an **い-adjective**, so it conjugates normally for tense and negation. For example, 書きます → 書き → 書きやすい / 書きにくい.","nuance":"This is a basic N4 pattern, not honorific; add です for politeness. Attach it only to the Vます stem, not to Vて or Vない.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"やすい","note":"Easy to ~ / tends to ~"},{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"にくい","note":"Difficult to ~ / not easy to ~"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"書きやすい","r":"かきやすい","m":"easy to write"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This kanji is easy to write.","scene":"Praising a kanji with simple strokes."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"かばん","m":"bag"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"開けにくい","r":"あけにくい","m":"difficult to open"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This bag is difficult to open.","scene":"The stiff lock takes several tries."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-057||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vやすい／にくいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／にくいです","patternReading":"やすい／にくいです","coreMeaning":"Easy to ~ / difficult to ~ (describing an action or object's quality)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **comment on an object or activity**: whether it is easy or difficult, convenient or obstructed. やすい can also express a **natural tendency**: 忘れやすい, 壊れやすい.","nuance":"Do not use it to express personal inability ('I can't do it'). Use にくい for difficulty caused by the nature or conditions of something. For bad habits or tendencies, use やすい.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"アプリ","m":"app"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"使いやすい","r":"つかいやすい","m":"easy to use"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This app is easy to use.","scene":"Recommending a useful app to a friend."},{"tokens":[{"t":"最近","r":"さいきん","m":"recently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"忘れやすい","r":"わすれやすい","m":"prone to forgetting"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I've been forgetful lately.","scene":"Complaining to a coworker about forgetfulness."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"道","r":"みち","m":"road"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"歩きにくい","r":"あるきにくい","m":"difficult to walk on"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This road is difficult to walk on.","scene":"The road is full of potholes."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-057||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused forms","sub":"Vやすい／にくいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／にくいです","patternReading":"やすい／にくいです","coreMeaning":"Easy to ~ / difficult to ~ (describing an action or object's quality)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 〜やすい／〜にくい from 可能形 to avoid focusing on the wrong meaning. 可能形 describes the performer's ability, while 〜やすい／〜にくi describes an object's or action's quality. This core contrast prevents common communication errors. For example, この本は読めます (I can read this book) differs from この本は読みやすいです (this book is easy to read).","nuance":"With 可能形, the subject is a person who has the ability; with 〜やすい/にくい, the subject remains a thing or activity whose ease or difficulty is described.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ペン","m":"pen"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"書きにくい","r":"かきにくい","m":"difficult to write with"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This pen is difficult to write with, though it is still usable—not the same as '書けません'.","scene":"Saying the pen is hard to use but not impossible."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V可能形","point":"可能形 = a person CAN do V; Vやすい = a thing/act is EASY to V. Example: この本は読めます (I can read it) ≠ この本は読みやすいです (this book is easy to read)."},{"vs":"V可能形（否定: 〜られません）","point":"Vにくい does not mean 'completely impossible'; it means 'difficult or awkward'. 書けません = cannot write; 書きにくい = difficult to write, but still possible with effort."}]}},"n4-057||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vやすい／にくいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／にくいです","patternReading":"やすい／にくいです","coreMeaning":"Easy to ~ / difficult to ~ (describing an action or object's quality)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Typical mistakes learners make with this pattern include confusing the connecting form (using Vて instead of Vます), using にくい to express personal inability, or adding an unnecessary particle. Master the structure to avoid these errors. A typical example: この本を読んでにくいです → wrong; この本は読みにくいです → correct.","nuance":"Remember to use the Vます stem; do not use にくい instead of the potential form; when evaluating an object, 私に is unnecessary.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"機械","r":"きかい","m":"machine"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"使いにくい","r":"つかいにくい","m":"difficult to use"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This machine is difficult to use.","scene":"The office printer is hard to operate."}],"pitfalls":["❌ この本を読んでにくいです。 → ✅ この本は読みにくいです。","❌ 私は日本語が話しにくいです。 → ✅ 私は日本語を話すのがまだ苦手です。","❌ このペンは書くのが難しいです。 → ✅ このペンは書きにくいです。","❌ このパソコンは私に使いにくいです。 → ✅ このパソコンは使いにくいです。"],"compare":null}},"n4-057||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vやすい／にくいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／にくいです","patternReading":"やすい／にくいです","coreMeaning":"Easy to ~ / difficult to ~ (describing an action or object's quality)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine やすい as sounding like 'easy to use' in Vietnamese, suggesting that something is easy to do. Imagine にくい as a 'snag' that creates resistance or difficulty.","nuance":"When you see a verb, remove ます and attach やすい/にくい, like 'read-easy' or 'write-hard'.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-057||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vやすい／にくいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vやすい／にくいです","patternReading":"やすい／にくいです","coreMeaning":"Easy to ~ / difficult to ~ (describing an action or object's quality)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Here are common やすい/にくい phrases that appear often on tests and in everyday conversation.","nuance":"Learn them as chunks for faster recall; you do not need to analyze the grammar every time you speak.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"間違えやすい","r":"まちがえやすい","m":"easy to confuse"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This kanji is easy to mistake when writing.","scene":"A teacher warns students about a commonly mistaken kanji."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-058||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V方 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V方","patternReading":"かた","coreMeaning":"How to ~ / method of ~","slug":"core","definition":"V方（かた）means the **way or method** of performing an action. Take the ます-form, **remove ます**, and add **方**: 使います → 使い方 ('how to use'), 作ります → 作り方 ('how to make'). This is a highly productive way to turn verbs into nouns in Japanese.","nuance":"This expression is neutral and works in both everyday conversation and writing. When asking how to do something, it often appears with 教えてください, 知っていますか, or 調べます. It is used with verbs; with nouns, use 'N + の + V方' (e.g. 漢字の読み方).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"読み方","r":"よみかた","m":"how to read"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please teach me how to read this kanji.","scene":"In class, a learner asks the teacher how to read a new kanji."},{"tokens":[{"t":"パソコン","m":"computer"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"使い方","r":"つかいかた","m":"how to use"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかりません","m":"do not understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't understand how to use a computer.","scene":"A beginner feels confused while using a computer."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-058||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and combinations","sub":"V方 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V方","patternReading":"かた","coreMeaning":"How to ~ / method of ~","slug":"forms","definition":"There is one basic combination: **Vます stem + 方**. Because the result is a noun, it can take particles such as を (object), が (subject), and は (topic).","nuance":"With する, Japanese usually uses **N + の仕方（しかた）** rather than *し方*: 勉強の仕方, 料理の仕方. In fixed compounds such as 使い方 and 読み方, the base verb is not する, so apply the normal rule.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます stem（Vます から ます を 取る）","rhs":"+ 方（かた）","note":"This applies to all verbs, e.g. 使います→使い方."},{"lhs":"N + の + Vます stem","rhs":"+ 方（かた）","note":"When modifying a noun: 漢字の読み方, 駅までの行き方."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"作り方","r":"つくりかた","m":"how to make"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知っています","r":"しっています","m":"know"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know how to make this dish?","scene":"Asking friends for a recipe."},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"行き方","r":"いきかた","m":"how to get"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"tell"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please tell me how to get to the station.","scene":"A lost traveler asks a local for directions."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-058||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"V方 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V方","patternReading":"かた","coreMeaning":"How to ~ / method of ~","slug":"usage","definition":"V方 is used to **ask about and explain methods**. For directions, ask 駅までの行き方を教えてください. A reply might be 行き方はまっすぐ行くだけです. In study contexts: 読み方・書き方. At work: 使い方・作り方.","nuance":"It is not especially formal. For a more polite request, use 〜方を教えていただけませんか. Casually, use 〜方、知ってる？","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"スマホ","m":"smartphone"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"使い方","r":"つかいかた","m":"how to use"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"簡単","r":"かんたん","m":"simple"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Using a smartphone is simple.","scene":"Explaining smartphone use to an older person."},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"書き方","r":"かきかた","m":"how to write"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Writing Japanese is difficult.","scene":"A foreign learner complains about writing Japanese."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-058||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"V方 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V方","patternReading":"かた","coreMeaning":"How to ~ / method of ~","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick distinction: V方 answers **どうやって?** (how?), while V可能形 answers **できる?** (can you?). Vこと refers to the action itself; V方 refers to the way or method.","nuance":"The easiest pair to confuse is V方 and Vことができます. Remember: 〜方 means 'how to', not 'can'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"泳ぎ方","r":"およぎかた","m":"how to swim"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please teach me how to swim.","scene":"Asking a swimmer friend for instruction."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V可能形（読めます）","point":"V方 is a noun for a method; V可能形 is a verb for ability. E.g. この漢字の読み方を教えてください ('teach me how to read it') vs この漢字が読めます ('I can read it')."},{"vs":"Vこと","point":"Vこと nominalizes an action (the act of ~); V方 describes how to do it. E.g. 泳ぐことはできます ('I can swim') vs 泳ぎ方を教えてください ('teach me how to swim')."}]}},"n4-058||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V方 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V方","patternReading":"かた","coreMeaning":"How to ~ / method of ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include: 1) forgetting to remove ます, 2) reading 方 as 'ほう', 3) attaching 方 directly to a noun, and 4) using 'し方' instead of 'N + の仕方'. Watch for these errors.","nuance":"Mistakes 1 and 3 are the most common. Japanese listeners may still understand you, but the result sounds unnatural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"書き方","r":"かきかた","m":"how to write"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I practice how to write kanji.","scene":"A learner talks about practicing writing every day."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べます方を知っています。 → ✅ 食べ方を知っています。 (remove ます before adding 方)","❌ 漢字の読みほう → ✅ 漢字の読み方（かた） (方 in V方 is read かた, not ほう)","❌ 料理方が難しい。 → ✅ 料理の作り方が難しい。 (use noun + の + V方, not noun + 方)","❌ 勉強し方を覚えました。 → ✅ 勉強の仕方を覚えました。 (with する, N + の仕方 is common)"],"compare":null}},"n4-058||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V方 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V方","patternReading":"かた","coreMeaning":"How to ~ / method of ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: 方 means 'method' or 'way', as in 方法, so V方 means the 'method of doing V'. Mechanically: **remove ます, add かた**. Say these aloud a few times: 使い方（つかいかた）、読み方（よみかた）、書き方（かきかた）、作り方（つくりかた）.","nuance":"This is a memory aid rather than an additional nuance.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-058||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V方 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V方","patternReading":"かた","coreMeaning":"How to ~ / method of ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common 〜方 expressions in the JLPT and daily life include 使い方 (how to use), 読み方 (how to read), 書き方 (how to write), 作り方 (how to make), and 行き方 (how to get there). Memorizing them helps you respond quickly.","nuance":"In N4 tests, 使い方 and 作り方 often appear in reading passages. Pay attention to the readings: 使い方(つかいかた), 行き方(いきかた).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"アプリ","m":"app"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"使い方","r":"つかいかた","m":"how to use"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"show"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please show me how to use this app.","scene":"After installing an app, you ask a coworker how to use it."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-059||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V／A／Nのばあいは · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／A／Nのばあいは","patternReading":"ばあいは","coreMeaning":"In the case of ~ (hypothetical condition/guidance)","slug":"core","definition":"〜場合は expresses “in the case of” ~—a hypothetical condition or specific situation followed by guidance, a rule, or a request. This pattern focuses on the circumstance that needs to be handled rather than a general condition.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, but formal in tone. It often appears in notices, regulations, and official documents. The tone is objective and does not express emotion. Compared with 〜ば or 〜なら, 場合は is more specific and rule-oriented.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"遅刻","r":"ちこく","m":"be late"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"場合","r":"ばあい","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"事前","r":"じぜん","m":"in advance"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In the case of being late, please contact us in advance.","scene":"A company notice about the rule for lateness."},{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"場合","r":"ばあい","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"中止","r":"ちゅうし","m":"cancel"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In the case of rain, the match will be canceled.","scene":"A sports club notice about bad weather."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-059||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V／A／Nのばあいは · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／A／Nのばあいは","patternReading":"ばあいは","coreMeaning":"In the case of ~ (hypothetical condition/guidance)","slug":"forms","definition":"〜場合は follows the plain form (Vる/Vた/Vない), i-adjectives, na-adjectives (keeping な), and nouns + の. Do not use the ます or です forms before 場合. The main clause is usually guidance, a request, or a decision.","nuance":"This is a fixed, rule-oriented structure. The following clause often uses 〜てください, 〜なければなりません, or the plain form in written texts.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"場合は","note":"Dictionary-form verb. Example: 遅刻する場合は"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"場合は","note":"i-adjective. Example: 忙しい場合は"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"場合は","note":"na-adjective keeping な. Example: 静かな場合は"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"の場合は","note":"Noun + の. Example: 雨の場合は"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"熱","r":"ねつ","m":"fever"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"場合","r":"ばあい","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"休んで","r":"やすんで","m":"take off"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you have a fever, please stay home from school.","scene":"A message to a student’s parents."},{"tokens":[{"t":"体調","r":"たいちょう","m":"condition"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"悪い","r":"わるい","m":"bad"},{"t":"場合","r":"ばあい","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"strain yourself"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しないで","m":"do not"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you are not feeling well, please do not overexert yourself.","scene":"A doctor’s advice to a patient."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-059||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"V／A／Nのばあいは · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／A／Nのばあいは","patternReading":"ばあいは","coreMeaning":"In the case of ~ (hypothetical condition/guidance)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜場合は states a specific situation and then gives guidance, a rule, or a request. It often appears in company rules, safety instructions, school notices, and medical leaflets. This pattern makes the sentence clear and objective.","nuance":"This pattern is formal and is not common in casual conversation. With friends, 〜ば or 〜なら is more natural. 場合は suits written instructions and official notices. The following clause is often a polite command (〜てください) or polite form (〜ます).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"火事","r":"かじ","m":"fire"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"場合","r":"ばあい","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"すぐに","m":"immediately"},{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"出て","r":"でて","m":"go out"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In the event of a fire, please go outside immediately.","scene":"An evacuation sign in a building."},{"tokens":[{"t":"質問","r":"しつもん","m":"question"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"場合","r":"ばあい","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"手","r":"て","m":"hand"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"挙げて","r":"あげて","m":"raise"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you have a question, please raise your hand.","scene":"A teacher speaking to students in class."},{"tokens":[{"t":"駐車場","r":"ちゅうしゃじょう","m":"parking lot"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"満車","r":"まんしゃ","m":"full"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"場合","r":"ばあい","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"隣","r":"となり","m":"next door"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"駐車場","r":"ちゅうしゃじょう","m":"parking lot"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使って","r":"つかって","m":"use"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If the parking lot is full, please use the neighboring lot.","scene":"A sign at a shopping center."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-059||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing 〜ば / 〜なら","sub":"V／A／Nのばあいは · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／A／Nのばあいは","patternReading":"ばあいは","coreMeaning":"In the case of ~ (hypothetical condition/guidance)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare 〜場合は with other conditional patterns to avoid using it in the wrong context. The key differences are formality and how the situation is emphasized.","nuance":"〜ば is common in conversation and expresses a general condition. 〜なら often responds to a suggestion or topic. 〜と expresses a natural law or inevitable result. 場合は is for a hypothetical situation requiring action, not for general rules.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"場合","r":"ばあい","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"事前","r":"じぜん","m":"in advance"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"して"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you are going, please contact us in advance.","scene":"A club notice about a field trip."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ば (nếu ~)","point":"〜ば expresses a general condition and is common in conversation. 場合は emphasizes a specific, formal situation. Example: 雨が降れば、試合をやめます (if it rains, we will cancel it in general) vs 雨の場合は、試合を中止します (a specific rule)."},{"vs":"〜なら (nếu là)","point":"〜なら is used in conversation to respond to a topic or make a suggestion. 場合は is used in written instructions. Example: 行くなら、早く行きなさい (if you are going, go early) vs 行く場合は、事前に連絡してください (if you go, contact us in advance)."},{"vs":"〜と (nếu ~, thì ~)","point":"〜と expresses an inevitable result or rule. 場合は describes a hypothetical situation, not a rule. Example: 春になると、桜が咲きます (when spring comes, cherry blossoms bloom) vs 熱がある場合は、学校を休みます."}]}},"n4-059||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V／A／Nのばあいは · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／A／Nのばあいは","patternReading":"ばあいは","coreMeaning":"In the case of ~ (hypothetical condition/guidance)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes learners make with 〜場合は include using the wrong verb form, omitting の after a noun, or confusing it with ば in formal contexts.","nuance":"Remember: before 場合, use only the dictionary form, an adjective, or noun + の. Never use the ます-form or た-form.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"分からない","r":"わからない","m":"not understand"},{"t":"場合","r":"ばあい","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"ask"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you do not understand, please ask your teacher.","scene":"A teacher’s advice to students."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 遅刻します場合は → ✅ 遅刻する場合は (do not use the ます-form before 場合)","❌ 雨場合は → ✅ 雨の場合は (a noun must take の before 場合)","❌ あります場合は → ✅ ある場合は (use the dictionary form, not the ます-form)","❌ 静か場合は → ✅ 静かな場合は (keep な before 場合)"],"compare":null}},"n4-059||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V／A／Nのばあいは · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／A／Nのばあいは","patternReading":"ばあいは","coreMeaning":"In the case of ~ (hypothetical condition/guidance)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: 場合 (ばあい) means “case.” When you encounter a specific situation, open the corresponding “page” in a guidebook and follow the instructions. The pattern takes Vる, Aい, Aな, or Nの—like “in the case of + noun.”","nuance":"Picture signs in Japan saying 火事の場合は...—each sign presents a specific situation.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-059||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"V／A／Nのばあいは · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V／A／Nのばあいは","patternReading":"ばあいは","coreMeaning":"In the case of ~ (hypothetical condition/guidance)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with 場合は that appear in JLPT questions and real-world texts. Memorizing them helps you recognize the pattern quickly.","nuance":"These phrases often appear in notices, regulations, and safety instructions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"熱","r":"ねつ","m":"fever"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"have"},{"t":"場合","r":"ばあい","m":"case"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"休みます","r":"やすみます","m":"take off"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I have a fever, I will take the day off work.","scene":"Messaging your boss to request leave."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-060||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜のに (逆接) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜のに (逆接)","patternReading":"のに","coreMeaning":"Although ~, ~ (an opposite, unexpected result)","slug":"core","definition":"のに connects two clauses with an **opposite relationship**: clause A presents a fact or condition that normally creates an expectation, while clause B is the actual result that is **not as expected**. It carries a subjective tone: the speaker feels surprised, confused, or slightly annoyed. Unlike けど, のに emphasizes “why did that happen?” and is often used to complain.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. It can end an unfinished sentence: 「時間があるのに…」 to express regret. The following clause cannot be a command, invitation, or the speaker’s intention.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たくさん","r":"たくさん","m":"a lot"},{"t":"練習","r":"れんしゅう","m":"practice"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"負けました","r":"まけました","m":"lost"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although we practiced a lot, we lost the match.","scene":"A team practiced hard but still lost"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"9時","r":"くじ","m":"9 o’clock"},{"t":"なのに","r":"なのに","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まだ","r":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"来ません","r":"きません","m":"has not come"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is already 9 o’clock, but he still has not arrived.","scene":"The other person has not appeared by the agreed time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-060||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜のに (逆接) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜のに (逆接)","patternReading":"のに","coreMeaning":"Although ~, ~ (an opposite, unexpected result)","slug":"forms","definition":"Before のに, always use the **plain form** (普通形). **な-adjectives** and **nouns** must add **な** before のに. Negative and past forms also use their corresponding plain forms.","nuance":"Polite forms such as ましたのに／でしたのに may appear in writing, but modern conversation usually uses the shorter form: のに. のに itself does not change; the meaning comes from the preceding clause.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（plain form）","rhs":"のに","note":"例：行くのに / 行かないのに / 行ったのに"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"のに","note":"例：安いのに / 安くなかったのに"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"のに","note":"例：静かなのに / 静かじゃないのに"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"な ＋ のに","note":"例：学生なのに / 学生じゃないのに"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"スマホ","r":"すまほ","m":"phone"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"安かった","r":"やすかった","m":"was cheap"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although this phone is new, it was cheap.","scene":"A shop sells a new phone at a low price"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"なのに","r":"なのに","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"とても","r":"とても","m":"very"},{"t":"落ち着いて","r":"おちついて","m":"calm"},{"t":"います","r":"います"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although she is a child, she is very calm.","scene":"A younger sister is unusually calm for a child"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-060||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜のに (逆接) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜のに (逆接)","patternReading":"のに","coreMeaning":"Although ~, ~ (an opposite, unexpected result)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when an event occurs contrary to what people think or expect. The sentence often sounds like a complaint, regret, or surprise. In conversation, listeners can often infer the unwanted result from the first clause alone.","nuance":"The following clause is usually an event that happened or a state in progress. To express strong emotion without finishing the sentence, Japanese speakers often stop at のに: 「せっかく来たのに…」(I went all that way, but…).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"せっかく","r":"せっかく","m":"especially"},{"t":"作った","r":"つくった","m":"made"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"くれなかった","r":"くれなかった","m":"did not eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went to the trouble of making it, but he did not eat it.","scene":"A husband leaves a lovingly cooked meal untouched"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"起きる","r":"おきる","m":"get up"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"なのに","r":"なのに","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","r":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"眠い","r":"ねむい","m":"sleepy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is already time to get up, but I am still sleepy.","scene":"The alarm rings, but your eyes remain heavy"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-060||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"〜のに (逆接) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜のに (逆接)","patternReading":"のに","coreMeaning":"Although ~, ~ (an opposite, unexpected result)","slug":"compare","definition":"N4 learners often confuse のに with けど, ので, and ても. The key point: **のに carries a sense of surprise**—it does not merely connect contrasting clauses; it shows that the speaker finds the result unreasonable.","nuance":"This section compares only the three patterns most easily confused with のに at this level.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降っている","r":"ふっている","m":"is raining"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出かけました","r":"でかけました","m":"went out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was raining, yet they went out.","scene":"Seeing a neighbor go out in heavy rain"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜けど／が","point":"けど simply connects contrasting information in a neutral tone; のに adds surprise or irritation: 雨だけど出かけた (a neutral account) → 雨なのに出かけた (it was raining, yet they went out—how unreasonable!)."},{"vs":"〜ので","point":"ので connects a reasonable cause with its result (because…so…); のに connects an unexpected result. Same situation: 時間がなかったので行けなかった (I could not go because I had no time) — 時間があったのに 行けなかった (I had time, yet I could not go)."},{"vs":"〜ても","point":"ても means “even if X, the result remains the same,” and may be hypothetical; のに describes a fact that really happened with an opposite result. 高くても買う (I will buy it even if expensive) → 高いのに買った (it was expensive, yet I bought it, with regret)."}]}},"n4-060||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Typical learner errors","sub":"〜のに (逆接) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜のに (逆接)","patternReading":"のに","coreMeaning":"Although ~, ~ (an opposite, unexpected result)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors with のに include: (1) omitting な after nouns and な-adjectives, as in incorrect 学生のに instead of 学生なのに; (2) using a command, invitation, or intention in the following clause, even though のに requires a real result; and (3) confusing のに with ので—ので gives a reason, while のに shows an opposite result. Read each incorrect→correct pair aloud to feel the difference.","nuance":"The incorrect examples are marked ❌ and the correct forms ✅. Read each pair aloud to notice the nuance.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちゃんと","r":"ちゃんと","m":"properly"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"のに","r":"のに","m":"although"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"テスト","r":"てすと","m":"test"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"できませんでした","r":"できませんでした","m":"could not do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Although I studied properly, I still could not do the test.","scene":"Talking to a desk mate after getting the test back"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 学生のに、車を買った。 → ✅ 学生なのに、車を買った。 (A noun before のに must add な)","❌ 雨が降っているのに、出かけましょう。 → ✅ 雨が降っているのに、出かけました。 (The following clause cannot be an invitation, command, or suggestion)","❌ 風が強いので、海へ行きました。 (intended contrast) → ✅ 風が強いのに、海へ行きました。 (It was very windy, yet they went to the beach)","❌ 高いのに、買うつもりです。 → ✅ 高いのに、買いました。 (The following clause should state a concrete event, not a present intention)"],"compare":null}},"n4-060||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"〜のに (逆接) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜のに (逆接)","patternReading":"のに","coreMeaning":"Although ~, ~ (an opposite, unexpected result)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember のに by linking it to “and yet.” Whenever you want to complain “...and yet...,” のに is often the right pattern.","nuance":"You do not need to remember the grammatical origin. Build the reflex: two conflicting clauses plus surprise or emotion → think of のに.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-061||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vるところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるところです","patternReading":"るところです","coreMeaning":"About to do ~ (just before the action starts)","slug":"core","definition":"〜Vるところです describes the moment **just before an action starts**: 'about to do something.' The action **has not happened yet** — the speaker is at the starting line. This pattern is common when someone asks what you are about to do.","nuance":"The tone is **objective and informative**, reporting an upcoming state without strong emotion. It works with friends and superiors, in conversation and gentle written language. In the past, **Vるところでした** adds the meaning 'I almost ~, but it did not happen.'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょうど"},{"t":"出かける","r":"でかける","m":"go out"},{"t":"ところです","r":"ところです","m":"about to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am just about to go out.","scene":"In the morning, your mother asks, 'You haven't left yet?' — you reply."},{"tokens":[{"t":"今から","r":"いまから","m":"from now"},{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"ところです","r":"ところです","m":"about to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am about to eat lunch now.","scene":"At lunchtime, a coworker asks, 'Have you eaten?' — you reply."},{"tokens":[{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone call"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","m":"make"},{"t":"ところでした","r":"ところでした","m":"almost"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was about to call you (but you called first).","scene":"You are about to call someone when they call first."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-061||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vるところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるところです","patternReading":"るところです","coreMeaning":"About to do ~ (just before the action starts)","slug":"forms","definition":"Standard formula: **V dictionary form + ところです**. The verb stays in its plain dictionary form (Vる); do not use the ます-form or て-form. In the past, change **です → でした**.","nuance":"This pattern has no direct negative form. To say 'have not done something yet,' Japanese uses **まだVていません**.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる（thể từ điển）","rhs":"ところです","note":"About to do ~ (now / very soon)"},{"lhs":"Vる（thể từ điển）","rhs":"ところでした","note":"Almost did ~ (usually did not happen)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もうすぐ"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"house"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出る","r":"でる","m":"leave"},{"t":"ところです","r":"ところです","m":"about to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am about to leave the house.","scene":"Texting a classmate in the morning."},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"聞く","r":"きく","m":"ask"},{"t":"ところでした","r":"ところでした","m":"almost"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I almost asked the teacher (but decided not to).","scene":"You are about to ask the teacher, then remember the answer."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-061||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vるところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるところです","patternReading":"るところです","coreMeaning":"About to do ~ (just before the action starts)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **announce the moment just before an action begins**. The listener understands that you are preparing to do it and will usually avoid interrupting. It can also answer the question: 'What are you about to do?'","nuance":"Combine it with **今から**, **ちょうど**, **もうすぐ**, or **そろそろ** to emphasize that the action is imminent. In the past, **Vるところでした** often appears in stories about something that 'almost happened,' creating surprise or relief.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"始まる","r":"はじまる","m":"start"},{"t":"ところです","r":"ところです","m":"about to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The movie is about to start.","scene":"In the theater, the advertisements have just ended."},{"tokens":[{"t":"お風呂","r":"おふろ","m":"bath"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入る","r":"はいる","m":"take"},{"t":"ところです","r":"ところです","m":"about to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am about to take a bath.","scene":"Your younger brother asks you to play a video game."},{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"降りる","r":"おりる","m":"get off"},{"t":"ところでした","r":"ところでした","m":"almost"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I almost got off the train (but realized it was the wrong stop).","scene":"On the train, you suddenly realize it is not your stop yet."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-061||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare related patterns","sub":"Vるところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるところです","patternReading":"るところです","coreMeaning":"About to do ~ (just before the action starts)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish these three important ところ patterns: **Vるところ** (about to do), **Vているところ** (in the middle of doing), and **Vたところ** (just finished doing). Their positions on the timeline are the key.","nuance":"These three patterns often appear on N4 tests because they are easy to confuse. Remembering **before – during – after** makes the contrast clear.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これから"},{"t":"出かける","r":"でかける","m":"go out"},{"t":"ところです","r":"ところです","m":"about to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am about to go out now.","scene":"You tell your family before leaving the house."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vているところです","point":"Vるところ = SẮP làm (chưa bắt đầu); Vているところ = ĐANG làm (giữa chừng). 食べるところです (sắp ăn) ≠ 食べているところです (đang ăn)."},{"vs":"Vたところです","point":"Vるところ = SẮP làm; Vたところ = VỪA mới làm xong. 出かけるところです (sắp ra ngoài) ≠ 出かけたところです (vừa ra ngoài)."}]}},"n4-061||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vるところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるところです","patternReading":"るところです","coreMeaning":"About to do ~ (just before the action starts)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using the wrong verb form and confusing this pattern with other **ところ** patterns.","nuance":"Quick fix: if the meaning is 'about to do,' the verb before **ところ** must be in the **dictionary form**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そろそろ"},{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"sleep"},{"t":"ところです","r":"ところです","m":"about to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am about to go to bed.","scene":"You tell your younger brother, who is trying to get you to play."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 出かけますところです。 → ✅ 出かけるところです。 (use the dictionary form, not Vます)","❌ 食べているところです、今から。 → ✅ 食べるところです。 (Vている means 'in progress,' so it does not fit 'about to')","❌ 今から食べたところです。 → ✅ 今から食べるところです。 (Vた means 'just finished,' which conflicts with 今から)"],"compare":null}},"n4-061||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"Vるところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるところです","patternReading":"るところです","coreMeaning":"About to do ~ (just before the action starts)","slug":"memory","definition":"Visualize **ところ** as a point on the timeline. Put the verb in **Vる** before that point → the action is about to begin.","nuance":"Do not think of ところ only as a 'place.' Think 'point in time' and remember: **Vる + ところ = ABOUT TO do**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-061||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vるところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vるところです","patternReading":"るところです","coreMeaning":"About to do ~ (just before the action starts)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These common combinations with **Vるところです** will help you understand and use the pattern naturally.","nuance":"Basic verbs such as **出かける**, **食べる**, **寝る**, and **始まる** frequently appear with this pattern in everyday life.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"ところです","r":"ところです","m":"about to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am about to eat now.","scene":"Friends invite you out during the lunch break."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-062||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vているところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vているところです","patternReading":"ているところです","coreMeaning":"In the middle of ~ right now","slug":"core","definition":"〜ているところです describes an action happening at the exact moment of speaking, emphasizing that it is still in progress and unfinished. It is often used to answer 『今、何をしていますか』 or explain why someone must wait.","nuance":"This is fairly polite and neutral, usable in speech and writing. Compared with ordinary 〜ています, 〜ているところ feels like 'right now, in the middle of it'—for example, when called while working, 『今、調べているところです』 sounds more natural than 『今、調べています』.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"レポート","m":"report"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いている","r":"かいている","m":"writing"},{"t":"ところです","m":"in progress"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'm writing a report right now.","scene":"Your boss calls while you are writing a report"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"meal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作っている","r":"つくっている","m":"cooking"},{"t":"ところです","m":"in progress"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'm cooking right now.","scene":"Your roommate asks what you are doing while you cook"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-062||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and connected forms","sub":"Vているところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vているところです","patternReading":"ているところです","coreMeaning":"In the middle of ~ right now","slug":"forms","definition":"Required structure: **Vて-form + いる + ところです**. The verb must be in the て-form, followed by いる and then ところです. ところ is a noun meaning 'time' or 'point,' so it is preceded by Vている as a modifying clause.","nuance":"This is the common polite form. In casual conversation, Japanese speakers often shorten **〜ているところです** to **〜ているとこ**. In the past, **〜ているところでした** means 'was in the middle of … when something else happened.'","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて-form","rhs":"いる＋ところです","note":"Present: in the middle of V"},{"lhs":"Vて-form","rhs":"いる＋とこ","note":"Casual; です omitted"},{"lhs":"Vて-form","rhs":"いる＋ところでした","note":"Past: was V-ing when…"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"待って","r":"まって","m":"wait"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"メール","m":"email"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いている","r":"かいている","m":"writing"},{"t":"ところです","m":"in progress"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please wait a moment. I'm writing an email.","scene":"Someone knocks while you are writing an email"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昼ご飯","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べている","r":"たべている","m":"eating"},{"t":"ところでした","m":"was in progress"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was eating lunch then.","scene":"You call, and the person explains why they missed it"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-062||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"Vているところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vているところです","patternReading":"ているところです","coreMeaning":"In the middle of ~ right now","slug":"usage","definition":"The main use is to emphasize that an action is happening right now and is unfinished. It is commonly used in three situations: (1) answering an unexpected question, (2) asking someone to wait or apologizing for being busy, and (3) reporting work progress.","nuance":"Use 今 or ちょうど to clarify 'right now.' At work, this is a common polite pattern for reporting progress: ただいま、〜しているところです.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"掃除している","r":"そうじしている","m":"cleaning"},{"t":"ところです","m":"in progress"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'm cleaning right now.","scene":"You are cleaning when a phone call comes in"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"チーム","m":"team"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"話し合っている","r":"はなしあっている","m":"discussing"},{"t":"ところです","m":"in progress"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We are discussing it right now.","scene":"Your boss asks about project progress during a team meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-062||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with るところ / たところ","sub":"Vているところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vているところです","patternReading":"ているところです","coreMeaning":"In the middle of ~ right now","slug":"compare","definition":"This pattern is easy to confuse with the related patterns **〜るところ** and **〜たところ**. All three use ところ to refer to a point in time, but they mark different stages: before starting, in the middle, and after finishing.","nuance":"The difference lies in the form of V before ところ: Vる = about to do, Vている = doing, Vた = just finished. Together they form a near past–present–future sequence around one time point.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食べている","r":"たべている","m":"eating"},{"t":"ところです","m":"in progress"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'm eating right now.","scene":"You are eating when someone asks what you are doing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜るところです","point":"About to V; not started. E.g. 食べるところです = about to eat; 食べているところです = eating."},{"vs":"〜たところです","point":"Just finished V. E.g. 食べたところです = just ate; 食べているところです = eating."},{"vs":"〜ています","point":"Simply says V-ing, without emphasizing the middle. Add ところ for 'right now, unfinished.'"}]}},"n4-062||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes and traps","sub":"Vているところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vているところです","patternReading":"ているところです","coreMeaning":"In the middle of ~ right now","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes Vietnamese learners make with 〜ているところです. Pay special attention to **omitting いる** and confusing the meaning of Vるところ.","nuance":"This confusion comes from Vietnamese 'đang ăn,' which does not distinguish 'about to eat' from 'in the middle of eating.' Learners often say 食べるところです for 'I'm eating,' but it actually means 'I'm about to eat.'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"している","m":"doing"},{"t":"ところです","m":"in progress"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'm doing my homework.","scene":"Your mother asks what you are doing while you study"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 今、食べるところです。 → ✅ 今、食べているところです。（食べるところ = about to eat, not eating）","❌ 毎日走っているところです。 → ✅ 毎日走っています。（Do not use 〜ているところ for habits）","❌ テレビを見てところです。 → ✅ テレビを見ているところです。（Without いる, the 'in progress' meaning is lost）","Note: 知っている、住んでいる describe states, so adding ところ sounds unnatural. Say: ここに住んでいます。"],"compare":null}},"n4-062||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vているところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vているところです","patternReading":"ているところです","coreMeaning":"In the middle of ~ right now","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine ところ as a **dot** on a timeline. Vている is an action in motion; adding ところ means **standing right in the middle of that action**.","nuance":"Remembering 'in the middle of doing' helps you avoid confusing 〜るところ (about to begin) with 〜たところ (just finished).","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-062||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vているところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vているところです","patternReading":"ているところです","coreMeaning":"In the middle of ~ right now","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases used with 〜ているところです in work and daily life include 調べる, 考える, 話し合う, 確認する, and 掃除する.","nuance":"They often include 今, ちょうど, or ただいま to emphasize 'right now.' This sounds very polite when reporting to a supervisor.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ただいま","m":"right now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"確認している","r":"かくにんしている","m":"checking"},{"t":"ところです","m":"in progress"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'm checking it right now.","scene":"Replying to a customer while checking information"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-063||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vたところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたところです","patternReading":"たところです","coreMeaning":"Just finished ~ moments ago (objective)","slug":"core","definition":"Vたところです describes an action that has **just been completed** immediately before speaking. The focus is on **timing**—the action happened very recently, and the speaker looks back from the point 'after completion.' This is an **objective** way to report that something has 'just finished.'","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing; neutral and polite. It does not express strong emotion or evaluation. Unlike 〜たばかり, which emphasizes the feeling that something is 'still new,' 〜たところ simply states when it happened. To stress 'just now,' add **たった今/ちょうど**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たった今","r":"たったいま","m":"just now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"着い","r":"つい","m":"arrive"},{"t":"たところです","m":"just finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just arrived here.","scene":"A coworker asks why you arrived late as you enter."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょうど","m":"just"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"やり終え","r":"やりおえ","m":"finish"},{"t":"たところです","m":"just finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just finished my homework.","scene":"Your mother asks whether your homework is done."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-063||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"Vたところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたところです","patternReading":"たところです","coreMeaning":"Just finished ~ moments ago (objective)","slug":"forms","definition":"Vたところです is formed with **V in the た-form** + **ところ** + **です**. ところ normally means 'place,' but here it functions as a **time point**, marking the moment something has just ended. **でした** is used when recounting this from the past: 'had just finished then.'","nuance":"There is no common negative form. To express something not yet finished, use **まだVていません**. Do not use まだ in the same sentence with this pattern. Because ところ is a noun, it must follow a clause in the た-form.","structure":[{"lhs":"V (thể た)","rhs":"ところです","note":"An action just completed"},{"lhs":"たった今 / ちょうど + Vた","rhs":"ところです","note":"Emphasizes 'just now'"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"ところでした","note":"Recounts 'had just finished' in the past"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たった今","r":"たったいま","m":"just now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"昼ご飯","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"たところです","m":"just finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just finished lunch.","scene":"A friend asks if you have eaten lunch."},{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"始まっ","r":"はじまっ","m":"begin"},{"t":"たところです","m":"just finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The meeting has just started.","scene":"You enter late and ask about the meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-063||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Vたところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたところです","patternReading":"たところです","coreMeaning":"Just finished ~ moments ago (objective)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use Vたところです to **report** that an action was completed immediately before speaking, often to explain the current situation. It is common in everyday conversation, the workplace, and phone calls.","nuance":"Polite and emotionally neutral. It often appears with たった今 or ちょうど to emphasize 'just finished.' When answering questions such as '〜ましたか?' or '〜終わりましたか?', たところ stresses that it finished right now.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","m":"sorry"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"出","r":"で","m":"leave"},{"t":"たところです","m":"just finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Sorry, I just left.","scene":"Someone calls asking where you are."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょうど","m":"just"},{"t":"お風呂","r":"おふろ","m":"bath"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"出","r":"で","m":"leave"},{"t":"たところです","m":"just finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just got out of the bath.","scene":"Your mother asks where you are."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-063||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Quick comparison","sub":"Vたところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたところです","patternReading":"たところです","coreMeaning":"Just finished ~ moments ago (objective)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes Vたところ from related time expressions: たばかり, Vるところ, and Vているところ.","nuance":"Key point: **Vたところ** marks 'just finished'; **Vているところ** marks an action 'in progress'; **Vるところ** marks 'about to begin.' **たばかり**, meanwhile, emphasizes the speaker's feeling that something is still new, not the exact time.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たった今","r":"たったいま","m":"just now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"着い","r":"つい","m":"arrive"},{"t":"たところです","m":"just finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just arrived at the station.","scene":"You text a friend after arriving at the station."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜たばかりです","point":"〜たところ objectively marks 'just finished'; 〜たばかり emphasizes that it still feels new to the speaker. Example: 先月着たばかりです (still feels new) vs たった今着たところです (just finished, timing only)."},{"vs":"Vるところです","point":"Vるところ means 'about to do'—the action has not started; Vたところ means 'just finished.' Compare: 今から食べるところです (about to eat) vs たった今食べたところです (just finished eating)."},{"vs":"Vているところです","point":"Vているところ focuses on an action in progress; Vたところ focuses on one that ended immediately before. Compare: レポートを書いているところです (writing now) vs レポートを書いたところです (just finished writing)."}]}},"n4-063||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vたところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたところです","patternReading":"たところです","coreMeaning":"Just finished ~ moments ago (objective)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Typical mistakes learners make with Vたところ include using it with まだ, using it for repeated habits, confusing it with Vるところ (the near future), and confusing its nuance with たばかり.","nuance":"Remember that Vたところ is for a **single event just completed**, tied to a specific time. It is not used for repeated actions or continuing states.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たった今","r":"たったいま","m":"just now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"降り","r":"おり","m":"get off"},{"t":"たところです","m":"just finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just got off the train.","scene":"Texting a friend that you just got off."}],"pitfalls":["❌ まだ着いたところです。 → ✅ まだ着いていません。","❌ 毎日ジョギングをしたところです。 → ✅ 毎日ジョギングをしています。","❌ 今から食べたところです。 → ✅ 今から食べるところです。","❌ 先月日本に来たところです。 → ✅ 先月日本に来たばかりです。 (たところ is only for something completed immediately before, not a distant time such as 先月)"],"compare":null}},"n4-063||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"Vたところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたところです","patternReading":"たところです","coreMeaning":"Just finished ~ moments ago (objective)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: ところ means 'place.' Imagine a **timeline**: the Vた action is an arrow that has passed the ● point, and you are standing right at that point.","nuance":"The phrase 'たった今 + た' creates the feeling of 'just touching' the point, making it easy to remember. Draw a timeline: past ▸ Vた ▸ ● (Vたところ) ▸ present.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-063||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vたところです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたところです","patternReading":"たところです","coreMeaning":"Just finished ~ moments ago (objective)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases with Vたところです that often appear on tests and in conversation.","nuance":"These phrases often combine with たった今 or ちょうど. In conversation, they answer questions about a state that has just been reached.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出","r":"で","m":"leave"},{"t":"たところです","m":"just finished"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just left home.","scene":"On the phone: 'Where are you?' — 'I just left home.'"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-064||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vたばかりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたばかりです","patternReading":"たばかりです","coreMeaning":"Just did ~; it still feels new and unfamiliar","slug":"core","definition":"Vたばかりです describes something that **just happened**, with emphasis on the speaker's **feeling that it is still new**: newly arriving, newly married, recently eating, and so on. The speaker still feels **unfamiliar with or unaccustomed to** the situation. The actual time may be several days or weeks, yet it can still feel 'new'.","nuance":"This is subjective and may convey **surprise, unfamiliarity, or slight awkwardness**. It is used in everyday speech and informal writing. Avoid it for objective reports; use 〜たところ instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来たばかり","r":"きたばかり","m":"just arrived"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","m":"still not"},{"t":"慣れて","r":"なれて","m":"accustomed"},{"t":"いません","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just came to Japan, so I am still not used to it.","scene":"Just arrived in Japan and telling someone you know."},{"tokens":[{"t":"食べたばかり","r":"たべたばかり","m":"just ate"},{"t":"なので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おなか","m":"stomach"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いっぱい","m":"full"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just ate, so I am still full.","scene":"Someone offers more food, but you are still full."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-064||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"Vたばかりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたばかりです","patternReading":"たばかりです","coreMeaning":"Just did ~; it still feels new and unfamiliar","slug":"forms","definition":"ばかり is preceded by a **た-form verb**. At the end of a sentence, the pattern asserts that someone has 'just done something'. It can also come before a noun (VたばかりのN) or connect clauses (Vたばかりで/なので).","nuance":"The negative form is **rarely used**; put the negative meaning in the following clause: 〜たばかりなので、まだ〜ていない。For past narration, use 〜たばかりでした when describing what had just happened at that time.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（た形）＋","rhs":"ばかりです","note":"At the end of a sentence: 来たばかりです。"},{"lhs":"Vた形＋","rhs":"ばかりの ＋ N","note":"Modifies a noun: 来たばかりの学生。"},{"lhs":"Vた形＋","rhs":"ばかりで／なので、〜","note":"Connects clauses: 食べたばかりなので、まだいっぱいです。"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"さっき","m":"a little while ago"},{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べたばかり","r":"たべたばかり","m":"just ate"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just finished eating lunch.","scene":"Your roommate asks what you ate for lunch."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来たばかり","r":"きたばかり","m":"just arrived"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"新人","r":"しんじん","m":"new employee"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That person is a new employee who just arrived.","scene":"Introducing a new employee at the company."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-064||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"Vたばかりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたばかりです","patternReading":"たばかりです","coreMeaning":"Just did ~; it still feels new and unfamiliar","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when the speaker wants to emphasize the **newness of a situation**: newly arrived, newly married, recently ate, and so on. It often comes with a **feeling of still being unaccustomed** to the current situation.","nuance":"It works for both **life milestones** and **everyday actions**. When answering a question about having just finished something, 〜たばかり conveys 'I finished it recently, and the experience still feels fresh'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"結婚したばかり","r":"けっこんしたばかり","m":"just got married"},{"t":"なので"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新居","r":"しんきょ","m":"new home"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"探して","r":"さがして","m":"looking for"},{"t":"います"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We just got married, so we are looking for a new home.","scene":"Telling a coworker about post-wedding housing plans."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"パン","m":"bread"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"焼いたばかり","r":"やいたばかり","m":"just baked"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This bread was just baked.","scene":"At a bakery, introducing a fresh-from-the-oven item."},{"tokens":[{"t":"さっき","m":"a moment ago"},{"t":"教室","r":"きょうしつ","m":"classroom"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"着いたばかり","r":"ついたばかり","m":"just arrived"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I only just arrived in class a moment ago.","scene":"Answering, 'Have you entered the classroom yet?'"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-064||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from 〜たところ","sub":"Vたばかりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたばかりです","patternReading":"たばかりです","coreMeaning":"Just did ~; it still feels new and unfamiliar","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 〜たばかり from 〜たところ, two patterns often confused. 〜たところ marks an **event that has just occurred** objectively, while 〜たばかり emphasizes the speaker's **feeling of still being unfamiliar**.","nuance":"〜たところ is generally not used for an event from several days ago, while 〜たばかり can be: '先週来たばかりです' (I only arrived last week) is fine. 〜たところ is also used in brief reports such as '今届いたところです'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"着いた","r":"ついた","m":"arrived"},{"t":"ところ","m":"just now (time)"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have just arrived here (objectively).","scene":"Calling to report your arrival, without an emotional emphasis."},{"tokens":[{"t":"先週","r":"せんしゅう","m":"last week"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来たばかり","r":"きたばかり","m":"just arrived"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I only came to Japan last week, and it still feels new.","scene":"Introducing yourself and explaining why you still feel unfamiliar."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vたところです","point":"Just finished in an objective, immediately-before-now sense. Vたばかり instead emphasizes the subjective feeling of 'new'."},{"vs":"まだVていません","point":"Says 'have not done it yet'—the complete opposite of Vたばかり, which says it is already finished."}]}},"n4-064||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"Vたばかりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたばかりです","patternReading":"たばかりです","coreMeaning":"Just did ~; it still feels new and unfamiliar","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common errors when using 〜たばかり. The most important points are **not using Vるばかり** for this meaning and **distinguishing it from 〜たところ**, which is objective.","nuance":"〜たところ focuses on the **time immediately after completion**, while 〜たばかり focuses on the **subjective feeling of newness**. Thus 〜たばかり can describe something from several days ago, whereas 〜たところ generally cannot.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たった","m":"only just"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"着いたばかり","r":"ついたばかり","m":"just arrived"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"まだ","m":"still not"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けて","r":"あけて","m":"open"},{"t":"いません","m":"not yet"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I just arrived, so I have not opened my luggage yet.","scene":"Just entering a hotel and replying to the staff."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べるばかりです。 → ✅ 食べたばかりです。","❌ さっき着いたところです、まだ慣れません。（feeling is not expressed） → ✅ さっき着いたばかりで、まだ慣れません。","❌ まだ食べたばかりではありません。 → ✅ まだ食べていません。（to say 'not yet eaten', use まだVていない）","❌ 彼は本ばかり読んでいます。（wrong if you mean 'just read'） → ✅ 彼は今読んだばかりです。","❌ 食べたばかりです。（when you just finished in front of someone） → ✅ 食べたところです。"],"compare":null}},"n4-064||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip (fresh bread)","sub":"Vたばかりです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vたばかりです","patternReading":"たばかりです","coreMeaning":"Just did ~; it still feels new and unfamiliar","slug":"memory","definition":"For an unforgettable memory aid, imagine a **loaf of bread fresh from the oven**, still hot—'new' and 'just finished'. The sound of ばかり can remind you of 'baked bread' and 'hot'.","nuance":"When you see たばかり, ask: 'Does the speaker still feel that this is new or unfamiliar?' If yes, use たばかり. If they only want to report that something just finished, use たところ.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-065||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜そうです (伝聞) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうです (伝聞)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"I hear that ~ (relaying information from another source)","slug":"core","definition":"〜そうです（伝聞）is used to **relay information** heard from someone else, the media, a forecast, and so on. The speaker does not need to verify it personally; they simply repeat the source's information. It often appears with **〜によると** (according to...).","nuance":"Compared with 〜らしいです, そうです（伝聞）is closer to “I hear that...” and implies a specific information source. Unlike 様態そう（Vます＋そう）, 伝聞 uses the plain form and **does not describe appearance**. It works in speech and writing, though casual conversation often uses 〜って instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"天気予報","r":"てんきよほう","m":"weather forecast"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the weather forecast, I hear it will rain tomorrow.","scene":"Checking the forecast before work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"病気","r":"びょうき","m":"illness"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"休んでいる","r":"やすんでいる","m":"absent"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear Tanaka is absent because he is sick.","scene":"A coworker reports the absence"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-065||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"〜そうです (伝聞) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうです (伝聞)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"I hear that ~ (relaying information from another source)","slug":"forms","definition":"Attach it directly to the verb, adjective, or noun's **plain form (普通形)**. Do not use the polite ます／です form before そうです. With **なA and N, keep だ** before そうです.","nuance":"This is an important contrast with 様態: 伝聞 does not change into そうに、そうな、そうで. For the past, simply change the preceding clause to the past form: 食べたそうです.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（普通形）","rhs":"そうです","note":"食べるそうです・食べたそうです"},{"lhs":"いA（普通形）","rhs":"そうです","note":"安いそうです・安かったそうです"},{"lhs":"なA","rhs":"だそうです","note":"静かだそうです"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"だそうです","note":"学生だそうです"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"終わった","r":"おわった","m":"finished"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear it is already finished.","scene":"A classmate says the assignment is done"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"面白い","r":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear this book is good.","scene":"Remembering a friend's praise"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お元気","r":"おげんき","m":"well"},{"t":"だそうです","r":"だそうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear the teacher is still well.","scene":"Asking a friend about the teacher"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-065||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜そうです (伝聞) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうです (伝聞)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"I hear that ~ (relaying information from another source)","slug":"usage","definition":"With そうです（伝聞）, stating the **information source** makes the sentence more natural: 〜によると (according to...), 〜の話では (according to...). Without a source, the sentence is still correct, but the listener may ask, “Who said that?”","nuance":"This pattern is neutral and not especially formal. It is common in news and journalism. In casual conversation, Japanese speakers often use **〜って** instead of そうです（伝聞）.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"佐藤さん","r":"さとうさん","m":"Sato"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"story"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"駅前","r":"えきまえ","m":"in front of station"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"カフェ","r":"カフェ","m":"cafe"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"できた","r":"できた","m":"opened"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to Sato, I hear a new cafe opened in front of the station.","scene":"A coworker mentions a new cafe"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース","r":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"よると","r":"よると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"台風","r":"たいふう","m":"typhoon"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the news, I hear a typhoon is coming.","scene":"Watching the morning news"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-065||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with 様態・らしい","sub":"〜そうです (伝聞) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうです (伝聞)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"I hear that ~ (relaying information from another source)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes three easily confused patterns: **様態そう** (looks/seems), **伝聞そう** (I hear), and **らしい** (seems/I hear). Look at the form before そう to identify the meaning.","nuance":"様態: Vます形 + そう expresses a visual prediction. 伝聞: plain form + そう relays heard information. らしい is more flexible and can express either inference or rumor, but it does not emphasize a clear source.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"俳優","r":"はいゆう","m":"actor"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"結婚","r":"けっこん","m":"marriage"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear that actor is getting married.","scene":"Reading entertainment rumors"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜そうです（様態）","point":"様態: Vます形 / adjective stem + そう = seems, looks likely. 伝聞: plain form + そう = I hear. Compare: 雨が降りそうです (it looks like rain) vs 雨が降るそうです (I hear it will rain)."},{"vs":"〜らしいです","point":"らしい: an inference from indirect evidence or rumor, without a clear source. そうです（伝聞）: emphasizes the source and often appears with によると."}]}},"n4-065||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"〜そうです (伝聞) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうです (伝聞)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"I hear that ~ (relaying information from another source)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse 様態 and 伝聞 because both use そうです. Look **before そうです**: Vます形 → 様態; V普通形・A普通形・Nだ → 伝聞.","nuance":"The most common error is using Vます形＋そう to mean “I hear”; it sounds like “it looks as if...” The second is forgetting だ before そうです with なA and N.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"閉店","r":"へいてん","m":"close"},{"t":"した","r":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear the shop has already closed.","scene":"Repeating a friend's report about the old shop"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 明日は雨が降りそうです。（when you mean 'I hear it will rain'）→ ✅ 明日は雨が降るそうです。","❌ 静かそうです。 → ✅ 静かだそうです。（なA needs だ）","❌ 明日は休みですそうです。 → ✅ 明日は休みだそうです。（do not use です before そうです；N + だ）"],"compare":null}},"n4-065||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜そうです (伝聞) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうです (伝聞)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"I hear that ~ (relaying information from another source)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture a phone **sending news** into your ear, and you repeat it unchanged with **plain form** + そうです. Since the news is unverified, you do not need to alter the form.","nuance":"To avoid confusion with 様態: if そう follows ます形, it is based on what you see; if it follows the plain form, it means “I hear.” When you see によると, it is almost certainly 伝聞.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-065||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"〜そうです (伝聞) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜そうです (伝聞)","patternReading":"そうです","coreMeaning":"I hear that ~ (relaying information from another source)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These source phrases are almost **set formulas**: [source] + によると + [clause] + そうです. They appear frequently in tests and news reports.","nuance":"そうですよ at the end conveys **new information** for the listener, with slight emphasis. In casual conversation, it can be replaced with 〜って.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"うわさ","r":"うわさ","m":"rumor"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"よると","r":"よると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"あの"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"辞める","r":"やめる","m":"quit"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to rumors, I hear that teacher is quitting next month.","scene":"Rumors about the teacher transferring"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-066||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning: INDIRECT inference","sub":"〜らしいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしいです","patternReading":"らしいです","coreMeaning":"Seems / I hear that ~ (inference from indirect signs)","slug":"core","definition":"〜らしいです expresses an **indirect judgment**: the speaker hears a rumor or observes outside signs, then concludes “it seems…” or “I hear that…”. It attaches to the plain form at the end of a sentence and is used with verbs, adjectives, and nouns.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. Certainty is about **80%**—less strong than はず (90%), and more than simply repeating information with そうです (伝聞). Add よ when sharing new information with the listener.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"山田","r":"やまだ","m":"Yamada"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"転職","r":"てんしょく","m":"change jobs"},{"t":"した","m":"did"},{"t":"らしいです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear Yamada-san changed jobs.","scene":"A coworker shares news about Yamada"},{"tokens":[{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"寒い","r":"さむい","m":"cold"},{"t":"らしいです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear it’s really cold outside.","scene":"Someone just came back from outside"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-066||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure: plain form + らしい","sub":"〜らしいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしいです","patternReading":"らしいです","coreMeaning":"Seems / I hear that ~ (inference from indirect signs)","slug":"forms","definition":"らしい attaches directly to the **plain form** of a verb, adjective, or noun. With nouns and な-adjectives, do not add だ (except in the full plain form). Add です at the end for polite speech; omit です in casual speech.","nuance":"For the past, use **Vた + らしい** or **Nだった + らしい**. For negatives, use **Vない + らしい** / **Nじゃない + らしい**. らしい does not change according to the subject.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + らしいです","rhs":"学生らしいです","note":"Attach directly to a noun; do not add だ or を."},{"lhs":"V 普通形 + らしいです","rhs":"転職したらしいです / 行くらしいです","note":"Use the verb’s plain form; the verb shows the tense."},{"lhs":"い-adj + らしいです","rhs":"寒いらしいです / 忙しいらしいです","note":"Keep the い-adjective unchanged; do not add だ."},{"lhs":"な-adj + らしいです","rhs":"静からしいです","note":"Drop だ from 静かだ and add らしい: 静かだ → 静からしい."},{"lhs":"Vた / Vない + らしい","rhs":"行ったらしいです / 行かないらしいです","note":"Use た/ない before らしい for past and negative forms."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"らしいです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear he is a student.","scene":"Talking with a friend about someone you know"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"帰った","r":"かえった","m":"went home"},{"t":"らしいです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear the teacher has already gone home.","scene":"The staff room is dark when you arrive"},{"tokens":[{"t":"来週","r":"らいしゅう","m":"next week"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"忙しい","r":"いそがしい","m":"busy"},{"t":"らしいです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear next week will be very busy.","scene":"The boss reviews next week’s schedule"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-066||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage: 『reported』 and 『seems』","sub":"〜らしいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしいです","patternReading":"らしいです","coreMeaning":"Seems / I hear that ~ (inference from indirect signs)","slug":"usage","definition":"There are two main uses: (1) **Reported**—receive information from someone else and pass it on; (2) **Seems**—infer from observable signs. Both express **indirect inference**, not direct witnessing.","nuance":"To emphasize the source, put **〜によると** at the start of the sentence. With family or friends, use らしい without です. Add よ for a friendly tone when sharing new information.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"らしいです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the news, I hear it will rain tomorrow.","scene":"Reading the weather forecast on your phone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"light"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ついている","m":"is on"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"まだ","m":"still"},{"t":"いる","m":"be there"},{"t":"らしい","m":"seems"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The light is on, so Tanaka is probably still there.","scene":"The office light is still on, so you infer your coworker has not left"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"残業","r":"ざんぎょう","m":"overtime"},{"t":"している","m":"is doing"},{"t":"らしい","m":"seems"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It seems he works overtime every day.","scene":"He always arrives late and leaves late"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-066||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare: らしい vs そう・よう・はず","sub":"〜らしいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしいです","patternReading":"らしいです","coreMeaning":"Seems / I hear that ~ (inference from indirect signs)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare 〜らしい with easily confused patterns. The key point is: **らしい = indirect information + inference**. Each other pattern has its own certainty level and information source.","nuance":"In conversation, みたい is more casual than らしい; よう leans toward perception; はず toward logic. らしい is safest when you have information from outside.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうやら","m":"apparently"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅れる","r":"おくれる","m":"be delayed"},{"t":"らしいです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Apparently, the train is delayed.","scene":"You hear an announcement that the train is late"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜そうです（伝聞）","point":"そう only reports “I hear” and does not express the speaker’s judgment; らしい includes both information and the speaker’s inference. E.g.: 雨が降るそうです (report) / 雨が降るらしいです (I hear + it seems)."},{"vs":"〜ようです","point":"よう is based on direct impressions from what you see immediately; らしい is based on what someone said or the general situation. E.g.: 空が暗いようです (the sky looks dark) / 明日雨らしいです (I hear from the forecast)."},{"vs":"〜はずです","point":"はず = logical inference, about 90% certain (“it should be so”); らしい = “I hear / it seems,” about 80% certain. E.g.: 彼は来るはずです (logical certainty) / 彼は来るらしいです (I hear he will come)."},{"vs":"〜みたいです","point":"みたい is casual and common with friends; らしい is more objective and natural at work. E.g.: 彼、来ないみたい / 彼は来ないらしいです."}]}},"n4-066||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors: wrong links, extra だ/を","sub":"〜らしいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしいです","patternReading":"らしいです","coreMeaning":"Seems / I hear that ~ (inference from indirect signs)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often add a particle or だ before らしい, or use らしい while **directly witnessing** something. Remember: らしい expresses indirect inference, and its connection is “plain form + らしい”.","nuance":"These errors make the sentence unnatural or grammatically incorrect. Use らしい when the context provides an outside source or indirect sign.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"day off"},{"t":"らしいです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I hear tomorrow is a day off.","scene":"The company announces a holiday schedule"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は学生をらしいです。 → ✅ 彼は学生らしいです。 (Attach directly to the noun; を is unnecessary)","❌ 外は寒いだらしいです。 → ✅ 外は寒いらしいです。 (Keep the い-adjective unchanged; do not add だ)","❌ 自分で見て「あっ、雨が降っているらしい」 → ✅ あっ、雨が降っている！ (Do not use indirect inference when seeing it directly)"],"compare":null}},"n4-066||memory":{"title":"🎯 Tip: らしい = typical / characteristic","sub":"〜らしいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしいです","patternReading":"らしいです","coreMeaning":"Seems / I hear that ~ (inference from indirect signs)","slug":"memory","definition":"Anchor らしい with the image of being **typical**. 男らしい = characteristic of a man; 学生らしい = characteristic of a student → らしい = “seems typical of”. When you hear information from outside, tell yourself, “That sounds typical.”","nuance":"Do not confuse “typical/characteristic” with “I hear”; they share the idea of matching a typical image. Remember: **information comes from outside → らしい**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-066||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases: によると・どうやら","sub":"〜らしいです · N4","v2":{"pattern":"〜らしいです","patternReading":"らしいです","coreMeaning":"Seems / I hear that ~ (inference from indirect signs)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common combinations with らしい in exams and conversation include **Nによると + らしい** (state the source), **どうやら + らしい** (apparently), and **らしいよ / らしいですね** in everyday conversation.","nuance":"Use によると for a specific source; どうやら for an inference based on several signs; らしいよ to share with friends, and らしいですね for a polite response.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"雪","r":"ゆき","m":"snow"},{"t":"らしいです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the news, I hear it will snow tomorrow.","scene":"Watching the evening weather forecast"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-067||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nによると · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nによると","patternReading":"によると","coreMeaning":"According to N — citing a source (news, forecasts, others' words)","slug":"core","definition":"〜によると cites an information source, meaning “according to N.” N may be a person (先生, 友達), a newspaper, the news, or a weather forecast. After によると, the sentence often ends in 〜そうです (I hear) or 〜らしいです (apparently / I hear). The speaker relays the information without confirming it.","nuance":"The tone is objective and neutral, suitable for both speech and writing. With そうです, the “I hear” sense is clear; with らしい, it adds an inference based on heard information. によると often appears at the start to frame the information.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大雨","r":"おおあめ","m":"heavy rain"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the news, I hear it will rain heavily tomorrow.","scene":"Watching the weather news"},{"tokens":[{"t":"天気予報","r":"てんきよほう","m":"weather forecast"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"晴れる","r":"はれる","m":"be sunny"},{"t":"らしいです","r":"らしいです","m":"apparently"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the weather forecast, tomorrow will apparently be sunny.","scene":"Hearing the forecast before an outing"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-067||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nによると · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nによると","patternReading":"によると","coreMeaning":"According to N — citing a source (news, forecasts, others' words)","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern 〜によると is simple: **N + によると + clause + そうです/らしいです**. N must be a noun naming an information source. によると never changes form; past or future tense appears at the end of the sentence.","nuance":"For a more formal style, especially in writing, use **によれば**. によると is neutral and comfortable in everyday conversation.","structure":[{"lhs":"N (source)","rhs":"によると、～そうです。","note":"N = information source: ニュース、天気予報、先生の話…"},{"lhs":"N (source)","rhs":"によると、～らしいです。","note":"Adds an inference from the source"},{"lhs":"N (source)","rhs":"によれば、～そうです。","note":"Formal; common in writing"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"words/story"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"来週","r":"らいしゅう","m":"next week"},{"t":"テスト","m":"test"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","r":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the teacher, I hear there will be a test next week.","scene":"Hearing a class announcement"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"あの店","r":"あのみせ","m":"that shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to my friend, I hear that shop is really cheap.","scene":"Relaying a friend’s recommendation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-067||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nによると · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nによると","patternReading":"によると","coreMeaning":"According to N — citing a source (news, forecasts, others' words)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜によると is used when you relay information heard from a specific source. Common openings include ニュースによると (according to the news), 天気予報によると (according to the forecast), and 先生の話によると (according to the teacher).","nuance":"For a person as the source, use **〜の話によると** or **〜によると**. In conversation, によると can begin the sentence to highlight the source. Do not use it for personal opinions; use 私は〜と思います.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新聞","r":"しんぶん","m":"newspaper"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"遅れる","r":"おくれる","m":"be delayed"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the newspaper, I hear the train will be delayed today.","scene":"Reading the morning paper"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"words"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"らしいです","r":"らしいです","m":"apparently"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the teacher, this kanji is apparently difficult.","scene":"Studying with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-067||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"Nによると · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nによると","patternReading":"によると","coreMeaning":"According to N — citing a source (news, forecasts, others' words)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 〜によると from similar patterns: **〜そうです** (reporting without naming a source), **〜らしい** (inference from evidence), and **〜とおりに** (following instructions exactly).","nuance":"The key point: によると **requires a source**. そうです and らしい can stand alone. とおりに means “as instructed” and describes following an action, not citing information.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"天気予報","r":"てんきよほう","m":"weather forecast"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the forecast, I hear it will rain tomorrow.","scene":"Comparing with a sentence without a source"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜そうです（伝聞）","point":"そうです only requires that you heard something; no specific source is needed. によると must identify the source. Example: 明日は雨だそうです。(I hear) / 天気予報によると、明日は雨だそうです。"},{"vs":"〜らしいです","point":"らしい expresses an inference from indirect evidence without naming a source. によると directly cites a source. Example: 明日は雨らしいです。(apparently) / 天気予報によると、明日は雨だそうです。"},{"vs":"〜とおりに","point":"とおりに means “exactly as” an instruction or procedure says; it does not cite news. Example: 言ったとおりにやってください。(Do exactly as I said) / ニュースによると、明日は雨だそうです。"}]}},"n4-067||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nによると · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nによると","patternReading":"によると","coreMeaning":"According to N — citing a source (news, forecasts, others' words)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using によると with a **personal opinion**, omitting そうだ/らしい at the end, and confusing it with **によって** (passive, means, or depending on).","nuance":"によると is for objective information sources. To say “in my opinion,” use 私の考えでは or 私は〜と思います. Without そうだ/らしい, the sentence becomes a direct assertion and is grammatically inappropriate here.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the news, I hear it will rain tomorrow.","scene":"A standard correct sentence"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私によると、明日は雨です。 → ✅ 私の考えでは、明日は雨です。 (によると is not for personal opinions)","❌ ニュースによると、明日は雨です。 → ✅ ニュースによると、明日は雨だそうです。 (The sentence must end with そうだ/らしい)","❌ 天気予報によって、明日は雨だそうです。 → ✅ 天気予報によると、明日は雨だそうです。 (によって means depending on/by means of, not citing a source)","❌ 友達によると、その店は安いです。 → ✅ 友達によると、その店は安いそうです。 (Add そうです when relaying information)"],"compare":null}},"n4-067||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"Nによると · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nによると","patternReading":"によると","coreMeaning":"According to N — citing a source (news, forecasts, others' words)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember によると as **よる** (rely on, be based on) + **と** (condition). Imagine: “If we rely on this source…” → “according to this source.” Core formula: **SOURCE + によると + NEWS + そうだ**.","nuance":"A useful check: when you see によると, ask “Where is the source?” and make sure the sentence ends in そうだ/らしい.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-067||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nによると · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nによると","patternReading":"によると","coreMeaning":"According to N — citing a source (news, forecasts, others' words)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Here are common phrases used with によると. Memorizing them helps you respond faster in speaking and listening.","nuance":"These phrases often appear **at the start of a sentence** in conversation and reading passages.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"によると","r":"によると","m":"according to"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"I hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"According to the news, I hear it will rain tomorrow.","scene":"Common phrase"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-068||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V使役形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V使役形","patternReading":"しえきけい","coreMeaning":"Make or let someone do something (causative)","slug":"core","definition":"The causative form expresses that the speaker or someone else makes, lets, or allows another person to perform an action. The causer controls whether the action happens; the caused person performs it. The force ranges from compulsion to permission depending on the particle and context.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. With an intransitive verb, 「NをVさせる」 often sounds forceful or causative; with a transitive verb, 「NにVさせる」 often means assigning a task or allowing someone to act. Polite ～ます / ～ました forms are common at work and home. Avoid using it with superiors unless using a polite form such as ～させていただく.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"manager"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"me"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"来させました","r":"こさせました","m":"made me come"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The manager made me come early.","scene":"The manager called and told me to come to work early"},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"好きな","r":"すきな","m":"favorite"},{"t":"服","r":"ふく","m":"clothes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"選ばせました","r":"えらばせました","m":"let choose"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The mother let her child choose clothes they liked.","scene":"The mother watches without interfering"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-068||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"V使役形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V使役形","patternReading":"しえきけい","coreMeaning":"Make or let someone do something (causative)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch organizes how to form the causative across the three verb groups, including tense, negative, and polite forms. Mastering these rules lets you accurately say “make or let someone do.”","nuance":"This is mainly a morphological change. Group I: change the final う sound to the あ row and add せる; Group II: drop る and add させる; Group III: する→させる and 来る→来させる (こさせる). Negative: 書かせない、食べさせない; past: 書かせた、食べさせた.","structure":[{"lhs":"Group I: 書く","rhs":"書か+せる → 書かせる","note":"く → か"},{"lhs":"Group I: 飲む","rhs":"飲ま+せる → 飲ませる","note":"む → ま"},{"lhs":"Group II: 食べる","rhs":"食べ+させる → 食べさせる","note":"Drop る"},{"lhs":"Group II: 見る","rhs":"見+させる → 見させる","note":"Drop る"},{"lhs":"Group III: する／来る","rhs":"させる／来させる (こさせる)","note":"Two irregular verbs"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"me"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲ませました","r":"のませました","m":"made drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother made me take my medicine.","scene":"Mother reminds me to take medicine after eating"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"遊ばせました","r":"あそばせました","m":"let play"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I let my child play outside.","scene":"Mother lets the child play outside with friends"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: 辞書形 → 使役形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 — う row → あ row + せる","Change the final sound from the う row to the あ row (remember: う→わ), then add せる","飲む（のむ） → `飲ませる（のませる）` (drink) ・ 書く（かく） → `書かせる（かかせる）` (write) ・ 話す（はなす） → `話させる（はなさせる）` (speak) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰らせる（かえらせる）` (return)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — 一段 — drop る + させる","Drop る, then add させる","食べる（たべる） → `食べさせる（たべさせる）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見させる（みさせる）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きさせる（おきさせる）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝させる（ねさせる）` (sleep)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — する → させる","する → させる (completely irregular)","する → `させる` (do) ・ 勉強する（べんきょうする） → `勉強させる（べんきょうさせる）` (study) ・ 運動する（うんどうする） → `運動させる（うんどうさせる）` (exercise)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — 来る → こさせる","来る → 来させる (こさせる)","来る（くる） → `来させる（こさせる）` (come)"]]}}},"n4-068||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"V使役形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V使役形","patternReading":"しえきけい","coreMeaning":"Make or let someone do something (causative)","slug":"usage","definition":"The causative has more than one meaning. Its three main uses are compulsion, permission, and causing a state or emotion, often with an intransitive verb. Use the context to translate it as “make,” “let,” “allow,” or “cause.”","nuance":"With a transitive verb, the caused person takes に and the direct object takes を. With an intransitive verb, the caused person or thing takes を. Using に with an intransitive verb can sound wrong or unnatural. In conversation, ～させてください and ～させていただきます are common polite permission forms.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"manager"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"me"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"materials"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読ませました","r":"よませました","m":"made read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The manager made me read the materials.","scene":"The manager asks me to read the materials before the meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"妻","r":"つま","m":"wife"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"好きな","r":"すきな","m":"favorite"},{"t":"もの","r":"もの","m":"thing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買わせました","r":"かわせました","m":"let buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I let my wife choose and buy what she liked.","scene":"The couple shops; the husband tells her to choose"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"寝させました","r":"ねさせました","m":"put to bed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I put my child to bed early.","scene":"Mother puts the child to bed at 9 p.m."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-068||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"V使役形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V使役形","patternReading":"しえきけい","coreMeaning":"Make or let someone do something (causative)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compared with similar patterns, the causative always involves someone causing or permitting the action, rather than the subject acting solely on their own ability.","nuance":"Pay special attention to させていただきます: the speaker asks permission and is not giving an order. 可能形 expresses the speaker’s ability and has no causer. With ～てもらいます, the speaker asks for help or benefits from someone’s action without using authority.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"課長","r":"かちょう","m":"section manager"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"me"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"materials"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"コピーさせました","r":"コピーさせました","m":"made copy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The section manager made me photocopy the materials.","scene":"The manager gives me the materials and tells me to copy them"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V可能形","point":"可能形: the subject can do something on their own (食べられる). 使役形: someone makes or lets them do it (食べさせる). The endings られる/させる can be confusing."},{"vs":"Vさせていただきます","point":"させていただきます: I humbly ask permission to do it. 使役形: I make or let someone else do it."},{"vs":"Vてもらいます","point":"てもらいます: I ask someone to do something or receive their help. 使役形: I use authority to make or allow the action."}]}},"n4-068||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"V使役形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V使役形","patternReading":"しえきけい","coreMeaning":"Make or let someone do something (causative)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common errors with the causative: incorrect verb forms, incorrect particles, and confusion between forcing someone and allowing them to act.","nuance":"The most common issue is choosing を or に. Safe rule: a transitive verb with an object takes に for the caused person, while the object keeps を; an intransitive verb takes を for the caused person. Another error is inventing forms such as 食べらせる, しさせる, or 来させる with き.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"帰らせました","r":"かえらせました","m":"let return"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I let my mother go home early.","scene":"Mother visits, and I tell her to leave early before dark"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べらせました → ✅ 食べさせました (Group II: drop る + させる)","❌ 先生は学生を作文を書かせました → ✅ 先生は学生に作文を書かせました (transitive: caused person takes に)","❌ 母を早く帰らせました (meaning permission) → ✅ 母に早く帰らせました (permission takes に)","❌ 子供を遊ばせました (meaning permission) → ✅ 子供に遊ばせました (intransitive: permission takes に)"],"compare":null}},"n4-068||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"V使役形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V使役形","patternReading":"しえきけい","coreMeaning":"Make or let someone do something (causative)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick tips for remembering the conjugation rules and the difference between を and に.","nuance":"Do not memorize mechanically; picture someone being pulled into an action. Group I changes the final sound to the あ row and adds せる: the “A-say” cue. Group II drops る and adds させる, making a longer form to picture as “more interference.” する and 来る are the two exceptions: させる / こさせる. For particles, を directly affects the person with an intransitive verb, while に assigns a task with a transitive verb.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-068||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful exam and daily phrases","sub":"V使役形 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V使役形","patternReading":"しえきけい","coreMeaning":"Make or let someone do something (causative)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These causative phrases appear frequently in test questions and everyday communication.","nuance":"Two especially important polite phrases are ～させてください (please let me ~) and ～させていただきます (allow me to ~). They are used constantly at work. ～させないでください means “please don't make me ~” and is also common.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","r":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"考えさせてください","r":"かんがえさせてください","m":"let me think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let me think for a moment.","scene":"A colleague asks your opinion, and you need time to think"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-069||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nを Vさせます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nを Vさせます","patternReading":"をさせます","coreMeaning":"Force or allow N to perform an action (causative)","slug":"core","definition":"〜をVさせます is the causative form used when the speaker or an authority figure forces or allows someone to perform an action. The affected person comes directly after を. This pattern is mainly used with intransitive verbs such as 行く, 立つ, 遊ぶ, and 休む, so it does not conflict with を marking a direct object.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, and fairly common. The nuance may be strong force or mild permission, depending on context. The commander usually has authority: a teacher over students, a manager over employees, or parents over children.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"立たせました","r":"たたせました","m":"made stand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher made the student stand.","scene":"The student was disruptive, so the teacher made them stand"},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"遊ばせました","r":"あそばせました","m":"let play"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The mother let her child play outside.","scene":"On a beautiful morning, the mother lets the child play outside"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-069||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nを Vさせます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nを Vさせます","patternReading":"をさせます","coreMeaning":"Force or allow N to perform an action (causative)","slug":"forms","definition":"Build it from the **Vない形**: remove ない, then add **せる / させる**. For Group I verbs, change the final sound to the あ-row and add せる: 書く→書かせる. For Group II, remove る and add させる: 食べる→食べさせる. する→させる and 来る→来させる. **させる** conjugates like a Group II verb: させます, させた, させない…","nuance":"The dictionary form is させる. With an intransitive verb, the causative person uses **を**. With a transitive verb (eat, read, do...), change the person marker to **に** to avoid two を particles: 子供に野菜を食べさせる.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（person）","rhs":"を + V Group I（remove ない from ない形） + せる","note":"書く→書かせる"},{"lhs":"N（person）","rhs":"を + V Group II（drop る） + させる","note":"食べる→食べさせる"},{"lhs":"N（person）","rhs":"を + V Group III","note":"する→させる / 来る→来させる"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department manager"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"山田さん","r":"やまださん","m":"Mr. Yamada"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行かせました","r":"いかせました","m":"sent"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The department manager sent Mr. Yamada to Osaka.","scene":"The company needs someone to handle the Osaka branch"},{"tokens":[{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"息子","r":"むすこ","m":"son"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"留学","r":"りゅうがく","m":"study abroad"},{"t":"させます","r":"させます","m":"let do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The father lets his son study abroad.","scene":"The family decides to send their son to study in Japan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-069||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Nを Vさせます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nを Vさせます","patternReading":"をさせます","coreMeaning":"Force or allow N to perform an action (causative)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern often appears in classrooms, companies, and families when someone higher-ranking forces or allows someone under them to do something. Context determines the meaning: if the person does not want to act, it means force; if they want to act or benefit from it, it means permission.","nuance":"Do not use it when the speaker is merely reporting something objectively; it always suggests authority or permission from the commander. In speech, people often use the short form させる followed by んだ / けど...","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"課長","r":"かちょう","m":"section manager"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"森さん","r":"もりさん","m":"Mr. Mori"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"休ませました","r":"やすませました","m":"let rest"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The section manager let Mr. Mori take a break.","scene":"Mr. Mori looks tired after working overtime"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"立たせました","r":"たたせました","m":"made stand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher made the student stand.","scene":"The student was disruptive, so the teacher made them stand"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-069||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Nを Vさせます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nを Vさせます","patternReading":"をさせます","coreMeaning":"Force or allow N to perform an action (causative)","slug":"compare","definition":"Quickly distinguish patterns that learners often confuse. Most importantly, whether to use **を** or **に** depends on the verb. If the verb is **transitive** (eat, read, do...), the causative person must take **に**, not を, to avoid two を particles in a row.","nuance":"Remember: **intransitive verb → を, transitive verb → に**. させていただきます is permission requested by the speaker, not forcing someone else.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べさせます","r":"たべさせます","m":"make eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The mother makes her child eat vegetables.","scene":"At lunchtime, showing に with a transitive V"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NにVさせる","point":"V is transitive (eat, read, do) → the causative person uses に: 子供に野菜を食べさせる. Using を gives 子供を野菜を食べさせる ❌."},{"vs":"Vさせていただきます","point":"させていただきます means the speaker humbly asks permission to act: 説明させていただきます = “Please allow me to explain.” It does not make someone else act."},{"vs":"Vてもらいます","point":"てもらいます means asking someone to do something and appreciating their help; させます uses authority to force or allow. A favor involves gratitude; causative involves authority."}]}},"n4-069||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nを Vさせます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nを Vさせます","patternReading":"をさせます","coreMeaning":"Force or allow N to perform an action (causative)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A summary of common causative mistakes. These errors often come from translating directly from another language or applying familiar verb-conjugation habits.","nuance":"Incorrect conjugation can confuse Japanese listeners or sound very unnatural. Memorize the three groups: Group I changes to the あ-row, Group II drops る, and Group III is irregular.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"社員","r":"しゃいん","m":"employee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出張","r":"しゅっちょう","m":"business trip"},{"t":"させました","r":"させました","m":"made go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The company president sent an employee on a business trip.","scene":"A client needs an urgent meeting at another branch"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 子供を野菜を食べさせます → ✅ 子供に野菜を食べさせます (use に with a transitive verb; avoid repeated を)","❌ 書きさせます → ✅ 書かせます (Group I changes to the あ-row, then adds せる)","❌ 食べらせます → ✅ 食べさせます (Group II: drop る, then add させる)","❌ 来らせます → ✅ 来させます (来る is irregular: 来させる)"],"compare":null}},"n4-069||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Nを Vさせます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nを Vさせます","patternReading":"をさせます","coreMeaning":"Force or allow N to perform an action (causative)","slug":"memory","definition":"Use this visual mnemonic to remember the causative form. Imagine **を** as a stick pointing at the person who acts, and **せる / させる** as someone shouting “Do it!”","nuance":"Do not memorize only a dry formula. When you see **を + Vせる**, think “this person is being caused to act.” With **に + Vせる**, think “this person is allowed or made to do an action with a direct object.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-070||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vさせていただきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vさせていただきます","patternReading":"させていただきます","coreMeaning":"May I ~ (humble, polite)","slug":"core","definition":"Vさせていただきます is the **humble** form of causative + もらう, meaning **may I do something**. The speaker lowers themself and places the listener above them. Do not use it for someone else's action.","nuance":"Very polite; often used at work, hotels, with customers, and with seniors. Compared with させてもらう, it is more formal and has a clearer nuance of asking permission.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"では"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"説明","r":"せつめい","m":"explain"},{"t":"させていただきます","r":"させていただきます","m":"may I explain"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Well then, may I explain?","scene":"Starting a company presentation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"考えさせていただきます","r":"かんがえさせていただきます","m":"may I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please give me a little time to think.","scene":"The boss asks urgently; you need time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-070||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vさせていただきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vさせていただきます","patternReading":"させていただきます","coreMeaning":"May I ~ (humble, polite)","slug":"forms","definition":"Vさせていただきます is formed from the **causative form** + て + いただきます. For Group 1 verbs, change the ます-stem ending from い to あ + せる. For Group 2, remove る + させる. する and 来る are irregular.","nuance":"There is no common negative form; to express negation, use ないでいただく or rephrase. More polite than させてもらう.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（Nhóm 1）","rhs":"行かせていただきます","note":"行く → 行かせる; ます stem: 行き → 行か"},{"lhs":"V（Nhóm 2）","rhs":"食べさせていただきます","note":"食べる → 食べさせる; remove る + させる"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"させていただきます","note":"する → させる"},{"lhs":"来る","rhs":"来させていただきます","note":"来る → 来させる; pronounced こさせる"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"document"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読ませていただきます","r":"よませていただきます","m":"may I read"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"May I read this document?","scene":"Asking to read a document from the boss"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"休ませていただきます","r":"やすませていただきます","m":"may I take leave"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tomorrow, I would like to take the day off.","scene":"Messaging the boss to request leave"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-070||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vさせていただきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vさせていただきます","patternReading":"させていただきます","coreMeaning":"May I ~ (humble, polite)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **ask permission** to do something or to **politely announce** what you are about to do. To ask permission explicitly, add **〜てもよろしいですか** after the verb.","nuance":"Formal and professional. Avoid it with close friends because it sounds distant. It is common at work, especially when speaking with customers.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"では"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"自己紹介","r":"じこしょうかい","m":"introduce myself"},{"t":"させていただきます","r":"させていただきます","m":"may I do so"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Well then, may I introduce myself?","scene":"First day at work, before the whole company"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お先に","r":"おさきに","m":"before you"},{"t":"失礼させていただきます","r":"しつれいさせていただきます","m":"may I take leave"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will take my leave first.","scene":"Leaving while coworkers are still working"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-070||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast: させてもらう / おVします","sub":"Vさせていただきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vさせていただきます","patternReading":"させていただきます","coreMeaning":"May I ~ (humble, polite)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare this with easily confused humble patterns. **させてもらう** is used with equals or people you are close to, while **させていただく** is polite. **ていただく** means someone else does an action for you. **おVします** is generally humble and does not necessarily ask permission.","nuance":"Formality: させてもらう is less formal than おVします; both おVします and させていただく are formal. させていただく emphasizes being allowed, so it is very polite and can sometimes be overly formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"説明させていただきます","r":"せつめいさせていただきます","m":"may I explain"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will explain now, with your permission.","scene":"Asking to explain at a meeting"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vさせてもらう","point":"Both mean 'ask permission to do', but させてもらう is casual and used with equals; させていただく is polite and more humble."},{"vs":"Vていただく","point":"いただく = receive from someone; ていただく means someone else DOES it FOR you. 例: 説明していただく ≠ 説明させていただく (I do it myself)."},{"vs":"おVします","point":"おVします is simply humble and does not imply permission; させていただく emphasizes 'being allowed to'."}]}},"n4-070||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vさせていただきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vさせていただきます","patternReading":"させていただきます","coreMeaning":"May I ~ (humble, polite)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse two points: **using it for someone else's action** and **forming the causative incorrectly**. Remember that させていただく ALWAYS describes MY action.","nuance":"Used for someone else, it sounds very wrong and may suggest \"I was allowed to make them do something.\"","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"では"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"質問","r":"しつもん","m":"question"},{"t":"させていただきます","r":"させていただきます","m":"may I ask"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Well then, may I ask a question?","scene":"Asking a question after a presentation"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 社長に資料を読ませていただきました。 → ✅ 社長に資料を読んでいただきました。（The president did the reading; use ていただく）","❌ 行きさせていただきます。 → ✅ 行かせていただきます。（Group 1: i→a + せて）","❌ 友達に「ちょっと待たせていただきます」 → ✅ ちょっと待ってね。（This sounds distant and unnatural with friends）"],"compare":null}},"n4-070||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"Vさせていただきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vさせていただきます","patternReading":"させていただきます","coreMeaning":"May I ~ (humble, polite)","slug":"memory","definition":"Simple memory aid: **させて** (tell/allow someone to do) + **いただく** (receive) → 'I receive permission to do it.' いただく is the honorific form of もらう.","nuance":"Because of いただく, this pattern naturally carries the nuance of 'being allowed to' and sounds very polite.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-070||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vさせていただきます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vさせていただきます","patternReading":"させていただきます","coreMeaning":"May I ~ (humble, polite)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are **very common phrases** at work and in meetings: asking a question, presenting, taking leave, and taking one's leave. Memorizing them lets you use the pattern right away.","nuance":"These polite phrases work with bosses and customers. Use a gentle intonation rather than sounding abrupt.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"帰らせていただきます","r":"かえらせていただきます","m":"may I leave"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I would like to leave early today.","scene":"Asking the boss to leave early for family reasons"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-071||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V受身形 (尊敬) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形 (尊敬)","patternReading":"うけみけい そんけい","coreMeaning":"Light honorific using the passive form (respecting superiors)","slug":"core","definition":"V受身形（尊敬）uses the passive form of a verb to show respect for the person being discussed. This is a **light honorific**, common in spoken workplace and classroom Japanese. The listener understands it as polite speech, **not a passive meaning**. Because it is honorific, **use it only for other people's actions**, not your own.","nuance":"Compared with the plain form, V受身形 creates clear **polite distance**. Compared with お帰りになりました, it is **less formal** and more natural in everyday conversation with familiar bosses and teachers. It can be used when speaking directly or asking about someone absent. The tone is friendly but respectful.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department manager"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"帰られました","r":"かえられました","m":"has left (hon.)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The department manager has already left.","scene":"Asking an assistant whether the manager has left"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"来られます","r":"こられます","m":"will come (hon.)"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When will the teacher arrive?","scene":"A student asking when the teacher will arrive"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-071||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"V受身形 (尊敬) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形 (尊敬)","patternReading":"うけみけい そんけい","coreMeaning":"Light honorific using the passive form (respecting superiors)","slug":"forms","definition":"The honorific passive conjugation is **exactly the same as the regular passive**. Group 1: remove ない from the ない-form and add れる → Vれます. Group 2: remove ます from the ます-form and add られます. 来る → 来られます (こられます), する → されます. Adding ます at the end gives the polite form.","nuance":"This form is usually used in the **ます-form** in polite conversation. Formal writing may use the plain form, but at N4, remember to use ます. For Group 2, note that 食べれます is **not used** (it is missing ら)—a common error.","structure":[{"lhs":"V nhóm 1","rhs":"Vない (bỏ ない) + れる → Vれます","note":"Example: 書く → 書かれます; 行く → 行かれます"},{"lhs":"V nhóm 2","rhs":"Vます bỏ ます + られる → Vられます","note":"Example: 食べる → 食べられます; 見る → 見られます"},{"lhs":"来る / する","rhs":"来られる / される","note":"Example: 来ます → 来られます; します → されます"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来られた","r":"こられた","m":"came (hon.)"},{"t":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知らせて","r":"しらせて","m":"inform"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please let me know when the company president arrives.","scene":"Telling an employee to report the president's arrival"},{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department manager"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読まれました","r":"よまれました","m":"read (hon.)"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What did the department manager read?","scene":"Asking what the manager was reading"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-071||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"V受身形 (尊敬) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形 (尊敬)","patternReading":"うけみけい そんけい","coreMeaning":"Light honorific using the passive form (respecting superiors)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this form to show respect for the **person performing the action**—usually a superior, customer, teacher, or similar person. At work: 'Where is the president going?' or 'Has the manager left?' In class: 'When will the teacher arrive?' This is **natural in speech** without sounding excessively formal.","nuance":"The respect level is **lighter than お〜になります** and special honorifics. With a familiar boss, it sounds friendly and does not create too much distance. **Do not use it for your own actions**. With friends, it sounds odd.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お客様","r":"おきゃくさま","m":"customer"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"来られました","r":"こられました","m":"has arrived (hon.)"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Has the customer arrived?","scene":"A receptionist asking a coworker about a customer"},{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department manager"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"出かけられました","r":"でかけられました","m":"has gone out (hon.)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The department manager has just gone out.","scene":"Telling a coworker the manager has gone out"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-071||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"V受身形 (尊敬) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形 (尊敬)","patternReading":"うけみけい そんけい","coreMeaning":"Light honorific using the passive form (respecting superiors)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare these forms to choose the appropriate level of formality. There are three honorific levels: V受身形 (light), おVになります (medium), and special honorifics (high). The same form can mean passive or honorific depending on the context.","nuance":"Honorific V受身形 is **more familiar than** おVになります. For example, 部長はもう帰られましたか sounds friendly, while 部長はもうお帰りになりましたか sounds more formal. With an important customer, おVになります is safer. With a familiar boss, V受身形 sounds more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"帰られました","r":"かえられました","m":"has left (hon.)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher has already left.","scene":"A student asking about the teacher"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"おVになります (お帰りになります)","point":"おVになります is more formal than V受身形; V受身形 is milder and more natural."},{"vs":"特別な尊敬語 (いらっしゃる, 召し上がる...)","point":"Special honorifics show the highest respect; V受身形 is lighter and is not used for familiar 'eating' or 'drinking'."},{"vs":"V受身形 (bị động thường)","point":"If the subject is a superior acting on their own → honorific; if someone acts on the subject → passive."}]}},"n4-071||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V受身形 (尊敬) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形 (尊敬)","patternReading":"うけみけい そんけい","coreMeaning":"Light honorific using the passive form (respecting superiors)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: (1) using the honorific for themselves, (2) using V受身形 as a regular passive when talking about a boss, and (3) dropping ら in Group 2.","nuance":"When talking about your own actions, use **humble language** (お〜します, いたします), never V受身形. For example, 'I have left' cannot be said as 私は帰られました.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department manager"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"帰られました","r":"かえられました","m":"has left (hon.)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The department manager has already left.","scene":"A proper example of honoring a superior"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私は帰られました。 → ✅ 私は帰りました。 (do not use honorifics for yourself)","❌ 部長に帰られました。 → ✅ 部長が帰られました。 (に makes it passive and troublesome, not honorific)","❌ 先生は食べれましたか。 → ✅ 先生は食べられましたか。 (Group 2 requires ら)"],"compare":null}},"n4-071||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V受身形 (尊敬) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形 (尊敬)","patternReading":"うけみけい そんけい","coreMeaning":"Light honorific using the passive form (respecting superiors)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: the passive form creates a feeling of being 'slightly farther away'—distance becomes politeness. Imagine that you are not directly doing the action; with a superior, you are respectfully acknowledging their presence and action.","nuance":"Tip: If the subject is a boss or teacher and you want to show respect, change the verb to the passive form. But ask: is the action actually being done to them? If not, the meaning is honorific.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-071||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"V受身形 (尊敬) · N4","v2":{"pattern":"V受身形 (尊敬)","patternReading":"うけみけい そんけい","coreMeaning":"Light honorific using the passive form (respecting superiors)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These sentence patterns are common in workplace and classroom conversation. Memorize them for quick responses when speaking about superiors.","nuance":"These phrases appear frequently in Minna no Nihongo Lesson 49 and in JLPT N4 listening and comprehension questions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"来られた","r":"こられた","m":"came (hon.)"},{"t":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知らせて","r":"しらせて","m":"inform"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please let me know when the company president arrives.","scene":"Employees telling each other to report the boss's arrival"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-072||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"おVますになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますになります","patternReading":"おますになります","coreMeaning":"Standard honorific for a superior's action","slug":"core","definition":"おVますになります is a standard honorific (尊敬語). Use it to elevate the actions of superiors such as teachers, bosses, and customers. Take the ます-stem of V, add お before it and になる after it: お + Vます(stem) + になります.","nuance":"Shows clear respect and is more formal than Vます and even V受身形 (Vられる). Common in offices, hotels, and restaurants. Do not use it for yourself or your family. With Sino-Japanese + する verbs, use ご…になる (ご説明になる).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"お帰りになりました","r":"おかえりになりました","m":"returned (hon.)"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Has the teacher gone home already?","scene":"A student asks in the hallway."},{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"お書きになった","r":"おかきになった","m":"wrote (hon.)"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is the letter the president wrote.","scene":"An employee presents the president's thank-you letter."},{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"お待ちになって","r":"おまちになって","m":"wait (hon.)"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please wait here.","scene":"A receptionist asks a guest to wait."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-072||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"おVますになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますになります","patternReading":"おますになります","coreMeaning":"Standard honorific for a superior's action","slug":"forms","definition":"The general structure is **お + Vます + になります**. Conjugate the ending like a group 1 verb: になります, になりました, になってください, になりません. Not every verb works naturally; short native-Japanese verbs are preferred.","nuance":"To politely ask a superior to do something, use お＋Vます＋になってください. It means “please …” in a formal way. With suru (漢語) verbs, use ご〜になる, for example ご連絡になります.","structure":[{"lhs":"お + Vます(thân)","rhs":"になります","note":"present/future"},{"lhs":"お + Vます(thân)","rhs":"になりました","note":"past"},{"lhs":"お + Vます(thân)","rhs":"になってください","note":"polite request"},{"lhs":"お + Vます(thân)","rhs":"になりません","note":"negative"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department manager"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"お読みになりました","r":"およみになりました","m":"read (hon.)"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Has the department manager read that book?","scene":"An employee asks about the book."},{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"お待ちになって","r":"おまちになって","m":"wait (hon.)"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please wait here.","scene":"A receptionist invites a guest to wait."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-072||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"おVますになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますになります","patternReading":"おますになります","coreMeaning":"Standard honorific for a superior's action","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when you want to show respect for the subject of an action. The subject is usually the listener or a respected third person: a boss, teacher, or customer. In Japanese society, honorifics are used when speaking about older people or superiors.","nuance":"Typical settings include restaurants, hotel front desks, and workplaces. Do not use it with close friends because it creates distance. Some verbs have special honorific forms and do not need お…になる, such as います→いらっしゃいます, 食べます→召し上がります, 言います→おっしゃいます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"課長","r":"かちょう","m":"section chief"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"お使いになった","r":"おつかいになった","m":"used (hon.)"},{"t":"パソコン","m":"computer"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"こちら","m":"this one"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is the computer the section chief used.","scene":"An employee shows a computer to a customer."},{"tokens":[{"t":"どうぞ","m":"please"},{"t":"お休みになって","r":"おやすみになって","m":"rest (hon.)"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please get some rest.","scene":"A nurse tells a patient to rest."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-072||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with nearby patterns","sub":"おVますになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますになります","patternReading":"おますになります","coreMeaning":"Standard honorific for a superior's action","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish おVますになります from nearby honorific patterns to choose the right form. Each has its own use: V受身形 is also honorific but milder; special honorifics replace the pattern entirely; お…します humbles the speaker's own action.","nuance":"For the highest level of respect, use お…になる or a special honorific. For your own action, use humble お…します, never お…になる.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お帰りになりました","r":"おかえりになりました","m":"returned (hon.)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher has gone home.","scene":"Telling someone the teacher left school."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V受身形 (Vられます)","point":"お…になる is more formal and strongly honorific. 例: 先生は帰られました → 先生はお帰りになりました (higher respect)."},{"vs":"特別な尊敬語 (いらっしゃる, おっしゃる…)","point":"Special honorifics replace the whole structure; do not use お…になる. VD: 先生がいらっしゃいます (not おいますになります)."},{"vs":"おVますします (khiêm nhường)","point":"お…します is for your own action (humble), while お…になる is for a superior's action. VD: 私がご案内します (mine), 社長がご案内になります (the boss's)."}]}},"n4-072||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"おVますになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますになります","patternReading":"おますになります","coreMeaning":"Standard honorific for a superior's action","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with おVますになります usually involve the wrong subject or verb type. Remember: do not use this pattern for yourself, do not apply it to verbs with special honorifics, and use ご…になる with Sino-Japanese + する verbs.","nuance":"Common errors: 1) using it for yourself; 2) applying it incorrectly to special verbs or Sino-Japanese + suru verbs; 3) confusing it with humble language.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうぞ","m":"please"},{"t":"お読みになって","r":"およみになって","m":"read (hon.)"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please read it.","scene":"Inviting a teacher to read a speech."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私がお書きになりました。 → ✅ 私が書きました。 (お〜になる is not for your own actions)","❌ 先生はお食べになりました。 → ✅ 先生は召し上がりました。 (食べる has a special honorific; do not use お食べになる)","❌ お説明になります。 → ✅ ご説明になります。 (Sino-Japanese + する verbs use ご, not お)","❌ こちらでお待ちします。（bảo khách chờ）→ ✅ こちらでお待ちになってください。 (use お〜になる with customers)"],"compare":null}},"n4-072||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"おVますになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますになります","patternReading":"おますになります","coreMeaning":"Standard honorific for a superior's action","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory aid: お is a “polite outer wrapper,” while になる means “become” → the action becomes honorific.","nuance":"Imagine ordinary action as ます; adding お…になる puts the action “on a high shelf.” By contrast, お…する is humble and “lowers” the speaker. Remember: お + なる = RAISE (a superior), お + する = LOWER (yourself).","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-072||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"おVますになります · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますになります","patternReading":"おますになります","coreMeaning":"Standard honorific for a superior's action","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases with おVますになります in offices and service settings include お帰りになる, お読みになる, お書きになる, お待ちになる, お掛けになる, and お使いになる. Memorizing frequent verbs helps you respond naturally in honorific speech.","nuance":"When you see familiar verbs such as 帰る, 読む, 書く, 待つ, 座る, 使う, and 休む, remember their お〜になる forms.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうぞ","m":"please"},{"t":"お掛けになって","r":"おかけになって","m":"sit (hon.)"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please have a seat.","scene":"Welcoming a guest to a meeting room."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-073||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"特別な尊敬語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な尊敬語","patternReading":"とくべつなそんけいご","coreMeaning":"Irregular honorifics for superiors' actions","slug":"core","definition":"特別な尊敬語 are irregular honorifics: instead of using the お〜になります pattern, each common verb has its own special form. They elevate the actions of the listener or the person being discussed (a boss, teacher, or customer) to a highly polite level. Speakers use them to show respect and maintain distance.","nuance":"These are especially formal honorifics, often heard at work, in shops, and in hotels. Do not use them for yourself; use humble language instead. For these six verbs, the special honorific is the most natural choice; alternatives such as お〜になる or 〜られる may sound unnatural or incorrect.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"いらっしゃいます","r":"いらっしゃいます","m":"is present"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Where is the company president?","scene":"Looking for the company president at work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"召し上がります","r":"めしあがります","m":"have"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Would you like some coffee, Professor?","scene":"Offering coffee during a break"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-073||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & special honorific chart","sub":"特別な尊敬語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な尊敬語","patternReading":"とくべつなそんけいご","coreMeaning":"Irregular honorifics for superiors' actions","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the six irregular honorific pairs to memorize. There is no general rule for forming them, so learning them by meaning is easier. The main pairs are: いる/行く/来る → いらっしゃる, 食べる/飲む → 召し上がる, 言う → おっしゃる, する → なさる, 見る → ご覧になる, and くれる → くださる.","nuance":"With ます, the four verbs ending in さる—いらっしゃる・おっしゃる・なさる・くださる—change to 〜います, not 〜ります. 召し上がる and ご覧になる conjugate normally: 召し上がります and ご覧になります.","structure":[{"lhs":"いる・行く・来る","rhs":"いらっしゃる","note":"ます形: いらっしゃいます"},{"lhs":"食べる・飲む","rhs":"召し上がる","note":"ます形: 召し上がります"},{"lhs":"言う","rhs":"おっしゃる","note":"ます形: おっしゃいます"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"なさる","note":"ます形: なさいます"},{"lhs":"見る","rhs":"ご覧になる","note":"ます形: ご覧になります"},{"lhs":"くれる","rhs":"くださる","note":"ます形: くださいます"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"召し上がります","r":"めしあがります","m":"have"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What would you like to have?","scene":"Serving a customer at a café"},{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department head"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"おっしゃいました","r":"おっしゃいました","m":"said"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What did the department head say?","scene":"Asking about the department head's view"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-073||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"特別な尊敬語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な尊敬語","patternReading":"とくべつなそんけいご","coreMeaning":"Irregular honorifics for superiors' actions","slug":"usage","definition":"Special honorifics are common when asking, offering, or requesting something from a superior or customer. Japanese speakers use them to ask where a boss is, offer tea, or ask someone to view documents. When you meet one of the six verbs, immediately switch to its special form.","nuance":"For teachers, bosses, and customers, the special form is natural and safe. With close friends, honorifics can create distance, so avoid them. In business and service settings, these forms are often required.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ご覧になってください","r":"ごらんになってください","m":"please view"}],"vi":"President, please take a look at this.","scene":"Presenting documents to the president"},{"tokens":[{"t":"どうぞ","m":"please"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"召し上がって","r":"めしあがって","m":"enjoy"},{"t":"ください"}],"vi":"Please enjoy your meal.","scene":"Offering food at a restaurant"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"なさいます","r":"なさいます","m":"do"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What is the teacher doing?","scene":"Greeting a teacher at school"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-073||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with regular honorifics","sub":"特別な尊敬語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な尊敬語","patternReading":"とくべつなそんけいご","coreMeaning":"Irregular honorifics for superiors' actions","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare special honorifics with other regular honorific patterns to avoid mistakes. When a verb belongs to the special group, use its special form; use the general pattern only for other verbs.","nuance":"Special honorifics are highly polite and generally preferred. 〜られます is lighter and can be used when you do not want to sound overly formal. お〜になります is polite but does not apply to the special verbs.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"帰られました","r":"かえられました","m":"went home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The company president has gone home.","scene":"A coworker asks whether the president is still here"},{"tokens":[{"t":"社長","r":"しゃちょう","m":"company president"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"いらっしゃいません","r":"いらっしゃいません","m":"is not here"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The company president is no longer here.","scene":"A more polite reply about the president"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"お〜になります","point":"Use this regular pattern with ordinary verbs; the six special verbs require their own forms. Example: お食べになります (❌) → 召し上がります (✅)."},{"vs":"〜られます","point":"This is also honorific but lighter and works with many verbs; いらっしゃる and おっしゃる express stronger respect and sound more formal."}]}},"n4-073||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"特別な尊敬語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な尊敬語","patternReading":"とくべつなそんけいご","coreMeaning":"Irregular honorifics for superiors' actions","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include conjugating special verbs incorrectly with ます or using honorifics for yourself. Correct these immediately to avoid serious problems in communication.","nuance":"The most serious mistake is using an honorific for yourself, which violates the social hierarchy. Remember: honorifics are for other people only.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department head"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"いらっしゃいます","r":"いらっしゃいます","m":"is present"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Has the department head arrived yet?","scene":"Asking reception whether the department head is present"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私がいらっしゃいます → ✅ 私が参ります. Do not use the special honorific いらっしゃいます for yourself; use humble language.","❌ くださります → ✅ くださいます. Special verbs ending in さる change to います with ます, not 〜ります.","❌ おっしゃります → ✅ おっしゃいます. Likewise: おっしゃる → おっしゃいます, not おっしゃります.","❌ なさります → ✅ なさいます. なさる is special; do not treat it like an ordinary group-1 verb.","❌ お食べになります → ✅ 召し上がります. 食べる/飲む are special verbs, so do not use お〜になります.","❌ 私が社長にいらっしゃいます → ✅ 私が社長に伺います. Special honorifics describe superiors, never yourself."],"compare":null}},"n4-073||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"特別な尊敬語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な尊敬語","patternReading":"とくべつなそんけいご","coreMeaning":"Irregular honorifics for superiors' actions","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tips: いらっしゃる combines three verbs for going, coming, and being present; さる changes to す with ます (いらっしゃいます, おっしゃいます, なさいます, くださいます).","nuance":"There is no special nuance here; these are simply memory aids.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-073||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"特別な尊敬語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な尊敬語","patternReading":"とくべつなそんけいご","coreMeaning":"Irregular honorifics for superiors' actions","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently in listening exercises and everyday conversations. Memorizing them helps you respond more quickly.","nuance":"They are all polite questions or statements commonly used with superiors.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お客様","r":"おきゃくさま","m":"customer"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"こちら","m":"this side"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"召し上がります","r":"めしあがります","m":"dine"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Will you dine here?","scene":"Seating a customer at a restaurant"},{"tokens":[{"t":"部長","r":"ぶちょう","m":"department head"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"いらっしゃいます","r":"いらっしゃいます","m":"is present"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Has the department head arrived yet?","scene":"Asking reception whether the department head is present"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-074||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"おVますします／いたします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますします／いたします","patternReading":"おしますいたします","coreMeaning":"Basic humble form: lower yourself when acting for others","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern **お＋Vます＋します/いたします** is a **humble form (謙譲語)** used to lower the speaker's action and elevate the listener. It is one of the most basic polite patterns in Japanese. **いたします** is more formal than **します**, often used with customers or superiors.","nuance":"Use it for **your own** actions, never for someone else's. It is less common in casual speech because it sounds formal, but very common in business, service settings, and conversations with older people.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お","m":"polite prefix"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"お持ちします","r":"おもちします","m":"carry for you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will carry your luggage for you.","scene":"A hotel employee offers to carry a guest's suitcase."},{"tokens":[{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"documents"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ご用意いたします","r":"ごよういいたします","m":"prepare for you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will prepare the documents for you.","scene":"Before a meeting, an employee tells their boss: I'll prepare docs."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-074||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"おVますします／いたします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますします／いたします","patternReading":"おしますいたします","coreMeaning":"Basic humble form: lower yourself when acting for others","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern consists of **お/ご** + the **ます-stem** (Vます without ます) + **します** or **いたします**. Use **お** with **和語** verbs and **ご** with **漢語 (する verbs)**. **いたします** is more formal.","nuance":"Negative and past forms follow the usual rules for します: お持ちしません, お持ちしました, ご案内しません, ご案内しました.","structure":[{"lhs":"和語動詞","rhs":"お + Vます + します","note":"Example: 持つ→持ち→お持ちします"},{"lhs":"漢語動詞","rhs":"ご + N + します","note":"Example: 案内→ご案内します、説明→ご説明します"},{"lhs":"丁寧度","rhs":"します / いたします","note":"いたします is much more formal and polite"},{"lhs":"否定・過去","rhs":"おV stem しません / しました","note":"Example: お持ちしません、ご案内いたしました"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"お読みします","r":"およみします","m":"read for you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will read it for you.","scene":"In class, asking the teacher to let you read."},{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"ご説明いたします","r":"ごせつめいいたします","m":"explain for you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will explain it here.","scene":"A staff member speaks to a customer."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-074||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"おVますします／いたします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますします／いたします","patternReading":"おしますいたします","coreMeaning":"Basic humble form: lower yourself when acting for others","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern in hierarchical communication: employees speaking to superiors or customers, and service staff speaking to guests. It shows respect by lowering the speaker's own action.","nuance":"Friends rarely use it in daily life because it creates distance. In companies and customer service, however, it is a standard expression.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新製品","r":"しんせいひん","m":"new product"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ご案内します","r":"ごあんないします","m":"introduce for you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will introduce the new product.","scene":"An electronics clerk introduces a new TV to a customer."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ご説明いたします","r":"ごせつめいいたします","m":"explain for you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will explain it myself.","scene":"An engineer answers a customer's product question."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-074||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"おVますします／いたします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますします／いたします","patternReading":"おしますいたします","coreMeaning":"Basic humble form: lower yourself when acting for others","slug":"compare","definition":"Clearly distinguish **謙譲語** from **尊敬語** and **させていただきます** so you use each with the right person and purpose.","nuance":"Using the wrong form can cause misunderstanding or make your speech sound impolite.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ご案内します","r":"ごあんないします","m":"show the way"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will show you the way.","scene":"A hotel receptionist leads a foreign guest to their room."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"おVますになります","point":"お〜になる = the OTHER PERSON's action (honorific); お〜します = MY action (humble). Example: 先生がお読みになる / 私がお読みします"},{"vs":"Vさせていただきます","point":"させていただきます emphasizes ASKING PERMISSION; お〜します simply humbly describes your action, without asking. Example: ご説明させていただきます / ご説明します"},{"vs":"参る・申す・伺う","point":"Irregular verbs such as 行く, 言う, and 来る use special words like 参る, 申す, and 伺う; do not use おVします. Example: 参ります (not お行きします)"}]}},"n4-074||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"おVますします／いたします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますします／いたします","patternReading":"おしますいたします","coreMeaning":"Basic humble form: lower yourself when acting for others","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make these mistakes by speaking too directly or by confusing honorific and humble language. Each example pairs an incorrect sentence with a corrected one, helping you avoid misunderstandings with superiors and customers. Pay special attention to irregular verbs, since not every verb fits this pattern.","nuance":"These mistakes make the sentence grammatically wrong or inappropriate in politeness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すぐに","m":"immediately"},{"t":"ご連絡します","r":"ごれんらくします","m":"contact you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will contact you right away.","scene":"An employee responds after receiving a customer's request."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生、お読みしますか？ → ✅ 先生、お読みになりますか？（the teacher's action requires an honorific）","❌ お案内します → ✅ ご案内します（漢語 requires ご）","❌ お行きします → ✅ 参ります（irregular verbs use a special form）"],"compare":null}},"n4-074||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"おVますします／いたします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますします／いたします","patternReading":"おしますいたします","coreMeaning":"Basic humble form: lower yourself when acting for others","slug":"memory","definition":"The quickest memory aid: **お** goes with **Japanese-origin** words (読む, 持つ), while **ご** goes with **Sino-Japanese** words (案内, 説明). **いたします** is the polite form of **します**, so use **いたします** when you need extra formality.","nuance":"Imagine **お** as a person bowing and **ご** as a distinguished guest wearing a hat—both signal respect.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"はい","m":"yes"},{"t":"確認いたします","r":"かくにんいたします","m":"confirm for you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yes, I will confirm that.","scene":"Confirming information with a customer by phone."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-074||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"おVますします／いたします · N4","v2":{"pattern":"おVますします／いたします","patternReading":"おしますいたします","coreMeaning":"Basic humble form: lower yourself when acting for others","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed expressions are very common at work and in customer service. Memorizing them will help you respond quickly.","nuance":"These expressions often appear in JLPT N4 questions and office communication.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"また","m":"again"},{"t":"ご連絡します","r":"ごれんらくします","m":"contact you"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will contact you again.","scene":"Ending a phone call with a customer."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-075||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning & mindset","sub":"特別な謙譲語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な謙譲語","patternReading":"とくべつなけんじょうご","coreMeaning":"Special humble forms: lowering yourself to honor the other person","slug":"core","definition":"A group of **irregular** humble verbs that replace the original word entirely to show the speaker's humility. They are mainly used for actions by **yourself or someone in your group** when speaking to superiors or customers. Each verb has its own form to memorize.","nuance":"Formal and common at work. Compared with おVします, these forms are shorter and **more natural** in set greetings (例: 申します, いたします).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"申します","r":"もうします","m":"be called (humble)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My name is Tanaka.","scene":"Introducing yourself to a customer or superior."},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"御社","r":"おんしゃ","m":"your company"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"伺います","r":"うかがいます","m":"go (humble)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will visit your company tomorrow.","scene":"Meeting a business partner to sign a contract."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-075||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & irregular verbs","sub":"特別な謙譲語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な謙譲語","patternReading":"とくべつなけんじょうご","coreMeaning":"Special humble forms: lowering yourself to honor the other person","slug":"forms","definition":"These are base verb → special humble form pairs. Instead of conjugating by a rule, **replace the entire word**. For example, 行きます becomes 参ります, 言います becomes 申します, and します becomes いたします. Note that いただく is both the humble form of もらう and a replacement for 食べる/飲む when referring to the speaker.","nuance":"Use these forms in formal contexts. Also learn their ます forms: 参ります, 申します, いたします, いただきます, 差し上げます, 伺います.","structure":[{"lhs":"行く・来る","rhs":"参る","note":"go/come (humble)"},{"lhs":"いる","rhs":"おる","note":"be/stay (humble)"},{"lhs":"言う","rhs":"申す","note":"say (humble)"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"いたす","note":"do (humble)"},{"lhs":"食べる・飲む・もらう","rhs":"いただく","note":"eat/drink/receive (humble)"},{"lhs":"あげる","rhs":"差し上げる","note":"give/present (humble)"},{"lhs":"訪ねる・聞く","rhs":"伺う","note":"visit/ask (humble)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","r":"こちら","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"いただきます","r":"いただきます","m":"eat/receive (humble)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will eat here.","scene":"At a restaurant after being invited to eat."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"差し上げます","r":"さしあげます","m":"present (humble)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I would like to present this to my teacher.","scene":"Giving a gift to a teacher."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-075||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & real-life situations","sub":"特別な謙譲語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な謙譲語","patternReading":"とくべつなけんじょうご","coreMeaning":"Special humble forms: lowering yourself to honor the other person","slug":"usage","definition":"Use these forms for your own actions when speaking to a superior or business partner. They often appear in greetings, requests, thanks, and work explanations. **Do not use them for the listener or a third person** unless that person belongs to your group.","nuance":"These forms are especially important in the workplace. They make speech polite and professional, and can combine with other polite forms to soften the tone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"では","r":"では","m":"well then"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"失礼","r":"しつれい","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"いたします","r":"いたします","m":"do (humble)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Well then, I will take my leave.","scene":"Ending a meeting with a customer."},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"いただきます","r":"いただきます","m":"receive (humble)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Thank you; I will accept this.","scene":"A coworker offers you a snack."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-075||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused forms","sub":"特別な謙譲語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な謙譲語","patternReading":"とくべつなけんじょうご","coreMeaning":"Special humble forms: lowering yourself to honor the other person","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish three directions: **special humility** (lower yourself), **special honorifics** (raise the other person), and the request pattern **-ていただく**. Verbs such as 行く, 言う, する, 食べる, and あげる have separate pairs: one for talking about yourself and one for talking about others.","nuance":"Special humble forms use you as the subject; special honorifics use the listener or another respected person as the subject. Mixing them up can sound seriously inappropriate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"伺います","r":"うかがいます","m":"go (humble)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will come.","scene":"Telling your boss you will meet the business partner."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"おVします","point":"特別な謙譲語 uses special irregular verbs and does NOT follow お〜します; お行きします is WRONG."},{"vs":"特別な尊敬語","point":"Humility: YOUR action (lower yourself); honorific: ANOTHER person's action (raise them). Example: 参る vs いらっしゃる."},{"vs":"Vていただきます","point":"いただく in 特別な謙譲語 means 'eat/drink/receive'; Vていただきます means 'have someone do' (a completely different structure)."}]}},"n4-075||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner mistakes","sub":"特別な謙譲語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な謙譲語","patternReading":"とくべつなけんじょうご","coreMeaning":"Special humble forms: lowering yourself to honor the other person","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with special humble forms include using the wrong subject, inventing forms by rule, and using いただく for another person's actions.","nuance":"These mistakes make the social hierarchy sound **misaligned**. Japanese listeners may understand, but the speech can sound noticeably inappropriate.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"参ります","r":"まいります","m":"go/come (humble)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will come tomorrow.","scene":"Telling your superior you will come to the office."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 明日、先生は参ります。 → ✅ 明日、先生はいらっしゃいます。 (参る lowers yourself; do not use it for others)","❌ 部長、お行きします。 → ✅ 部長、参ります。 (the お〜します formula does not apply to irregular verbs)","❌ どうぞ、いただいてください。 → ✅ どうぞ、召し上がってください。 (use an honorific when inviting someone else to eat; not いただく)"],"compare":null}},"n4-075||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"特別な謙譲語 · N4","v2":{"pattern":"特別な謙譲語","patternReading":"とくべつなけんじょうご","coreMeaning":"Special humble forms: lowering yourself to honor the other person","slug":"memory","definition":"Ways to remember irregular forms through images or short stories.","nuance":"Do not rely on rote memorization; create your own associations.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-076||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vていただけませんか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただけませんか","patternReading":"ていただけませんか","coreMeaning":"Please (to a superior) do ~ for me — the most polite request","slug":"core","definition":"〜ていただけませんか is the **humble** form of てください, used to **ask** someone to do something for you, usually a superior or customer. It means: “Could you do ~ for me, please?” This is one of the **most polite and formal** request patterns at beginner level.","nuance":"This pattern emphasizes the speaker’s **humility**, treating the other person’s action as a favor (いただく = receive). Use it with bosses, older people, customers, and new acquaintances. With close friends or younger people, it sounds **overly formal** and unnatural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"more"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"ゆっくり","m":"slowly"},{"t":"話して","r":"はなして","m":"speak"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you speak a little more slowly, please?","scene":"A student asks a teacher to slow down in class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いて","r":"かいて","m":"write"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you write your name here, please?","scene":"A receptionist asks a customer to fill in their name"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"待って","r":"まって","m":"wait"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you wait a moment, please?","scene":"A server asks a customer to wait briefly"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-076||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vていただけませんか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただけませんか","patternReading":"ていただけませんか","coreMeaning":"Please (to a superior) do ~ for me — the most polite request","slug":"forms","definition":"Formula: **Vて** + **いただけませんか**. いただく is the humble form of もらう; いただける is its potential form (“can receive”); ませんか creates a soft negative invitation. Together, the sentence literally means “Could I receive the action of ~?” → “Could you please ~?”","nuance":"This is a **formal** pattern, used mainly at work or in customer service. Without か, いただけません has a completely different meaning, so remember that **か is required**.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いただけませんか","note":"V is the TE-form verb; most polite ending"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いただけますか","note":"Affirmative form - still polite but slightly firmer"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"もらえますか","note":"Moderately polite - used with friends and coworkers"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you help me for a moment, please?","scene":"Asking an older colleague for help at work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"読み方","r":"よみかた","m":"pronunciation"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you tell me how to read this kanji, please?","scene":"Asking a Japanese coworker"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-076||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vていただけませんか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただけませんか","patternReading":"ていただけませんか","coreMeaning":"Please (to a superior) do ~ for me — the most polite request","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch covers **real-life contexts**: asking for an explanation, requesting someone to wait, asking for a document check, or asking someone to take a photo. This pattern is also useful with strangers because it sounds **polite and non-pressuring**.","nuance":"This is a typical pattern in **Japanese workplaces and customer service**. The speaker shows respect and gratitude. With close friends or family, however, it sounds **distant** and may even sound humorous.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮って","r":"とって","m":"take"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, could you take a photo for me, please?","scene":"Asking a stranger to take a photo in a park"},{"tokens":[{"t":"課長","r":"かちょう","m":"section chief"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この"},{"t":"書類","r":"しょるい","m":"document"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"チェックして","m":"check"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you check this document for me, please?","scene":"An employee asks a superior to review it before submission"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電話番号","r":"でんわばんごう","m":"phone number"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"tell"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you tell me your phone number, please?","scene":"Asking a new acquaintance for contact information"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-076||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare: vs てください / てもらえる / いただける","sub":"Vていただけませんか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただけませんか","patternReading":"ていただけませんか","coreMeaning":"Please (to a superior) do ~ for me — the most polite request","slug":"compare","definition":"These patterns all express a request, but differ in **politeness** and **relative status**. Choosing the wrong one can sound rude or overly formal.","nuance":"〜てください is a mild command, suitable from teachers to students or superiors to subordinates. 〜てもらえますか works with friends and coworkers. 〜ていただけますか and 〜ていただけませんか are for customers, bosses, and older people.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"サイン","m":"signature"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you sign here, please?","scene":"An employee asks a customer to confirm a signature"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてください","point":"Polite command - the listener is a subordinate or close acquaintance; Vていただけませんか is a humble request - the listener is a superior. 例: ここに書いてください。 vs ここに書いていただけませんか。"},{"vs":"Vてもらえますか","point":"Normally polite, suitable for friends/coworkers; Vていただけませんか is much more formal, suitable for bosses/customers."},{"vs":"Vていただけますか","point":"Both are very polite, but the negative ませんか? sounds softer and easier to accept than the affirmative form."}]}},"n4-076||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vていただけませんか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただけませんか","patternReading":"ていただけませんか","coreMeaning":"Please (to a superior) do ~ for me — the most polite request","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common mistakes learners make with 〜ていただけませんか. Because this pattern is sensitive to **politeness**, errors are often not grammatical but contextual.","nuance":"The more polite the pattern, the more distance it creates. Used in the wrong context, it can sound **strange or excessively deferential**. Always identify the superior-subordinate relationship before choosing it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう"},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"one more time"},{"t":"説明","r":"せつめい","m":"explain"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you explain it one more time, please?","scene":"Asking an older person to explain an exercise again"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 手伝ういただけませんか。 → ✅ 手伝っていただけませんか。 (use Vて, not the dictionary form)","❌ 手伝いいただけませんか。 → ✅ 手伝っていただけませんか。 (use Vて, not Vます without ます)","❌ 手伝いていただけませんか。 → ✅ 手伝っていただけませんか。 (connect with Vて, not Vます)","❌ ここに書いていただけません。 → ✅ ここに書いていただけませんか。 (a negative question needs か)"],"compare":null}},"n4-076||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vていただけませんか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただけませんか","patternReading":"ていただけませんか","coreMeaning":"Please (to a superior) do ~ for me — the most polite request","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory aid: break 〜いただけませんか into **いただく** (receive) → **いただける** (can receive) → **ませんか** (could it be?). The literal idea is: “Could I receive the action of ~?” → a gentle request.","nuance":"Imagine **holding out your hands to receive** a favor. This helps you remember that the pattern is for **superiors**, not close friends.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-076||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vていただけませんか · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Vていただけませんか","patternReading":"ていただけませんか","coreMeaning":"Please (to a superior) do ~ for me — the most polite request","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common Vていただけませんか phrases appear frequently on the JLPT N4 and in everyday conversation: 教えて, 手伝って, 待って, 言って, 読んで, 書いて, and more.","nuance":"Learning familiar verb pairs helps you respond **quickly and naturally**. When practicing, change the subject and context to make the sentences more realistic.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"more"},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"one more time"},{"t":"言って","r":"いって","m":"say"},{"t":"いただけませんか","r":"いただけませんか","m":"could you please..."},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could you say that one more time, please?","scene":"Asking someone to repeat something unclear"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-077||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"がみえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"Can see / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"core","definition":"〜が見えます／聞こえます expresses the **natural ability** of sight or hearing: objects and sounds come to the speaker **automatically**, without effort. It is not an intentional action, but a **state of perception**.","nuance":"The nuance is **objective**, often used to describe scenery or report what the eyes or ears perceive. Do not use it for learned ability; use the potential form instead. For example, 日本語が**聞けます** means you can listen to Japanese thanks to study, while 隣の声が**聞こえます** means you naturally hear the voice next door. This distinction is key.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"富士山","r":"ふじさん","m":"Mt. Fuji"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えます","r":"みえます","m":"can see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can see Mt. Fuji from the window.","scene":"Standing at a hotel window; the view appears"},{"tokens":[{"t":"隣","r":"となり","m":"next door"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"声","r":"こえ","m":"voice"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえます","r":"きこえます","m":"can hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can hear voices from the room next door.","scene":"Working at home; a neighbor's voice carries over"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-077||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"がみえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"Can see / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"forms","definition":"Fixed patterns: **N + が + 見えます** for something perceived visually, or **N + が + 聞こえます** for something perceived aurally. N is the **subject** of the state of seeing or hearing, so **が** is required.","nuance":"These are **natural-perception** verbs, so they do not take を. The negative and past forms follow group 2: **ません／ました**. A Vて form such as 見えています also exists, but it is less common; focus on the three main forms first.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"が見えます","note":"N = object, scene, or person naturally seen"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"が聞こえます","note":"N = sound, voice, or music naturally heard"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"が見えません","note":"Negative: cannot see"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"が聞こえません","note":"Negative: cannot hear"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"夜","r":"よる","m":"at night"},{"t":"なので","r":"なので","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"anything"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"見えません","r":"みえません","m":"cannot see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Because it is nighttime, I cannot see anything.","scene":"Walking in the dark and unable to see the road"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"変な","r":"へんな","m":"strange"},{"t":"音","r":"おと","m":"sound"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえました","r":"きこえました","m":"heard"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yesterday, I heard a strange sound.","scene":"Recalling hearing a strange noise last night"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-077||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"がみえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"Can see / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **describe the current environment**: from a certain position, the speaker can see scenery or hear a sound. It is common in descriptions of scenery, homes, and routes.","nuance":"The sentence often includes a **place adverbial** (窓から、隣の部屋から、外で) to set the context. Do not use it for personal experiences such as having watched someone do something; use 見ました/聞きました instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"ホテル"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"海","r":"うみ","m":"sea"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えます","r":"みえます","m":"can see"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can see the sea from this hotel room.","scene":"Showing a friend the view from a hotel room"},{"tokens":[{"t":"外","r":"そと","m":"outside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"声","r":"こえ","m":"voices"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえます","r":"きこえます","m":"can hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can hear children outside.","scene":"Hearing children playing in a park"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-077||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing the potential form","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"がみえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"Can see / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"compare","definition":"This pattern is easily confused with the **potential form** (可能形) because both may translate as “can.” The key difference is that 見えます/聞こえます describe **something naturally reaching the senses**, while 見られます/聞けます describe **intentional ability** after effort or when conditions allow it.","nuance":"For example: **窓から富士山が見えます** – Fuji naturally comes into view; **チケットがあれば富士山が見られます** – if you have a ticket, you can go see it. Use 聞こえます for sounds and 聞けます for listening enabled by access or language ability.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"隣","r":"となり","m":"next door"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"テレビ"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"音","r":"おと","m":"sound"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"聞こえます","r":"きこえます","m":"can hear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can hear the TV from the room next door.","scene":"Studying while a neighbor's TV carries over"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"見られます","point":"見えます = see naturally; 見られます = can watch intentionally. Example: 窓から海が見えます / 時間があれば映画が見られます"},{"vs":"聞けます","point":"聞こえます = hear naturally; 聞けます = can listen or understand through study or practice. Example: 隣の声が聞こえます / 日本語が聞けます"}]}},"n4-077||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common usage mistakes","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"がみえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"Can see / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse this because English uses “see” and “hear” as transitive verbs, leading them to use **を**. But 見えます/聞こえます are **intransitive verbs** (自動詞), so the object is marked as the subject with **が**.","nuance":"Learners also use 見られます when they simply mean “see,” but 見られます emphasizes that conditions make intentional viewing possible. For a scene naturally visible before you, use 見えます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"ahead"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えません","r":"みえません","m":"cannot see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I cannot see ahead.","scene":"A driver complains about poor visibility in heavy rain"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 窓から海**を**見えます。 → ✅ 窓から海**が**見えます。 (States do not use を)","❌ 隣の部屋の声**を**聞こえます。 → ✅ 隣の部屋の声**が**聞こえます。","❌ 前**を**見えません。 → ✅ 前**が**見えません。"],"compare":null}},"n4-077||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"がみえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"Can see / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine standing still while scenery or sound **comes to your senses on its own**. 見える is like the English “be visible,” and 聞こえる is like “be audible”—nobody intentionally “does” the seeing or hearing.","nuance":"Memory rule: always use **が** because N is the subject of the state. Never use **を**; this is a crucial recognition cue.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n4-077||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nが見えます／聞こえます · N4","v2":{"pattern":"Nが見えます／聞こえます","patternReading":"がみえます／きこえます","coreMeaning":"Can see / hear naturally, without trying","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions in exams and daily life include **〜が見える** for scenery or objects and **〜が聞こえる** for sounds. **前が見えません** is used when the view is blocked, or figuratively when someone “cannot see the future.”","nuance":"In conversation, 聞こえますか？ means “Can you hear me?” On the phone: もしもし、聞こえますか？","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"山","r":"やま","m":"mountain"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上","r":"うえ","m":"top"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"町","r":"まち","m":"town"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えます","r":"みえます","m":"can see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can see the town from the mountaintop.","scene":"Climbing to a summit and looking out"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}}}}